window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"leonard-cohen-songs-of-love-and-hate":["Eight songs, darker and more anguished than Cohen's first two albums. It opens with Avalanche, his fingerpicked guitar racing underneath a voice that speaks as someone crippled and resentful; Dress Rehearsal Rag is a bleak monologue before a mirror; Famous Blue Raincoat is a letter written late at night to a man who once had an affair with the writer's partner; Joan of Arc is a duet between the saint and the fire. Strings and female voices appear like weather, then vanish, leaving just voice and guitar.","Cohen was a Montreal poet and novelist who had turned to song in his thirties. After the Isle of Wight festival of 1970, where he calmed a restless crowd of hundreds of thousands, he recorded this album with producer Bob Johnston, who had worked with Bob Dylan and Johnny Cash, in Nashville, with further recording and string arrangements done in London. One track, Sing Another Song, Boys, comes from the Isle of Wight performance itself.","Famous Blue Raincoat ends with the letter signed in Cohen's own name, which blurs the line between character and author, and Cohen later said he could no longer remember the real story behind the love triangle it describes. Listen also to the opening of Avalanche: the guitar pattern is a flamenco-derived figure Cohen learned in his youth from a Spanish guitarist in Montreal, and it drives the song like a nervous pulse.","Some reviewers found it unbearably gloomy and it sold less than his debut, and the joke that Cohen was music to slit your wrists by dates largely from this record. Its standing has risen steadily: Famous Blue Raincoat is among his most covered songs, Nick Cave recorded Avalanche, and many listeners now consider it his most intense album. The balance between Cohen's voice and the orchestrations is still debated.","Start with Famous Blue Raincoat and follow it as a letter, noticing how gently he sings the most accusing lines. Then hear Avalanche for the guitar's relentless drive. Compare Nick Cave's version of Avalanche, and Cohen's later I'm Your Man, where the same sensibility meets synthesizers."],"leonard-cohen-you-want-it-darker":["Nine songs sung by a voice that had sunk to a deep, cracked murmur. The title track opens on a throbbing bass and a synagogue choir, and Cohen, half speaking, addresses God directly, offering himself up and declaring he is ready. Other songs are quieter: Treaty is a plea for reconciliation with piano and strings; Traveling Light is a courtly farewell with a Greek-sounding guitar; Leaving the Table is a man retiring from love. The textures are sparse and warm, with organ, violin and male choir.","Cohen made the record in Los Angeles in his early eighties, seriously ill and working from a medical chair, with his son Adam Cohen producing and bringing the tracks to him to sing over. It was released by Columbia in October 2016, and Cohen died on 7 November, less than three weeks later. The choir and cantor come from the Shaar Hashomayim synagogue in Montreal, the congregation where Cohen grew up and his grandfather had served.","The title song uses a Hebrew word that is Abraham's reply when God calls him before the sacrifice of Isaac, meaning roughly here I am, and the cantor who sings it is from Cohen's childhood synagogue, so the record closes the circle of his life. Treaty appears twice, as a song and then as a string reprise that ends the album, a musical signature that the record is taking its leave.","Reviewers greeted it as one of his best records even before his death, and afterward it was heard as a deliberate farewell, an interpretation Cohen had partly invited in interviews while also saying he intended to live forever. The title track won a Grammy for Best Rock Performance. It sits with David Bowie's Blackstar, released the same year, as one of the great late albums made in the shadow of death.","Play the title track loudly enough to feel the bass and listen for the moment the choir first enters behind his voice. Then hear Treaty and its closing string reprise. Compare his first album, Songs of Leonard Cohen, to hear the same themes of devotion and surrender fifty years earlier, sung by a young man."],"elvis-presley-suspicious-minds":["Mark James's song about a couple trapped by jealousy, turned into a four-minute soul-pop epic. It opens on a tense, circling guitar figure, builds through Presley's anguished verses to a chorus about being caught in a trap, then breaks into a slow bridge where he pleads for trust before the music drives back in with horns, strings and gospel backing voices. The record seems to end, fades out, then fades back in for another round, as if the argument cannot stop.","After years of formula films, Presley had returned to live performance with the 1968 television special, and in January 1969 he went to American Sound Studio in Memphis to record with producer Chips Moman and the studio's house band, the same players heard on many soul and pop hits of the day. It was his first serious session in his home city since the Sun days. Released that August, the single became his first US number one in seven years and his last.","The famous false fade near the end was not in Moman's mix; it was added afterward by Presley's regular producer Felton Jarvis, who also added overdubs, and Moman is said to have disliked the change. Listen for the join: the fade-out, a moment of near-silence, and then the song returning at the chorus. Mark James's own earlier recording had gone nowhere.","Hailed at the time as proof that Presley was an artist again rather than a film star, it is still widely named his finest later record. The arguments are about production: some prefer Moman's leaner original mix, released on later reissues, to Jarvis's version with its false ending. Presley made the song the centrepiece of his Las Vegas shows, where it grew longer and more theatrical.","Listen for the slow bridge, where the tempo drops and the voice nearly breaks, and then for the fade and the return. Next, hear the From Elvis in Memphis sessions for the same band in a more country-soul vein, and Dusty Springfield's Dusty in Memphis from the same months for another singer reborn in that city."],"buddy-holly-everyday":["Just over two minutes of the gentlest rock and roll ever recorded. There is no drum kit: the rhythm is someone slapping his knees, a soft acoustic guitar strums, a stand-up bass plucks, and a celesta, the bell-like keyboard of the Nutcracker's Sugar Plum Fairy, plays a tinkling music-box line. Holly's voice is light and hiccupping, singing about love coming closer day by day with total, almost childlike confidence. It is the sound of a lullaby sung by a teenager.","Holly recorded at Norman Petty's small studio in Clovis, New Mexico, a long drive from his home in Lubbock, Texas, where Petty let him experiment for hours rather than book a three-hour session as the big labels did. Released in 1957 as the B-side of Peggy Sue, the song did not chart in its own right. Petty, who managed and produced Holly, took a share of the writing credit, as he did on many of Holly's songs.","The knee-slapping is the Crickets' drummer Jerry Allison, patting his thighs because a kit was too loud for the delicate arrangement, and the celesta is reportedly played by Vi Petty, Norman Petty's wife. Listen for how dry and close the sound is: Petty's room was small, and the intimacy is a studio effect rather than a mood, an early case of a producer shaping a rock record's colour as much as its notes.","Ignored as a flip side at first, it grew into one of Holly's best-loved songs after his death, covered by artists from Don McLean to James Taylor and many more. Critics now point to it as proof of how much range Holly had within the new style, from the drive of Peggy Sue to this quiet charm, and as a sign of the chamber-pop textures that the Beatles and Brian Wilson would later explore.","Listen with headphones for the knee slaps and the celesta, then play Peggy Sue, its A-side, to hear the same musicians at full force. Compare the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds, where bells and unusual percussion are used to similar tender effect, nearly a decade later."],"buddy-holly-words-of-love":["A soft, dreamy two-minute love song in which Holly sings in harmony with himself. The voice is layered twice, in close parallel lines, and the chiming electric guitar is layered as well, so a single musician sounds like a small group. The tempo is slow and swaying, the drums brushed, and the song simply asks a lover to hear the words of love the singer whispers. What you recognise it by is the shimmer of those twin guitar lines and the matched voices.","Recorded at Norman Petty's studio in Clovis, New Mexico, in April 1957 and released as a Holly solo single on Coral that summer. Overdubbing, recording one part and then adding another on top, was still unusual; Les Paul and Mary Ford had made it famous, but few rock and roll records used it. Petty's patient approach to studio time made the experiment possible. The single did not chart for Holly, while a cover by the vocal group the Diamonds did.","Everything you hear as a duet is one man: Holly overdubbed his own harmony and his own second guitar, a technique that would become basic to pop records within a decade. The Beatles, who learned from Holly's records, recorded the song on Beatles for Sale in 1964 with John Lennon and Paul McCartney splitting the two vocal lines, so the harmony that Holly faked became a real partnership.","Commercially a flop, the record has been reappraised as one of the first rock and roll recordings to treat the studio as a compositional tool. Critics trace a line from it to the multitracked harmonies of the Beatles and the Beach Boys. It is sometimes called Holly's most forward-looking record, though it remains less well known to general listeners than his hits.","Listen for the two voices and try to hear that they are the same man. Then play the Beatles' version on Beatles for Sale to hear two singers take the parts. The Everly Brothers' close harmonies from the same year are the natural comparison: real brothers doing what Holly built with tape."],"lee-perry-return-of-django":["An instrumental credited to the Upsetters, Lee Perry's studio band. A honking, gravelly tenor saxophone plays a cheerful, slightly off-kilter tune over a choppy organ and guitar shuffle and a bouncing bass. The rhythm is early reggae, faster and jerkier than the later roots style, and the whole thing lasts under three minutes. The title nods to the Italian western Django, and the sax line has a cowboy swagger that sounds both comic and tough.","Perry had worked for Coxsone Dodd's Studio One and for Joe Gibbs before setting up his own Upsetter label in Kingston in 1968. Jamaican audiences loved spaghetti westerns, which filled the island's cinemas, and several producers named records after them. Released in Britain on Trojan, the track became a surprise UK Top 5 hit in the autumn of 1969, carried by the skinhead youth of London and the Midlands, who danced to Jamaican records.","The saxophonist is usually identified as Val Bennett, a veteran of the Kingston dance-band scene, and his raspy tone was deliberate: Perry favoured rough, characterful sounds over clean ones even at this early stage. Listen to the drop-outs and odd little effects in the mix, hints of the producer who would later turn the studio into a laboratory at the Black Ark.","At the time it was a novelty hit, and some Jamaican musicians regarded the Upsetters' instrumentals as lightweight. Historians now see it as one of the records that carried reggae into British popular culture before Bob Marley, and as early evidence of Perry's instinct for texture. It is widely anthologised in collections of skinhead and early reggae singles.","Enjoy it as a dance record first, then listen to the bass and organ interlocking under the sax, the rhythm that reggae was just then inventing. Compare Toots and the Maytals' Do the Reggay from the same moment, and then jump to Perry's Super Ape to hear how far the same producer travelled in seven years."],"lee-perry-roast-fish-collie-weed-and-corn-bread":["Perry's own vocal album from the last years of the Black Ark, and one of the strangest records in reggae. His thin, eccentric voice mutters, chants, laughs and preaches over thick, murky rhythms full of phased cymbals, cowbells, odd vocal noises and echo. The songs range from a tribute to the simple food of the title and the Rastafarian diet to warnings about Babylon and wry, comic street scenes. It sounds like listening to a sermon through a wall of smoke.","By 1978 Perry had built the Black Ark studio behind his house in Washington Gardens, Kingston, recorded Junior Murvin, Max Romeo and the Congos there, and seen Island Records reject several of his productions as too uncommercial. Jamaica was torn by political violence between the two main parties. This album was released on Perry's own small Lion of Judah label with little promotion, and it sold poorly; within a few years the studio had closed and Perry had left for Europe.","The strange sounds are made with very modest gear: a four-track recorder, a Roland Space Echo tape delay and a phaser pedal, which Perry used to bounce and blur tracks until four channels felt like dozens. Listen for his habit of burying murmured asides and stray sounds deep in the mix; they are not accidents but part of a private cosmology of nature, God and the studio as a living thing.","It was barely noticed on release and long hard to find, but reissues in the 1990s and 2000s made it a cult favourite, and many now hear it as the purest expression of Perry's own vision rather than his vision serving other singers. Some find his singing an acquired taste; admirers say the voice is the point, a prophet's voice rather than a crooner's.","Let the sound wash over you first: the phased cymbals and deep bass are the Black Ark signature. Then listen for the odd noises buried in the mix. Compare Super Ape, his instrumental-dub album of two years earlier, and the Congos' Heart of the Congos, made in the same room."],"lee-perry-arkology":["A three-disc anthology of about fifty tracks from Perry's Black Ark studio, 1975 to 1979, the most important gathering of his golden period. It includes Junior Murvin's Police and Thieves, sung in a high, keening falsetto; Max Romeo's War Ina Babylon; tracks by the Congos, the Heptones and George Faith; and many dub versions, where Perry strips the vocal away and lets bass, drums and echo take over. The sound throughout is dense and swirling, with phased cymbals, deep bass and a haze of reverb.","Island Records, which had issued several Black Ark productions in the 1970s and rejected others, assembled this compilation in 1997, when Perry's reputation had been revived by hip-hop, trip-hop and electronic producers who admired his methods. The Black Ark itself had closed around 1979 and burned in the early 1980s, and many master tapes were lost or scattered, so the set was for many listeners the first chance to hear this music together, in good sound.","Listen for how often a song and its dub version follow one another: the pairing lets you hear exactly what Perry removed and added, and it shows that the dub is not a remix of a finished song but a second composition built from the same multitrack. Police and Thieves was later covered by the Clash on their first album, a sign of how directly punk London listened to these records.","It was greeted as a definitive document, and it is often cited as the best way into Perry's work, though collectors note the gaps: some famous productions sit with other labels and are missing, and the compilers had to work around lost tapes. Debate continues over how much of the Black Ark sound was Perry's gear and how much his unique ear; the album's variety argues for the ear.","Start with Police and Thieves and then its dub, listening for what disappears and what the echo leaves behind. Then pick a Congos track for the harmony singing. Afterwards try the Clash's cover of Police and Thieves, and King Tubby's dub records, to hear the other great engineer of the style."],"marvin-gaye-here-my-dear":["A double album about the collapse of Gaye's marriage to Anna Gordy, told from their first meeting through the bitterness of divorce to a hope of rebirth. The grooves are slow, loose and jazzy, often built from one vamp repeated for minutes while Gaye layers dozens of his own voices, murmuring, arguing and falsettoing over it. Songs like Anger, When Did You Stop Loving Me, When Did I Stop Loving You and the spoken-word title track carry an unusual frankness; the recurring theme returns in several forms.","Gordy was the sister of Motown's founder, Berry Gordy, and seventeen years older than Gaye. Under the divorce settlement Gaye was to pay a large sum from his royalties for his next album, and he first planned to make something careless. Instead, working at his own studio in Los Angeles during a period of tax trouble and drug use, he made a confessional record about the marriage itself. Motown released it in December 1978.","The album is full of self-quotation: melodies from When Did You Stop Loving Me return in altered form, and the whole set is built like a suite whose themes circle back. Listen to A Funky Space Reincarnation, a long science-fiction funk track that seems out of place until you hear it as an escape fantasy from the courtroom. Anna Gordy reportedly considered suing over it.","It was widely panned and sold poorly; reviewers found it self-indulgent and bitter, and Motown barely promoted it. Reappraisal began in the 1990s, and it is now often called one of the great breakup albums, a precursor of later confessional R&B. Some still find it too long and unfocused; its admirers say the length is the point, a record that refuses to tidy up grief.","Listen to it as one long piece, noticing how melodies from early tracks reappear later. Start with When Did You Stop Loving Me, When Did I Stop Loving You. Then compare What's Going On for the same suite-like design put to social rather than private ends, and Frank Ocean's Blonde for a modern descendant."],"marvin-gaye-sexual-healing":["A slow, swaying love song built on a Roland TR-808 drum machine: a dry, ticking beat with a clap and a small electronic cowbell, a warm synthesizer bass, and a rocking reggae-tinged guitar chop. Over this Gaye sings in a soft, pleading voice, stacked with his own falsetto harmonies, asking a lover for comfort and physical closeness as a cure for loneliness. The sound is spacious and cool, and the drum machine's pattern is what everyone recognises from the first bar.","Gaye had fled America's tax authorities and his own troubles and was living in Ostend, Belgium, after a period in London. There he wrote the song with keyboardist Odell Brown, and it was released in October 1982 as the lead single of Midnight Love, his first album after leaving Motown for Columbia. It topped the R&B chart for many weeks, reached the pop Top 3 and won Gaye his first Grammy awards, a comeback a year and a half before his death.","The writer David Ritz, who was working on Gaye's biography, claimed he suggested the title and sued for a co-writing credit, which he eventually received, a dispute still reflected on the song's label. Listen to how few elements there are: the machine plays almost the same pattern throughout, and the drama comes from Gaye's layered voices changing around it, a method he had long used on his own records.","An immediate hit, it was also one of the first major soul records to make the drum machine the centre of the groove rather than a novelty, and it helped establish the 808 as an instrument. Critics now place it at the root of the slow electronic R&B that runs through the 1980s and into hip-hop. Some fans still prefer the richer live band on his Motown records.","Listen first to the beat alone for the opening seconds and notice how thin and dry it is; then listen to the voices filling the space. Compare Let's Get It On, with live musicians, from nine years earlier, and then Kanye West's 808s & Heartbreak to hear the same machine carry heartbreak instead of desire."],"phil-spector-hes-a-rebel":["A two-and-a-half-minute girl-group record, released as the Crystals but actually sung by Darlene Love and the Blossoms. Gene Pitney's song has a girl defending her boyfriend, a leather-jacketed outsider whom everyone disapproves of, insisting he is good and loyal. Love's big gospel-trained voice drives it over a band that is already heavy, with pianos, guitars and handclaps doubled up and a saxophone break; it is the Wall of Sound in its first full form, but brighter and brisker than it would become.","Spector heard the song while it was destined for the singer Vikki Carr and raced to record it first in Los Angeles at Gold Star Studios, with arranger Jack Nitzsche and the session players later nicknamed the Wrecking Crew. The actual Crystals were in New York and unavailable, so he used Love and her group and released it under the Crystals' name on his own Philles label. It went to number one in the United States in November 1962.","This is the case that most clearly shows why Spector's records belong to Spector: the group named on the label is not on the record at all. Listen to the drums and piano, doubled and smeared by the echo chamber at Gold Star; that blur, rather than any particular singer, is the brand. Darlene Love received little credit for years and later made a separate career partly on the strength of stories like this.","At the time it was a hit and nothing more; now it is cited as the first great Spector production at Gold Star and as an early example of the girl group's rebel-boyfriend theme, which the Shangri-Las would take to melodrama. Its story has become a standard example in arguments about credit in the pop industry, and Love's induction into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 2011 was in part a correction.","Listen for Love's voice cutting through the band at the chorus, then for the saxophone solo. Next, play Be My Baby from a year later to hear the sound grown slower and deeper, and the Shangri-Las' Leader of the Pack for the same story turned into tragedy."],"phil-spector-back-to-mono":["A four-disc box set gathering Spector's productions from the Teddy Bears' To Know Him Is to Love Him through the Crystals, the Ronettes, Darlene Love, Bob B. Soxx and the Blue Jeans, the Righteous Brothers and Ike and Tina Turner's River Deep – Mountain High, with the complete Christmas album as its last disc. Heard in sequence, it is the story of one sound: many guitars, several pianos, castanets, strings and booming drums, all played at once in one room and mixed together in mono.","In the 1960s Spector insisted on mono mixes, believing a single-speaker transistor radio was where his records would live, and he wore a badge with the slogan that gives this set its name. By 1991, when ABKCO, the label that controlled the Philles catalogue, issued the box, many compilations had used fake stereo or poor tapes. This set restored the mono masters and became the standard edition of the work, released long before Spector's 2009 murder conviction transformed how his name was heard.","The key to the sound is that the instruments are not separable: several players doubled the same parts, and the result was fed through Gold Star's echo chambers so the attack of each note blurs into the next. Listen across the discs for Hal Blaine's drums, a constant through most of the sessions, and for how the tempos slow and the sound grows heavier from 1962 to 1966, peaking in River Deep – Mountain High.","The box was received as the definitive document of the Wall of Sound and remains the best way to hear it. Opinion on Spector the man has changed utterly since his conviction for the killing of Lana Clarkson, and many listeners now struggle with how to hear the records; others argue the work belongs as much to the singers, arrangers and session players as to him. The music's influence, on Brian Wilson above all, is not in dispute.","Take it one disc at a time rather than straight through. Listen for the way no single instrument stands out, then hear Pet Sounds to see how Brian Wilson learned from this sound and opened it up. Play it in mono, as intended, on a single speaker if you can."],"wendy-carlos-well-tempered-synthesizer":["The sequel to Switched-On Bach widens the lens from one composer to four. Side one moves through Monteverdi, a group of Domenico Scarlatti's harpsichord sonatas and a suite from Handel's Water Music; side two gives the whole of Bach's Fourth Brandenburg Concerto. Every note is played on a Moog modular synthesizer and built up line by line on tape. The recognisable sound is clean, bright and bouncy: plucked, reedy and whistling timbres that each carry one strand of the counterpoint.","Switched-On Bach had become one of the best-selling classical albums ever made in 1968, and Columbia wanted another quickly. Carlos worked in a small New York home studio with producer Rachel Elkind, patching sounds by hand on an instrument that could play only one note at a time and had to be retuned constantly as its oscillators drifted with the heat. The Moog was still a laboratory curiosity to most listeners; this record helped make it a household word.","Listen to how the voices of a Scarlatti sonata are dressed differently: the two hands of a single harpsichord part become two distinct instruments, so crossings that blur on a real keyboard become easy to follow. In the Brandenburg, the two recorder parts are given matching but not identical colours, and Carlos adds tiny shifts of attack and vibrato, played by hand, that no sequencer did for her. None of it is automatic; the phrasing is performance.","It sold far less than its predecessor, which set a mark nothing could meet, and some reviewers treated it as a novelty follow-up. Musicians have been kinder: many electronic players rate its arranging above the first record, with more daring colour choices and a surer sense of Baroque rhythm. The standing argument remains whether synthesizer Bach is interpretation or translation; Carlos always insisted it was simply another way of playing, with its own touch and articulation.","Start with the Scarlatti sonatas: short, fast and full of darting leaps, they show the synthesizer at its wittiest. Then hear the Brandenburg next to a period-instrument recording, such as one by an early-music ensemble with recorders, and notice which lines Carlos brings forward that the orchestra hides. Seek the later remastered edition, which Carlos supervised, for cleaner tape sound."],"wendy-carlos-tron":["Carlos's score for Disney's computer-world adventure blends a full symphony orchestra, a choir and a large pipe organ with her own analogue and early digital synthesizers, so that the acoustic and the electronic trade phrases rather than sitting in separate boxes. The main theme is a broad, noble fanfare; around it come pulsing electronic ostinatos, glassy chords and cues in shifting, uneven metres for the light-cycle chases. The recognisable sound is brass and synthesizer doubling the same heroic line.","Tron, directed by Steven Lisberger, was among the first feature films to use extended computer-generated imagery, and Disney wanted music that sounded like the inside of a machine but still told an old-fashioned hero story. Carlos had scored Kubrick's A Clockwork Orange and The Shining; here she got a real orchestra, the London Philharmonic, alongside her studio. The soundtrack album also carried two songs by the rock band Journey, a sign of how the studio hoped to reach teenagers.","The orchestral sessions were reportedly rushed and the players struggled with Carlos's irregular metres, so she spent weeks in her own studio editing, reinforcing and sometimes replacing orchestral passages with synthesizer lines. Listen for places where a string phrase seems to acquire an electronic shadow: that seam is often a repair made into a colour. The organ, too, is used less for grandeur than for sustained electric-sounding chords that bridge the two worlds.","The film was a modest box-office performer on release and the score drew little attention next to the imagery. Its reputation grew with the film's cult, and later electronic musicians came to treat it as a model of hybrid scoring; when Daft Punk scored the 2010 sequel, Tron: Legacy, they followed its orchestra-plus-synthesizer plan closely. Critics still debate whether the orchestral writing, compromised by the sessions, fully matches Carlos's electronic imagination.","Start with the main title and the light-cycle cue, and listen for the moment the brass and synthesizer lock onto the same tune. Then compare Vangelis's Blade Runner from the same year, which lets the synthesizer do everything alone. Carlos later issued an expanded edition of the score; it contains more of the electronic cues than the original album."],"frank-zappa-uncle-meat":["A double album by the Mothers of Invention, billed as the soundtrack to a film that did not yet exist. Most of it is instrumental: tightly composed chamber-rock miniatures for woodwinds, electric keyboards, vibraphone and marimba, many of them variations on the same crooked, cheerful main theme, broken up by doo-wop parodies, studio chatter and tape collage. The finale is a long, multi-part version of King Kong, with solos on reeds and keyboards. The recognisable sound is mallets and saxophones chirping in unison at impossible speed.","Recorded in 1967 and 1968, largely in New York, where Zappa had access to one of the first twelve-track tape machines in a commercial studio. The Mothers now included classically trained players such as Ian Underwood and Bunk Gardner, and Zappa was writing more like a composer than a bandleader, filling notebooks with scores. The film, a science-fiction satire, stalled for lack of money; a cut-together version of the footage finally appeared on video in 1987.","Many of the small ensemble pieces were built by overdubbing the same few players many times, often with the tape running at a different speed, so a marimba or a clarinet comes back sounding higher, brighter and faster than any person could play it. That trick is why the Uncle Meat theme sounds like a toy orchestra. Listen too for how Dog Breath and the Uncle Meat Variations share material with the main theme, turned inside out.","Reviewers in 1969 were puzzled by its mixture of avant-garde composition, rock and apparently pointless dialogue; some heard self-indulgence. Musicians took note, especially those moving towards jazz-rock, and the album is now often called the most complete statement of the early Mothers. The spoken fragments remain divisive, and the CD reissue's extra dialogue from the film is usually singled out as the part listeners skip.","Begin with the Uncle Meat main title and Dog Breath, then jump to King Kong on the last side. Listen for the moment the ensemble snaps from a gentle melody into a spiky, stop-start figure. After that, hear Varèse's Ionisation, the piece Zappa adored as a teenager, and notice the shared love of percussion. The original vinyl sequence reads more clearly than the expanded CD."],"frank-zappa-the-yellow-shark":["A live concert record of Zappa's ensemble music, played by Germany's Ensemble Modern, a large chamber group of winds, brass, strings, keyboards and a heavy percussion section. It gathers old tunes rearranged, such as Dog Breath, Uncle Meat and Be-Bop Tango, alongside new concert pieces and ferociously intricate works first created on Zappa's Synclavier computer. The shape is a programme of about twenty short and medium pieces. The recognisable sound is brittle, exact, fast-moving ensemble playing with sudden jokes.","The Ensemble Modern approached Zappa, and after long rehearsals in his Los Angeles studio the programme was premiered at the Frankfurt Festival in September 1992, then taken to Berlin and Vienna. Zappa was already seriously ill with prostate cancer; he conducted a few pieces himself and left most to Peter Rundel. The album appeared in November 1993, and Zappa died in December, so it stands as his farewell. Earlier orchestral projects with other orchestras had left him bitter about under-rehearsed performances.","G-Spot Tornado was composed on the Synclavier and released in 1986 as music Zappa thought no humans could play; here a chamber ensemble plays it, and listeners in the hall can be heard reacting. Welcome to the United States sets the official text of a visitor's entry form for America as a mock-bureaucratic song, a pointed joke for a German audience. Listen too for players shouting, breathing and hitting objects: Zappa writes theatre into the score.","The Frankfurt concerts were greeted with long ovations, and the record was welcomed as proof that Zappa's concert music worked when it was properly rehearsed. Zappa himself reportedly called it the most accurate performance of his orchestral writing he had heard. Classical critics remain divided about how much of his idiom is original and how much is Varèse and Stravinsky filtered through rock; admirers answer that the humour and rhythmic precision are entirely his own.","Start with G-Spot Tornado, the closing showpiece, and listen for the moment the melody is passed from instrument to instrument without a seam. Then try Outrage at Valdez, a slow, bleak piece about the oil spill, for the other side of him. Compare the old Mothers version of Uncle Meat with the one here to hear a rock tune become a chamber score."],"hancock-empyrean-isles":["Four long originals for a quartet with no saxophone: Freddie Hubbard on cornet, Hancock on piano, Ron Carter on bass and Tony Williams on drums. The pieces imagine an invented island group. One Finger Snap and Oliloqui Valley are fast, harmonically slippery post-bop; Cantaloupe Island is a relaxed, loping groove over a repeating piano riff; The Egg is a quarter-hour of open, semi-free exploration built on a short repeated figure. The recognisable sound is that insistent, chunky piano vamp on Cantaloupe Island.","Hancock was twenty-four and a member of Miles Davis's second great quintet, alongside Carter and Williams, when he recorded this for Blue Note in 1964. The label's house style was hard bop, but this rhythm section was loosening time and harmony in Miles's band every night, and here they had room to push further without Miles. Hubbard, a star soloist, brought brilliance; the absence of a saxophone left more space for the piano.","Cantaloupe Island reuses the idea of Hancock's earlier hit Watermelon Man: a funky, repeated left-hand pattern that sounds simple while the harmony moves in unexpected steps. Listen to Tony Williams throughout, still a teenager, as he treats the ride cymbal as a lead voice and keeps shifting the pulse under the soloists. In 1993 the British group Us3 sampled Cantaloupe Island for a worldwide hit, bringing the tune to hip-hop listeners.","The album was admired on release as a strong Blue Note session, then gradually recognised as one of the key records of mid-1960s post-bop, the link between Hancock's early funk-tinged tunes and Maiden Voyage. Cantaloupe Island became a jazz standard. Opinion divides over The Egg: some hear a bold step toward freer forms, others a sketch that goes on too long.","Play Cantaloupe Island first, then One Finger Snap, and notice how the same four players switch from a laid-back groove to a sprint. On The Egg, follow the bass and drums rather than the soloists. Afterwards hear Maiden Voyage, recorded the next year, and a Miles Davis quintet album of the period to hear the rhythm section at home."],"hancock-speak-like-a-child":["A piano-trio record with a small, unusual horn choir behind it: flugelhorn, alto flute and bass trombone, playing only written ensemble parts and never soloing. Hancock's piano carries almost all the improvising. The six tracks are mostly gentle and dreamy, including the title piece, Toys and Goodbye to Childhood, with the fast Riot and The Sorcerer for contrast. The recognisable sound is soft, low, blended horn chords floating like a cloud behind the piano.","Recorded for Blue Note in March 1968, while Hancock was still with Miles Davis and the jazz world was splitting between free jazz and the first electric experiments. Hancock was hearing the orchestration of Gil Evans, who had arranged Miles's big orchestral albums, and wanted that texture in a small band. The cover photograph, a silhouetted couple kissing, shows Hancock with Gigi, who became his wife.","Riot and The Sorcerer had first been recorded by the Miles Davis Quintet; hearing Hancock's own versions shows how differently he imagined them when he controlled the colours. The three horns are chosen to occupy the middle and low registers, so they wrap around the piano instead of competing with it. Listen for chords that seem to have no clear home key: Hancock sustains them long enough that the ambiguity becomes the mood.","Initially received as a pleasant, perhaps too quiet, Blue Note date, it has grown in reputation as one of Hancock's most personal albums and a model for arranged small-group jazz. Pianists and arrangers cite the voicings constantly. Some listeners find the horns too restrained; others hear exactly the restraint as the point, a jazz record about innocence rather than display.","Start with the title track and listen only to the horns for one chorus, then only to the piano. Next play Riot to hear the same band at speed. Compare Gil Evans's Out of the Cool for the ancestry of the sound and Miles Davis's Filles de Kilimanjaro, recorded the same year, to hear where Hancock's day job was heading."],"king-tubby-grass-roots-of-dub":["An early dub album that sets two of Jamaica's great studio minds side by side, with one side of mixes credited to King Tubby and the other to Lee Perry's Upsetter camp. Reggae rhythm tracks are stripped to drum and bass, then instruments and fragments of voice are dropped in and out, echoed and filtered. The recognisable sound is a heavy bass line alone in a large space, with a guitar chop suddenly flung into echo and left to decay.","By 1974 Tubby, an electronics engineer who built and repaired equipment for his own Home Town Hi-Fi sound system, was running a small mixing studio in the Waterhouse district of Kingston. Producers brought him finished rhythm tracks and he made instrumental versions for the flip sides of singles, which sound-system deejays used to talk over. Albums of these versions were a new idea, aimed at sound-system fans and the Jamaican diaspora in New York and London.","Exactly who mixed which track is still argued; the sleeve's billing makes it a contest between the two men, but several tunes may be Tubby's work on Perry productions or the other way round. Listen to the drop-outs: the whole band vanishes for a bar and the reverb tail becomes the music. Tubby's trademark was a filter that sweeps a sound from thin to full, which you can hear rising under the hi-hat.","Little noticed outside Jamaica at first, the album became a collector's grail as interest in dub grew in Britain during the late 1970s and again in the 1990s, when reissues brought it to electronic producers. It is now valued as a document of the moment dub became an album form. Critics disagree on which side is stronger, which is part of its appeal.","Listen on speakers or headphones with real low end; without the bass, dub makes no sense. Pick one track and follow only what disappears and returns. Then play Lee Perry's Blackboard Jungle Dub from the previous year for comparison, and afterwards Augustus Pablo's King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown for Tubby at his most refined."],"king-tubby-the-roots-of-dub":["Twelve dub versions of rhythms produced by Bunny Lee and played by his session band, the Aggrovators. The tracks are short, around three minutes, and each takes a vocal hit of the day and pulls it apart: the voice survives only as a stray syllable, the drums crack like a whip in echo, and the bass carries the melody. The recognisable sound is the rolling, splashing hi-hat pattern associated with Bunny Lee's productions of the mid-1970s, sent spinning through reverb.","Bunny Lee was one of Kingston's busiest producers, recording rhythms quickly and re-using them for many singers and deejays. Tubby became his mixing engineer of choice, and by 1975 the pair were issuing dub albums to feed a growing market in Jamaica and in the diaspora record shops of London and New York. Tubby's studio had a mixing desk he had rebuilt himself, with his own filters and reverb units.","Many of the rhythms here also carry famous vocal records by singers such as Johnny Clarke, so the dubs are a kind of X-ray of songs Jamaican listeners already knew; the pleasure lay in hearing the familiar fall apart. Listen to how Tubby uses the equaliser as an instrument, carving the snare drum down to a thin crack and then letting it swell back. The echo times are set to the tempo, so repeats fall on the beat.","Made quickly for a local market, the record was not reviewed as art at the time. Later critics and reissue labels placed it among the core Tubby albums, a clear demonstration of his method at its most confident. Some purists prefer his singles to his albums, arguing that dub was a B-side art; this album is often cited as proof that it could hold a whole LP.","Try playing a Johnny Clarke vocal of the period, then the corresponding dub here, and notice what Tubby chose to remove. Keep the volume up enough to feel the bass. Afterwards move forward to Scientist's early-1980s dubs to hear how Tubby's apprentice exaggerated the method, and to Basic Channel to hear it rebuilt in Berlin techno."],"king-tubby-dub-conference":["Dub with violins. Producer Harry Mudie had added lush string arrangements to many of his reggae productions, and here King Tubby mixes those rhythms into dub, so the usual skeleton of drums and bass is haunted by sweeping orchestral strings that fade in and out, echo and hang in the air. The mood is softer, sadder and more cinematic than most dub. The recognisable sound is a lone string phrase floating over a deep bass line, then dissolving into reverb.","Mudie ran the Moodisc label from Spanish Town and was known for a smoother, more orchestral reggae than most Kingston producers. Pairing him with Tubby brought an unusual raw material into the dub studio. The album was successful enough that further volumes followed. It appeared in the same mid-1970s rush of dub LPs that made the form a genre rather than a by-product of singles.","The strings were recorded for vocal records, not for dub, so their phrases were written to answer a singer who is no longer there; listen for string lines that sound like replies to a question you cannot hear. Tubby's mix exposes details, such as the players' bow noise and slight tuning differences, that the original records buried. The result is an accidental dialogue between light orchestral arranging and Jamaican studio experiment.","It was a popular record in the reggae market but, like most dub, received little critical attention until reissues in the 1990s. Since then it has been widely praised as one of the most beautiful dub albums, and a proof that the form could hold melody as well as space. A minority find the strings sentimental; most hear them as the element that makes the album unique.","Listen late at night, at moderate volume, and follow the strings rather than the drums. Notice how a phrase is often cut off mid-gesture and echoed. Then compare Augustus Pablo's melodica on King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown, another melodic voice drifting over Tubby's rhythms, and Burial's Untrue, whose ghostly fragments owe something to this spirit."],"king-tubby-dub-gone-crazy":["A compilation of dub mixes made at King Tubby's studio in the second half of the 1970s, largely on rhythms produced by Bunny Lee, mixed by Tubby himself and by the younger engineers he trained, including Prince Jammy and Scientist. It works as a short history lesson: the early tracks sound spacious and steady, the later ones more abrupt, harder and more playful with sudden effects. The recognisable sound is a snare drum exploding into a long echo that falls away down the stereo field.","Blood and Fire was a British reissue label founded in the 1990s with the aim of presenting classic Jamaican records with proper sound and scholarly notes. Its compilations arrived just as British electronic producers in jungle, trip-hop and techno were discovering dub as an ancestor, and they introduced Tubby's studio to a new audience. By then Tubby himself was dead, killed outside his home in Kingston in 1989.","The subtitle's word evolution is the key: listen for the moment the mixing voice changes, when the more restrained Tubby style gives way to the apprentices' bolder moves, such as sharper cuts and more aggressive filtering. Several of the rhythms will be familiar from other Bunny Lee records, which makes it easy to compare engineers working on the same material. The careful remastering also exposes studio noise that vinyl pressings hid.","Reviewers greeted it as one of the best entry points into Tubby's world, and it is often placed on lists of essential reggae compilations. Some collectors object to compilations in principle, preferring the original albums; others argue that dub was always a studio process rather than a fixed album art, and a well-annotated sampler is its honest form.","Listen straight through in order to hear the evolution the title promises, and read the liner notes as you go. Then pick one track and compare it with an original Tubby album such as Dub from the Roots. Finish with Scientist's own albums to hear where the apprentices went next."],"caetano-veloso-caetano-veloso-1968":["Caetano Veloso's first solo album is a manifesto in twelve songs. Orchestral arrangements by avant-garde São Paulo musicians collide with electric guitars, bossa nova delicacy, Latin American dance rhythms, marching bands and the song form of the Beatles. The opener, Tropicália, is a montage of images of Brazil, holy and trashy side by side. Alegria, alegria and the Spanish-language Soy loco por ti, América sit beside tender songs. The recognisable sound is Veloso's soft, precise voice carried through a noisy, collage-like orchestra.","Brazil had been under military rule since 1964, and left-wing students expected popular song to be earnest, acoustic and anti-American. Veloso, a young Bahian writer who adored João Gilberto, provoked them by embracing electric guitars and international pop while writing lyrics full of Brazilian history and politics. The arrangements were by composers from São Paulo's avant-garde classical scene, and the album's title song borrowed its name from Hélio Oiticica's installation Tropicália. Within a year Veloso would be arrested and sent into exile.","Tropicália opens with a spoken, mock-ceremonial preamble recorded in the studio as a joke and left on the tape; it parodies the solemn language of Brazil's colonial founding documents. The song itself lists images in the manner of a cut-up poem, setting modernist Brasília against beach kitsch. Listen too for how the arrangers quote Brazilian and international styles in quick succession, a sonic version of Oswald de Andrade's idea of cultural cannibalism.","It divided Brazil at once: some young listeners heard liberation, while nationalist critics and parts of the student left heard a sell-out to foreign rock. The movement it launched was treated as dangerous by the regime too. Today it is regarded as a founding document of Tropicália and of modern Brazilian popular music, and the debates it provoked, about authenticity and influence, are studied as much as the songs.","Begin with Tropicália and let the collage wash over you before worrying about the words; then find a translation of the lyric to read alongside. Next play Alegria, alegria and hear the rock band behind a Brazilian marcha. Follow with the collective album Tropicália ou Panis et Circencis, and back with João Gilberto's Chega de Saudade to hear what Veloso was both honouring and breaking."],"caetano-veloso-araca-azul":["Veloso's most experimental album: a collage of vocal overdubs, fragments of samba, Bahian street percussion, spoken word, sound-poems and tape effects, made largely by himself in the studio with a few collaborators. There are almost no conventional songs. Pieces range from a samba de roda sung over hand percussion to Sugar Cane Fields Forever, whose title nods at the Beatles, and to the long, abstract De palavra em palavra, built from the sounds of words. The recognisable texture is his own voice layered into a choir.","Veloso had returned to Brazil in 1972 after nearly three years of exile in London, where he had recorded Transa. Home again, and still under the watch of the military government, he chose not to make a hit-friendly record but an art object in the spirit of Brazil's concrete poets, who worked with the shape and sound of words. Philips released it with little sense of how to sell it.","The album is famous in Brazil for being returned to shops by disappointed buyers in large numbers; its reputation as the most rejected record of its time is part of its legend. Listen for how Veloso treats language as music, repeating and fragmenting words until they turn into rhythm. Several tracks use the sounds of his home region of Bahia, including a street samba style older than the samba of Rio.","It was a commercial failure and baffled many admirers. Critics at the time were divided between those who saw self-indulgence and those who saw Brazil's pop music meeting its avant-garde poetry. Its standing has risen steadily, and it is now regarded as a daring touchstone of Brazilian experimental music, influential on later artists who mixed song, noise and poetry.","Do not expect songs. Listen once straight through, preferably on headphones, and treat it as a radio play about voice and memory. Then return to De palavra em palavra and follow the way a single word changes meaning when repeated. Compare Transa before it and the Beatles' Strawberry Fields Forever, whose title Veloso bends into one of his own."],"caetano-veloso-livro":["A late-career summation that sets Veloso's quiet voice and acoustic guitar against two strong forces: a thundering Bahian percussion section and brass and string arrangements in the manner of Gil Evans, many of them shaped with cellist Jaques Morelenbaum. Songs about books, city life, dancers and memory alternate with a setting of Castro Alves's nineteenth-century poem about a slave ship. The recognisable sound is massed drums dropping suddenly away to leave a whispered vocal and a muted horn chord.","By the late 1990s Veloso was an elder statesman of Brazilian culture, and in 1997 he also published a book, Verdade Tropical, his memoir of the Tropicália years. Livro, its musical companion, looks back at Brazilian history and forward to a global audience that had discovered him through world-music labels. It won the Grammy Award for Best World Music Album, bringing him his widest American recognition.","The percussion comes from the Afro-Bahian bloco tradition of Salvador, where huge drum groups parade at carnival; Veloso is deliberately placing the music of his home state at the centre of a sophisticated record. Listen to how the brass voicings echo Gil Evans's arrangements for Miles Davis, a debt often noted by critics. The slave-ship poem is recited and sung over drumming, turning a school-book text into a living accusation.","Critics in Brazil and abroad received it as one of his finest records of the 1990s, praising the arrangements and the balance of intimacy and scale. The Grammy made it his best-known album in the United States. Some Brazilian reviewers found it too polished compared with his riskier work; most place it among the essential late albums.","Start with Livros, the song about books, then the percussion-driven Os passistas, and notice how quickly the arrangement moves from a full parade to a solo voice. Then find a translation of the Castro Alves poem to read during its setting. Compare Gil Evans's Out of the Cool for the brass colours, and Milton Nascimento's Clube da Esquina for another Brazilian orchestral pop."],"jimi-hendrix-axis-bold-as-love":["The Jimi Hendrix Experience's second album is gentler, more varied and more studio-conscious than the first. Thirteen tracks range from the jazzy Up from the Skies and the soul ballad Little Wing to the hard Spanish Castle Magic and the long, phased finale Bold as Love. Hendrix's guitar is often quiet and melodic, using chord-melody playing borrowed from soul guitarists. The recognisable sound is the shimmering, watery guitar tone of Little Wing, lasting barely two and a half minutes.","Recorded in London at Olympic Studios within months of the first album's release in 1967, with Chas Chandler producing and Eddie Kramer engineering, and issued in Britain that December. The contract demanded a second album that year, and Hendrix, Noel Redding and Mitch Mitchell were touring constantly. Four-track tape and new effects, especially phasing and wah-wah, gave Hendrix more tools, and he spent more time on the mixes than before.","It opens with EXP, a fake radio interview about flying saucers in which Mitchell plays the announcer, before a wall of guitar feedback flies from speaker to speaker. The finale's swooping phasing is among the earliest stereo flanging on a rock record. Reportedly, Hendrix lost the master mix of side one, leaving it in a taxi, so it had to be remixed overnight before the deadline.","It sold well but was overshadowed by Are You Experienced and later by Electric Ladyland. Guitarists have long ranked it higher than many critics did, and Little Wing became one of the most covered pieces in rock guitar. The main debate is whether the rushed schedule left it uneven; many listeners now hear it as Hendrix's most personal and lyrical studio album.","Start with Little Wing, then Castles Made of Sand, and listen for how the guitar fills the space between the vocal lines. Then play Bold as Love to hear the final guitar solo move across the stereo field. Afterwards compare Curtis Mayfield's guitar with the Impressions for the source of this style, and Prince's guitar ballads for its descendants."],"jimi-hendrix-band-of-gypsys":["A live album by Hendrix's short-lived trio with bassist Billy Cox and drummer Buddy Miles, recorded at the Fillmore East in New York. The grooves are heavier and funkier than the Experience's, with Miles's steady, soul-based drumming and long, riff-driven jams. At the centre is Machine Gun, over twelve minutes long, an anti-war piece in which the guitar imitates gunfire, sirens and screaming. The recognisable sound is that rattling guitar figure, sustained into feedback and then bent into a wail.","Recorded across four concerts on New Year's Eve 1969 and New Year's Day 1970 and released in the spring of 1970. Hendrix owed an album to a producer with whom he had an old contract dispute, and this record settled it. The Vietnam War was at its height and American cities were full of protest; Hendrix had just played his version of the Star-Spangled Banner at Woodstock. The all-Black trio also signalled his interest in soul and funk audiences.","Machine Gun is built on a snare pattern from Miles and a single bass riff from Cox, which barely changes, so all the drama comes from Hendrix's control of feedback, a Uni-Vibe effect and his volume knob. Listen for the passages where the guitar sustains one note for many seconds, swelling and dying: he is playing the amplifier as much as the strings. Two songs are Buddy Miles's, sung by him.","It sold strongly, but Hendrix himself was reportedly dissatisfied with the performances and the band broke up within weeks. Critics were split between those who found the jams long and loose and those who heard a new, funk-based direction. Over time Machine Gun has come to be widely regarded as one of his greatest recorded solos, and the record as a hinge between rock and funk.","Put on Machine Gun loud and follow the guitar sound, not the notes: listen for each time Hendrix turns a noise into a voice. Then play Who Knows for the easier groove. Compare his Woodstock Star-Spangled Banner, made months earlier, and Funkadelic's Maggot Brain, whose long guitar lament grows from this record."],"joni-mitchell-ladies-of-the-canyon":["Joni Mitchell's third album moves from guitar towards piano and widens from solo performance to small arrangements with cello, woodwinds and backing voices. Twelve songs portray life in Laurel Canyon, the hillside neighbourhood of Los Angeles musicians, and beyond it. The closing trio became standards: Big Yellow Taxi, a bright, joking ecology protest; Woodstock, a slow, electric-piano hymn to the festival; and The Circle Game, a campfire singalong about growing up. The recognisable sound is her voice leaping from low to high within a phrase.","Mitchell was living in Laurel Canyon among Crosby, Stills, Nash and other stars of the Los Angeles scene, and was becoming famous as a writer through other singers' versions of her songs. She produced the album herself, which was unusual for a woman at the time. It was released in 1970, when the counterculture was turning hopeful and anxious in equal measure after Woodstock and Altamont.","Mitchell wrote Woodstock without attending; she watched coverage from a New York hotel room after her manager advised her to keep a television appearance, and imagined the event from outside, which is why it sounds like a vision rather than a report. The Circle Game was written as a reply to Neil Young's song Sugar Mountain about the fear of turning twenty-one. The cover drawing is hers.","It became her first gold record and was widely praised, though some reviewers found the arrangements busier than they wanted from a folk singer. Big Yellow Taxi has had a long second life through cover versions. Critics now tend to see the album as the bridge between her early folk records and Blue, with the piano songs pointing directly to what came next.","Start with For Free, a piano song about a street clarinettist, and listen for the tiny woodwind line at the end. Then hear the last three tracks in order. Compare the Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young rock version of Woodstock with hers, and continue to Blue, made a year later, to hear the piano take over."],"joni-mitchell-the-hissing-of-summer-lawns":["A cycle of songs about suburban wealth, marriage, status and restlessness, set to jazz-inflected arrangements played by Tom Scott's L.A. Express and guests. The songs move through a Saturday night in town, a gangster's girlfriend, a wife in a comfortable suburban house, and a jazz standard. The strangest track, The Jungle Line, sets her voice and Moog synthesizer over a field recording of drummers from Burundi. The closer, Shadows and Light, is a hymn for multiple overdubbed voices and a synthesizer drone.","After the commercial peak of Court and Spark in 1974, Mitchell turned away from confessional songwriting towards character portraits and social observation, and towards jazz harmony. She was living in Los Angeles among the wealthy people she describes, and she was reading novels and anthropology. Asylum released it in late 1975; it was her first album to swap straightforward hit songs for a concept.","The Jungle Line's drumming comes from an existing recording of the Drummers of Burundi, looped and layered by Mitchell years before sampling was common, and years before Western pop took up the same drummers. Harry's House, about a businessman's wife, turns midway into the jazz tune Centerpiece, making a song within a song. Listen for how her guitar parts use tunings she devised herself, giving chords no textbook name.","Many reviewers were hostile, including a notoriously scornful review in Rolling Stone, and fans missed the directness of Blue. Its reputation reversed over the following decades: Prince repeatedly named it as a favourite, and critics now treat it as a daring, prophetic record that anticipated both world-music collaboration and art-pop. The central argument is whether its chilly satire is cruel or compassionate.","Start with The Jungle Line to hear how far she was reaching, then play the title track and listen for the way the bass and piano circle her melody. Read the lyrics as short stories. Afterwards hear Hejira, which followed, and Kate Bush's The Dreaming, which took a similar leap into rhythm and texture."],"neil-young-everybody-knows-this-is-nowhere":["Neil Young's first album with Crazy Horse, the ragged, heavy trio of Danny Whitten on guitar, Billy Talbot on bass and Ralph Molina on drums. Seven songs, with two extended jams at the centre: Down by the River and Cowgirl in the Sand, each around ten minutes, in which Young's distorted guitar solos wander over a slow, stubborn groove. Cinnamon Girl opens with a thick, stomping riff. The recognisable sound is raw guitar noise played simply and loudly, with thin, high harmonies above.","Young had left Buffalo Springfield and released a carefully produced solo debut in late 1968 that he disliked. He found Crazy Horse, then called the Rockets, in Los Angeles and recorded this album quickly in early 1969 for Reprise, aiming for the feeling of a live band. Shortly afterwards he also joined Crosby, Stills and Nash, beginning his double life between gentle harmony and loud guitars.","Young has said he wrote Cinnamon Girl, Down by the River and Cowgirl in the Sand on one day while running a high fever. Cinnamon Girl uses a guitar tuning with both E strings lowered to D, which gives its riff that droning weight; its famous solo is little more than one note repeated with feeling. Listen for Whitten's second guitar answering Young's lines rather than simply backing them.","Received well but modestly on release, it became Young's commercial breakthrough over the following year as the long songs found an audience on FM radio. Critics later identified it as the start of the Crazy Horse sound, and in the 1990s it was hailed as a root of grunge. The long solos still divide listeners between those who hear primitive power and those who hear meandering.","Play Cinnamon Girl first, loudly, then give Cowgirl in the Sand its full length and follow the guitar as if it were a voice arguing with itself. Compare the gentle Round and Round in the middle of the record. Then jump forward to Rust Never Sleeps and to Nirvana's Nevermind to hear how far that sound travelled."],"neil-young-harvest":["Ten songs, mostly gentle country rock, played by a Nashville-based band Young called the Stray Gators, with Ben Keith's pedal steel guitar drifting through them. Heart of Gold and Old Man are acoustic, harmonica-led and full of longing; Alabama and Words turn up the electric guitars; A Man Needs a Maid and There's a World add the London Symphony Orchestra; The Needle and the Damage Done is a short solo live recording. The recognisable sound is Young's high, fragile voice over acoustic guitar and harmonica.","Young was suffering serious back problems in 1971 that made heavy electric guitar difficult, and he turned to quieter songs. Much of the album was recorded in Nashville and at his ranch in northern California, with orchestral sessions arranged by Jack Nitzsche in London. It was released early in 1972 and became the best-selling album of that year in the United States, with Heart of Gold a number one single.","James Taylor and Linda Ronstadt sing harmony on Heart of Gold and Old Man, the latter written about the caretaker of the ranch Young had just bought. Listen for the gap between the rural tunes and the heavy orchestra on A Man Needs a Maid: the strings feel almost too large, which suits a song about loneliness. The Needle and the Damage Done addresses addiction among friends, including his guitarist Danny Whitten.","It was enormously popular, but early reviews were mixed, with some critics calling it soft or derivative of After the Gold Rush. Young himself later wrote that the album's success put him in the middle of the road and that he deliberately swerved away, towards Tonight's the Night. It is now broadly considered a classic of country rock, though fans still argue whether his harder records are the truer ones.","Start with Old Man, then Heart of Gold, and listen to how the steel guitar answers the voice. Then play A Man Needs a Maid for the contrast of orchestra and harmonica. Afterwards go to Tonight's the Night to hear his reaction, and to Carole King's Tapestry and Joni Mitchell's Blue for its neighbours on the Los Angeles and Nashville scene."],"dolly-parton-9-to-5":["A brisk, upbeat song written and sung by Parton, with a clacking rhythm, punchy horns and a gospel-flavoured chorus. In her own blunt, humorous way she describes the grind of office work: waking up, rushing to the job, being underpaid and overlooked by a boss who takes the credit, and dreaming of something better. The song runs under three minutes and never slows down. The recognisable sound is that clicking rhythm at the start, like a typewriter.","Parton wrote the song for the film 9 to 5, her first movie role, a comedy in which three secretaries, played by Jane Fonda, Lily Tomlin and Parton, turn the tables on a sexist boss. The film grew out of the working women's movement of the 1970s. Released in late 1980, the song reached number one on both the pop and country charts early in 1981 and won two Grammy Awards.","Parton has said she wrote the song on the film set, tapping out the rhythm with her long acrylic fingernails, and that nail-clicking is used as percussion on the record. Listen for how the verses sit low and conversational while the chorus leaps into a gospel shout, a structure borrowed from church music. The song is also the most direct piece of workplace protest she ever recorded, wrapped in a pop hook.","It was a huge hit and earned an Academy Award nomination for Best Original Song. Some country traditionalists saw it as Parton's final move into pop; others heard a Tennessee storyteller simply finding a new subject. It has since become an anthem for working women, returning in a stage musical and in political campaigns, and is now more widely known than the film.","Listen first for the fingernail rhythm, then follow how the horns punch the ends of lines. Next, hear Jolene to compare her storytelling in a darker, slower country setting. Then compare Loretta Lynn's The Pill for another working-class woman's song that caused a stir, and Tammy Wynette's Stand by Your Man for the opposite argument."],"dolly-parton-trio":["Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Emmylou Harris sing eleven songs in close three-part harmony, backed by an acoustic string band. The repertoire mixes old mountain songs and gospel, such as Rosewood Casket and Farther Along, with a Jimmie Rodgers song, Jean Ritchie's My Dear Companion, Parton's own Wildflowers and the Phil Spector hit To Know Him Is to Love Him. The recognisable sound is the blend itself: Parton's bright high voice, Ronstadt's full middle and Harris's plaintive tone locked together.","The three friends first tried to record together in the late 1970s, but record-company contracts and schedules stopped the project. By 1987 each was a star in her own right, and country music was in a traditionalist revival, the movement sometimes called neotraditional country. George Massenburg produced the album for Warner Bros. It won a Grammy for best country vocal performance by a group and sold more than a million copies.","Listen to which singer takes the lead in each song: the three rotate, and the harmony parts shift depending on who is on top, so the blend is always changing colour. Wildflowers, Parton's song about leaving home, is really a portrait of her own escape from the Smoky Mountains. The mountain songs come from the same repertory the Carter Family recorded in the 1920s, so the album quietly links pop stars to that lineage.","The album was acclaimed by critics and was a great commercial success, producing several country hit singles. It was praised as a rare meeting of stars that served the music rather than the egos. A sequel appeared in 1999. Its reputation has only grown, and it is often cited as a model for later roots and harmony-singing records.","Start with My Dear Companion, then Wildflowers, and try to follow each voice separately. Then play Farther Along for the gospel harmonies. Compare the Carter Family's Can the Circle Be Unbroken to hear the tradition's roots, and Alison Krauss and Robert Plant's Raising Sand for a later duet record made in the same spirit."],"patti-smith-piss-factory":["Five minutes of a poet talking herself out of a dead-end job. Patti Smith half-speaks, half-chants a monologue over a loose, circling piano figure from Richard Sohl and Lenny Kaye's scratchy guitar. There is no chorus and no real verse structure; the band simply keeps a jazzy vamp going while her voice rises from flat reportage to fury to a kind of ecstatic promise. Its shape is a single long crescendo, and the moment it breaks into escape is the moment you remember.","Smith recorded it at Electric Lady Studios in New York in June 1974, paid for largely by her friend Robert Mapplethorpe, and pressed it on a tiny label of their own, Mer. She was then a poet on the downtown reading circuit and a regular at the St. Mark's Poetry Project, just beginning to put her words over music. The piece draws on a teenage summer spent working on a factory line in South Jersey, among older women who resented the dreamy girl reading Rimbaud on her break.","It is a B-side: the A-face was a cover of Hey Joe with a spoken preface about the heiress Patty Hearst, so the whole single is a diptych about women escaping the lives assigned to them. Listen to how the piano and guitar never change harmony underneath her. All the drama is in the voice, which is why it sounds less like a rock song than a Beat poem accidentally caught on tape with a backing band.","It sold modestly at first, mostly through downtown record shops, but it is regularly named as one of the first records of the New York punk scene and a model of the do-it-yourself single that British punks would copy three years later. Some writers argue about whether it is punk at all, since it has no fast beat and no three-chord thrash; its rebellion lies in its attitude and its refusal of song form.","Listen straight through without skipping; the payoff depends on the long build. Notice the point where she stops describing the factory and starts describing the train to New York, and hear the band lift with her. Then play the opening of Horses to hear how the same method, poetry riding a vamp, became an album. Compare Lou Reed's talking songs with the Velvet Underground."],"patti-smith-radio-ethiopia":["The Patti Smith Group's second album is louder, heavier and messier than Horses. Side one is a run of tight rock songs, among them the anthem Ask the Angels, the slow-burning Pissing in a River and the tumbling Pumping (My Heart). Side two ends in the title piece, a ten-minute storm of feedback, detuned guitar and chanted invocation that barely holds together as a song. The record swings between garage-band directness and a deliberate collapse into noise.","It was made in 1976 in New York with the hard-rock producer Jack Douglas, known for his work with Aerosmith, and credited for the first time to the Patti Smith Group, a sign that the band now wanted equal billing. The punk scene at CBGB was at its height and Smith was its best-known figure. In January 1977, touring the album, she fell from a stage in Tampa and broke vertebrae in her neck, which halted the group for a year.","Ethiopia is not a random exotic word: Smith's hero Arthur Rimbaud abandoned poetry and spent his last years trading in Abyssinia, and the long title track is in effect a séance for him, with the closing section named after that old kingdom. Listen for Smith herself playing guitar in the title piece; she could barely play, and the untutored scraping is the point, an attempt to reach something beyond technique.","Critics who had championed Horses were largely disappointed, calling the album bloated and the title track self-indulgent; some accused Smith of turning into a conventional heavy-rock act. The judgment has softened. Pissing in a River is now counted among her best songs, and the noise experiment of the title track looks ahead to Sonic Youth and the No Wave bands more than it looks back to 1970s arena rock.","Start with Pissing in a River, which builds from a quiet piano confession to a full band surge, and listen for how long the band holds back before the drums arrive. Then try the title track at volume, as sound rather than song. Compare it with the Velvet Underground's Sister Ray, its obvious ancestor, and with Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation, where the approach was refined."],"patti-smith-easter":["Smith's comeback album is her most accessible and her most openly religious. It opens with the crashing Till Victory, carries her one hit, Because the Night, with its big piano intro and pounding chorus, and moves through the spoken prose-poem Babelogue into a furious, chanting rock track that reclaims a racial slur as a badge of outsiderdom. Ghost Dance draws on Native American ritual; the title song closes the record as a hushed, bell-like resurrection hymn. The sound is fuller and more polished than before.","It was recorded in late 1977 and early 1978 after Smith's long recovery from a neck injury suffered on stage in Tampa, and the title marks that personal resurrection. The producer was the young Jimmy Iovine, who was also working with Bruce Springsteen at the Record Plant. Springsteen had an unfinished song he could not complete; Iovine passed it to Smith, who wrote new words about love and desire at night, and it became Because the Night, co-credited to both.","The cover photograph by Lynn Goldsmith, showing Smith in a simple slip with her arms raised and underarms unshaved, caused as much discussion as the music, and it was a deliberate refusal of how women on album covers were expected to look. Listen to the transition from the spoken Babelogue straight into the next track: they were conceived as one piece, the poem acting as a preacher's lead-in to the band's explosion.","Because the Night reached the US Top 20, her only record to do so, and brought her to a mass audience; some punk purists called that a sell-out. The track with the slur in its title remains the album's most argued-over moment, defended by Smith as an identification with the despised and rejected, criticised by others as a white artist's careless use of a word that was not hers. The rest of the record is now respected, if less revered than Horses.","Begin with Because the Night and then hear Springsteen's own later live version to see how two writers pulled one song in different directions. Then go to the title track at the end, and listen for the tolling piano and the way Smith's voice drops almost to a whisper. Horses shows her at her most daring; Easter shows the same voice learning to fill a big room."],"patti-smith-gone-again":["An album of mourning, played mostly slow and acoustic. The title track is a stomping, drone-heavy lament; Beneath the Southern Cross builds from strummed guitar into a swelling, raga-like climax; About a Boy is a long, murky elegy; and there are spare folk ballads and a Dylan cover. Smith's voice is lower and rougher than in the 1970s, and the arrangements lean on droning strings and repetitive guitar figures that give the record the feel of a wake held over several days.","Smith had retired from public life in 1979, moving to Detroit to marry the MC5 guitarist Fred 'Sonic' Smith and raise two children. Between 1989 and 1994 she lost Robert Mapplethorpe, her keyboard player Richard Sohl, her husband, who died in November 1994, and then her brother Todd a month later. Encouraged by friends, she returned to music and made this record, her first in eight years, in 1995 and 1996, with Lenny Kaye and Jay Dee Daugherty from her old band.","The title song was one of the last pieces Fred Smith worked on with her, so the album opens with a collaboration with the man it mourns. About a Boy is written for Kurt Cobain, who had died in 1994, and borrows its frame from the title of a Nirvana song. Listen for Jeff Buckley, who plays the Indian bowed instrument called the essraj on a couple of tracks; he himself would drown the following year, which gives those passages an extra shadow.","It was greeted warmly as a return rather than a comeback, admired for its restraint and honesty even by critics who found some songs shapeless. It has come to be seen as the start of Smith's second career, the long later phase as a touring elder of rock and a memoirist, leading to the National Book Award for Just Kids in 2010. Some listeners still prefer it to anything after Horses for its lack of posturing.","Start with Beneath the Southern Cross and let the drone carry you; its climb from quiet to full is the album's emotional peak. Then play the title track and notice how the drum pattern sounds like a funeral march played by a garage band. Listen to it late, straight through. For companions, try Nick Cave's Skeleton Tree, another record made in the shadow of death."],"flatt-and-scruggs-earls-breakdown":["A fast instrumental showcase for Earl Scruggs's three-finger banjo, played by Flatt and Scruggs's Foggy Mountain Boys. The banjo rolls out a torrent of evenly spaced notes over Lester Flatt's steady guitar, handing off breaks to fiddle and mandolin before taking the tune back. What makes it unforgettable is a few seconds in the banjo's break where a note bends downward and then slides back up, a sound like a siren or a sigh that no banjo had made on record before.","The duo had left Bill Monroe's Blue Grass Boys in 1948 and within a few years were recording for Columbia, rivals to their old boss. Recorded in 1951, this was a statement of Scruggs's independence: the tune bears his name, and it shows off a technique of his own devising. Bluegrass was then just a name for Monroe's band style, carried to rural audiences on radio barn dances and by records sold through country stores and jukeboxes.","That bending note is mechanical as much as musical. Scruggs retuned a string in the middle of the tune by turning its tuning peg while he played, dropping the pitch and then bringing it back precisely, and later fitted his banjo with special cam tuners so he could do it reliably. Banjo players spent years trying to work out how the effect was made. Listen for it twice, and hear how exactly the pitch lands home again.","It became a standard for bluegrass banjo players, and together with Flint Hill Special it defined the tuner trick as part of the instrument's vocabulary. Historians of the music now treat Scruggs as the figure who turned the five-string banjo from a comic or old-time accompaniment into a lead virtuoso instrument, and this track is a clear exhibit. It is less famous to the public than Foggy Mountain Breakdown, but players rate it as high.","Listen once only to the banjo, following the unbroken roll of notes under and around the melody. Then listen for the detuned bend and try to hear exactly where it starts and finishes. Compare it with Foggy Mountain Breakdown, recorded two years earlier, and then with a modern player such as Béla Fleck to hear how far the Scruggs vocabulary has travelled."],"flatt-and-scruggs-foggy-mountain-jamboree":["The first long-playing album by Flatt and Scruggs gathers a dozen of their early 1950s Columbia singles into one portrait of the band at full strength. Banjo instrumentals such as Flint Hill Special sit beside sentimental songs of home and lost love sung by Lester Flatt in his relaxed, conversational tenor, with close harmony on the choruses. Each track is two to three minutes, the band trading short solos in strict order, and the balance between Flatt's easy singing and Scruggs's driving banjo is the record's signature.","In the mid-1950s the LP was still a medium for classical music and show albums; country acts mostly sold singles. Columbia's decision to package Flatt and Scruggs's work as an album reflected their success on radio and on the Martha White flour-sponsored broadcasts that made them household names in the South. It arrived just as a college folk revival in the North was beginning to discover mountain music, and it became a way in for many young city listeners.","Notice how rarely the band plays all at once. The Foggy Mountain Boys arrange their sound like a small jazz combo: rhythm guitar and bass keep time, and one lead instrument at a time takes the spotlight while the others drop to a whisper. Listen too for Flatt's trademark guitar run, a quick bass-string phrase that closes the end of each vocal line and became known as the Lester Flatt G-run.","It has long been treated as one of the essential bluegrass albums and a primer for the style; many later players say it was the record that taught them. In the argument between purists who follow Bill Monroe's high, keening style and those who prefer the smoother Flatt and Scruggs approach, this album is the latter camp's founding text. Reissues on CD added extra tracks from the same sessions.","Put it on as a set and notice the pattern: song, instrumental, song. Listen to how Scruggs changes his playing behind a vocal, backing Flatt with softer rolls, then steps forward on the break. Pair it with Bill Monroe's recordings to hear the difference between the two schools, and with Doc Watson's first album to hear the folk revival that picked this music up."],"flatt-and-scruggs-ballad-of-jed-clampett":["A short, brisk narrative song, told in a talking-blues style, about a poor mountain man who strikes oil while hunting and moves his family to Beverly Hills. Lester Flatt sings it in a friendly, storytelling voice while Earl Scruggs's banjo supplies a rolling, cheerful accompaniment and a bright opening figure that millions of Americans could hum. It is barely two minutes long and built entirely from a simple chord pattern; the banjo intro is the instantly recognisable hook.","The song was written by Paul Henning as the theme for his CBS situation comedy The Beverly Hillbillies, which began in September 1962. Flatt and Scruggs played the theme on the show, with a studio singer, and then recorded their own version for Columbia with Flatt on vocals. The single climbed to number one on the Billboard country chart, the first bluegrass record to do so, and the duo appeared on the show several times playing themselves.","The words are gentle comedy, but the premise carries a real tension: city America laughing at hill country people while also envying their simplicity. Flatt and Scruggs were playing into a stereotype that bluegrass musicians had spent years fighting, yet the fame it brought paid for their move onto college campuses and the folk circuit. Listen for how deliberately rustic the arrangement is compared with their own records of the same year.","It made them the best-known bluegrass act in America and remains the most widely heard bluegrass performance ever, thanks to endless television reruns. Purists at the time grumbled about novelty records, and later critics have debated whether The Beverly Hillbillies was affectionate or mocking. Musically, few doubt that it put the sound of the Scruggs banjo into more living rooms than any other record.","Listen for the banjo intro and then for how Scruggs keeps the roll going under the narrative without ever drowning Flatt's voice. It rewards hearing next to Foggy Mountain Breakdown, where the same band shows what it can do at full speed. Then look for footage of their television appearances, which show how the players held their instruments and grouped around one microphone."],"flatt-and-scruggs-at-carnegie-hall":["A live concert album catching the Foggy Mountain Boys in front of a New York audience at the height of their fame. The set mixes banjo showpieces, gospel numbers, old ballads and comic songs, joined by Lester Flatt's easy stage patter and applause that erupts after every banjo break. Josh Graves's dobro, a steel-bodied guitar played with a sliding bar, is prominent, bringing a bluesy moan to the band sound, and the energy of a live stage pushes the tempos faster than on the studio records.","The concert took place on 8 December 1962, only weeks after The Beverly Hillbillies had made the band household names. Carnegie Hall, the temple of classical music, had hosted jazz and folk shows, and a bluegrass band there marked how far the northern folk revival had come. Louise Scruggs, Earl's wife and the band's manager, had worked hard to book them on campuses and folk stages rather than just the southern country circuit.","Listen to the audience as much as the band. The applause and laughter come from young New Yorkers who knew this music from the folk revival, and Flatt adjusts his introductions for them, explaining songs he would never have needed to explain at a southern schoolhouse show. Notice also Paul Warren's fiddle, which carries the older dance-tune repertoire into the set and connects the band back to pre-war string music.","It was well received and is regularly counted among the best live bluegrass records. Historians use it to mark the moment bluegrass moved from rural radio and schoolhouses to concert halls and college audiences, part of the same wave that took Doc Watson and the old-time musicians of the Harry Smith Anthology to Newport. Later tensions between Flatt, who wanted to stay traditional, and Scruggs, who wanted to reach younger audiences, split the duo in 1969.","Hear it as a show rather than a set of songs: follow the sequence of introductions, jokes and applause. Listen for the dobro solos and compare them with the banjo breaks for tone and attack. Then hear The Weavers at Carnegie Hall to see how the same hall had welcomed folk music a few years earlier, and Doc Watson's recordings from the same revival."],"stevie-wonder-fingertips":["A live recording of a twelve-year-old boy blowing a jazzy harmonica tune, calling and answering his own audience, and refusing to leave the stage. The released hit is the second part of the performance: a jumpy, loose band groove, the boy leading the crowd in yes-and-no chants, a false ending and then a sudden restart as he comes back for more. It is chaotic, happy and utterly alive, and the harmonica's whooping phrases are what everyone recognises.","Stevland Morris, blind from infancy, signed to Berry Gordy's Motown as a child and was billed as Little Stevie Wonder. Motown's revue toured theatres across the country, and this track was taped at a show in Chicago in 1962. Released in 1963 as a two-part single, the second part took off on radio, and the song reached number one on both the pop and R&B charts, making him the youngest artist ever to top the Hot 100 and one of the first live recordings to reach the top.","The best moment is an accident. After Wonder has seemingly finished, the band for the next act is already arriving on stage when he bounds back to start again, and you can hear a musician in the confusion shouting to ask what key they are in. Motown kept it in. The song itself was not Wonder's; it was written by the label's Clarence Paul and Henry Cosby as a showcase for his instrumental talent.","Critics at the time treated it as a novelty from a child prodigy, and few predicted the serious artist of the 1970s. Its reputation has grown with his: it is now heard as the first sign of his command of an audience and of improvisation, and a vivid document of the Motown revue in its early days. It is also an early example of how much energy a live single could carry on radio.","Listen for the call-and-response with the crowd and how the boy controls it; he makes them wait and then lets them go. Then wait for the false ending and the chaos after it. Follow it with Innervisions or Songs in the Key of Life to hear how far the same musician travelled in ten years, and with James Brown's Live at the Apollo for another stage caught in the act."],"stevie-wonder-music-of-my-mind":["The first album on which Stevie Wonder wrote, arranged and played nearly everything himself. The sound is warm and homemade: layered synthesizers, electric piano, clavinet and his own drumming, sometimes slightly loose, behind soft, intimate vocals. Superwoman is an eight-minute suite in two parts, moving from a bright pop song into a slow, reflective second half; Love Having You Around rides a long funk groove; Evil closes on a brooding, questioning note. It plays less like a Motown hit collection than a diary.","Wonder turned twenty-one in May 1971 and his childhood contract with Motown lapsed. He renegotiated from strength, gaining artistic control and his own publishing, which almost no Motown artist had. He had meanwhile begun working in New York with the synthesizer pioneers Malcolm Cecil and Robert Margouleff, whose huge custom instrument, TONTO, filled a room. Music of My Mind, released in March 1972, was the first fruit, and it opened the run that led to Innervisions and Songs in the Key of Life.","Many sounds that seem like strings or horns are in fact synthesizers, programmed by Cecil and Margouleff and played by Wonder, at a time when synthesizers were mostly used for sound effects or classical novelties. Listen to the drums: Wonder plays them himself, and his slightly behind-the-beat style gives the grooves their loose swing. The gear change in the middle of Superwoman is a sign of the suite-like structures that would fill his next albums.","It was less commercially successful than what followed, and some critics found it uneven, but it was widely recognised as a declaration of independence, not only from Motown's production line but from the idea of the soul singer as an interpreter. Many writers now see it as the true beginning of Wonder's classic period and as one of the earliest albums to make synthesizers sound soulful rather than mechanical.","Start with Superwoman and listen for the moment the song changes into its slower second half. Then play Love Having You Around and notice how long he lets the groove run. Compare it with Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach, made with similar machines to very different ends, and then go on to Talking Book, released later the same year, where the same approach becomes sharper."],"larry-levan-instant-funk-i-got-my-mind-made-up":["A Philadelphia funk band's party record, stretched and reshaped by Larry Levan into a nine-minute twelve-inch mix. The groove is heavy and bass-driven, with chanted vocals, bright horn stabs and a rubbery guitar. Levan's version strips the track back to its rhythm for long passages, then brings in voices and horns one by one, giving the song a drama the three-minute radio edit does not have. The breakdown, with little but drums and bass, is the part dancers waited for.","Instant Funk were a Philadelphia group produced by Bunny Sigler for Salsoul, the New York label that pioneered the extended twelve-inch single for clubs. Salsoul hired leading DJs to remix its records, and Levan, then in his early twenties and resident at the Paradise Garage in SoHo, was one of the youngest. Released at the end of 1978, the record became a big R&B hit in 1979, just as disco was reaching its commercial peak and approaching the backlash that followed.","Levan built his mixes for the Garage's sound system, one of the most powerful and carefully tuned in New York, and his remixes often favour deep bass and space over clutter. Listen for how he uses silence: sections where almost everything drops out so that when the full band comes back the effect is physical. The idea of a remix as a new composition rather than a longer copy was still new, and this is one of the records that proved it.","It became one of Salsoul's biggest sellers and helped build Levan's reputation outside the Garage. Club historians now cite it as an early model of the remix as a creative act; DJs still play it, and later dance producers have sampled and re-edited it. The ongoing debate about how much a remixer should reshape a band's work begins with records like this.","Hear the original radio length first, then the full twelve-inch mix, and notice what Levan adds by taking things away. Play it loud, on speakers with real bass. Then follow it with Chic's Good Times, from the following year, to hear another Salsoul-era groove, and with Levan's own Paradise Garage recordings to hear how he placed such records in a night."],"larry-levan-taana-gardner-heartbeat":["Taana Gardner's slow, sensual club record, mixed by Larry Levan, sounds like disco played at half speed. The tempo is unusually slow for dance music of the time, and the whole track rests on a thick, lurching bassline and a loose drum pattern. Gardner's voice floats high and girlish above it, repeating a simple phrase about love's physical pull. Levan's mix leaves big spaces, letting the bass carry the track and introducing voice and keyboards sparingly.","It was written and produced by Kenton Nix and released in 1981 on West End Records, the small New York label run by Mel Cheren, a close ally of Levan. By then disco had crashed commercially after the backlash of 1979, but it lived on in New York's gay and Black clubs, where DJs such as Levan at the Paradise Garage shaped a slower, deeper, more soulful sound that later took the name garage.","The slow tempo worried the label; accounts suggest nobody expected a dance record this relaxed to work until Levan played it at the Garage and the floor filled. Listen to how the bassline sits slightly behind the drum beat, a lazy feel that became a signature of post-disco and early house. The groove was later borrowed by reggae and hip-hop producers, and several 1990s hits echo it.","It was a club hit before it was a chart record, and it is now regarded as one of the defining Paradise Garage records. Critics of dance music cite it as evidence that the so-called death of disco was really a move underground. Its sluggish groove still divides first-time listeners, who sometimes find it too slow to dance to, until they hear it on a big sound system.","Play it loud and concentrate on the bassline; the rest of the track is ornament. Notice how long the mix takes to reveal the full voice. Then hear Levan's Don't Make Me Wait by the Peech Boys, from a year later, to hear another slow, spacious Garage record, and follow it with Frankie Knuckles's Your Love to hear how Chicago took the same sensibility."],"larry-levan-gwen-guthrie-padlock":["A short record of five remixes of songs by the singer Gwen Guthrie, reworked by Larry Levan into long, spacious club versions. The originals were produced by the Jamaican rhythm section Sly Dunbar and Robbie Shakespeare, and Levan pushes their reggae instincts further: the bass is huge, the drums crisp, the vocals appear in fragments, and whole instruments drop out and return with echo trailing behind them. The effect is a meeting of New York club music and Jamaican dub.","Guthrie had been a session singer and songwriter in New York before cutting her first solo album with Sly and Robbie at Compass Point Studios in Nassau for Island Records, the label of Chris Blackwell, which was also releasing Grace Jones's work from the same studio. Blackwell brought in Levan, whose Paradise Garage audience responded to that crossover of reggae and dance. Released in 1983 on a short-lived Island imprint named after the club, it gathered Levan's mixes on one disc.","Levan is doing on a dance-floor remix what King Tubby and Lee Perry had done in Kingston a decade earlier: treating the mixing desk as an instrument, cutting out tracks and adding echo to rebuild a song in real time. Listen for the moments when Guthrie's voice is suddenly cut off and left to repeat into the distance. Those are the fingerprints of dub, moved from a Jamaican yard to a Manhattan warehouse club.","It was never a big seller in its time, but it has become a prized record among DJs and collectors, and critics often point to it as the clearest example of Levan's dub-inspired approach. It is sometimes cited as one of the first albums sold on the strength of the remixer rather than the artist, a sign of how the DJ was becoming an author. Guthrie later had a big hit with Ain't Nothin' Goin' On but the Rent.","Start with the title track and listen for how the bass carries the whole song. Try it on headphones to catch the echo effects moving across the stereo picture. Then play King Tubby's Dub from the Roots or Lee Perry's Super Ape to hear the Jamaican source of the method, and Levan's Live at the Paradise Garage to hear the room it was made for."],"frankie-knuckles-baby-wants-to-ride":["A dark, hypnotic house track built from a stiff drum-machine pattern, a looping bassline and cold synthesizer chords, with Jamie Principle's voice moving between sung hook and spoken monologue. The lyric is frankly erotic but drifts, halfway through, into a spoken passage mixing religious language with wider, darker thoughts about the world. The sound is sparse and machine-like; the feeling is tense and slightly menacing rather than joyful. Principle's half-whispered delivery is what stays in the memory.","Jamie Principle was a young Chicago songwriter whose home-made tapes of electronic soul had reached Frankie Knuckles, then DJing at the Power Plant after leaving the Warehouse, the club that gave house music its name. Knuckles played Principle's songs from reel-to-reel tape long before they were pressed; Your Love was the first. Baby Wants to Ride was released on the Trax label in the house boom of 1987, when Chicago records were beginning to reach Britain and Europe.","The song borrows the eroticism of Prince, whom Principle admired, and the chilly electronics of European synth-pop such as Kraftwerk and Depeche Mode, fused on Chicago machines. Listen for the moment it stops being a seduction and becomes something closer to a sermon; that shift, from bedroom to pulpit, is an old gospel-and-soul move translated into electronic dance music, and it is why the track feels unsettling rather than simply sexy.","It was a Chicago underground hit and later a favourite of European DJs, but it never crossed over like the city's biggest records. Critics now treat it as an early example of how house could be more than party music, carrying the sexual and spiritual intensity of soul. Like many Trax releases, it has long been caught up in arguments over pressings, credits and royalties.","Listen for the change halfway through when the monologue turns from desire towards religion. Notice how little happens in the backing track and how much the voice carries. Then hear Your Love, the earlier Principle and Knuckles collaboration, and Prince's Dirty Mind to hear where the vocal style came from. Kraftwerk's Computer World shows the electronic side."],"frankie-knuckles-beyond-the-mix":["Frankie Knuckles's first album under his own name, a glossy set of house tracks built around lush keyboard chords, warm piano and guest vocalists. It includes The Whistle Song, with its lilting flute-like melody, and vocal tracks such as Rain Falls that aim for the soulful end of dance music. The sound is smooth and polished, a long way from the raw drum machines of 1980s Chicago; each track is built to work both in clubs and on radio.","By the late 1980s Knuckles had moved to New York and joined David Morales and the manager Judy Weinstein in Def Mix Productions, one of the busiest remix teams of the day, reworking records for major labels. House music had swept Britain and Europe, and Virgin signed Knuckles to make an album. Beyond the Mix, released in 1991, shows how house was turning into a professional craft with studios, session players and major-label budgets.","Many of the lush chords were probably played by Satoshi Tomiie and Eric Kupper, studio partners who shaped much of the Def Mix sound; the album is a team effort as much as a solo statement. Listen for how the grooves still keep a steady four-on-the-floor kick drum under all the polish, the Chicago skeleton beneath the New York clothing. The title itself argues that a DJ-remixer can be an album artist.","It had modest sales, but The Whistle Song topped the US dance chart and became one of the defining sounds of early-1990s house. Some fans of the raw Chicago records found the album too slick, while others see it as the high point of soulful, song-based house. It has since been reissued and is valued as a document of how the underground turned into an industry.","Start with The Whistle Song and listen for how the melody floats over a relaxed groove. Then compare it with Your Love, made five years earlier, to hear the change from Chicago minimalism to New York polish. Put it on in the evening, as warm-up music rather than peak-time dance, and follow it with Kevin Saunderson's Inner City records to hear the Detroit parallel."],"grandmaster-flash-superrappin":["A long party record on which the Furious Five pass the microphone round in turns, boasting, joking and trading rhymes over a funky live band vamp that never changes. The track runs well past ten minutes, like a live set in the South Bronx caught on tape. Every rapper gets a spotlight, the group chants choruses together, and the energy comes from the interplay of voices rather than from any tune. The routines of five MCs sharing a groove were the group's calling card.","Grandmaster Flash was a Bronx DJ who pioneered cutting between two copies of a record, and his Furious Five were Melle Mel, Kid Creole, Cowboy, Scorpio and Rahiem. After the Sugarhill Gang's Rapper's Delight became a hit in 1979, labels rushed to record the real Bronx crews. Bobby Robinson, a veteran Harlem record-shop owner whose Enjoy label had put out R&B since the 1960s, signed them first, and Superrappin' was their debut on record.","Grandmaster Flash himself is barely on it. The turntable art that made his name could not yet be captured in a recording studio, so Enjoy used a studio band to replay a groove, and the DJ is reduced to a name on the label. Listen for how the rappers keep counting and calling out to the crowd; they are performing for an imagined party, because that is the only way they knew how to rap.","It was not a big hit, but it established the group and is now seen as one of the key first-generation rap records, closer to the real park-jam style than Rapper's Delight. Historians note that it preserves the group-routine format of early Bronx hip-hop, which later records, focused on single stars, left behind. The absence of Flash's own turntable skills on the record remains a much-discussed irony.","Listen for the handoffs between rappers and try to tell the five voices apart. Notice how long the band holds the groove. Then play The Adventures of Grandmaster Flash on the Wheels of Steel, from two years later, to hear what the DJ actually did, and Rapper's Delight to hear the record that made this one possible."],"grandmaster-flash-freedom":["An upbeat, disco-funk party record in which the Furious Five take turns introducing themselves, boasting about their skills and urging the crowd to move. The groove is bright and fast, with a chugging bassline and choppy guitar, and the group harmonises on chanted hooks between verses. Its bouncy, celebratory mood is the opposite of the grim The Message two years later; this is the Furious Five as a party band for a new national audience.","After their debut on Enjoy, the group signed with Sylvia and Joe Robinson's Sugar Hill Records in New Jersey, the label behind Rapper's Delight. Freedom, released in 1980, was their first Sugar Hill single and their first hit, reaching the R&B charts and making them one of the first rap groups to tour nationally. Sugar Hill's house band replayed grooves from current funk and disco records, the only practical way to bring hip-hop into the studio before sampling.","The groove is based on Get Up and Dance, a 1979 funk record by a band named Freedom, which is where the title comes from; the rap tradition of building on someone else's break lived on even when the break was replayed rather than cut from vinyl. Listen for Flash's name being chanted in the choruses: though his turntables are again absent, the group still frames everything around their DJ.","It was a genuine hit and helped make rap a commercial genre. Critics tend to treat it as a transitional record, fun but conventional, a stepping stone between the Bronx party routines and the social realism of The Message. Recent writing gives more credit to Sugar Hill's house band, whose players shaped the sound of early recorded rap as much as the rappers did.","Put it on as party music and listen to how the group divides the song, each rapper taking a short turn. Then play The Message straight after to hear the same group change mood entirely. For the groove's roots, hear Chic's Good Times, which Rapper's Delight had also borrowed, and James Brown's funk records that lie behind them all."],"madonna-madonna":["Madonna's debut album is a lean set of eight dance-pop songs built on synthesizers, drum machines and bright, bouncy basslines. Holiday is an invitation to celebrate, with a cheerful keyboard hook; Lucky Star and Borderline set her small, determined voice against glittering synth patterns; Everybody and Burning Up are closer to the New York club records she loved. The songs are simple, the production crisp and dry, and each track sounds made to be danced to first and heard on the radio second.","Madonna Ciccone had moved from Michigan to New York in 1978 to be a dancer, drifted through rock bands and began making dance music in the downtown club scene around Danceteria. The DJ Mark Kamins helped her get a deal with Sire Records, and she made the album in 1982 and 1983 with the producer Reggie Lucas, with her then partner, the club DJ John 'Jellybean' Benitez, producing Holiday. MTV was new and hungry for pop videos.","Holiday was not written by Madonna but by Curtis Hudson and Lisa Stevens of the group Pure Energy; it had reportedly been offered to other singers before she took it. Listen to the album's roots in post-disco and early electro: its drum programming and synth bass belong to the same downtown world as Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock and the Paradise Garage. Madonna was, at first, marketed to Black radio and dance clubs.","Reviews were polite; many critics dismissed her as a lightweight dance act that would not last. The singles built slowly and made her a star by 1984. Later writers have given the album credit for bringing club-culture sounds into mainstream pop and for showing an artist in control of her image from the outset. Some still find it thin compared with her later work; others prize its unpretentious energy.","Start with Holiday and notice how the keyboard hook, bassline and simple chant do all the work. Then play Borderline, where the tune has more feeling than its frothy production suggests. Pair it with Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock and Chic's Good Times to hear the club sounds behind it, and then Like a Virgin to hear her step into global stardom."],"madonna-like-a-virgin":["Madonna's second album is sharper and more confident than her first, powered by the playing of Chic's rhythm section. The title song sets a light, girlish vocal against a bouncing Motown-style bassline; Material Girl is a crisp, knowing satire about a woman choosing wealth over romance; Dress You Up and Angel keep up the bright, synth-heavy dance-pop; and a cover of Rose Royce's Love Don't Live Here Anymore adds a slow, echoing ballad. The mood is playful, provocative and entirely in control.","It was made in 1984 at the Power Station in New York with Nile Rodgers as producer, fresh from David Bowie's Let's Dance, and his Chic bandmates Bernard Edwards on bass and Tony Thompson on drums. The title song was written by Billy Steinberg and Tom Kelly. Madonna performed it at the first MTV Video Music Awards in September 1984, rolling on the stage in a wedding dress atop a giant cake, and the album became her first US number one.","The bouncing bassline under the title track echoes the 1960s Motown sound, so a song about feeling brand new rides on a deliberately old-fashioned groove. Listen to Material Girl as satire: Madonna has said it was a character, but many heard it as her creed, and the misreading became part of her image. Into the Groove was added to later international pressings after the film Desperately Seeking Susan.","It was a massive hit and made Madonna the most talked-about pop star of the mid-1980s. Critics were split between those who saw a calculating product and those who found a sharp wit playing with sexual and religious taboos. The title song was attacked by moralists, and the MTV performance became one of the most discussed moments in pop television. Feminist critics have since read her as a pioneer of female self-invention.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass and drums, which are Chic in a new setting. Then play Material Girl and hear it as a sketch rather than a confession. Compare it with Chic's Le Freak to hear the rhythm section's own work, and with Like a Prayer, five years later, to hear her turn the same provocations into something more serious."],"madonna-vogue":["A house-music single that invites everyone to strike a pose and forget their troubles on the dance floor. Built on a steady, clicking drum-machine groove, stabbing piano chords and a whispered spoken intro, it rises into a simple, chanted chorus. In the middle, Madonna speaks a rhythmic list of Hollywood stars of the 1930s to 1950s over the beat, a roll call of glamour. The sleek production and the idea of the dance floor as a place of self-transformation are the song's heart.","Madonna wrote and produced it with Shep Pettibone, a New York remixer steeped in house and garage, and it was released in March 1990 and added to I'm Breathless, the album linked to the film Dick Tracy. Voguing was a dance style from Harlem's ballroom scene, where Black and Latino gay and trans people competed in houses. Madonna had seen dancers from that scene, including Jose and Luis Xtravaganza, and hired them for the video and her Blond Ambition tour.","The video, directed by David Fincher in black and white, copies the look of 1930s and 1940s Hollywood studio portraits, making the song's spoken list visible. Listen to the backing track, which is pure New York house of the period: the chords and drum patterns belong to the clubs where Pettibone worked, not to mainstream pop. The whispered intro borrows the voguers' own language, bringing ballroom slang to the world's radio.","It reached number one in the US, Britain and many other countries and one of the biggest-selling singles of 1990. From the start critics debated whether Madonna had celebrated ballroom culture or appropriated it, especially after Jennie Livingston's documentary Paris Is Burning (1990) showed the dancers' own poverty and exclusion. That debate continues, alongside praise for the song's role in bringing queer club culture into the mainstream, a thread Beyoncé's Renaissance later picked up.","Listen first to the groove: the piano stabs and drum machine are the house music of the time. Then listen to the spoken middle section and let the names paint a picture. Watch the video, which is part of the work. Follow it with Frankie Knuckles's Your Love and Beyoncé's Renaissance to hear the club lineage it borrows from and feeds."],"michael-jackson-off-the-wall":["Michael Jackson's first adult solo album is a glossy, joyous blend of disco, funk and soul. It opens with Don't Stop 'Til You Get Enough, a long, percussive dance track sung in a breathless falsetto with whoops and gasps, and moves through the silky Rock with You, the bouncing title track, and Paul McCartney's Girlfriend, to the ballad She's Out of My Life, which ends with Jackson's voice cracking. Horns are tight, strings sweep, and the bass and drums never let up.","Jackson was twenty, still best known as the child star of the Jackson 5, when he met the producer and arranger Quincy Jones while filming The Wiz in 1978. Jones, a jazz veteran, gathered a crew including the songwriter Rod Temperton, the engineer Bruce Swedien and top Los Angeles session players. Released in August 1979 on Epic, it became the first solo album to produce four US Top 10 singles, just as the disco boom was beginning to crash.","Don't Stop 'Til You Get Enough was the first song Jackson wrote to be a single on his own record, and he worked out its parts at home by singing them into a tape recorder; accounts say he and his brother Randy played glass bottles for the tinkling percussion heard on the finished track. Listen at the end of She's Out of My Life: Jackson reportedly broke down on take after take, and Jones kept the catch in his voice.","It sold massively and was praised as a joyful and sophisticated pop record, but it won only one Grammy, a slight Jackson deeply resented and which fuelled his ambition for Thriller. Critics now often rank it as highly as Thriller, some higher, for its looseness and unforced joy. It is regularly cited as the album that carried disco's dance-floor energy into the mainstream pop of the 1980s.","Start with Don't Stop 'Til You Get Enough and listen for the breathy spoken intro and the moment the groove kicks in. Then play Rock with You for its smooth bass and strings. Compare it with Chic's Good Times, from the same summer, and then play Thriller, where the same team made everything bigger and darker."],"michael-jackson-bad":["A hard, bright, machine-driven pop album. The title song is a strutting, synth-bass swagger; Smooth Criminal is a tense, galloping dance track; The Way You Make Me Feel a finger-snapping swing; Man in the Mirror a gospel-tinged plea for personal change that builds with a choir to a big key change; Dirty Diana a rock ballad with blazing guitar. The drums and synthesizers are sharp-edged, and Jackson's voice is full of clipped hiccups, grunts and cries that became his signature.","Bad was the long-awaited successor to Thriller, the best-selling album in history, and it carried enormous commercial pressure. Jackson and Quincy Jones worked on it for years; it was their last album together. Jackson wrote nine of the eleven songs. Released in August 1987 and backed by a world tour, it produced five consecutive US number one singles, the first album to do so. Martin Scorsese directed the long video for the title song.","Much of the album's sound comes from the Synclavier, an expensive digital synthesizer and sampler, which gives it the gleaming, hard edge of late 1980s production; the famous gong-like sounds and stabs were programmed rather than played. Man in the Mirror was not written by Jackson but by Siedah Garrett and Glen Ballard, and Garrett also sings with him on I Just Can't Stop Loving You.","Critics found it impressive but inevitably compared it with Thriller, and many thought it slicker and colder. Its sales were enormous, if smaller than Thriller's. Opinions have warmed; Smooth Criminal and Man in the Mirror are among Jackson's most performed songs, and some critics now value the album's tough, anxious edge. The tabloid circus around Jackson in these years has often overshadowed discussion of the music.","Start with Smooth Criminal and listen to the tight, relentless bassline and the frantic vocal. Then play Man in the Mirror and wait for the key change and the choir. Compare the digital sound with the warmer Off the Wall, and hear Janet Jackson's Control, made in the same years, to hear a sibling take pop in a sharper direction."],"michael-jackson-dangerous":["A long, sprawling album that opens in a flurry of hard, clanking new jack swing beats and ends in huge ballads. Jam starts with the sound of breaking glass and a heavy swing groove; Remember the Time is a smooth, sensual dance song; Black or White pairs a bright rock guitar riff with a message against racial division; and Heal the World and Will You Be There are grand, choir-backed anthems. At seventy-seven minutes, it fills the whole of a CD.","It was Jackson's first album without Quincy Jones. He turned to Teddy Riley, the young producer behind new jack swing, which fused hip-hop drum programming with R&B singing, and to Bill Bottrell for rockier tracks. Released in November 1991, it came with lavish videos, including the long Black or White clip that premiered simultaneously in many countries, and a world tour. It arrived just as grunge and gangsta rap were changing the landscape.","Will You Be There opens with a recording of the choral finale of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, performed by the Cleveland Orchestra, before Jackson's gospel-tinged song begins, a pop star claiming the most famous hymn to brotherhood in the classical repertoire. Listen to how Riley's drums are dry, punchy and swung, very different from the glossy Quincy Jones sound, and to the breaking glass that opens the album, a signal that the old sound is being smashed.","In January 1992 Nirvana's Nevermind replaced Dangerous at number one on the US album chart, and that swap became a symbol of pop's generational shift towards alternative rock. Critics were mixed at the time, finding it overlong, but many now regard it as Jackson's most adventurous and underrated album, especially its first half. Its new jack swing tracks have influenced later R&B and pop producers.","Begin with Jam and Remember the Time to hear the new jack swing sound in full. Then play Black or White for the rock-pop crossover. Listen to Will You Be There after Beethoven's Ninth to hear what Jackson borrows. Compare with Janet Jackson's Rhythm Nation 1814, and with Nirvana's Nevermind to hear the record that pushed it aside."],"prince-kiss":["A startlingly bare funk song: a crisp drum-machine beat, a scratchy, choppy guitar, a few keyboard stabs, and Prince's falsetto squealing and purring over the top. There is no bassline at all, which gives the track a hollow, taut energy. The lyric is a playful list of things he does not need in a partner, set against the one thing he does. The squeaky vocal, the guitar chop and the sudden silences make it instantly recognisable within two seconds.","Prince first wrote Kiss as a slow acoustic blues sketch and gave it to Mazarati, a band on his Paisley Park label, whose producer David Z rebuilt it with a new groove. Hearing the result, Prince took the song back, added his vocals and guitar and put it on Parade, the soundtrack to his film Under the Cherry Moon. Released in February 1986, it reached number one in the US.","The missing bass is the song's great trick: Prince stripped it out to make space, a move he had already used on When Doves Cry, and the effect is of a groove with its floor removed. Listen for the tiny clipped guitar chords, which act almost like percussion, and the backing vocals, which are said to survive from the Mazarati version. Warner Bros. reportedly disliked the stripped-down track and was reluctant to release it as a single.","It became one of Prince's best-loved hits and won a Grammy. Critics have praised it as a masterclass in minimalism, proof that funk could be made from almost nothing. Its playful gender-bending, with Prince's falsetto and androgynous image, was both celebrated and mocked at the time. Tom Jones's 1988 cover with the Art of Noise made the song a hit again in a very different style.","Listen for what is missing: try to hear the bass and notice how the song manages without it. Then focus on the guitar chops and how they lock with the drum machine. Compare with James Brown's Sex Machine for the funk roots and with Dirty Mind to hear Prince's early minimal style. Finish with Purple Rain to hear him at his most expansive."],"the-beach-boys-california-girls":["The record opens with something no surf band had tried: a slow, stately prelude of about twenty seconds, guitars and horns and bass climbing through rich chords like the overture to a film. Then the shuffle kicks in, a loping bouncing groove under Mike Love's lead vocal, with the band's stacked harmonies answering behind him and a chorus that rises into a round of overlapping voices. The song is a cheerful tour of American regions, praising the young women of each before crowning California's.","Released in the summer of 1965 on the album Summer Days (And Summer Nights!!), it came from Brian Wilson's first year off the road. He had stopped touring after a breakdown on a flight in December 1964 and now stayed in Los Angeles, building tracks with the session players of the Wrecking Crew while the rest of the band toured. It was also the first Beach Boys record to feature Bruce Johnston, who had just joined as Brian's stage replacement.","Listen to how little the famous prelude has to do with the song that follows. It is a separate piece of music, in a grander mood, and Wilson has often named it as one of the things he is proudest of. Under the verses the bass and organ lock into a pulsing figure borrowed from the Phil Spector records Wilson idolised, and the chorus stacks entries of the same words at different moments.","It was a major hit in the United States and has become one of the most familiar songs of the decade, the sound of an idealised Southern California. Critics now hear it as the hinge between the early surf-and-cars Beach Boys and the ambitious studio records to come. Its regional roll call has also drawn gentle mockery for its innocence, and David Lee Roth's 1985 cover played it purely for fun.","Play the first twenty seconds twice before you let the song begin, and hear how it sounds like a different, more serious record. Then follow the bass through the verses, and in the chorus count the separate voices entering. Next play Be My Baby by the Ronettes for the model, and Pet Sounds, the following spring, for where the prelude was pointing."],"the-beach-boys-the-smile-sessions":["Smile is a suite of short fragments rather than a set of songs: a wordless chorale for six voices, banjo and harpsichord episodes, cowboy chants, a barbershop tag, a raging string evocation of fire, bird calls, and three movements that sweep across American history from Plymouth Rock to Hawaii. Pieces return in new keys and moods, and the whole thing ends with Good Vibrations. The best-known songs are Heroes and Villains, Cabin Essence, Wonderful and Surf's Up.","Brian Wilson began Smile in 1966 with the lyricist Van Dyke Parks, meaning it to outdo Pet Sounds and the Beatles. He recorded it in modules, dozens of tiny sections to be spliced together later. By spring 1967 pressure from the band, the label, his own fragile health and the sheer difficulty of assembly stopped it. A thinner substitute, Smiley Smile, came out that autumn, and the tapes stayed in the vault for decades until Capitol assembled this edition in 2011.","The fire section, known as Mrs. O'Leary's Cow, has its own legend: nearby fires broke out after the session, and Wilson, perhaps half seriously, feared the music had caused them. Listen too for the way a few melodic cells recur everywhere; the tune of Heroes and Villains resurfaces inside other pieces, and the opening chorale's harmonies return at the end. The 2011 sequence follows the order Wilson finally chose for his own 2004 recording, Brian Wilson Presents Smile.","For forty years Smile was the most famous unreleased album in rock, a myth sustained by bootlegs and by Surf's Up, which critics treated as proof of what had been lost. When Wilson rerecorded it in 2004 the reception was ecstatic; the 2011 box, drawing on the original tapes, won a Grammy for historical album. Debate remains over whether any assembled Smile is authentic, since it was never finished in 1967.","Treat it as a suite, not a playlist: listen straight through, headphones on, and notice fragments coming back transformed. Start with Our Prayer for the voices alone, then Surf's Up for the piano and the long, winding melody. Compare Charles Ives, who also built American collage out of hymns, parlour songs and noise, and Sgt. Pepper, the record that did get finished that year."],"juan-atkins-cybotron-alleys-of-your-mind":["A stiff, clanking drum-machine beat, a rubbery synthesizer bassline that bounces between a few notes, and a voice pushed through a vocoder so that it sounds half human and half radio signal. Bright synth chords sweep in and out. The song is about paranoia and the mind turning on itself, delivered in a flat, robotic chant. It is funky but cold, a dance record that sounds like it was made inside a machine.","Juan Atkins was a teenager from Belleville, outside Detroit, when he formed Cybotron with Rick Davis, a Vietnam veteran with a collection of synthesizers and a taste for futurist theory. They pressed Alleys of Your Mind on their own Deep Space label in 1981. It got its break on WGPR, where the late-night DJ the Electrifying Mojo mixed Kraftwerk, Prince and funk, and it reportedly sold some thousands of copies around the city.","Listen to the bass. It is not a German-style sequencer pulse but a syncopated funk line, the kind Parliament's Bernie Worrell played, rendered on a synthesizer. That hybrid of Kraftwerk's machines and Detroit funk is the seed of techno. The vocoder, too, sat beside the talk-box voices of Zapp and the vocoders of funk that Detroit listeners already knew, so the record sounded futuristic without sounding foreign.","It was a local success rather than a national one, and for years it was known mainly to Detroit dancers and collectors. Histories of techno now treat it as one of the genre's first stirrings, alongside Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock in New York, which it slightly predates. Some argue it belongs more to electro than to techno proper, and the distinction is still debated.","Start with the bassline and let the rest fall into place around it; it is the funk inside the machine. Then notice how few elements there are, perhaps five or six sounds in all. Play Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine first for the model, then Clear, Cybotron's next record, and finally Model 500's No UFO's to hear Atkins strip the idea down into techno."],"juan-atkins-cybotron-techno-city":["A brisk, clattering drum-machine rhythm, a busy synth bass and layers of glassy keyboard chords under a vocal that describes an imagined city of the future, half utopia and half factory. The texture is thicker and more melodic than Cybotron's earlier records, with little hook phrases passing between synthesizers. It sits somewhere between electro-funk and the stripped techno that was about to arrive, and its title gave that music a word.","By 1984 Cybotron had a deal with Fantasy, the Berkeley label that had reissued their earlier singles, and Techno City was their last record with Juan Atkins. He and Rick Davis were pulling apart: Davis wanted guitars and rock ambition, Atkins wanted to push further into pure machine dance music. Atkins had absorbed Alvin Toffler's book The Third Wave, whose talk of techno-rebels seems to have fed the vocabulary he later gave the Detroit sound.","The word techno was not yet a genre name; here it is simply part of a science-fiction place name. Listen to the track as a manifesto in hindsight: the city Atkins imagines, where industry, machines and people fuse, is a mirror of Detroit itself, a motor town whose car plants were automating and shedding workers through the early 1980s. The utopia has a faint undertone of threat.","It made little commercial mark and Cybotron dissolved as Atkins left to found Metroplex and Model 500. Its reputation grew backwards once techno had a name: historians of Detroit treat it as the moment the concept and the word came together. Some listeners find it dated, closer to early-1980s synth-pop than to the rigorous tracks that followed; others hear its warmth as the missing link.","Listen for the moment the full band of synthesizers is running together, then ask yourself how much of it Atkins would throw away a year later on No UFO's. That comparison is the birth of techno in two records. Follow with Kraftwerk's Computer World, which imagines a similar electronic society from Düsseldorf, and Derrick May's Strings of Life for the Detroit city at full flight."],"juan-atkins-model-500-night-drive":["A slow-burning, hypnotic track: a steady drum-machine pattern, a deep bass pulse and eerie, shimmering synth tones that drift past like streetlights. Over it a low, murmured, electronically treated voice narrates a drive through a nocturnal city, describing what it sees rather than singing. Nothing builds to a climax; the track simply keeps moving, cool and watchful, until the car, so to speak, disappears into the dark.","After leaving Cybotron, Juan Atkins set up his own label, Metroplex, in Detroit in 1985 and began issuing records as Model 500, a name chosen to sound anonymous, like a machine's model number. Night Drive followed No UFO's in that first year. Metroplex was a one-man operation in a city whose radio and club scene was small, and many of its early records sold mainly through a few shops and to DJs in Chicago and Europe.","The title's Babylon is a way of naming Detroit as a fallen, glittering city, a word probably borrowed from Rastafarian and gospel imagery. Listen to how the voice is placed: low in the mix, nearly swallowed, as if heard over a car radio. The sparse arrangement leaves long gaps in which the listener supplies the city. It is closer to a film soundtrack or a radio play than to a club tool.","It is less celebrated than No UFO's but has been admired by DJs and critics as the darker, more cinematic side of early Model 500, anticipating the moody, narrative electronic music of the 1990s. Some listeners find the spoken voice gimmicky; others hear it as a Detroit answer to Kraftwerk's deadpan narrators. Its reputation rose with R&S's later reissues of Model 500's early catalogue.","Play it at night, in a car or with headphones, at low volume, and let the voice come and go. Notice how the drum pattern barely changes and how the atmosphere is carried by the high synth tones. Then hear Kraftwerk's Autobahn, the ancestor of every electronic drive, and Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission for where Detroit's darker current went next."],"derrick-may-it-is-what-it-is":["A cooler, more reflective piece than Strings of Life: a rolling drum-machine pattern, a gently looping synth figure and soft chords that shift underneath it, with small melodic details glinting at the edges. It moves steadily rather than exploding, circling through a few ideas and letting them overlap. The mood is poised between melancholy and calm, the sound of someone thinking while dancing, and there are no vocals at all.","Derrick May released it in 1988 as Rhythim Is Rhythim on Transmat, the label he ran in Detroit, in the year the city's music suddenly found an audience abroad. British DJs and the compilation Techno! The New Dance Sound of Detroit had just carried the sound to London, and acid house was sweeping British clubs. May was touring Europe and being treated as a star in a way he never was at home.","The title is not a shrug but a statement of aesthetic: the track is meant to be taken on its own terms, without a story or a singer. Listen to how the chords never quite resolve; May leaves the harmony hanging, so the piece feels both finished and open. The drum programming, as ever with May, pushes slightly ahead, giving the calm surface a nervous undercurrent.","It never had the anthem status of Strings of Life, but DJs and critics have long valued it as the more introspective side of May's small output, and it became a model for the melodic, headphone-friendly strain of 1990s techno, the music British writers called intelligent techno. It is often cited alongside Nude Photo as proof that Detroit techno could be emotional without being euphoric.","Listen once for the whole mood, then again following only the chords underneath and how rarely they settle. Compare it directly with Strings of Life, same producer, opposite temperament. Then try Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works 85–92 to hear how British producers took this reflective Detroit strain home and slowed it down."],"derrick-may-beyond-the-dance":["A lush, driving track in which rich chords, played on synthesizers with a string-like sheen, ride over a busy, syncopated drum-machine rhythm. Melodic fragments rise and fall above them, and the whole piece surges forward in long waves rather than repeating a single loop. It is warm and romantic, with a jazz-like sense of harmony, and it sounds like an attempt to make dance music that could stand on its own as composition.","It appeared in 1989 on Transmat under the Rhythim Is Rhythim name, at the height of May's international reputation. Detroit techno had become a phenomenon in Britain and Germany; May was travelling constantly, and Transmat was issuing records by younger Detroit producers as well as his own. He released very little of his own music, working slowly and obsessively on each track, and each one was awaited in Europe as an event.","The title is the argument. May often said he wanted techno to reach beyond the dancefloor toward something more like classical or jazz, and here the chords do the work that melody does in a song. Listen to how the harmony changes under a steady beat: the rhythm stays in the club while the chords go somewhere else. His admiration for European orchestral music is audible in the string-like voicings.","It has always been admired as one of the most beautiful Detroit records, and critics often place it with Strings of Life as May's most orchestral work. Some DJs found it too dense and too rich for peak-time sets, which is part of its point. Its lavish harmony is widely heard in the melodic techno and house that came out of Detroit and Berlin in the 1990s.","Listen first for the chords and how they seem to breathe; then shift attention to the drums and notice how busy they are underneath the calm. Try it back to back with Strings of Life to hear two versions of May's orchestral dream. Then go to Underground Resistance's Hi-Tech Jazz, the next Detroit generation's answer to the same ambition."],"derrick-may-innovator":["A retrospective collection that gathers nearly all the music Derrick May released as Rhythim Is Rhythim, from the first Transmat singles of 1987 to the early 1990s. Strings of Life, Nude Photo, The Dance, It Is What It Is and Beyond the Dance sit alongside later, darker pieces and alternate versions. Heard together, the tracks reveal a consistent voice: restless drum programming, romantic chords, orchestral ambition and no vocals. It is the nearest thing to a Derrick May album.","May never made a conventional album. By the mid-1990s he had largely stopped releasing new music, concentrating on DJing around the world and on running Transmat, and his singles had become expensive collector's items. Innovator was assembled to put the whole body of work back into circulation, at a time when Detroit techno was being canonised by European labels and magazines as the founding music of a global dance culture.","The most surprising thing is how small the output is: a producer spoken of as one of techno's founders is represented by perhaps a couple of hours of music. Listen for the recurring devices across years, especially the way May sets rapid, busy drum programming under slow, sweeping harmony. The alternate versions reveal how carefully he reworked tracks, sometimes changing little more than the balance of layers.","It was received as an essential document, and critics have since listed it among the key electronic collections of the decade. The question it raises has never gone away: why did May stop? Some see his silence as perfectionism, others as a refusal of the market; either way, admirers argue that the quality of this small body of work justifies his reputation entirely.","Do not play it straight through at first. Begin with Strings of Life and Nude Photo for the two sides of May, then It Is What It Is and Beyond the Dance for the reflective and orchestral sides. Return later for the rarer pieces. Pair it with Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission to hear how the next Detroit wave hardened May's sound."],"the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground":["After two records of noise and drone, the band's third album is startlingly quiet: soft guitars, murmured vocals, gentle ballads. Candy Says opens it with Doug Yule singing in a fragile voice; Pale Blue Eyes is a hushed love song; Jesus is a whispered prayer; What Goes On and Beginning to See the Light are chiming, driving rock. The Murder Mystery runs two spoken texts at once in opposite speakers, and Maureen Tucker sings the closing After Hours alone.","John Cale, the band's avant-garde pole, had been pushed out in 1968 and replaced by the bassist Doug Yule. The group recorded the album in Los Angeles late that year and MGM released it in March 1969. Lou Reed was now firmly in charge and writing more personal songs about love, loneliness and faith. The label's promotion was minimal and the album went almost nowhere commercially.","Two different mixes exist. Lou Reed made his own, drier version, known as the closet mix because it sounds as if the band were recorded in a cupboard, with the vocals up front and the instruments muted; engineer Val Valentin made the more conventional one. Different pressings used different mixes, and hearing both changes the record's mood: the closet mix is intimate to the point of claustrophobia.","Little noticed on release, it grew into the favourite Velvet Underground album for many listeners, especially musicians drawn to its quietness. Its gentle songs fed directly into the 1980s indie tradition of R.E.M., the Feelies and Galaxie 500. Fans still argue about which mix is canonical, and about whether the album is a retreat from the band's radicalism or a different kind of daring.","Start with Pale Blue Eyes and Candy Says, then play What Goes On loud and hear the guitars chiming like an organ. Listen for how much space is left around the voices. Compare the brutal White Light/White Heat from a year earlier to feel the reversal, and Nick Drake's Pink Moon for another record made from almost nothing."],"the-velvet-underground-loaded":["The Velvet Underground's most straightforward rock-and-roll record: bright, tuneful, full of piano and hand-played riffs. Who Loves the Sun opens it with sweet, Beatles-like harmonies; Sweet Jane and Rock & Roll are two of Lou Reed's most famous songs; Cool It Down and Head Held High are barrelling rhythm-and-blues; New Age is a slow, sad portrait of a faded film star; Oh! Sweet Nuthin' closes the album with a long, slow-burning jam.","The band moved to Atlantic's subsidiary Cotillion and recorded in New York in the spring and summer of 1970. Atlantic wanted a record loaded with hits, and the title nods at that. Maureen Tucker was away on maternity leave, so Doug Yule and several session drummers covered for her, and Yule sang lead on several songs. Lou Reed quit the band in August, before the album was finished, and it came out that November without him.","Rock & Roll is a small autobiography: a girl in the suburbs whose life is saved when she hears rock music on the radio, which is really Reed's own story, and a creed for everything the band did. Listen too to New Age: the released version was edited after Reed left, and he complained that songs had been cut behind his back. The expanded Fully Loaded edition of 1997 restores the longer takes.","It was greeted as the band's most accessible work and has become one of its most loved, though purists suspect it of compromise. Reed's later disavowals complicated its reputation, as did the fact that the band's drummer barely plays on it. Today it is often called the bridge between the Velvets' underground legend and Reed's solo career, and a textbook in how to write a simple rock song.","Begin with Rock & Roll and listen to how the song describes its own effect as it happens. Then play Who Loves the Sun for the sweetness and Oh! Sweet Nuthin' for the long, patient guitar build. Afterwards hear the Velvet Underground & Nico to feel how far the band had travelled in three years, and Television's Marquee Moon for the guitar-band tradition this feeds."],"the-velvet-underground-sweet-jane":["A chiming three-chord guitar riff, instantly recognisable, rings out over a steady mid-tempo beat. Lou Reed sings in a half-spoken drawl about an ordinary couple, a banker and a clerk, their quiet home life, and the solace of rock music, before defending decent everyday people against the jaded and the cynical. The riff returns between verses like a refrain, and the song builds to a chorus of simple, shouted syllables.","Reed had been playing Sweet Jane live since 1969, in a slower, more reflective arrangement; a version recorded on tour that year was released in 1974 on the double album 1969: The Velvet Underground Live. The studio version was cut in New York in 1970 for Loaded, the band's last album with Reed. It became the most performed song of his whole career, a fixture of his solo concerts for decades.","The version on the original Loaded is missing a whole section: a slow, dreamlike bridge that was cut in the final edit after Reed had left. It can be heard on later reissues, and it changes the song from a jaunty rocker into something more wistful. Compare too the 1969 live take, where Reed sings different words and the tempo drags almost to a standstill.","Little noticed at first, it became one of the most-covered songs in rock. Mott the Hoople recorded it in 1972, Reed's own 1974 live album Rock n Roll Animal gave it a lengthy twin-guitar overture, and the Cowboy Junkies' hushed 1988 version, built on the slow 1969 arrangement, was reportedly Reed's favourite. Critics now treat its riff as a building block of alternative rock.","Play the Loaded version first and notice how the riff carries the whole structure. Then find the restored version with the missing bridge, and the 1969 live recording, and hear three different songs grow from the same chords. Finish with the Cowboy Junkies' slow cover to hear how much tenderness is hidden inside the riff."],"dr-dre-still-dre":["The whole track hangs on a staccato piano figure of three stabbing chords, repeated with metronomic precision over a slow, heavy drum beat and a low bass. Strings sweep in behind it. Dr. Dre and Snoop Dogg trade verses in a calm, confident tone, Dre announcing his return and insisting he never lost his place, Snoop reaffirming their partnership. There is almost no ornament; the space around the piano is the point.","It was the lead single from 2001, Dre's second solo album, released at the end of 1999. After leaving Death Row in 1996 and launching his Aftermath label, Dre had faced suggestions that his best work was behind him, and the album was framed as a comeback. Snoop Dogg, his protégé from The Chronic, returned alongside him. Jay-Z, then New York's most successful rapper, is widely credited with writing Dre's verses.","The famous piano part was played by the young keyboardist Scott Storch, who became one of the most sought-after producers of the 2000s. Listen to how dry and clipped it is: each chord is cut off almost at once, so the track feels spacious and cool rather than lush. The ghostwritten verses are another hidden layer: a Los Angeles icon voicing his return in lines written by a New Yorker.","It was an immediate hit and has become one of the most recognisable beats in hip-hop, endlessly sampled, parodied and played at stadiums. Critics at the time noted that it moved away from the rolling funk of G-funk toward something sparer and more orchestral. The ghostwriting has occasionally been raised as a question of authorship, but in hip-hop, where production is authorship, it rarely troubled anyone.","Listen once only for the piano and the drums; notice how rarely anything else intrudes. Then compare Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang from 1992 to hear how Dre's sound changed from warm funk samples to cold, clean playing. Follow with Eminem's The Marshall Mathers LP, made in the same Aftermath circle with the same dry precision."],"dr-dre-compton":["A dense, cinematic album with a huge cast: Kendrick Lamar, Ice Cube, Snoop Dogg, Eminem, Xzibit, the Game and, most memorably, the singer and drummer Anderson .Paak, alongside younger rappers. The production is lavish and restless, full of live instruments, orchestral swells, sudden beat changes and radio-drama interludes. It opens with a mock newsreel about the history of Compton, and its songs move between celebration of success and anger at police violence and poverty.","Dr. Dre had spent more than a decade promising an album called Detox, which he eventually abandoned. Compton appeared in August 2015, sixteen years after 2001, inspired by the making of the film Straight Outta Compton about his old group N.W.A. By then Dre was enormously rich from the sale of his Beats headphone company to Apple, and the album was released first on Apple Music. He pledged his royalties to a new performing-arts centre for the city.","Listen for how the album hands the microphone to others. Dre himself raps less than his guests, and the record often feels like a showcase for Anderson .Paak, whose raspy singing and drumming appear on several songs and launched his career. The newsreel opening frames the whole album as a documentary about a place, so individual songs become scenes in a longer film.","It was well received, particularly for its production and for Kendrick Lamar's contributions, though some critics found it overstuffed and unfocused. It also revived discussion of Dre's past violence against women, prompting him to issue a public apology, and some reviewers said the album could not be heard apart from that history. It remains, so far, his last album.","Treat it like a film soundtrack. Begin with the tracks featuring Anderson .Paak and Kendrick Lamar, then listen from the start for the transitions and interludes. Compare Kendrick's To Pimp a Butterfly from the same year, which uses a similar live-band palette, and The Chronic to hear how far Dre's sound has travelled."],"janet-jackson-janet":["A long, warm, sensual album that ranges widely: the relaxed groove of That's the Way Love Goes, the hard, grinding dance track If, the tender piano ballad Again, the rock guitars of This Time, and the house-influenced Throb. Short spoken interludes link the songs so that the album flows like a mixtape. Jackson's voice is soft and intimate throughout, placed close to the microphone, and the sound is looser and more organic than her earlier records.","It was her first album for Virgin, which had signed her in 1991 to a contract reported at the time as the richest ever given to a recording artist. She again worked with Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis in Minneapolis, now writing more of the material herself. The title, lower-case with a full stop, declared that she was simply janet, not a member of the Jackson family. It followed the socially conscious Rhythm Nation 1814.","The interludes are not filler: they carry the album's theme of intimacy and trust between songs, including snatches of laughter and conversation. Listen to how Jam and Lewis sample and loop older soul and funk records beneath new playing, a hip-hop method brought to pop. That's the Way Love Goes, for instance, is built on a looped groove from a James Brown record.","It sold enormously, spending weeks at number one, and That's the Way Love Goes became the longest-running number-one single of her career; Again was nominated for an Academy Award. Its sensual frankness was controversial at the time, though critics now see it as a step in Black women artists claiming their own sexuality in pop, and as a bridge to the neo-soul of the later 1990s.","Start with That's the Way Love Goes and notice how little happens: a lazy groove, a quiet voice, lots of air. Then play If for the contrast in energy and Again for the ballad. Listen through with the interludes intact. Follow it with TLC's CrazySexyCool and Janet's own The Velvet Rope to hear what came next."],"janet-jackson-nasty":["A hard, clanking, metallic groove: drum machines hit like hammers on sheet metal, a synth bass snaps underneath, and shouted backing voices punch in like a street crew. Janet Jackson sings with a clipped, commanding tone, rebuffing men who harass her and demanding respect. The track is built from sharp, short sounds rather than smooth ones, and it has a swagger and toughness that surprised listeners who knew her as a Jackson family sister.","It was the second single from Control, her breakthrough album of 1986, made in Minneapolis with Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis, former members of the Time. Jackson had just broken from her father's management, and the album was framed as a declaration of independence. The song reportedly grew from her own experience of being harassed on the street. It reached the top three in the United States.","The sounds that make the track so distinctive are sampled and treated noises rather than traditional instruments, part of a Minneapolis production style that Jam and Lewis had learned alongside Prince. Listen to the drum sounds, which have an industrial bite closer to Kraftwerk than to Motown. The shouted backing vocals are a kind of chorus, a crowd on Janet's side.","It was a huge hit and gave Jackson a new public identity: independent, confrontational and in control. Critics later heard it as one of the defining productions of the 1980s, a foundation for new jack swing and for the hard-edged dance-pop that followed. The music video, with choreography by Paula Abdul, was as influential as the song.","Listen first for the drums and the way each hit is short and hard, then notice how Jackson's voice matches that sharpness. Compare Prince's 1999 for the Minneapolis sound it grew out of, and Rhythm Nation 1814 for where Jam, Lewis and Jackson took it next. Watch the video to see the choreography that matched the beat."],"sly-and-the-family-stone-dance-to-the-music":["The song introduces the band one member at a time, like a demonstration: drums first, then bass, guitar, keyboards, then trumpet and saxophone, each layer joining the groove. Voices are split between male and female, and at one point the instruments drop out for an a cappella passage in which the band's singers, including Larry Graham's very low voice, chant rhythmic syllables. The result is a mixed-sex, mixed-race band celebrating its own sound.","Sly Stone was a former radio DJ and record producer in San Francisco who put together a band that was racially and sexually integrated, rare in 1967. Their first album had not sold, and Epic pushed for something more commercial. Dance to the Music was the result, released at the end of 1967, and it became their first hit. It arrived just as San Francisco's psychedelic scene was at its height.","Sly reportedly thought of the song as a deliberately simple formula written to satisfy the label, and was a little embarrassed by it. But the idea at its heart, instruments entering one by one to show the groove being built, is a lesson in how funk works: it reveals the separate parts before joining them together. Listen for the gospel roots in the a cappella section; Sly grew up singing in church.","Its impact was immediate and enormous. Motown producer Norman Whitfield took the sound directly into the Temptations' Cloud Nine, psychedelic soul was born, and many black musicians of the time spoke of the band as a revelation. Critics now see the record as a hinge between soul and funk, and between the separate audiences of black and white pop.","Count the entries: listen for each instrument as it joins and try to keep all of them in your ear at once. Then enjoy the a cappella passage and listen for the lowest voice. Follow with the Temptations' Cloud Nine, which borrows the approach, and with Everyday People to hear how Sly turned this energy into social comment."],"the-stooges-the-stooges":["A short, brutal record of simple, heavy riffs repeated until they become trance, with Iggy Pop's sneering, bored, provocative vocals on top. 1969 opens it with a jaded shrug at a whole year of youth; I Wanna Be Your Dog lurches on three chords and a jingling bell; No Fun is a sulky, stomping anthem; Real Cool Time and Not Right are blunt and fast. We Will Fall is a ten-minute droning chant, almost a religious meditation, featuring the producer's viola.","The Stooges came from Ann Arbor, Michigan, and were signed to Elektra in 1968 alongside Detroit's MC5. They recorded the album in New York in 1969 with John Cale of the Velvet Underground producing. The band reportedly arrived with only a handful of songs and wrote more almost overnight to fill the album. Elektra and Iggy Pop remixed Cale's version before release, and it came out that August.","Cale's original mix, released decades later, is darker and more droning, closer to the Velvet Underground; the released version is punchier and more conventional. Listen for his viola on We Will Fall, which connects the album to the drone music of La Monte Young, in whose ensemble Cale had played. The simple riffs are really minimalist: the pleasure lies in repetition, not in variation.","It sold poorly and was mocked or ignored by most critics, who heard it as incompetent noise. A few rock writers defended it, and by the mid-1970s it was treated as an ancestor of punk. Today it is regarded as one of the founding documents of that music, though its minimalist drone side is still underappreciated next to its violence.","Play 1969 and No Fun loud, and notice how little the riffs change; the power comes from sameness. Then sit with We Will Fall and hear the drone connect to La Monte Young. Afterwards play the Ramones' first album and the Sex Pistols to hear what the next generation took from it."],"the-stooges-i-wanna-be-your-dog":["Three chords, a distorted descending guitar riff, and a relentless, pounding beat that never lets up. A single piano note is hammered in the background and a sleigh bell jingles all the way through. Iggy Pop's vocal is flat, bored and menacing as he offers himself in submission to someone, a song of degradation delivered with defiance. It is short, loud and utterly unchanging, and its riff is one of the most recognisable in rock.","It was recorded in New York in 1969 for the Stooges' first album, produced by John Cale of the Velvet Underground. The Stooges were then almost unknown outside Michigan, where their performances had become notorious for Iggy's self-lacerating stage antics. Elektra released it as a single, but it made no commercial impact. The band's sound was rooted in the industrial Midwest, with its factories and cold, boring suburbs.","The piano and the sleigh bell are both Cale's, and they come straight from his avant-garde background: the single hammered note is a drone in the manner of La Monte Young, and the bell is almost a joke on Christmas pop. Listen to how they add texture without adding harmony. The riff itself is three chords stepping downward, so simple a beginner could play it in minutes.","Ignored at first, it became a punk standard and one of the most covered songs of its kind: Sonic Youth recorded it in 1983, and a long line of punk and alternative bands have played it. Critics hail it as the archetypal proto-punk song, and its riff is often cited as the basic unit of punk rock. Its lyrical theme still unsettles some listeners.","Listen first for the piano note and the sleigh bell, which most people never notice. Then feel the riff's repetition, and notice the absence of variation. Follow with the Velvet Underground & Nico, which also builds on drone and repetition, and with the Ramones to hear how the riff became a whole style."],"the-stooges-raw-power":["Faster, sharper and more violent than the earlier Stooges records, driven by James Williamson's slashing, overdriven guitar. Search and Destroy opens it at full speed with a vocal of soldierly aggression; Gimme Danger broods and builds; Penetration is slinky and creepy; Raw Power and Shake Appeal are pounding rockers; I Need Somebody is a slow blues. Death Trip ends the album in chaotic, noisy disintegration. Iggy Pop's voice is at its most snarling.","The band had collapsed in 1971 amid drug problems. David Bowie's management revived Iggy's career and brought him to London, where the album was recorded in 1972 as Iggy and the Stooges, with Williamson on guitar and Ron Asheton moved to bass. Bowie mixed it in a hurry in Los Angeles, and Columbia released it in early 1973. It sold poorly, and the band fell apart again within a year.","The mix is famously strange: Bowie pushed the vocals and guitar up front while leaving the bass and drums thin, so the record sounds both savage and oddly weightless. In 1997 Iggy remixed it himself, making it one of the loudest records ever released; many listeners prefer one or the other, and the argument remains. Listen to how the guitar solos seem to explode rather than develop.","Critics were divided at the time, but punk musicians of the mid-1970s treated it as scripture, and Kurt Cobain and many others called it a favourite album. It is now considered one of the most influential rock records of the decade. The debate between the Bowie mix and the Iggy remix is one of the classic arguments in rock about loudness and authenticity.","Start with Search and Destroy and listen for the guitar's aggression. Then hear Gimme Danger, which shows the band could also build atmosphere slowly. Compare the 1973 Bowie mix with the 1997 remix and decide which sounds more like the band. Afterwards play the Sex Pistols and Nirvana's Nevermind to hear the album's heirs."],"jay-z-hard-knock-life":["The hook is a sampled chorus of children singing from the Broadway musical Annie, looped over a hard, bouncing drum beat. Jay-Z raps in a relaxed, confident voice about his rise from the Marcy housing projects in Brooklyn to wealth and fame, comparing his tough upbringing with the orphans' song. The combination of a sweet theatre tune and street autobiography is instantly memorable.","It was the lead single from Vol. 2... Hard Knock Life, his third album, released on Roc-A-Fella and Def Jam in 1998. The producer Mark James, known as the 45 King, built the beat from the Annie cast recording; the musical, by Charles Strouse and Martin Charnin, had opened on Broadway in 1977. The album became Jay-Z's best-selling record and won a Grammy.","The sample was reportedly hard to clear: Jay-Z is said to have written to the rights holders explaining that the song captured his own childhood, and they agreed. Listen to how the orphans' complaint becomes a universal statement of poverty. It is a model of hip-hop's practice of recontextualising pop sources, the same method used by Biggie's Juicy and later by Kanye West.","It was a major hit and turned Jay-Z from a respected New York rapper into a mainstream star. Some purists thought it too commercial, but critics now see it as a masterclass in crossover, and the Annie sample is widely admired. The album's success made Roc-A-Fella a powerhouse and set the stage for The Blueprint.","Listen for the children's chorus and notice how the drums make it tough. Then listen to Jay-Z's verses and how they turn the orphans' song into autobiography. Then find the original song from the Annie cast recording and hear how little the producer changed; the meaning shifts entirely with the new setting. Follow with Biggie's Juicy, another rags-to-riches anthem built on a pop sample, and The Blueprint for where Jay-Z took soul sampling next."],"missy-elliott-under-construction":["Missy Elliott's fourth album is a love letter to the hip-hop of her childhood, remade with Timbaland's elastic, bass-heavy machinery. Old-school breakbeats, electro handclaps and roller-rink samples sit beside the lopsided futurist rhythms the pair had pioneered. The shape is loose and generous: comic skits, brash party records, and slower soul numbers, with guests including Jay-Z on 'Back in the Day', Ludacris on 'Gossip Folks' and Beyoncé on 'Nothing Out There for Me'. 'Work It' is the centrepiece.","The album was made in the shadow of loss. Missy's close friend and collaborator Aaliyah had died in a plane crash in August 2001, and Lisa 'Left Eye' Lopes of TLC was killed in a car accident in April 2002. The spoken intro reflects on the feuds that had cost hip-hop Tupac and Biggie and asks the culture to look after itself, which gives the backward-looking title a double meaning: a genre rebuilding itself by remembering where it came from.","The nostalgia is literal. 'Gossip Folks' is built on Frankie Smith's 1980 funk novelty 'Double Dutch Bus', a playground record, and much of the album borrows the sound of early-eighties electro and block parties. 'Can You Hear Me', with the surviving members of TLC, is a quiet farewell addressed to Aaliyah and Left Eye; it is easy to skip past between louder tracks, and it explains the whole record's mood.","Reviewers welcomed it as Missy's most focused and joyful album, and it became the best-selling record of her career, carried by the enormous success of 'Work It'. Some critics found the skits and the old-school homage a little self-conscious beside the shock of Supa Dupa Fly. Its reputation now rests on how seamlessly it joined hip-hop's past and future at a moment when the genre was grieving.","Listen straight through the first half, where the old-school references pile up, and notice how Timbaland updates each borrowed sound rather than simply looping it. Then go to 'Can You Hear Me' for the emotional key. Compare Run-DMC's Raising Hell to hear the era Missy is saluting, and Supa Dupa Fly to hear how far her own sound had travelled in five years."],"missy-elliott-work-it":["A sparse, playful dance record in which almost every sound is a joke or a surprise. Timbaland's beat is a clipped electro rhythm with a booming bass drum, handclaps and odd interjections, including a trumpeting elephant. Missy raps in a nimble, conversational flow about attraction and confidence, in terms that are frankly sexual but delivered with comic timing. The hook contains a phrase played backwards, a gimmick that became the song's signature and set the whole world trying to decode it.","The lead single from Under Construction, released in the late summer of 2002, it arrived as Missy was redefining her public image: she had lost a good deal of weight and was talking openly about body image, and the song's swagger was read as a celebration of women's desire on their own terms. Dave Meyers's video, full of dancers, bees and a young Alyson Stoner out-dancing everyone, played constantly on MTV and BET.","The reversed phrase is not a studio accident. Missy recorded a line, flipped it, and let it stand in for a word she could not say on the radio, which made censorship itself part of the hook. Underneath, the track borrows from early hip-hop, including Run-DMC's 'Peter Piper', whose bell pattern came from a Bob James jazz-funk record: three generations of sampling folded into one beat.","It became the biggest hit of Missy's career, reaching number two in the United States, and won the Grammy for Best Female Rap Solo Performance. Critics praised its wit and its economy; many consider it the purest statement of the Missy and Timbaland partnership. Its frankness drew some predictable complaints, but it is now regularly listed among the finest singles of the 2000s.","Put on headphones and track the individual sounds: the dry claps, the bass drum that drops out and returns, the animal noises. Then listen for the backwards phrase in the chorus and try to hear the words inside it. Afterwards play 'Peter Piper' to hear what Missy is winking at, and 'Get Ur Freak On' for the same partnership at its most exotic."],"missy-elliott-lose-control":["A breathless dance-floor record featuring the singer Ciara and the New York hype man Fatman Scoop. The beat is pure electro: a cold, pulsing synthesizer figure, machine drums and chopped vocal commands, with Scoop's shouted interjections driving the crowd forward. Missy raps in quick bursts about letting go on the dance floor, and Ciara floats a sung hook over the top. The track builds and breaks down like a DJ set, and the sense of physical momentum never lets up.","It was the lead single from The Cookbook, Missy's sixth album, released in 2005 after she had parted ways with Timbaland for most of the record and began producing more of her own material. Hip-hop in 2005 was dominated by Southern crunk and snap music; Missy answered by reaching back two decades to Detroit electro, the machine music that had fed both early hip-hop and techno. Ciara, then a rising star from Atlanta, brought her dancer's credibility.","The backbone of the beat is Cybotron's 'Clear' of 1983, the early electro record Juan Atkins made in Detroit before he helped invent techno, combined with Hot Streak's 'Body Work' from the same year. So a 2005 pop-rap hit is quietly a tribute to the Black Detroit futurists who took Kraftwerk's machines and made them dance. Once you know it, the Cybotron synth line leaps out.","It reached number three in the United States and won the Grammy for Best Short Form Music Video for the frantic, choreography-packed clip Missy co-directed with Dave Meyers. Critics welcomed it as proof that Missy could still set the pace without Timbaland. It has had a long afterlife as a party staple and was part of her set when she joined Katy Perry's Super Bowl halftime show in 2015.","Play Cybotron's 'Clear' first, then 'Lose Control', and listen to how little Missy needs to change to make a 1983 track sound brand new. Notice how Fatman Scoop functions almost as a percussion instrument. Then trace the line forward to Derrick May's 'Strings of Life' and back to Kraftwerk to hear the whole Detroit-Düsseldorf family tree."],"2pac-changes":["Tupac raps over a warm, rolling piano figure taken from a 1986 pop hit, with a soft sung hook from the R&B group Talent. The mood is weary rather than angry. In three long verses he surveys American life as he saw it: racism, poverty, the war on drugs and the violence it fed, police killings, and his own doubt that anything will improve. The refrain insists that change is needed and that people must make it themselves.","Recorded around 1992, early in Tupac's career, the song sat unreleased until it was reworked and issued in 1998, two years after his murder, as the new track on his Greatest Hits collection. By then Tupac had become a posthumous industry, with a stream of albums built from his enormous archive. 'Changes' stood out because it recalled the political son of a Black Panther rather than the Death Row outlaw of his last year.","The piano comes from Bruce Hornsby and the Range's 'The Way It Is', a soft-rock hit about how the civil rights laws had not ended poverty and prejudice. Tupac did not just borrow a pleasant riff; he borrowed an argument, and Hornsby's own theme runs straight into his. Some of the verses also appear, in different form, in his earlier song 'I Wonder If Heaven Got a Ghetto', showing how he recycled lines across versions.","It became one of his most successful singles worldwide and was nominated for a Grammy for Best Rap Solo Performance. Critics have sometimes questioned the cynicism of the posthumous releases, but 'Changes' is widely treated as an exception, a core statement of his social vision. Its lines about a Black president were much discussed after 2008. It remains one of the most-played political songs in hip-hop.","Listen first for the dialogue between Tupac's voice and the piano, which keeps lifting while the words sink. Then play Hornsby's 'The Way It Is' and hear how naturally the two meanings fit. Put it beside Grandmaster Flash's 'The Message' and Kendrick Lamar's 'Alright' to hear three decades of the same argument, each generation with a different degree of hope."],"eminem-the-slim-shady-lp":["Eminem's major-label debut introduces Slim Shady, a cartoonish alter ego who says the most outrageous thing in any room. The beats, mostly by Dr. Dre and the Detroit producers Mark and Jeff Bass, are loose and funky, full of live bass and bouncing keyboards. The rapping is the attraction: dense internal rhymes, abrupt changes of voice and pace, and a comic timing that turns cruelty, violence and drug references into slapstick. 'My Name Is', 'Guilty Conscience' and 'Role Model' are the calling cards.","Marshall Mathers was a struggling Detroit rapper working low-wage jobs when he came second at the 1997 Rap Olympics in Los Angeles. A tape of his Slim Shady EP reached Dr. Dre, who signed him to Aftermath. A white rapper from a Black art form, Eminem was treated warily; Dre's backing gave him legitimacy. The album appeared in February 1999, as MTV was at the height of its power and moral panics about youth culture were a regular news story.","'My Name Is' is built on Labi Siffre's 1975 song 'I Got The...', and Siffre, a gay British songwriter, refused to clear the sample until Eminem removed homophobic lines, so the version everyone knows is itself a negotiated edit. On 'Guilty Conscience' Dre plays the angel to Eminem's devil, and the joke turns when Eminem throws Dre's own violent past back at him.","It went multi-platinum and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, and critics were divided from the start: many praised the technique and humour, while others found the misogyny and violence hard to excuse as satire. That debate has never quite closed. Hip-hop listeners who first saw him as a novelty came to rank him among the most technically gifted rappers of his generation.","Start with 'My Name Is', then play Labi Siffre's original to hear what Dre kept. Listen to how Eminem stretches and compresses syllables against the beat; follow one verse just for its rhyme patterns. Then compare The Marshall Mathers LP, a year later, where the joke curdles into something more personal and far darker."],"eminem-the-eminem-show":["Eminem's fourth album is his most musically ambitious and most rock-inflected, with heavy guitars, stomping drums and anthemic hooks. He produced most of it himself, with Dr. Dre contributing several tracks. The subject is fame: its absurdity, its cost to his family, his fights with politicians and critics. 'Without Me' revives Slim Shady at his most gleeful; 'Cleanin' Out My Closet' is a bitter account of his childhood; 'White America' tackles his own privilege; 'Sing for the Moment' defends rap to its enemies.","By 2002 Eminem was the biggest-selling rapper in the world, the target of congressional hearings and parent groups, and about to star in the film 8 Mile. The album was rushed out in late May, weeks ahead of schedule, because bootleg copies were circulating online, a sign of the new file-sharing era. It became the best-selling album of the year in the United States, in the months after September 11 when American pop culture was unusually anxious and patriotic.","'Sing for the Moment' is built on Aerosmith's 1973 ballad 'Dream On' and ends with Joe Perry's guitar solo from the record, a nod to the rap-rock bridge Run-DMC and Aerosmith built with 'Walk This Way'. 'Till I Collapse' uses the stamp-stamp-clap rhythm of Queen's 'We Will Rock You'. Listen too for his mother and ex-wife as recurring characters across the album, a family drama told in instalments.","It won the Grammy for Best Rap Album and was praised as his most mature and musically varied work, with more self-examination and less shock. Some hip-hop critics felt the guitars pushed it towards stadium rock and away from the genre's centre. Many listeners regard it as the peak of his commercial and creative powers; everything after, for a long stretch, sounded like a struggle to return here.","Begin with 'White America' and 'Cleanin' Out My Closet' to hear the two sides, public and private. Then play 'Sing for the Moment' straight after Aerosmith's 'Dream On'. Notice how often the beats leave space for his voice alone. For contrast, Kanye West's The College Dropout two years later shows a completely different idea of hip-hop's direction."],"notorious-big-big-poppa":["A slow, luxurious party record in which Biggie plays the smooth host of the club. The beat is a glossy loop of soft synthesizers and a lazy, heavy drum pattern; nothing hurries. Biggie's delivery is relaxed and conversational, full of humour and self-mockery about his looks, as he describes the pleasures of success, fine clothes and flirting. The easy groove made it a crossover hit, a counterweight to the darker street tales elsewhere on Ready to Die.","Released as a single from Ready to Die, it was produced by Chucky Thompson with Sean 'Puffy' Combs, whose Bad Boy label was building a polished New York sound that could compete on the radio with the West Coast's G-funk. Biggie had grown up in Brooklyn and sold drugs as a teenager before Puffy signed him; this song marks the moment he turned from street chronicler into a star with pop appeal.","The warm synth bed comes from the Isley Brothers' 1983 slow jam 'Between the Sheets', one of the most-sampled soul records of the era; Bad Boy's strategy of pairing hard rhymes with familiar R&B came together here. Listen to how Biggie places his words slightly behind the beat, making even a boast sound effortless, and how his humour about his own weight disarms the listener.","It reached the American top ten and was nominated for a Grammy for Best Rap Solo Performance. Some hip-hop purists grumbled that Bad Boy's slick sampling was too radio-friendly, a complaint that dogged the label all decade. Today it is admired as a model of charisma and ease, the record that showed hardcore rap and romance could share a single voice.","Play the Isley Brothers' 'Between the Sheets' first, then this, and notice how little is added. Listen for Biggie's breath control: long lines delivered without strain. Compare Dr. Dre's 'Nuthin' but a G Thang' for the West Coast version of the same leisurely swagger, and 'Juicy' for Biggie's other crossover mood."],"notorious-big-hypnotize":["A sleek, bouncing single built on a sampled bass line that loops without pause, with a female chorus chanting the hook. Over it Biggie delivers some of his most agile verses, full of internal rhyme and shifting rhythm, boasting of wealth, style and his effect on the room. It is swaggering and light on its feet, with none of the dread of his earlier work, and its groove made it one of the defining club records of the late nineties.","The lead single from Life After Death, produced by Puff Daddy with Deric 'D-Dot' Angelettie, it was released in early March 1997. On 9 March Biggie was shot dead in Los Angeles, days after the video's filming, amid the East Coast-West Coast feud that had already claimed Tupac the previous September. The song rose to number one in the United States in the weeks after his death: his first chart-topper, and a posthumous one.","The bass line comes from Herb Alpert's 1979 jazz-pop instrumental 'Rise', and the chanted chorus reworks a phrase from Slick Rick and Doug E. Fresh's 1985 'La Di Da Di', so the track rests on two very different ancestors, one smooth, one old-school. In the video, a speedboat chase with Puffy, Biggie barely moves while the camera races around him; his stillness is part of the persona.","It was received as a triumph and, inevitably, as an elegy, and its success cemented Bad Boy's commercial dominance. Critics who disliked the label's glossy sampling still praised Biggie's rapping here as among his most dexterous. Over time it has become one of the most-quoted and most-sampled hip-hop singles of the decade, and it is often cited to show how much he had left to do.","Follow Biggie's rhythm rather than his words: he speeds up, stalls and then catches the beat again. Then play Herb Alpert's 'Rise' and Slick Rick's 'La Di Da Di' to hear the sources. Set it beside 'Juicy' to hear how far his flow had developed in three years."],"nas-ny-state-of-mind":["The first full song on Illmatic, produced by DJ Premier, and one of the most admired pieces of rapping ever recorded. A dark, circling piano figure and a hard, dusty drum loop set a mood of night-time menace; Premier scratches in fragments of Rakim's voice for the hook. Nas, twenty years old, delivers two long verses without a pause, painting the Queensbridge housing projects in cinematic close-up: gunfights, drug dealing and survival, described with the detachment of a reporter and the precision of a poet.","Illmatic was released in April 1994, when New York hip-hop was trying to reclaim ground from the West Coast. Nas had been hailed as a prodigy since a guest verse on Main Source's 'Live at the Barbeque' in 1991, and Columbia assembled a team of the city's best producers around him. Premier, of Gang Starr, was the master of chopped jazz samples, and this track shows the style at its most austere.","Listen to the brief spoken moment before the first verse, when Nas seems to wonder aloud how to begin: it gives the whole performance the feel of improvisation, though the verses are intricately built. The eerie piano comes from a jazz record by the drummer Joe Chambers. And notice how the rhymes run across bar lines, so sentences begin in the middle of one musical phrase and end in the next.","Critics recognised it at once as a high-water mark for lyricism, and it is regularly placed among the greatest hip-hop tracks. Its first verse in particular is studied by rappers as a model of breath, imagery and rhythm. The only debate is whether it or 'The World Is Yours' is Illmatic's finest moment; the answer usually depends on whether a listener prefers darkness or light.","Listen once for the sound alone, the piano and the scratched hook, then again following only the rhymes, noticing how many fall inside lines rather than at the ends. Compare Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full, the model Nas and Premier are honouring, and Mobb Deep's 'Shook Ones Part II' for another view of Queensbridge."],"nas-it-was-written":["Nas's second album trades Illmatic's grainy jazz loops for a glossier, more cinematic sound, much of it produced by the Trackmasters duo, with contributions from Dr. Dre, DJ Premier and others. He adopts a new persona, Nas Escobar, a mafia-film kingpin, and tells longer, more elaborate stories. The hits were 'If I Ruled the World (Imagine That)', with Lauryn Hill, and 'Street Dreams'; the deeper cuts include 'I Gave You Power', a story narrated by a gun.","Illmatic had been a critical triumph but only a modest seller, and Columbia wanted a star. In 1996, with Biggie, Jay-Z and Tupac selling millions and the mafioso style fashionable in New York, Nas followed the market. The album entered the chart at number one and went multi-platinum. It also introduced his short-lived supergroup The Firm, with AZ, Foxy Brown and Cormega, on 'Affirmative Action'.","The borrowings are unusually pop: 'If I Ruled the World' reworks Kurtis Blow's 1985 song of the same title, 'Street Dreams' reshapes the Eurythmics' 'Sweet Dreams', and 'The Message' is built on Sting's 'Shape of My Heart'. 'I Gave You Power', produced by Premier, is the key to the album's conscience: the gun, passed from hand to hand, is weary of the violence it causes.","At the time many critics and fans saw it as a retreat from Illmatic's purity, and the word sell-out followed Nas for years. Reassessment has been kind: the storytelling and technique are now widely admired, and 'I Gave You Power' is counted among his best songs. The album is often cited as the pivot between hip-hop's underground nineties and its commercial peak.","Start with 'I Gave You Power' and 'The Message', then the hit singles, and notice how the rapping stays as dense as on Illmatic even when the beats turn shiny. Compare Jay-Z's Reasonable Doubt and Mobb Deep's The Infamous, released around the same time, to hear three New York answers to the same moment."],"nas-stillmatic":["The comeback album whose title promised a return to Illmatic's standard. The beats are leaner and harder than on Nas's late-nineties records, and the writing is sharp and varied. 'Ether' is a withering diss track aimed at Jay-Z; 'One Mic' swings from whispered verses to shouted climaxes; 'Rewind' tells a violent story entirely in reverse; 'Got Ur Self A...' strides over a string figure lifted from a television theme. Reflective songs about family and history balance the combat.","By 2001 Nas's reputation had slipped after two uneven albums, and Jay-Z had become the undisputed king of New York. On 'Takeover', from The Blueprint in September, Jay-Z mocked Nas's decline. Nas answered in December with Stillmatic and 'Ether', and the feud became the biggest story in hip-hop, argued over on radio and in magazines. The album went platinum and restored his standing.","'Rewind' is a real formal trick: Nas narrates events from the end back to the beginning, so causes arrive after effects, like a film running backwards, and the story only makes sense once it finishes. 'Got Ur Self A...' samples the opening theme of The Sopranos, then the most talked-about drama on television. On 'One Mic' the drums drop out and return with the intensity of Nas's voice, making the dynamics part of the argument.","Critics greeted it as a genuine return to form, and many listeners still consider 'Ether' the most devastating diss record ever made, though Jay-Z's partisans dispute the verdict. The album is usually ranked below Illmatic but above everything in between. The feud eventually ended in public reconciliation in 2005, which makes the record a time capsule of hip-hop's appetite for spectacle.","Hear 'Takeover' from Jay-Z's The Blueprint first, then 'Ether', to follow the exchange. Then play 'One Mic' loudly and track how the beat breathes with the voice. Finish with 'Rewind', paying attention to how each verse reveals what came before, and compare Illmatic to judge the comeback yourself."],"ramones-blitzkrieg-bop":["The song that opens the Ramones' first album and, for many, punk rock itself. It starts with a three-word chant shouted like a football terrace cry, then Johnny Ramone's guitar attacks with relentless downstroked chords at a speed rock had rarely tried. Joey sings a simple invitation to a gig, with kids piling in and a new noise starting. It lasts barely two minutes, has no guitar solo, and never lets the tempo drop. That is the whole point.","The Ramones came from Forest Hills, Queens, and were regulars at CBGB on the Bowery, playing sets of twenty-minute bursts. Released in February 1976 as their first single, recorded cheaply at Plaza Sound in Radio City Music Hall with producer Craig Leon, it was a deliberate return to the short, catchy rock and roll of the fifties and sixties, stripped of the long solos and grandeur of seventies stadium rock.","The chant was reportedly inspired by the Bay City Rollers' 'Saturday Night': bubblegum pop, not the blues, lay behind punk's first anthem. Tommy Ramone wrote most of the song, with Dee Dee reworking lyrics. On the original stereo mix the bass and guitar are split hard left and right, like early Beatles records, so on headphones you can hear exactly how simple, and how exact, each part is.","The single did not chart, and the album sold poorly in America, but its influence was immediate: British bands who heard the Ramones in 1976 formed or sped up accordingly. Over decades the song became inescapable in stadiums, films and advertisements, a strange afterlife for music born in a Bowery dive. It now appears on nearly every list of the greatest rock songs.","Play it loud and try to count Johnny's guitar strokes; there are no rests. Notice that the chord pattern could come from a girl-group record. Then play Chuck Berry's 'Johnny B. Goode' and the Sex Pistols' 'God Save the Queen' to hear what came before and what it sparked. The original 1976 mix is the one to seek."],"ramones-road-to-ruin":["The Ramones' fourth album keeps the buzzsaw guitar and the bubblegum melodies but widens the frame: some songs pass three minutes, there are acoustic guitars, brief guitar solos and a ballad or two. 'I Wanna Be Sedated', a jittery song about boredom and burnout on tour, became their best-known track. There is a heartfelt cover of 'Needles and Pins', the sixties hit written by Sonny Bono and Jack Nitzsche, and 'She's the One' is pure girl-group pop played at punk speed.","Drummer Tommy Ramone had grown weary of touring and stepped back to co-produce with Ed Stasium; he was replaced by Marc Bell, formerly of Richard Hell and the Voidoids, who became Marky Ramone. Released in 1978, the album was an attempt to break into American radio, which had ignored the band while punk they had inspired took over Britain. The cartoon cover is by John Holmstrom of Punk magazine.","Listen to the drumming: Marky plays with more swing and fills than Tommy's metronomic style, which subtly changes the band's feel. The guitar solos, a small heresy for a group famous for having none, are short and melodic. 'I Wanna Be Sedated' has a key change and a bright, almost sugary arrangement that disguises how bleak its subject is, a trick the Ramones used often.","The album sold modestly and failed to deliver the hit the band and label wanted, and some fans at the time heard the solos and ballads as softening. Its reputation has risen with the fame of 'I Wanna Be Sedated', and it is now seen as the bridge between their raw first records and their later pop ambitions, the moment the formula learned to stretch.","Start with 'I Wanna Be Sedated' and 'She's the One', then 'Needles and Pins' to hear how much the band loved sixties pop. Compare Rocket to Russia to notice the new drummer. Then play Buzzcocks' 'Ever Fallen in Love', from the same year, to hear British punk taking the same turn toward melody."],"ramones-end-of-the-century":["The Ramones produced by Phil Spector, and it sounds like it: the stripped-down band is wrapped in echo, keyboards, saxophone and, on a cover of the Ronettes' 'Baby, I Love You', a full string section with Joey alone at the microphone. 'Do You Remember Rock 'n' Roll Radio?' opens with a radio-dial collage and celebrates the pop of their youth; 'Rock 'n' Roll High School' is a teen-film anthem; 'Danny Says' is a tender ballad about life on the road.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1979, it was the band's most determined bid for mainstream success, pairing them with the architect of the Wall of Sound, whom Joey in particular revered. Spector was by then notorious for eccentricity, and the sessions were long and tense. The band had just appeared in Roger Corman's film Rock 'n' Roll High School. The album became their highest-charting record in America, though still no smash.","Band members later told stories of Spector making Johnny repeat a single opening chord for hours and of an intimidating display of a handgun; such accounts vary with the teller, but the friction is audible in how the guitars sit behind the production. 'Chinese Rock', written by Dee Dee with Richard Hell, deals with heroin addiction under its cheerful surface. Joey sings 'Baby, I Love You' without the rest of the band.","'Baby, I Love You' became their biggest British hit, but many punk fans considered the album a sell-out, and Johnny disliked it. Critics have been kinder since, hearing a sincere collision between two eras of New York and Los Angeles pop. It remains a test case in the debate about whether a band's sound can survive a producer this strong.","Play the Ronettes' 'Be My Baby', then 'Baby, I Love You', and listen for what Spector brings from 1963. Then 'Danny Says', the quiet heart of the record. Compare the first Ramones album for the band undressed. The Clash's London Calling, from the same winter, shows another punk band reaching for a bigger canvas on its own terms."],"talking-heads-talking-heads-77":["The band's debut is clean, nervy and oddly cheerful: bright guitars, dry drums, Tina Weymouth's springy, melodic bass, and David Byrne's yelping, anxious voice. The songs are about ordinary things observed from a strange angle: government, buildings, love, being a good citizen. 'Psycho Killer', with a verse in French and a menacing bass line, became the calling card, but 'Uh-Oh, Love Comes to Town' and 'Don't Worry About the Government' show the sunnier, stranger side.","Byrne, Weymouth and drummer Chris Frantz met at the Rhode Island School of Design, moved to New York and played CBGB alongside the Ramones, Television and Patti Smith. They were the art-school outsiders of that scene, dressed in polo shirts, playing tightly and without rock posturing. Keyboardist and guitarist Jerry Harrison, formerly of the Modern Lovers, joined before the album, which appeared in September 1977, the same year as the Sex Pistols' only album.","Byrne has said that 'Psycho Killer' began as an attempt to imagine a song Alice Cooper might sing, written by someone like Randy Newman: a character study rather than a threat. Listen to Weymouth's bass throughout; it often carries the melody while the guitars chop rhythm, a reversal that looks forward to the band's later funk. The optimism of 'Don't Worry About the Government' is so sincere it becomes unsettling.","Critics praised its intelligence and originality, though some found Byrne's persona cold or too clever. It sold modestly but established the band as the thinking wing of New York punk. In retrospect it is heard as a founding text of new wave, and its angular, danceable minimalism was followed by countless indie bands two decades later.","Start with 'Psycho Killer' and focus on the bass. Then 'Don't Worry About the Government' for the wide-eyed humour. Compare Television's Marquee Moon and Patti Smith's Horses to hear how different the CBGB bands were. Then jump to Remain in Light to hear how far this four-piece travelled in three years."],"talking-heads-more-songs-about-buildings-and-food":["The first of three albums the band made with Brian Eno, and the moment they started to groove. The rhythm section is pushed up front, tight and funky, while Eno adds subtle treatments and electronic textures. The songs still observe modern life with deadpan curiosity: 'The Big Country' looks down on America from an aeroplane, 'Found a Job' is about a couple making their own television. The hit was a slow, swampy cover of Al Green's 'Take Me to the River'.","Recorded in 1978 at Compass Point Studios in Nassau, in the Bahamas, it marked the start of Eno's partnership with Byrne, a relationship that would lead to Remain in Light and My Life in the Bush of Ghosts. Eno had just made Another Green World and Bowie's Berlin albums, and he treated the studio as a composing tool. The title mocked the idea that rock records had to be about anything grand.","The cover is a mosaic of dozens of close-up Polaroid photographs of the four members, assembled into a slightly misaligned group portrait: a visual pun on the album's pieced-together, studio-built sound. 'Take Me to the River' reaches back to Memphis soul and a preacher's baptismal imagery, and its success told the band that Black American music, not art-rock, was where their future lay.","It was well received and 'Take Me to the River' gave the band their first American top-thirty single. Critics noted Eno's influence, with some worrying he would overwhelm the group, a debate that grew louder with each collaboration. Today it is often seen as the transitional record: less startling than the debut, less radical than what followed, but the first time their funk ambitions became audible.","Play Al Green's original first, then the Talking Heads version, and listen to how they slow it and darken it. Then follow the bass and drums on 'I'm Not in Love' and 'Found a Job'. Continue to Fear of Music and Remain in Light to hear the funk take over entirely."],"talking-heads-stop-making-sense":["A concert film by Jonathan Demme, with its live album, and often called the greatest rock film ever made. It opens with David Byrne alone on a bare stage with an acoustic guitar and a tape machine playing 'Psycho Killer'. With each song another musician walks on and more of the stage is assembled, until a nine-piece funk band is in full flight for 'Burning Down the House', 'Life During Wartime' and 'Once in a Lifetime'. Byrne later appears in his enormous boxy suit.","Filmed over several nights at the Pantages Theatre in Hollywood in December 1983, on the tour for Speaking in Tongues, it captured the band at its largest, expanded with the P-Funk keyboardist Bernie Worrell, guitarist Alex Weir, percussionist Steve Scales and singers Lynn Mabry and Ednah Holt. Demme, later the director of The Silence of the Lambs, avoided audience shots and quick cutting, letting whole songs play in long, patient takes. It was among the first films with an entirely digital soundtrack.","The staging is built on theatrical ideas Byrne took from Japanese Kabuki and Noh, in which stagehands in black are meant to be invisible; here the crew rolling on platforms is part of the show. The big suit, he said, was a way of making his head look small, a comment on the body taking over from the mind. The audience is almost never seen until the very end.","It was acclaimed on release and has only grown in reputation; a 4K restoration in 2023 returned it to cinemas and found a new generation. The original soundtrack album was cut to nine songs to fit a single disc, a decision later reversed by expanded reissues. Debate now concerns which edition to hear: the full film remains the definitive experience.","Watch the film rather than just hearing the album, and follow how the stage fills song by song. Listen to Bernie Worrell's keyboards as the band grows. Compare Remain in Light for the studio version of this funk, and Fela Kuti's Zombie for the Afrobeat that inspired the interlocking parts."],"beyonce-beyonce":["A visual album: fourteen songs, each with its own video, released as a single package. The music is darker and more experimental than her earlier pop, with murky synthesizers, slowed tempos and songs that change direction mid-stream. It is frank about sex and marriage, in 'Drunk in Love' with Jay-Z and in 'Partition', while also exploring body image in 'Pretty Hurts', feminism in 'Flawless' and motherhood in 'Blue'. Producers include Timbaland, Pharrell Williams, Boots and Hit-Boy; Frank Ocean and Drake are guests.","It appeared without warning on iTunes at midnight on 13 December 2013, with no singles, no promotion and no press, and it broke the store's sales records in its first days. Beyoncé had become a mother and had taken control of her own career through her company Parkwood. The surprise release was a bet that a star could command attention directly online, and the industry took note; within two years other artists were copying the model.","'Flawless' contains a passage from a TEDx talk by the Nigerian novelist Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie, 'We Should All Be Feminists', which put a literary argument on to pop radio. 'Partition' includes a line of French dialogue lifted from the dubbed version of the Coen brothers' film The Big Lebowski. Several songs are really two songs joined at the middle, a structural habit she took further on Lemonade.","It was widely praised as her boldest and most personal work, and critics debated whether its frank sexuality and its feminist claims fitted together; she argued that they did. The release strategy has become a case study. The album is now often seen as the start of her late period, an artist treating the album as a unified work of art again.","Watch it with the videos, as intended, at least once. Then listen for the songs that split in two, 'Partition' and 'Haunted', and notice how the beat changes shape. Compare Janet Jackson's Rhythm Nation 1814, an earlier long-form film-and-album, and then continue to Lemonade."],"beyonce-cowboy-carter":["A sprawling, twenty-seven-track journey through American roots music, framed as a broadcast from an imaginary country radio station. Banjo, fiddle and acoustic guitar sit beside gospel choirs, trap drums, rock and soul. 'Texas Hold 'Em' is a hoedown for the line-dance floor; '16 Carriages' is a reflective ballad about work and sacrifice; 'Jolene' is Dolly Parton's song rewritten so the singer is confident rather than pleading. Dolly Parton and Willie Nelson appear as radio hosts.","Released on 29 March 2024 as the second act of a trilogy begun with Renaissance, the album grew in part from Beyoncé's 2016 appearance at the Country Music Association Awards, where some viewers objected to her presence. Its argument is historical: country music was built partly by Black musicians whose role was later erased. Linda Martell, the first Black woman to play the Grand Ole Opry as a solo artist, speaks on the record, and a generation of young Black country singers join her.","On 'Daughter' Beyoncé sings a verse of 'Caro mio ben', the eighteenth-century Italian aria taught to almost every classical voice student, in a full operatic tone: country, classical and her own church training in one song. 'Ya Ya' works in the Beach Boys' 'Good Vibrations' and Nancy Sinatra's 'These Boots Are Made for Walkin''. 'Blackbiird' is the Beatles' civil-rights song sung with four Black women country artists.","'Texas Hold 'Em' made her the first Black woman to top Billboard's country songs chart, and the album won the Grammys for Album of the Year and Best Country Album. Country radio's reluctance to play it became part of the story. Critics praised its ambition and historical argument; others found it too long and more Americana than country. The debate about who owns the genre continues.","Start with 'Texas Hold 'Em' and 'Jolene', then Dolly Parton's original 'Jolene' for comparison. Hear 'Daughter' for the aria, then 'Blackbiird'. Listen through in order to follow the radio-station frame. For background, play Rosetta Tharpe and Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger to hear the roots she is claiming."],"metallica-kill-em-all":["Ten songs played at a speed and with a precision that heavy metal had not heard before. James Hetfield's rhythm guitar is palm-muted into a clipped, machine-like chug; Lars Ulrich's drums push the tempo past anything Black Sabbath or Judas Priest attempted; Kirk Hammett's solos arrive in squealing bursts. The record runs from the opening \"Hit the Lights\" to the anthem \"Seek & Destroy\" and the closing \"Metal Militia\", with Cliff Burton's distorted bass solo, \"(Anesthesia) Pulling Teeth\", as a strange centrepiece.","Metallica formed in Los Angeles in 1981, moved to the San Francisco Bay Area, and drove across the country in 1983 to record for Jon Zazula's tiny New Jersey label Megaforce. Weeks before the sessions in Rochester, New York, they fired guitarist Dave Mustaine and brought in Hammett from the band Exodus. The band's heroes were the New Wave of British Heavy Metal and Motörhead, bands then barely known in America, and the label reportedly refused the band's cruder original title.","Mustaine was gone but his music stayed: he shares writing credit on four songs, and \"The Four Horsemen\" is a reworking of his earlier song \"The Mechanix\", which he later recorded with Megadeth in its original form, so two bands released rival versions. Listen too for how Burton plays his bass through a wah pedal and distortion in \"Pulling Teeth\", treating it like a lead guitar, an unheard-of move on a metal record in 1983.","Reviewers in the mainstream press ignored it; the fanzines and tape-trading underground that had spread the band's demos made it a word-of-mouth success. It is now seen as the founding document of American thrash, alongside early records by Slayer, Megadeth and Anthrax. Critics tend to rank it below the next three albums for its thin production and simple songwriting, but many fans hold that its raw energy was never recaptured.","Start with \"Whiplash\", the purest statement of the new speed, then \"Seek & Destroy\" for the mid-tempo riff that crowds still shout along to. Put \"Motorbreath\" beside Motörhead's \"Ace of Spades\" to hear where the attack came from. Then listen to \"Pulling Teeth\" as a portrait of Burton, whose musical curiosity would shape the far more ambitious records that followed."],"metallica-ride-the-lightning":["Eight songs that widen thrash into a set of moods. It opens with a gentle acoustic guitar figure that is smashed by the fastest song the band had written, \"Fight Fire with Fire\". The title track sings from inside the electric chair; \"For Whom the Bell Tolls\" begins with a tolling bell and a bass line that sounds like a guitar; \"Fade to Black\" is a bleak ballad building to a long solo; \"Creeping Death\" retells the plagues of Egypt; and \"The Call of Ktulu\" is a nine-minute instrumental.","Recorded in early 1984 at Sweet Silence Studios in Copenhagen with engineer Flemming Rasmussen, far from home and on a tight budget, the album was first released on Megaforce and soon picked up by the major label Elektra, which signed the band on its strength. Metallica were barely twenty and touring small clubs. Their subjects widened to books and films: Hemingway's novel on the Spanish Civil War, the Book of Exodus, capital punishment and H. P. Lovecraft's horror stories.","The sophistication came largely from bassist Cliff Burton, who had studied music theory and loved Bach; the harmonised guitar lines and the chorale-like chord changes in \"Fade to Black\" and \"The Call of Ktulu\" carry his fingerprints. The opening of \"For Whom the Bell Tolls\" is not a guitar at all but Burton's bass through distortion and a wah pedal. Two songs still carry writing credits for the departed Dave Mustaine.","\"Fade to Black\" caused a small scandal: some fans called a thrash ballad a betrayal, and some adults read the song's despair as dangerous, though many listeners have since described it as a comfort in dark times. Over the years the record has come to be seen as the moment thrash grew a mind, the bridge between the raw debut and Master of Puppets, and many musicians name it as their favourite Metallica album.","Play the first ninety seconds for the whole argument: acoustic calm, then the storm. Follow \"Fade to Black\" from its quiet guitar picking to the soaring coda to hear the band's slow-to-loud template, which returns on every later album. Save \"The Call of Ktulu\" for last and listen for the way it builds by adding layers, almost like a classical variation set."],"metallica-one":["A seven-and-a-half-minute song that moves from a hushed, clean-toned guitar ballad to one of the most violent passages in metal. It opens with the sound of battlefield explosions and helicopters, then a soft picked guitar and a quiet vocal from a soldier who cannot move or speak. Each chorus grows heavier, and in the last third the band switches to a pounding rhythm on the bass drums that imitates machine-gun fire, under a long tapping solo from Kirk Hammett.","The song closes the first side of ...And Justice for All, Metallica's first album with bassist Jason Newsted after Cliff Burton's death in a tour-bus crash in 1986. Its subject comes from Dalton Trumbo's anti-war novel Johnny Got His Gun, about a First World War soldier left without limbs, sight or hearing. The band had long refused to make music videos; for this song they made their first, a black-and-white film intercut with scenes from the 1971 film version of the novel.","The song follows a plan the band had used before, on \"Fade to Black\" and \"Welcome Home (Sanitarium)\": quiet verses, heavier choruses, then a final sprint. Here the change of pace tells the story, as the soldier's inner voice shifts from confusion to despair. To use the film clips, the band reportedly bought the rights to the 1971 film outright. The famously dry, bass-light sound of the whole album is audible here too.","The video put Metallica on MTV for the first time without their changing the music, and in 1990 the song won the first Grammy given for Best Metal Performance, the year after the band had lost a hard-rock award to Jethro Tull in a notorious upset. It remains a centrepiece of their concerts, though some early fans see it as the start of the drift towards the mainstream that the next album completed.","Listen for the exact moment the machine-gun rhythm arrives and notice how the quiet opening makes it land harder. Then watch the video, where the film clips carry the story the lyrics tell. To hear the same arc in other hands, try Britten's War Requiem, a very different work that also sets a soldier's first-person voice against the machinery of war."],"run-dmc-king-of-rock":["The second Run-D.M.C. album makes the case its title claims: that rap belongs on the same stage as rock. The title track pairs the group's shouted, overlapping rhymes with a loud distorted guitar riff and bare drum-machine beats. Elsewhere the record ranges widely: \"Roots, Rap, Reggae\" brings in the Jamaican deejay Yellowman, \"Jam-Master Jammin'\" shows off Jam Master Jay's scratching, and \"You Talk Too Much\" is a dry, funny put-down. The sound is spare and hard, with almost nothing but beat, voice and scratch.","Joseph Simmons, Darryl McDaniels and Jason Mizell came from Hollis, Queens, and were produced by Larry Smith and Run's brother Russell Simmons for the independent label Profile. In 1985 rap was still widely called a passing fad and rock radio and MTV rarely played Black artists. The group's black leather, hats and unlaced sneakers were a deliberate break from the costumes of earlier rap acts, presenting street style as the new stage wear.","In the title-track video the trio break into a mock rock-and-roll museum and are told by a guard that rap does not belong there, a joke on the institutions that shut them out. The guitar sound, following their earlier \"Rock Box\", is played by session guitarist Eddie Martinez rather than sampled. A young LL Cool J is credited as a co-writer on \"Can You Rock It Like This\".","It was the first of their albums to draw serious rock-press attention and sold well, but it has been overshadowed by the debut, which invented their sound, and by Raising Hell, which took it to the mass audience. Critics now treat it as the essential middle step: the record where rap's claim on rock was stated outright, a year before the \"Walk This Way\" collaboration with Aerosmith made it undeniable.","Start with the title track and notice how little is in it: drum machine, a guitar, two voices finishing each other's lines. Then play \"Roots, Rap, Reggae\" for the link to Jamaican sound-system culture that hip-hop grew from. Follow it with Beastie Boys' Licensed to Ill, made by the same circle a year later, to hear the idea taken further."],"a-tribe-called-quest-can-i-kick-it":["A relaxed, playful single built on one of the most recognisable bass lines in pop, looped from Lou Reed's \"Walk on the Wild Side\". Over it Q-Tip and Phife Dawg trade easy, conversational verses, and the chorus is a call-and-response between the group and a crowd of friends, like a game in a schoolyard. The drums are loose and light, and the mood is friendly rather than confrontational: a group introducing itself by inviting the listener in.","A Tribe Called Quest came from St. Albans, Queens, and belonged to the Native Tongues collective with De La Soul and the Jungle Brothers, whose bright, Afrocentric, jazz-loving style offered an alternative to the hard-edged rap of the time. The song appeared on their debut, People's Instinctive Travels and the Paths of Rhythm, released by Jive in 1990, when sampling was still mostly unregulated and labels were just starting to demand payment for borrowed recordings.","Beneath the Lou Reed loop sit fragments from other records, including the organist Lonnie Smith's version of \"Spinning Wheel\". Clearing the Reed sample reportedly cost the group most of the song's earnings, which Phife later spoke about ruefully. Much of its charm comes from the group's friends crowding around the microphone.","It was a modest hit on release and grew in stature as the group's later albums were canonised. Today it is among the most licensed and quoted rap songs, used in films and adverts, and its sample-clearance story is often told as a case study in how the new copyright rules of the early 1990s changed hip-hop production.","Listen first to how the bass loop never changes, and how the voices and small drum shifts keep it from feeling repetitive. Then play Lou Reed's original to hear how a tossed-off bass part became a new song's backbone. Follow with De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising for the same Native Tongues humour."],"a-tribe-called-quest-we-got-it-from-here":["The group's final album, and their first in eighteen years: sixteen tracks that blend the warm jazz samples of their classic records with live guitars, keyboards and a denser, more restless production. Q-Tip, Phife Dawg and a returning Jarobi White pass verses back and forth as they did in the early 1990s, joined by Busta Rhymes, André 3000, Kendrick Lamar, Anderson .Paak, Jack White and Elton John. Songs range from the anti-racist chant of \"We the People....\" to the tender \"Lost Somebody\".","The members reunited after an appearance on television in late 2015 and recorded largely in Q-Tip's home studio in New Jersey. Phife Dawg died in March 2016 from complications of diabetes while the album was unfinished, and the others completed it around his recorded verses. It was released by Epic on 11 November 2016, three days after Donald Trump's election, and its songs about exclusion, policing and immigration were heard at once as a response.","The track \"The Donald\" is not about the new president: the title is one of Phife's own nicknames, and the song closes the album as his memorial. \"Solid Wall of Sound\" weaves Elton John's \"Bennie and the Jets\" into the mix, with Elton himself singing. Listen for how often voices overlap and finish each other's lines, recreating the group conversation of their early records.","Reviewers greeted it with near-unanimous praise, calling it one of the rare reunion albums that adds to a group's legacy rather than trading on it, and it went to number one in the United States. Their performance on Saturday Night Live days after release became a widely shared moment of protest. Debate centres only on whether it belongs beside The Low End Theory or just below it.","Begin with \"The Space Program\" for the mood of the whole album, then \"We the People....\" and \"Dis Generation\", where four voices pass the microphone in quick succession. End with \"Lost Somebody\" and \"The Donald\" as a pair of farewells. Compare it with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, which drew on the same jazz roots a year earlier."],"public-enemy-rebel-without-a-pause":["A track built around a sound like a boiling kettle: a single high, shrieking saxophone squeal, looped until it becomes a siren. Beneath it runs a hard, clipped drum break and Terminator X's scratching, which cuts in like a second voice. Chuck D raps with a booming, preacher's authority about his own power as a voice for Black America, while Flavor Flav punctuates his lines with shouts. Nothing resolves; the song simply keeps up its pressure for five minutes.","Public Enemy's first album had made little impact, and Chuck D later said this song was his answer to Eric B. & Rakim's \"I Know You Got Soul\", which he felt had raised the bar for everyone. Produced by the Bomb Squad team of Hank Shocklee, Eric Sadler and Chuck D, it came out on Def Jam in 1987 and was then placed on It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back the following year. The title plays on the 1955 James Dean film Rebel Without a Cause.","The kettle shriek comes from \"The Grunt\" by the J.B.'s, James Brown's backing band, and the drums come from Brown's \"Funky Drummer\", so the whole track is built out of the Brown workshop. Terminator X's scratch solo is played with a turntable on a record he treats like an instrument. The tempo is also said to have been pushed faster than the rap norm of the day.","It was the record that turned Public Enemy from a promising act into a movement, and critics at the time heard in it something new: rap as organised noise, closer to free jazz or industrial music than to pop. It is now routinely listed among the most important hip-hop singles, and producers still study the Bomb Squad's layering here as a template for how sampling could be aggressive rather than decorative.","Listen first only to the shriek and notice how quickly it stops feeling like a sample and becomes the song's weather. Then focus on Terminator X's scratch break in the middle. Play Eric B. & Rakim's \"I Know You Got Soul\" next to hear what Chuck D was answering, then James Brown's \"Funky Drummer\" for the source."],"eric-b-and-rakim-i-know-you-got-soul":["A tough, lean single built on a looped funk groove and scratched vocal fragments, over which Rakim delivers verses of calm, rolling intricacy. Where earlier rappers shouted, he speaks almost quietly, placing rhymes inside lines rather than only at their ends and letting sentences run across the beat. The song's subject is writing and performing itself: the rapper as a craftsman who takes his art seriously. Eric B.'s turntable cuts break the verses like punctuation.","Eric Barrier and William Griffin Jr., from Long Island and Queens, released the song as a single on 4th & B'way in 1987 and placed it on their debut album Paid in Full, a duo whose first records grew out of producer Marley Marl's Queensbridge circle. It borrows its title and groove from a 1971 song by Bobby Byrd, the singer and organiser who had discovered James Brown and worked beside him on \"Sex Machine\". Funk records from Brown's circle were becoming hip-hop's core material.","One spoken phrase from this record, lifted by the British group M|A|R|R|S, became the hook of their 1987 dance hit \"Pump Up the Volume\", so Rakim's voice crossed into house music almost at once. The sampling of Byrd's record without permission was one of several at the time that pushed labels toward the licensing system of the 1990s. Chuck D said the song pushed him to write Public Enemy's \"Rebel Without a Pause\".","Fellow rappers heard it at once as a leap in technique, and it is still cited as a turning point in the history of rhyme: the moment rapping became as much about internal patterning and composure as about volume. Critics tend to rank it alongside \"Paid in Full\" and \"Eric B. Is President\" as the group's defining singles, and it is regularly named on lists of the greatest hip-hop records.","Listen to Rakim's voice rather than the words: notice how he rarely raises it, and how rhymes fall in unexpected places mid-line. Then put on Run-D.M.C.'s \"Sucker M.C.'s\" from three years earlier to hear how different rap sounded before him. Follow with Bobby Byrd's original to hear the groove in its first life."],"eric-b-and-rakim-let-the-rhythm-hit-em":["The duo's third album is slower, darker and more serious than the two before it. The beats are heavy and brooding, built from dense funk and soul samples, and Rakim's voice has dropped lower and grown more deliberate. The title track opens with a pounding, almost martial rhythm; \"In the Ghetto\" is a meditation on where he came from that reaches toward the cosmic; \"Mahogany\" is a smooth, slow love song. The whole record moves at a measured, patient pace.","Released by MCA in 1990, it arrived after the rapid rise of Public Enemy, Boogie Down Productions and the Native Tongues, when the duo that had defined rap's craft three years earlier had to show they still led. Rakim, a member of the Five Percent Nation, was increasingly weaving its teachings on knowledge and self-mastery into his verses. Behind the scenes the partnership was fraying, and the duo would make only one more album.","The young producer Large Professor has said he helped put together several of the beats, though he was not credited, which makes the album an early link to the Queensbridge sound he later brought to Nas's Illmatic. Listen for how often Rakim now stretches a thought over many bars, and for the lower register of his voice compared with Paid in Full.","It sold less than its predecessors and was sometimes described at the time as heavy going. The Source magazine gave it its top rating, and critics and rappers have since rated it among the group's best work, a template for the sombre, introspective East Coast albums of the mid-1990s. The debate is whether its slowness is a strength or the sign of a partnership running out of energy.","Start with the title track at high volume, then \"In the Ghetto\", where Rakim's writing is at its most ambitious. Play it after Paid in Full to hear the change in tempo and mood. Then go on to Nas's Illmatic and hear how much of its unhurried, reflective storytelling is already here."],"kendrick-lamar-not-like-us":["A buoyant, bouncing West Coast party record that is also a ferocious attack. Mustard's beat is spare: a plucked, repeating synth riff, handclaps and a bass that invites dancing. Over it Kendrick Lamar raps in a sharp, mocking tone at his rival Drake, and the song ends in a chant that turns the attack into a celebration of Los Angeles and of Black communities who, he argues, own the culture Drake borrows. It is a diss track made to be played at block parties.","It was released on 4 May 2024 as the climax of a weeks-long public feud between the two most commercially powerful rappers of their generation, fought in a rapid volley of songs. Lamar had released \"Meet the Grahams\" just the day before. The feud played out in real time across streaming and social media, and \"Not Like Us\" became the song that, for most listeners, ended it.","Among its charges the song makes grave personal allegations against Drake, which he strongly denied. What most listeners miss is how deliberately the music is chosen: Mustard's bouncing Los Angeles sound, and shout-outs to Compton and its history, frame the song as a West Coast community rallying round one of its own. The accusation of cultural borrowing reaches back to arguments about Atlanta and New Orleans rap.","It went to number one in the United States, dominated the summer of 2024, and at the 2025 Grammys won five awards including Record and Song of the Year; Lamar performed it at that February's Super Bowl halftime show. Drake later brought a lawsuit against the record label, arguing the song defamed him, which opened a wider debate about how far a diss record should be read as a statement of fact.","Listen once just to the beat and notice how little is in it and how much it makes you move. Then listen for the switch in the final minute, when the song turns into a crowd chant. Compare it with Dr. Dre's \"Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang\" and 2Pac's \"California Love\" to hear the West Coast lineage it claims."],"nirvana-bleach":["Nirvana's first album is slow, heavy and murky: sludgy guitar riffs tuned low, pounding drums and Kurt Cobain's voice switching from a mutter to a raw scream. Songs like \"Blew\", \"Negative Creep\" and \"School\" are built on grinding riffs and a single repeated shout. Then comes \"About a Girl\", a bright, jangly pop song that sounds like it wandered in from another band, and the Shocking Blue cover \"Love Buzz\", with its circling bass line.","Recorded mostly in late 1988 at Reciprocal Recording in Seattle with engineer Jack Endino, on a budget of around six hundred dollars, and released in June 1989 by Sub Pop, the independent label that was marketing a raw Seattle sound. Cobain and bassist Krist Novoselic came from the logging town of Aberdeen, Washington; the drummer was Chad Channing, with some earlier tracks drummed by Dale Crover of the Melvins, the band's local heroes.","The album credits a second guitarist, Jason Everman, who paid the recording costs but did not play on it. Cobain later said he deliberately wrote heavy, simple songs to fit the label's grunge image, hiding his pop instincts; \"About a Girl\" is the giveaway, reportedly written after listening to a Beatles record for hours. Listen for Cobain's melodies buried under the distortion even in the heaviest tracks.","It sold modestly at first and was treated as one Sub Pop release among many. After Nevermind it was reissued and sold in huge numbers, and critics reassessed it as the missing link between the Melvins' sludge and the polished songcraft to come. Many still prefer its rawness; others hear a band not yet sure of its voice. The 2009 remaster added a live set that made the case for its power on stage.","Play \"Blew\" and \"About a Girl\" back to back: the two halves of Cobain in eight minutes. Then listen to \"Negative Creep\" for the heaviest, most unhinged side of the band. Follow the whole album with Nevermind and notice how the same songwriting instinct is simply given more room and a cleaner sound."],"outkast-atliens":["OutKast's second album is spacious, slow and otherworldly. The Southern funk of their debut is replaced by echoing drums, deep bass that throbs like a heartbeat and reverb borrowed from Jamaican dub, with space-age keyboards drifting over the top. André 3000 and Big Boi rap about being outsiders, alien to both the mainstream and their own scene. \"Elevators (Me & You)\" moves with a slow, lurching stride; \"ATLiens\" and \"Jazzy Belle\" shift between warmth and cool detachment.","Released by LaFace in August 1996, a year after André had been booed at an awards show in New York for daring to say the South had something to say. OutKast produced much of the record themselves for the first time, with help from their Atlanta mentors Organized Noize. André had reportedly given up drinking, smoking and meat and started dressing unusually, and the album's alien theme follows his sense of distance from rap's conventions and coastal rivalries.","The echo and space come from dub reggae: listen for drums that drop out and bounce back through delay, as on a Jamaican mixing desk. The CD booklet was a comic book casting the pair as superheroes. The album's cosmic imagery connects them to Parliament and Sun Ra, the Afrofuturist tradition of Black artists imagining themselves in outer space.","It sold strongly but reviews were more mixed than for the debut; some found it too slow and strange. It has since been reassessed as one of the great second albums in hip-hop and the moment Southern rap declared independence from the coasts. The debate is mostly about whether it or Aquemini is the pair's peak.","Start with \"Elevators (Me & You)\" on good headphones and listen for the echo on the drums and the low bass underneath. Then put on Parliament's Mothership Connection to hear the older cosmic funk it descends from. Follow with Aquemini, where the same ideas are opened up with live instruments and even more ambition."],"outkast-speakerboxxx-the-love-below":["A double album that is really two solo records under one name. Big Boi's Speakerboxxx is dense, funky Southern rap: brass, pounding drums and party energy, with \"The Way You Move\" and \"GhettoMusick\" as its peaks. André 3000's The Love Below barely raps at all; it is a sprawling pop, funk and jazz record about love, sung in falsetto, with live guitars and a drum-and-bass reading of \"My Favorite Things\". Its best-known track is \"Hey Ya!\", a frantic acoustic-guitar song in an odd, lopsided metre.","Released by Arista in September 2003, it arrived as the two partners' interests were drifting apart: Big Boi still rooted in Atlanta rap, André listening to jazz and rock and learning guitar. Rather than compromise, they split the album. It came out during the peak of the CD era and just before streaming, when a two-hour double album was a commercial gamble. It became one of the best-selling rap albums ever.","\"Hey Ya!\" is harder to dance to than it sounds: its phrases run in groups of bars that do not add up evenly, so the song keeps tripping forward. Behind its party mood, it describes a relationship falling apart. A passing line about shaking a Polaroid photograph prompted the company to issue a statement warning people not to shake their prints.","It won the Grammy for Album of the Year, only the second rap album to do so after Lauryn Hill's, and \"Hey Ya!\" was everywhere. Critics nonetheless debate whether it is a great OutKast album or two uneven solo records, and many argue it is too long. The Love Below is often credited with widening what a rap artist could be, from Kanye West to Tyler, the Creator.","Treat it as two albums. Start with Big Boi's \"The Way You Move\" and \"GhettoMusick\", then André's \"Hey Ya!\", \"Roses\" and \"Prototype\". Notice how the partners appear on each other's discs as guests. Compare The Love Below with Prince's Sign o' the Times, the double album it most resembles in ambition and variety."],"wu-tang-clan-protect-ya-neck":["The first Wu-Tang Clan record: nearly five minutes, no chorus, eight rappers taking one verse each over a grimy, lo-fi beat. It opens with dialogue from a kung-fu film and a group introduction, then Inspectah Deck, Raekwon, Method Man, U-God, Ol' Dirty Bastard, Ghostface Killah, RZA and GZA follow in turn, each with a different voice: gruff, sly, slurred, precise. The drums are dusty and slightly off-kilter, and the whole thing sounds like a basement tape made by a gang.","The group formed in Staten Island around RZA, his cousins GZA and Ol' Dirty Bastard, and friends, united by kung-fu films and the teachings of the Five Percent Nation. They pressed the single independently in 1992 and sold it themselves, reportedly out of car trunks, which led to a deal with the new label Loud. RZA's plan was unusual: a group deal that let each member sign solo contracts with other labels.","The final verse, from GZA, is an attack on the music industry and on rappers who sign bad contracts, which makes the song a statement of the group's business plan as well as a showcase. The kung-fu dialogue at the start comes from the 1983 film Shaolin and Wu Tang, which gave the group its name. Notice how RZA's beat is deliberately raw and unpolished.","It became an underground sensation in New York, and critics now see it as a hinge in hip-hop history: the end of the polished late-1980s sound and the start of a grittier, more collective era. The group's model of solo deals, seeded here, became as influential as the music. Its placement as the lead single of Enter the Wu-Tang the following year sealed its reputation.","Listen for the changes between rappers: try to name each voice as it arrives, since this is the group's calling card. Then compare the raw sound with Dr. Dre's polished The Chronic, released the same year. Finally play the whole of Enter the Wu-Tang to hear this blueprint expanded into an album."],"wu-tang-clan-wu-tang-forever":["A sprawling double album, nearly two hours long, that shows the whole Wu-Tang Clan at the height of its fame. RZA's production is lusher and more cinematic than on the group's debut, with string arrangements, soul samples and mournful piano loops. Tracks rotate through all nine members and allies such as Cappadonna, alternating posse cuts like \"Triumph\" with sombre pieces such as \"A Better Tomorrow\" and \"Impossible\", and the violin-driven \"Reunited\" opens the first disc after a short introduction.","It came four years after Enter the Wu-Tang, during which the members had released a run of acclaimed solo albums, including GZA's Liquid Swords and Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx. Released by Loud in June 1997, just after the killings of 2Pac and the Notorious B.I.G., it arrived when hip-hop's biggest acts were shifting to double albums and grand statements. RZA shared production with a small group of in-house producers.","RZA was increasingly thinking like a film composer, and the string parts and ominous piano loops show it; a few years later he began scoring films. \"Impossible\", a funeral-like song about losing a friend to violence, is one of the group's most unsparing pieces. The album's teachings on self-knowledge and the Five Percent Nation run through many verses.","It went straight to number one and was nominated for a Grammy, but critics then and now call it too long, with its best tracks surrounded by filler. The general view is that the group's solo records of 1994 to 1996 were stronger. Yet \"Triumph\" and \"Reunited\" are regularly ranked among their finest, and the album marks the peak of their commercial reach.","Do not start at the beginning of disc one: try \"Triumph\", \"Reunited\", \"Impossible\" and \"A Better Tomorrow\" first. Listen for the strings and how they give RZA's beats the grandeur of a film score. Then compare with Notorious B.I.G.'s Life After Death, another sprawling 1997 double album, to hear two different ideas of rap at scale."],"wu-tang-clan-triumph":["A nearly six-minute track with no chorus, in which every member of Wu-Tang Clan and their ally Cappadonna take a verse in turn. After a manic spoken introduction from Ol' Dirty Bastard, Inspectah Deck opens with a verse full of cinematic images of destruction and wit. The beat is dark and grinding, with heavy drums and a menacing loop, and the song feels like a relay race in which each runner tries to outdo the one before.","It was the lead single from Wu-Tang Forever, released by Loud in 1997, the group's first collective record after four years of solo projects. With so many established stars in one room, the song works as a roll call and a competition. Its video, directed by Brett Ratner, showed the members as giants and a swarm of killer bees over a city, reportedly at great expense.","Inspectah Deck's opening verse is so admired that it is often called one of the best in hip-hop; he was the least famous member, and the song made his name. The group's emblem, the swarm of bees, appears in the video and is part of their shared mythology of a hive of individuals working together. Listen to how each voice makes its entrance distinct from the last.","It became one of the group's biggest hits and is regularly listed among the finest posse cuts ever made. Critics praise it as a model of collective rap without a hook, where interest comes entirely from the changing voices. Some think it sums up the Wu-Tang ethos better than the double album around it.","Listen once only to Inspectah Deck's verse and notice how quickly it sets the tone. Then play the whole song and try to follow the order of the rappers. Compare with the group's first single, \"Protect Ya Neck\", made five years earlier, to hear how the same format grew richer and heavier."],"ewan-maccoll-singing-the-fishing":["An hour of radio built from two kinds of material cut together on tape: the recorded voices of East Anglian and Scottish herring fishermen, talking about nets, weather, hunger and the sea, and new songs written by Ewan MacColl in the manner of traditional ballads, sung by MacColl and others over fiddle, concertina, guitar and banjo arrangements by Peggy Seeger. The best known of those songs, The Shoals of Herring, has since passed into the folk repertoire as if it were centuries old.","This was the third of the Radio Ballads MacColl made for the BBC Home Service with the producer Charles Parker and Peggy Seeger between 1958 and 1964. Parker carried a portable tape recorder to the ports of Winterton, Great Yarmouth and the Moray Firth, collecting hours of conversation with men whose industry was already in decline. The central voice is Sam Larner, a Norfolk fisherman in his eighties who was also a fine traditional singer, and much of the script follows the arc of his working life.","Listen to how closely the new songs are built from the old men's own words. MacColl wrote The Shoals of Herring almost as a paraphrase of Sam Larner's account of going to sea as a boy, so that when the song begins a moment after Larner has spoken, the line between documentary and composition vanishes. The instrumental links often borrow the rhythm of the recorded speech just heard.","The programme was widely praised on broadcast and won the Prix Italia, confirming that the form invented with The Ballad of John Axon was more than a one-off. Later critics have been more divided about MacColl's authority as a songwriter imitating tradition, and some folk purists disliked newly made songs being taken for old ones. Oral historians, by contrast, now treat the Radio Ballads as pioneering fieldwork that let working people speak in their own accents on national radio.","Hear it whole, ideally in the Topic CD reissue, rather than picking out the songs, because the point is the collision of talk and music. Start by noticing Sam Larner's voice and then listen for the moment a sung melody takes over his story. Afterwards, hear MacColl's own studio recording of The Shoals of Herring and compare it with the version on the broadcast."],"ewan-maccoll-the-manchester-rambler":["A jaunty strophic song with a marching chorus, sung in the voice of a young worker from Manchester who spends his week in the mill and his Sunday walking the high moors of the Peak District. The verses tell of rain and bog, a run-in with a landowner's gamekeeper who orders him off the hill, and a failed romance, and the chorus insists on the rambler's freedom to be out on the tops. It is short, singable, and meant for a crowd.","MacColl, then a twenty-year-old unemployed Salford lad still called Jimmie Miller, wrote it in 1932 in the wake of the Mass Trespass on Kinder Scout, when several hundred walkers deliberately crossed grouse moors closed to the public. He had helped publicise the event through the Communist-led British Workers' Sports Federation. Five trespassers were jailed, and the case became a rallying point for the access campaign that led eventually to the creation of Britain's national parks after the Second World War.","Notice how little of the song is angry. The gamekeeper scene is played for comedy, and most of the verses are about weather, landscape and the pleasure of walking; the politics sit in the chorus's simple claim to the hills rather than in any slogan. That mixture of good humour and conviction is the template MacColl returned to in later industrial songs such as Dirty Old Town.","For decades it lived mainly among ramblers, youth hostellers and left-wing clubs rather than in the concert folk scene, and MacColl himself later described it as a youthful piece. Its standing rose with the Kinder Scout anniversaries of 1982 and 2007, when it was sung on the moor itself. Historians of the access movement now quote it as a primary document, and it is often cited as the earliest song in MacColl's long output.","Find MacColl's own later recording, where his rough, clear voice carries the chorus alone, and then look for a group performance by walkers or a folk club, because it only really comes alive when others join in. Listen for the change of mood in the gamekeeper verse. Pair it with Woody Guthrie's This Land Is Your Land, which makes a related argument across the Atlantic."],"david-bowie-blackstar":["Seven tracks in forty-one minutes, played by Bowie with a New York jazz quartet: Donny McCaslin on saxophones and flute, Jason Lindner on keyboards, Tim Lefebvre on bass and Mark Guiliana on drums, with Ben Monder on guitar. The ten-minute title track opens with skittering drum-and-bass patterns under a chant-like vocal, breaks into a warm, almost hymn-like middle section, then returns. Saxophone lines wander freely around the songs, and the whole record sounds dark, restless and unexpectedly spacious.","Bowie made it in secret at the Magic Shop studio in New York in 2015 with his long-time producer Tony Visconti, while being treated for liver cancer, a fact known to almost no one. He had heard McCaslin's band in a Manhattan club, and Visconti has said they were listening closely to Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly during the sessions. The album was released on 8 January 2016, Bowie's sixty-ninth birthday. He died two days later, and the record was instantly heard as a farewell.","On the closing track, I Can't Give Everything Away, a harmonica phrase enters that recalls A New Career in a New Town from Low, a quiet backward glance to his Berlin years. The vinyl sleeve, designed by Jonathan Barnbrook, has a star cut out of it and printing that only appears when the record is left in sunlight. Girl Loves Me mixes Nadsat, the invented slang of A Clockwork Orange, with the old gay London argot Polari.","Early reviews, written before his death, already called it his boldest record in decades; after 10 January every line was reread as a message about mortality, and the song Lazarus in particular was heard as a self-written epitaph. It won five Grammy Awards in 2017. Some critics have since argued that the grief reading narrows a record that is also playful and musically adventurous, and that McCaslin's band deserves as much credit as the circumstances.","Play the title track first and follow the drums, which never settle into a rock beat, then wait for the gentle middle section. Next hear Lazarus with its slow saxophone and heavy bass. Compare with Low to hear how far Bowie still roamed, and with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly for the jazz-meets-pop model. Headphones reveal the layered saxophones and flute."],"sandy-denny-fotheringay":["The only album released in its lifetime by Fotheringay, the band Sandy Denny formed after leaving Fairport Convention: Denny on vocals, piano and guitar with Trevor Lucas, Jerry Donahue, Pat Donaldson and Gerry Conway. It mixes Denny originals such as Nothing More, The Sea and Winter Winds with traditional ballads and songs by Bob Dylan and Gordon Lightfoot. The sound is warmer and more relaxed than Fairport's, built around Denny's piano and Donahue's lyrical, country-tinged electric guitar.","Denny left Fairport in late 1969, reluctant to commit to a band that toured relentlessly and wanted to focus on traditional material. With her partner, the Australian singer Trevor Lucas, she assembled a group that took its name from her song about Mary, Queen of Scots, recorded on Fairport's What We Did on Our Holidays. Joe Boyd produced the album for Island, released in 1970. The band broke up within a year, part way through a second album, as Boyd and others pushed Denny towards a solo career.","Listen to the traditional Banks of the Nile, a soldier's farewell sung by Denny over an eight-minute arrangement that slowly builds; it is one of her finest recorded performances and a model of how to pace a long ballad. The sessions for the unfinished second album surfaced in 2008 as Fotheringay 2, completed by Jerry Donahue from the surviving tapes.","Reviews were good but sales modest, and for years Fotheringay was treated as a footnote between Fairport and Denny's solo records. Many listeners now rank it among her most satisfying albums, precisely because the band gave her room without overwhelming her. The debate over whether breaking up the group was a mistake, pushed by commercial ambitions for Denny as a solo star, runs through every biography of her.","Start with Nothing More, which shows off Denny's piano and the band's easy swing, then go to Banks of the Nile and let it take its time. Notice how Donahue's guitar answers her voice rather than competing with it. Afterwards hear Liege & Lief to compare the more driven Fairport approach to the same kind of material."],"sandy-denny-sandy":["Denny's second solo album and her most polished: ten songs, mostly her own, arranged for a studio band with guest colour from pedal steel guitar, Dave Swarbrick's violin and a brass section. It moves between rolling mid-tempo songs such as It'll Take a Long Time and Listen, Listen, the quiet piano ballad The Lady, a Dylan cover, and a largely unaccompanied, multi-voiced setting of Richard Fariña's The Quiet Joys of Brotherhood to an Irish air.","By 1972 Denny had been voted Britain's top female singer by Melody Maker readers two years running and Island hoped for a commercial breakthrough. Trevor Lucas produced the album in London, bringing in players from Fairport and Fotheringay alongside Sneaky Pete Kleinow of the Flying Burrito Brothers on pedal steel. The cover portrait was taken by David Bailey. American country-rock and the singer-songwriter boom of Joni Mitchell and Carole King shaped the label's expectations.","Listen to The Quiet Joys of Brotherhood with care: Denny overdubbed her own voice several times so that she sings in a small choir of herself, with Swarbrick's violin weaving between the lines, a technique borrowed from traditional harmony singing but achieved entirely by tape. The brass on For Nobody to Hear was arranged by the New Orleans producer Allen Toussaint, an unexpected link between English folk rock and Southern soul.","Critics were warm and the album is often called her best solo record, but it sold poorly and did nothing to make her the star her label intended. Later writers have argued about whether the gloss of the production suited her, some preferring the rougher Fotheringay sound. Its reputation has grown steadily since the 1980s through deluxe reissues and the attention of younger singers who cite her as a model.","Begin with It'll Take a Long Time and listen for the pedal steel sliding under her voice, then hear The Quiet Joys of Brotherhood on good headphones to pick out the separate vocal layers. The Lady shows her piano at its simplest. Follow with Joni Mitchell's Blue to hear a contemporary working a similar vein from the other side of the Atlantic."],"sandy-denny-like-an-old-fashioned-waltz":["Denny's third solo album turns away from folk towards piano ballads with orchestral strings, arranged by Harry Robinson. Her own songs, among them Solo, Dark the Night, Friends and the title track, a gentle waltz about dancing to old music, sit beside two pre-war popular standards, Whispering Grass and Until the Real Thing Comes Along. The sound is lush and intimate, closer to a torch-song record than to anything by Fairport Convention.","Recorded in 1973 with Trevor Lucas again producing, shortly before the couple married, the album came as Denny was drifting from the folk circuit and hoping to reach a wider pop audience. The choice of 1930s and 1940s standards reflects the music of her parents' generation, which she loved. Island had little idea how to market it, and the album appeared in a crowded year for singer-songwriters.","Listen to how Harry Robinson's string writing on the closing song, No End, grows into a long instrumental coda that carries on after Denny has stopped singing, almost a small tone poem. The standards are not pastiche: she sings Whispering Grass straight, with the phrasing of a dance-band vocalist, which shows a side of her voice her folk records never used.","Reviews were polite and sales disappointing, and for years the record was seen as a misstep that pulled Denny away from the tradition that made her name. That judgment has softened. Many listeners now hear it as her most personal album, with songs about loneliness and doubt that sound painfully candid given her early death in 1978, and admirers argue that the orchestral setting was a deliberate artistic choice rather than a commercial compromise.","Start with Solo, which opens the album with piano and her voice at its most direct, then play the title track and listen for its lilting triple time. End with No End and let the strings finish the record. Compare it with Nick Drake's Bryter Layter, another English record where orchestral arrangements frame a quiet, private voice."],"kate-bush-wuthering-heights":["A four-and-a-half-minute pop song sung in the voice of Cathy, the ghost of Emily Brontë's novel, begging at the window to be let back in to Heathcliff. Over rippling piano and a tense, off-kilter rhythm, Kate Bush sings almost entirely in a piercing high register, swooping between words in a way no chart singer had tried. The chorus rises in pitch, the arrangement swells with strings, and the track ends on a long electric guitar solo that fades away.","Bush wrote it at eighteen, after catching the last minutes of a BBC television adaptation and then reading the novel; she has noted that she shares a birthday with Emily Brontë. EMI, which had signed her as a teenager on David Gilmour's recommendation, wanted another song as her debut single, but she insisted. Produced by Andrew Powell and released in January 1978, it reached number one in Britain, making her the first woman to top the UK chart with a song she wrote herself.","The song dramatises one specific moment in the book: the scene early on in which the visitor Lockwood dreams of a ghost child scratching at the window, not the love story most people half remember. Notice too that the guitar solo by Ian Bairnson is still going when the fade begins, as if the music refuses to end. Bush trained in mime with Lindsay Kemp, and her movements in the two 1978 videos are part of the piece.","Many listeners and some critics in 1978 found the voice shrill or merely eccentric, and it was parodied on television. The record's success nevertheless opened the way for a generation of self-directed female artists, and its standing has only risen. Since 2013 fans have gathered around the world in red dresses to re-enact the video, a sign of how thoroughly it has entered popular culture.","Listen first just to the voice and how it leaps across the melody, then to the piano, which keeps a restless figure going under everything. Watch the red-dress video, filmed on Salisbury Plain, to see the choreography. Then read the novel's third chapter and hear the song again. Compare with The Kinks' Waterloo Sunset for a very different kind of English literary pop."],"kate-bush-never-for-ever":["Bush's third album, eleven songs that each tell a different story in a different voice: a wife testing her husband's loyalty in Babooshka, a mother mourning a son killed in the army in the waltz Army Dreamers, a revenge drama in The Wedding List, a tribute to the composer Delius, and a closing song, Breathing, sung by an unborn child sensing nuclear fallout. Pianos and guitars now share space with sampled sounds from the new Fairlight computer, including breaking glass.","After two albums produced for her, Bush co-produced this one with the engineer Jon Kelly, the first step towards the full control she took on later records. She had met the Fairlight CMI, then an extremely expensive instrument, while singing on Peter Gabriel's third album, and used it here among the first British pop artists to do so. Recorded in London in 1979 and 1980, amid Cold War anxiety about nuclear missiles in Europe, it became the first album by a British female solo artist to reach number one in the UK.","Delius (Song of Summer) takes its subtitle from Ken Russell's television film about the blind, paralysed composer and his young amanuensis Eric Fenby; a male voice calling out as if dictating music represents Fenby. Army Dreamers is a lilting waltz with the sound of a rifle being cocked set into the rhythm. The spoken passage near the end of Breathing, describing a nuclear flash, is the album's single most chilling moment.","It was a commercial peak but critics at the time were divided, some finding the theatricality too much. The record now reads as a hinge between the precocious early albums and the self-produced The Dreaming and Hounds of Love. Breathing, which Bush has called one of her favourite songs, is widely considered among the best political songs in British pop precisely because it never makes a speech.","Play Breathing first, on its own and loud, and follow how the music builds from quiet to overwhelming. Then Babooshka, listening for the sampled glass, and Army Dreamers for its waltz time. Hear Delius after Delius's own On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring. Continue to Hounds of Love to hear where the Fairlight experiments led."],"kate-bush-aerial":["A double album in two halves. A Sea of Honey gathers seven separate songs: King of the Mountain, about Elvis Presley and fame; Pi, in which Bush sings the digits of the mathematical constant; Bertie, a love song to her young son set for Renaissance-style strings and guitar; and Mrs. Bartolozzi, where a woman watches clothes turn in a washing machine. A Sky of Honey is a continuous suite following one summer day from dawn to dawn, full of birdsong, painting and light.","It was Bush's first album in twelve years, after The Red Shoes in 1993, a silence during which she had withdrawn from public life, lost her mother and become a parent. She recorded mostly at her home studio, playing much of the keyboard work herself, and produced it alone. The long gap made its appearance in November 2005 a major media event; it reached number three in Britain and went platinum there.","Listen closely to the birdsong in A Sky of Honey: much of it is real, and Bush also answers the birds with her own voice, imitating wood pigeons and blackbirds until you cannot tell which is which. The closing track dissolves into laughter that turns into birdsong. Listeners who checked the digits in Pi found that she sings accurately for a long stretch and then wanders from the true sequence.","Reviews were rapturous, many calling it a return to form and some her finest work, though a minority found the domestic subject matter indulgent. Its reputation has held, and A Sky of Honey was performed in full in her Before the Dawn concerts of 2014. For a 2018 remaster Bush replaced the spoken part of a guest performer with a new recording by her son, a change that divided some collectors.","Treat the two discs differently. Dip into A Sea of Honey song by song, starting with King of the Mountain and Mrs. Bartolozzi. Give A Sky of Honey forty minutes in one sitting, ideally on a summer evening, and follow the day's light as it passes. Compare it with Vaughan Williams's The Lark Ascending, another English piece where a bird carries the music upward."],"the-rolling-stones-sticky-fingers":["Ten songs that swing between hard rock and slow, country-soul ballads. Brown Sugar opens with a jagged open-tuned riff; Wild Horses is a gentle acoustic lament; Can't You Hear Me Knocking starts as a snarling rocker and turns into a seven-minute Latin-tinged jam with saxophone and congas; Dead Flowers is a sly country pastiche; and Moonlight Mile closes with string arrangements by Paul Buckmaster. Mick Taylor's fluid lead guitar is everywhere, the first full album built around his playing.","The Stones had come out of the chaos of 1969, the death of Brian Jones and the violence at Altamont, and out of their contract with Decca. Sticky Fingers was the first release on their own Rolling Stones Records, and the first to carry the tongue-and-lips logo designed by John Pasche. Some songs were cut at Muscle Shoals Sound Studio in Alabama in December 1969, the rest in England with the band's mobile studio. Andy Warhol conceived the cover, with a working zip on the jeans.","The long jam that ends Can't You Hear Me Knocking was never planned: Keith Richards has said the band simply carried on playing after the song, not knowing the tape was still running, and the producer Jimmy Miller kept it. Richards does not play on Moonlight Mile, which grew from a Mick Taylor idea. The Spanish edition had an entirely different cover, a can of treacle with fingers, because the Franco government censored Warhol's design.","It topped the charts on both sides of the Atlantic and was well reviewed, though some critics missed the conceptual unity of Beggars Banquet. It is now counted among the band's great run of four albums. The song Brown Sugar has been reassessed: its lyrics touching on slavery and sexual violence drew increasing criticism, and the band stopped performing it live in 2021.","Start with Wild Horses and Moonlight Mile to hear the band's tender side, then Can't You Hear Me Knocking, waiting for the moment the song gives way to Bobby Keys's saxophone. Listen for how Taylor's melodic lines contrast with Richards's rhythm playing. Follow with Muddy Waters, whose Rollin' Stone gave the band its name, and then Exile on Main St."],"the-rolling-stones-exile-on-main-st":["A double album of eighteen songs that sounds like a crowded, sweaty room: rock and roll, country, gospel, Chicago blues and soul, all mixed so that the voice sits down inside the band rather than on top of it. Rocks Off and Rip This Joint tear along; Tumbling Dice lopes; Sweet Virginia and Torn and Frayed lean towards country; Shine a Light and I Just Want to See His Face reach for gospel. Keith Richards sings lead on Happy.","Facing ruinous British tax bills, the band left England in 1971 and moved to the south of France. Much of the album was recorded in the humid basement of Nellcôte, the villa Richards rented near Villefranche-sur-Mer, using the Stones' mobile studio parked outside, with musicians drifting in and out over months. Jimmy Miller produced, and the record was finished in Los Angeles, where gospel singers including Clydie King and Venetta Fields added backing voices.","Happy was cut one afternoon at Nellcôte by Richards, the saxophonist Bobby Keys and Jimmy Miller on drums before the rest of the band turned up. Shine a Light had been started years earlier and is often heard as a farewell to Brian Jones. The murky mix is partly the basement itself: heat and damp kept knocking the guitars out of tune. The album also covers songs by Robert Johnson and Slim Harpo.","Initial reviews were mixed, several critics finding the sound muddy and the songs undistinguished, and Jagger himself later expressed doubts. Over two decades it was completely reassessed and is now often called the band's greatest album and one of the great rock records, prized for exactly the density that first put people off. The 2010 reissue with previously unreleased tracks renewed the debate about how finished it was meant to sound.","Do not expect to hear every word; listen to the whole band as a single moving mass. Start with Tumbling Dice, then Shine a Light, and notice the gospel voices rising at the end. Play the four sides in order if you can, as the record was designed. Follow with Robert Johnson's King of the Delta Blues Singers to hear one root of it."],"pink-floyd-the-wall":["A double-album rock opera of twenty-six songs telling the story of Pink, a rock star who builds a psychological wall against the world out of a father killed in the war, a smothering mother, cruel schoolteachers and failed love, until he collapses into a fascist fantasy and is put on trial in his own mind. It ranges from the disco-beat protest of Another Brick in the Wall, Part 2 to the slow, soaring guitar solos of Comfortably Numb and orchestral passages scored by Michael Kamen.","Roger Waters conceived it after the band's 1977 stadium tour, when he felt so alienated from the crowd that he spat at a fan in Montreal and imagined building a wall across the stage. His father had been killed at Anzio in 1944 when he was a baby. The album was co-produced by Bob Ezrin and recorded in France and Los Angeles amid financial trouble and deep tension in the band; the keyboard player Richard Wright was forced out during the sessions.","The record is a loop: the few words spoken at the very end of the last track complete a sentence begun in the half-heard murmur at the very start of the first, so the story can go round forever. Between songs on the first side a message is buried backwards, a joke for fans who hunted for secrets. The children's choir on Another Brick in the Wall, Part 2 came from Islington Green School in north London.","It was a huge commercial success and the single topped the charts in several countries, but critics split between admiring its ambition and finding it self-pitying and overbearing. The staged concerts, Alan Parker's 1982 film with Gerald Scarfe's animations, and Waters's 1990 performance in Berlin after the Wall fell made it a lasting spectacle. Many still argue that David Gilmour's contributions, especially on Comfortably Numb, rescue Waters's bleakest writing.","Hear it in order at least once, following the story through, then return to the peaks: Mother, Hey You and Comfortably Numb, listening for the second guitar solo. Notice how short fragments of melody recur across the four sides. Compare with The Who's Tommy, an earlier rock opera, and with Britten's Peter Grimes, another portrait of an outsider turning on himself."],"fairport-convention-what-we-did-on-our-holidays":["Fairport's second album and the first with Sandy Denny, whose voice shares the lead with Ian Matthews. The record still leans towards American singer-songwriters, with a Joni Mitchell song and a Bob Dylan song, but traditional material now enters: a haunting She Moves Through the Fair and a droning, menacing arrangement of Nottamun Town. Denny contributes Fotheringay, about Mary, Queen of Scots, and Richard Thompson's Meet on the Ledge closes the first side as a quiet anthem.","Denny joined in 1968 after the band's first singer, Judy Dyble, left, bringing with her a deep knowledge of the English and Scottish traditional repertoire from the folk clubs. Joe Boyd produced for Island, released in January 1969. The London underground scene around the UFO club, where the band had played, was still shaped by American West Coast rock, and Fairport was sometimes described as the English Jefferson Airplane.","Listen to Nottamun Town, a riddling nonsense song best known from the Kentucky singer Jean Ritchie: here drums pound like a ritual while Denny and the band sing in a flat, eerie unison, the first sign of the electric folk sound that would define Liege & Lief. Denny's Fotheringay imagines the captive queen at her window, a song that later gave its name to her own band.","It sold modestly but was admired as the moment Fairport found its own voice. Meet on the Ledge became the band's signature and is still sung at the end of their annual Cropredy festival, taking on the weight of a memorial after the losses that followed. Critics now see the album as the essential hinge between the band's American beginnings and its turn to English tradition.","Start with Fotheringay to meet Denny as a writer, then Nottamun Town and She Moves Through the Fair to hear the traditional songs being electrified. End with Meet on the Ledge. Afterwards hear Jean Ritchie's own family recordings to compare, and then Unhalfbricking, which followed a few months later."],"fairport-convention-unhalfbricking":["Eight tracks that capture the band mid-transformation. Three are Dylan songs, one of them, Si Tu Dois Partir, sung in French as a boisterous Cajun romp. Richard Thompson contributes Genesis Hall and Cajun Woman, Sandy Denny her own Autopsy and Who Knows Where the Time Goes?, and at the centre stands A Sailor's Life, an eleven-minute traditional ballad that grows from quiet guitar into a furious electric improvisation with Dave Swarbrick's fiddle.","Recorded in early 1969 and produced by Joe Boyd, the album was completed before disaster: in May 1969 the band's van crashed on the M1, killing the drummer Martin Lamble, aged nineteen, and Thompson's girlfriend Jeannie Franklyn. Released that July, it reached the UK top twenty, and the Dylan song gave them a small hit single. The shock of the crash led directly to the band's retreat to a country house and the wholly traditional direction of Liege & Lief.","The cover photograph shows Denny's parents standing by the gate of their house in Wimbledon, with the band barely visible behind the fence. The title is a word Denny coined while playing a word game. A Sailor's Life was recorded in essentially one take with Swarbrick as a guest; it was his first session with the band, and it persuaded him to join.","It was well received, and Who Knows Where the Time Goes? was quickly recognised as a great song. Over time critics have come to see A Sailor's Life as the real turning point in English folk rock, the moment a traditional ballad was treated with the weight of a Coltrane or a Grateful Dead improvisation. The Martin Lamble tragedy colours every retrospective reading.","Hear A Sailor's Life first and let it unfold; listen for the moment the drums and fiddle take over. Then Who Knows Where the Time Goes? for Denny at her most reflective, and Si Tu Dois Partir for the band's sense of fun. Follow with Liege & Lief to hear what the new direction became."],"fairport-convention-full-house":["The first Fairport album with no female voice: the singing is shared among Richard Thompson, Dave Swarbrick and Dave Pegg. It pairs new songs by Thompson and Swarbrick, among them the brooding Sloth and the rousing Walk Awhile, with traditional ballads such as Sir Patrick Spens and Doctor of Physick and a blistering medley of fiddle tunes, Dirty Linen. The sound is leaner and louder than before, driven by Swarbrick's fiddle and Thompson's guitar trading lines.","Sandy Denny and the bassist Ashley Hutchings left in late 1969, Denny for Fotheringay and Hutchings to found Steeleye Span. Swarbrick joined full time and Pegg came in on bass. The band now lived and rehearsed together in a former pub, the Angel, at Little Hadham in Hertfordshire. Joe Boyd produced the album, released in 1970; it was Thompson's last studio album as a member before he left for a solo career.","One song, Poor Will and the Jolly Hangman, was pulled from the album at the last minute because Thompson was unhappy with his vocal; it was restored on later CD reissues. Listen in Sloth for the way the guitar and fiddle solos pass back and forth almost like a conversation, over a slow, heavy beat that anticipates Thompson's later ballads. Dirty Linen strings together tunes in a way that became standard for later English dance bands.","Critics received it well, and the live power of this line-up became legendary, preserved on recordings from the same year. Some listeners missed Denny, and the album has always stood in the shadow of Liege & Lief. Many Thompson admirers now value it highly as the purest record of his playing within a band, and Sloth remains a staple of Fairport concerts.","Start with Dirty Linen for sheer energy, then Sloth for the extended solos, and Sir Patrick Spens to hear an old ballad sung in a rock setting. Compare with the traditional Sir Patrick Spens in the catalogue to hear what the band added. Then follow Thompson into I Want to See the Bright Lights Tonight."],"black-sabbath-vol-4":["Ten tracks that push the band's heavy sound in several directions at once. Wheels of Confusion opens as a long, multi-part epic; Supernaut is built on a tight, fast riff that many later guitarists singled out; Cornucopia and Under the Sun grind at slow tempos; Snowblind is a heavy ballad. There are also surprises: Changes, a soft piano ballad with Mellotron strings, Laguna Sunrise, a delicate acoustic instrumental with orchestra, and FX, a short experiment in echo.","For the first time the band produced themselves, having parted from Rodger Bain, and they recorded at the Record Plant in Los Angeles in 1972 while living in a rented mansion in Bel Air. They were exhausted from constant touring and, by their own accounts, consuming heavy quantities of drugs; Snowblind, the working title, refers to cocaine. Tony Iommi continued the lower guitar tuning he had adopted on Master of Reality, deepening the sound.","FX was made almost by accident: Iommi's crucifix pendant struck the strings of his guitar while it was running through a heavy echo, and the band recorded themselves tapping the strings for fun. On Changes, Iommi, a guitarist, taught himself enough piano to play the part, which is why it sounds so plain and unadorned. Listen for the Mellotron doubling the string line.","Critics largely dismissed it in 1972, as they did most early Sabbath, but it sold strongly. Its standing among musicians has grown, and Supernaut in particular was admired by players from Frank Zappa to later grunge bands. Changes found a new life when Ozzy Osbourne recorded it as a duet with his daughter Kelly in 2003, reaching the top of the British chart.","Begin with Supernaut, listening to how the guitar and drums lock tight, then Wheels of Confusion to hear its sections unfold. Then try Changes and Laguna Sunrise, which show a gentler band few people expect. Compare with Master of Reality, its predecessor, and with Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear the lineage."],"black-sabbath-sabbath-bloody-sabbath":["Eight tracks that bring keyboards, synthesizers and orchestral strings into the Sabbath sound. The title track sets a crushing riff against a lighter, jazzier verse, then shifts gear into a ferocious final section. Sabbra Cadabra features piano and Minimoog; A National Acrobat meanders through several riffs; Fluff is a gentle acoustic instrumental; Who Are You? is dominated by synthesizer; and the closing Spiral Architect opens with acoustic guitar and adds strings. It is the band's most ambitious and varied early album.","After Vol. 4 the band returned to Los Angeles to write, but Tony Iommi found himself blocked. They moved to Clearwell Castle in the Forest of Dean, where in a cold, reputedly haunted room Iommi found the title riff and the ideas flowed again. The album was recorded at Morgan Studios in London in 1973, when progressive rock was at its peak, and Rick Wakeman of Yes played keyboards on Sabbra Cadabra.","Wakeman is said to have refused a fee for his playing and asked to be paid in beer instead. The quiet instrumental Fluff is named after the Radio 1 disc jockey Alan Freeman, whose nickname it was and who was one of the few broadcasters to play the band. Drew Struzan's lurid cover, of a man dying in bed surrounded by demons, contrasts with a peaceful scene on the back.","It was the first Sabbath album to win over critics in large numbers, with even some previously scornful rock papers praising its range. The band later said it was the last time all four were fully united creatively. Its fusion of metal heaviness with keyboards and strings has been cited as an influence by progressive metal bands, and its title riff is among the band's most imitated.","Start with the title track and listen for the switch from the crushing chorus to the airy verses, then the ferocious final minutes. Next hear Spiral Architect for the strings, then Sabbra Cadabra for Wakeman's piano. Compare with Yes's Close to the Edge to see how far the two scenes had converged."],"king-crimson-islands":["Six tracks that move from jazz-rock to chamber music. Formentera Lady opens with double bass, flute and a wordless soprano; Sailor's Tale is a propulsive instrumental built on Robert Fripp's strummed, banjo-like guitar and walls of Mellotron; The Letters and Ladies of the Road are dark and bluesy. Prelude: Song of the Gulls is a short piece for strings and oboe with no rock instruments at all, and the title track ends the album quietly with piano, cornet and harmonium.","It was the fourth King Crimson album in less than two years, with a new line-up around Fripp: Mel Collins on saxophones and flute, Boz Burrell on bass and vocals, Ian Wallace on drums, and Peter Sinfield writing lyrics and adding synthesizer. Guests from the London jazz scene joined them, including the pianist Keith Tippett. Recorded in London in 1971, it was Sinfield's last album with the group; Fripp dismissed him soon after.","After the title track fades, there is a short pause and then a hidden recording of the chamber musicians tuning up while Fripp talks them through the session, a fly-on-the-wall glimpse of a rock band directing classical players. Listen for Keith Tippett's piano and Mark Charig's cornet in Islands, both drawn from free jazz. Prelude: Song of the Gulls grew from a melody Fripp had written years before.","Reviews were lukewarm and the line-up fell apart during the following tour. Fripp himself long spoke coolly of the album, and Ladies of the Road, about life on tour, has been criticised for its attitudes to women. Admirers, however, now defend it as the most lyrical and chamber-like King Crimson record, and Sailor's Tale is regarded as one of Fripp's finest pieces of guitar writing.","Start with Sailor's Tale and listen for how Fripp's strumming speeds up and becomes a blur of sound. Then play the album from Prelude: Song of the Gulls onwards into the title track, with the lights low. Compare Song of the Gulls with Vaughan Williams's Fantasia on a Theme by Thomas Tallis, and then hear Larks' Tongues in Aspic to hear how far the next band went."],"king-crimson-discipline":["Seven tracks built from interlocking patterns. Robert Fripp and Adrian Belew play two guitars that weave repeating figures around each other, sometimes in different time signatures so that they drift in and out of alignment, over Tony Levin's Chapman Stick and Bill Bruford's electronic and acoustic drums. Elephant Talk features Belew making animal noises with his guitar; Frame by Frame races; Matte Kudasai is a gentle ballad; Indiscipline explodes from quiet to chaos; and the closing Discipline is almost pure pattern.","Fripp had disbanded King Crimson in 1974 and spent the late 1970s in New York, working with Brian Eno, David Bowie and Peter Gabriel. In 1981 he formed a new band, first called Discipline, with Belew, who had just toured with Talking Heads, and later renamed it. The new music drew on Balinese gamelan, the minimalism of Steve Reich and Philip Glass, and the angular post-punk of New York. The album was produced by the band with Rhett Davies.","In Frame by Frame the two guitars start together, then one drops a single note from each cycle and they slide out of phase, a technique straight from Reich's early phase pieces. The title Thela Hun Ginjeet is an anagram of 'heat in the jungle', and Belew's spoken account over it describes a real frightening encounter he had on a London street while carrying a tape recorder.","Longtime fans were split, some missing the older epic style, while others welcomed a leaner sound that matched the new wave. Critics increasingly admired it, and it is now regarded as one of the band's finest albums and a meeting point of rock and minimalism. Its interlocking guitars influenced math rock, Tool and post-rock bands, and the four-piece line-up became one of the most celebrated in progressive rock.","Start with Frame by Frame and try to follow each guitar separately, then listen to how they merge. Next hear Elephant Talk for Belew's sound effects, then Indiscipline for Bruford's drumming. Compare Discipline itself with Reich's Electric Counterpoint and with Balinese gamelan, and with Talking Heads' Remain in Light, where Belew played a year earlier."],"led-zeppelin-iii":["Ten songs in two moods. Side one opens with 'Immigrant Song', a galloping two-minute war chant driven by a single stabbing guitar note and Robert Plant's wordless howl, and contains the long slow blues 'Since I've Been Loving You', with John Paul Jones on organ. Side two turns almost wholly acoustic: strummed and fingerpicked guitars, mandolin and banjo on 'Gallows Pole', the gentle 'Tangerine' and 'That's the Way', and a stomping country-blues romp.","After two years of relentless American touring, Jimmy Page and Plant retreated in 1970 to Bron-Yr-Aur, a cottage without electricity in the Welsh hills, and wrote much of the album there on acoustic instruments. Recording continued at Headley Grange, a draughty Hampshire house, using the Rolling Stones' mobile studio. Page had long admired the British folk guitarists Bert Jansch and Davy Graham, and the album let that side of him into the open.","'Gallows Pole' is a very old ballad, the one folklorists know as 'The Maid Freed from the Gallows', which Page learned from an American twelve-string recording descended from Lead Belly's version; listen for how the band builds it from lone guitar to full gallop. The original sleeve had a rotating cardboard wheel behind cut-out holes, so the pictures changed as you turned it.","Reviewers in 1970 were puzzled and often dismissive: the heavy band of the first two albums had gone soft, some said, and sales fell short of Led Zeppelin II. Time reversed the verdict. The acoustic side is now heard as the hinge of their career, the moment they proved the folk and blues strands could sit together, and it made the mixture of Led Zeppelin IV possible.","Play 'Immigrant Song' loud, then jump straight to 'That's the Way' to hear how far apart the two moods are. Stay with 'Since I've Been Loving You' for Plant's most controlled blues singing and Bonham's slow, heavy drumming. Afterwards listen to Bert Jansch or Pentangle and you will hear where the fingerpicking came from."],"led-zeppelin-physical-graffiti":["A double album of fifteen tracks, sprawling on purpose. At its centre is 'Kashmir', eight minutes of a slowly climbing guitar riff set against orchestral strings and brass, over a drumbeat that never hurries. Around it sit the eleven-minute slide-guitar gospel blues 'In My Time of Dying', the funky clavinet workout 'Trampled Under Foot', the riff-heavy 'Custard Pie' and 'The Wanton Song', and short acoustic pieces and leftovers from earlier sessions.","In 1974 the band went back to Headley Grange with a mobile studio and recorded eight new songs; there was too much for one LP, so they filled out a double with unused tracks from the III, IV and Houses of the Holy sessions. It was the first release on their own label, Swan Song, and came out in February 1975 at the height of their fame, when they were among the biggest touring acts in the world.","The lyrics of 'Kashmir' were written after Plant drove through the desert of southern Morocco, not Kashmir, which he had never visited. 'In My Time of Dying' descends from the gospel song Blind Willie Johnson recorded in 1927 as 'Jesus Make Up My Dying Bed'. The famous cover shows a New York tenement on St Mark's Place, with die-cut windows that reveal different faces as the inner sleeves slide.","It sold enormously and critics largely welcomed it, though some found a double album bloated with filler. Punk, arriving eighteen months later, made records of this scale the very thing to reject. Yet 'Kashmir' became the band's own favourite and the track most often cited as their finest, and the album now stands as their most varied, a summary of everything they could do.","Start with 'Kashmir' and notice that the riff and the strings rise while the drums stay stubbornly level, which is where the sense of vast slow travel comes from. Then hear 'Trampled Under Foot' for the funk side. Treat the rest as a big house to wander through; the 2015 remaster is clean and punchy."],"planxty-cold-blow-and-the-rainy-night":["Planxty's third album mixes songs and dance tunes in roughly equal measure: long unaccompanied-sounding ballads carried on a bed of interlocking bouzouki, mandolin and guitar, and sets of jigs and reels led by Liam O'Flynn's uilleann pipes. The title song is a traditional night-visiting song, sung lightly over a rolling accompaniment, and Christy Moore's 'The Lakes of Pontchartrain' is the album's slow, tender heart.","Dónal Lunny, whose bouzouki had helped define the band's sound, left in 1973, and Johnny Moynihan, the man who had first brought the Greek bouzouki into Irish music, took his place, adding fiddle and whistle. Irish traditional music was in the middle of a revival, with Planxty drawing young rock-era audiences to pipes and old ballads. The band was also tired from touring, and this line-up would soon break apart.","Listen to the strings under the voices: the band never simply strum chords behind a singer but weave separate lines, a bouzouki melody answering a mandolin figure, so the accompaniment behaves almost like counterpoint. That method, rather than any single tune, is the real Planxty invention, and here Moynihan's looser, slightly wilder playing changes its texture from the earlier albums.","Coming after two much-loved records, it was received warmly but has sometimes been treated as the least of the first three, the sound of a band changing members. Many listeners now hear it as the most relaxed and rounded of the early albums, and 'The Lakes of Pontchartrain' became a standard of the Irish song repertoire in Moore's version.","Start with 'The Lakes of Pontchartrain' and listen to how little the instruments do behind Moore's voice. Then play a set of tunes and follow O'Flynn's pipes, especially the drones and chanter decorations. Compare the band's first album to hear how Lunny's bouzouki and Moynihan's differ."],"planxty-after-the-break":["The reunion album, and the fullest-sounding Planxty record so far. Irish ballads and emigrant songs sit beside tune sets for pipes and flute, and Andy Irvine brings in a dance from Bulgaria, 'Smeceno Horo', played at speed in an uneven, limping metre that no Irish reel uses. The textures are brighter and busier than the early albums, with the bouzouki and mandolin woven tight under the melodies.","Planxty had split in 1975, its members moving through other bands, among them the Bothy Band and Irvine's duo with Paul Brady. In 1978 the original four, Christy Moore, Irvine, Dónal Lunny and Liam O'Flynn, came back together, with Matt Molloy on flute. Irvine had travelled in the Balkans, and the traditional revival he had helped start had in the meantime become a much bigger movement.","The Bulgarian tune is the key to the album's ambitions: listen to how the Irish instruments cope with a pulse grouped in unequal lengths, short and long, rather than the even beats of a jig or reel. That Balkan strand, first heard here, would grow into a whole side of Irvine's later career and eventually into the 'Irish-Balkan' fusions of the 1980s and 1990s.","Fans greeted the reunion with relief, and the album was seen as proof that the group could pick up where it had left off. Critics have argued whether the added polish and extra flute take something away from the rough spontaneity of 1973, but the record is widely counted among their best, and the Balkan experiment is now heard as far-sighted.","Start with 'Smeceno Horo' and try to tap your foot to it; you will feel the uneven groups at once. Then go back to a slow song and hear how the band's woven accompaniment has thickened since the first album. Bartók's Bulgarian-rhythm dances make a revealing companion piece."],"planxty-the-woman-i-loved-so-well":["The second album of the reunited band, darker and more song-centred than its predecessor. Its long centrepiece is Christy Moore's 'Little Musgrave', an old ballad of adultery and revenge unfolding over many verses, the instruments slowly gathering behind him. The title song is a slow love song, and pipe tunes from Liam O'Flynn break up the long narratives with bursts of dance.","Recorded in Dublin as the reunion line-up settled into steady work, the album came at the end of the 1970s traditional boom, just before Moore left again for a solo career that would make him the most popular singer in Ireland. Dónal Lunny's arrangements now drew on everything the members had learned in other bands, and the recording is closer and warmer than their 1970s records.","'Little Musgrave' is one of the English and Scottish ballads Francis Child collected, and Moore reshaped it for his own voice, trimming and reworking the story. Listen to how the accompaniment changes with each turn of the plot, thin and bright for the lovers, heavier as the husband approaches, so the instruments tell the story alongside the voice.","It was well received, and 'Little Musgrave' in particular became one of Moore's best-known performances, sung in folk clubs across Ireland and Britain. Some listeners find the album more subdued than the band's earlier records; others think it their most mature, the moment the arrangements stopped showing off and started serving the songs.","Set aside the ten or so minutes for 'Little Musgrave' and listen to it as a story, following the plot the way you would a short film. Then hear the title song for Moore's quieter voice. Compare the ballad singing of Shirley Collins or Fairport Convention for the English side of the same repertoire."],"sex-pistols-anarchy-in-the-uk":["The Sex Pistols' first single: a mid-tempo, grinding rock song built on a descending guitar chord pattern, with Steve Jones's guitars stacked in layer upon layer until they form a single wall. Over it Johnny Rotten sneers, rolls his r's and stretches his vowels, casting himself as an anarchist and a sort of antichrist, and the song ends in a burst of feedback and a final snarled threat to destroy.","The band, managed by Malcolm McLaren and dressed from his King's Road shop, had built a following in London clubs through 1976. EMI signed them, and the single, produced by Chris Thomas, appeared in November. Days later the group swore on Bill Grundy's teatime television show, the tabloids exploded, concerts on the following tour were cancelled across Britain, and in January 1977 EMI dropped them.","For all its reputation, it is not a fast song: it moves at a deliberate stomp, much slower than the Ramones, and the menace comes from the weight of the overdubbed guitars, a studio trick borrowed from glossy 1970s rock. Listen also to Rotten's words tripping over the beat; many of the lines are sung slightly ahead or behind, as if he refuses to keep time.","It reached the lower half of the British top forty before EMI withdrew it, and for many young listeners it was the first sign that punk had arrived beyond London. Critics now tend to hear it as the Pistols' best recording, the one where the politics, the humour and Thomas's production balance, and as the opening shot of British punk on record.","Listen once for Rotten's voice alone, the pantomime relish in every rolled consonant. Then listen again for the guitars and count how many seem to be playing. Afterwards, compare the Ramones' 'Blitzkrieg Bop' to hear how different New York and London punk sounded in the same year."],"sex-pistols-pretty-vacant":["The catchiest thing the Sex Pistols recorded. A ringing single-note guitar figure opens it, the band crashes in, and the song swings between a tight verse and a chanted chorus that is almost a football-terrace singalong. Rotten sings of a generation with nothing to offer and nothing it wants, turning boredom into a boast, and he lands the last word of the chorus with a drawl that made it sound ruder than it was.","It was written largely by the bassist Glen Matlock, who by the time of its release in July 1977 had been replaced by Sid Vicious. The band were now on Virgin after being dropped by two labels, and after the ban on 'God Save the Queen' this was the single that finally got them onto Top of the Pops, the BBC's weekly chart show.","Matlock has said the opening riff was inspired by ABBA's 'SOS', which says much about how pop-literate punk really was. Listen too to the chorus: Rotten's split of the word 'vacant' into two drawn-out syllables smuggles an obscenity past the censors, which is why the BBC could play it.","It became a UK top ten hit, and its tunefulness helped persuade sceptics that punk could write songs as well as cause scandals. Some purists found it too poppy even then. It has aged very well, covered by many later bands, and is often chosen as the best introduction to the group for those who find the album heavy going.","Listen to the opening guitar alone and try humming it; it is a pop hook, however loud. Then notice how Paul Cook's drumming keeps a steady, almost danceable beat underneath the noise. Compare Buzzcocks' 'Ever Fallen in Love' for the same marriage of punk and pop melody."],"sex-pistols-holidays-in-the-sun":["The last Sex Pistols single before their album, and their most unsettling. It opens with the sound of marching boots, then a charging guitar riff, and Rotten describes a cheap holiday turned nightmare: bored in a sunny tourist resort, then drawn to the Berlin Wall, staring across it at the East and feeling, in his own panicked way, that the watchtowers are watching him. The song ends in shouted repetition, as if trapped.","Released in October 1977, weeks before Never Mind the Bollocks, it was written after the band's disastrous trip to the Channel Islands and a visit to Berlin. The Cold War was a daily fact, the Wall had stood for sixteen years, and David Bowie was then recording in the city. The picture sleeve adapted artwork from a Belgian travel brochure, and the agency's legal action had it withdrawn.","The opening riff closely resembles that of the Jam's 'In the City', released a few months earlier, and the Jam noticed. Listen past it to Rotten's lyric, which is a clever inversion of the Bowie-era romance of Berlin: where others found glamour and escape at the Wall, he finds only claustrophobia and paranoia on both sides of it.","It reached the British top ten and was praised as the group's most ambitious song, a punk record about geopolitics rather than just about England. Some critics rate it above 'Anarchy' as writing. It is now read as the point where Rotten's lyrics pointed toward the stranger, more literate direction he took with Public Image Ltd.","Listen for the marching boots at the start and hear how they return as the drumbeat. Then follow the story like a short travelogue. Play it next to Bowie's '\"Heroes\"', recorded in the same city the same year, to hear two opposite views of the Wall."],"joy-division-transmission":["Joy Division's first single for Factory. Peter Hook's bass carries the melody high up the neck while Stephen Morris drums with machine-like drive, and Bernard Sumner's guitar scrapes and rings over the top. Ian Curtis starts in a low, flat baritone and rises to a desperate shout as the song builds, urging the listener to dance to the radio in a chorus that sounds anything but carefree.","Recorded in Stockport with the producer Martin Hannett around the time of Unknown Pleasures and released in late 1979, it gave the Manchester band their first real song for the radio, and they played it on BBC television. Factory Records, run by Tony Wilson, was a new independent label in a city of empty mills and warehouses, and the post-punk scene there was inventing its own sound.","The irony is in the title and the chorus: a song about losing yourself in radio music, dancing alone to a transmission, written by a singer whose lyrics elsewhere are about isolation and loss of control. Listen to Hannett's production too, the drums dry and separate, each part in its own space, as though every instrument were being broadcast from a different room.","It did not chart at first but became a staple of late-night radio and independent charts, and Curtis's jerking dance to it on television became part of his legend. It is now one of the most covered and anthologised Joy Division songs, heard as the bridge between their punk beginnings and the grander sound of 'Love Will Tear Us Apart'.","Follow the bass first; it is the tune. Then listen for the point where Curtis's voice suddenly lifts in pitch and volume, the whole song hinging on it. Play it next to 'Love Will Tear Us Apart' to hear how quickly the band grew in a single year."],"joy-division-atmosphere":["A slow, processional song, closer to a hymn than to rock. Stephen Morris rolls around his toms in a funereal pattern, a synthesiser holds a thin, chiming line above, and Ian Curtis sings at the bottom of his voice, urging someone not to walk away in silence. There is almost no guitar; the space is filled by echo, so the whole piece seems to move through a large, empty church.","It first appeared in March 1980 on a limited French single titled 'Licht und Blindheit', issued by the small label Sordide Sentimental. Two months later Curtis took his own life, on the eve of an American tour. Factory reissued the song as 'Atmosphere', and it became one of the pieces through which a grieving audience came to know him.","Martin Hannett's production is the real instrument: listen to how much of the sound is reverberation rather than playing, the drums blooming and decaying in a manufactured space. The original French title, meaning 'light and blindness', hints at the song's theme better than the English one, a plea across a distance that cannot be crossed.","Initially a rarity for collectors, it became widely known after Curtis's death and again in 1988, when a new video by Anton Corbijn accompanied its reissue. Many fans now rank it as Joy Division's most moving recording; some critics find its solemnity overpowering, but its influence on gothic rock and on film soundtracks is beyond argument.","Listen with the lights low and follow the toms; the whole song is built on that rolling pattern. Notice when the synthesiser rises in the final minute and how nothing else changes. Compare Closer, recorded around the same time, and then Arvo Pärt's Für Alina for a different kind of hushed, sacred space."],"the-clash-white-man-in-hammersmith-palais":["A punk band playing reggae at its own tempo. After a jagged guitar intro the song settles into a loping off-beat skank, and Joe Strummer tells of going to an all-night reggae show in west London expecting militant roots music and finding lightweight showband entertainment instead. From there he widens the lens: on punk groups chasing fame, on the rise of the far right, on how easily rebellion is sold back to its audience.","Released in June 1978 between the Clash's first and second albums. London punk and reggae shared clubs, sound systems and politics, and the Clash had already covered Junior Murvin's 'Police and Thieves'. It was the era of the National Front and of the first Rock Against Racism carnivals, when the question of what a white band could honestly take from Black music was live.","The song is really about the band itself: Strummer turns his disappointment with the reggae show into a sharper disappointment with his own scene. Listen to how the music keeps changing gear, from reggae verse to punk surge and back, so the arrangement itself enacts the argument about borrowing and belonging.","It stalled in the lower reaches of the British chart but critics seized on it at once, and Strummer often named it his favourite Clash song. It is now widely considered one of the best British singles of the punk era, and the model for the Clash's later habit of mixing genres on London Calling and Sandinista!.","Follow the rhythm guitar on the off-beats in the verses, then feel the jolt when the band surges in the choruses. Read the lyric sheet as a short essay. Then play Culture's Two Sevens Clash or Toots and the Maytals to hear what Strummer went to the Palais hoping to hear."],"the-clash-sandinista":["A triple album of thirty-six tracks that tries almost everything: dub reggae, New York rap in 'The Magnificent Seven', rockabilly, calypso, gospel, a waltz, jazz, Motown pastiche in 'Hitsville U.K.', and straight punk. Dub versions of earlier songs sprawl across the last side, echo and backwards tape everywhere. It is less an album than a notebook, full of sketches, jokes and a handful of the band's most ambitious songs, among them 'Police on My Back' and 'Washington Bullets'.","Recorded in 1980 in London, New York and Kingston, with the Jamaican producer and DJ Mikey Dread as collaborator. The title and catalogue number salute the Sandinista revolution in Nicaragua, and several songs address American intervention in Central America. The Clash insisted it sell for the price of a single album, accepting reduced royalties to do so, and CBS resisted.","'The Magnificent Seven' was among the first records by a white rock band to take up rapping, made only months after the first hip-hop singles appeared, and it was played on New York black radio. Listen too for guest voices: the remake of 'Career Opportunities' is sung by keyboardist Mickey Gallagher's young sons, turning the first album's snarl into a nursery rhyme.","Critics were split. Many said the band should have edited it to one great album; others thought the excess was the point, a map of the world music pouring into London. Its reputation has risen steadily, as later listeners hear its dub, rap and global experiments as prophetic of 1980s and 1990s music, though the argument about its length has never ended.","Don't play it straight through at first. Start with 'The Magnificent Seven', 'Police on My Back', 'Somebody Got Murdered' and 'Washington Bullets', then dip in by side. Listen for Mikey Dread's echo and toasting on the dub tracks, and compare Lee Perry's Super Ape."],"the-smiths-this-charming-man":["Under three minutes of bright, bouncing guitar pop. Johnny Marr's chiming lead line opens the song and never really stops, dancing over Andy Rourke's busy bass and Mike Joyce's brisk drums. Morrissey sings of a young man stranded with a punctured bicycle on a hillside, picked up by a smooth older stranger in a car, and the flirtation, class unease and comedy of the encounter are all left just ambiguous enough.","The Smiths' second single, released on the independent Rough Trade label in late 1983, before their first album. It became their first hit, and their appearance on Top of the Pops, Morrissey waving a bunch of gladioli, is remembered by a generation of British teenagers as a revelation: a band that was bookish, funny, sexually ambiguous and entirely unlike the synthesiser pop of the charts.","One of the charming man's lines is borrowed from Anthony Shaffer's thriller Sleuth, a sign of Morrissey's lifelong habit of stitching lyrics together from old films and books. Listen carefully to Marr's guitar part, which is several guitars layered to sound like one bright twelve-string, the jangling sound that became the template for indie guitar music.","It reached the UK top thirty and made the group stars of the independent scene. Critics at once recognised Marr as a major guitarist and Morrissey as a new kind of lyricist. It is now frequently named among the best British singles of the 1980s, and its guitar sound can be heard in a great many bands that followed.","Listen to the first ten seconds of guitar and try to count the parts. Then follow the story in the lyric as a short scene from a film. Compare the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man', the ancestor of that jangle, and then 'How Soon Is Now?' to hear how differently Marr could sound."],"the-smiths-the-smiths":["The debut album: ten songs, most of them slower and moodier than the bright singles. 'Reel Around the Fountain' opens it at a slow sway, Johnny Marr's guitar arpeggios circling; 'Still Ill' and 'Hand in Glove' push harder; 'Pretty Girls Make Graves' and 'What Difference Does It Make?' mix wit with misery. It closes with 'Suffer Little Children', a slow, haunted song about the child victims of the Moors murders near Manchester.","The band had abandoned a first set of sessions with Troy Tate as too rough and re-recorded the album with John Porter, releasing it in February 1984. Manchester was a city of closed factories and high youth unemployment, and Morrissey's lyrics of shyness, celibacy, class and longing spoke to young listeners who found the glamour of 1980s chart pop remote from their lives.","The cover shows the actor Joe Dallesandro, cropped from Andy Warhol's film Flesh, the first of the band's famous sleeves of old film stars and 1960s icons. Listen to Andy Rourke's bass lines, which often move independently of the guitar as a second melody, one reason the band sound fuller than a quartet should.","It went to number two in the UK, but the band and many critics felt Porter's production smoothed the edges, and some fans still prefer the rawer radio-session versions of the songs. 'Suffer Little Children' and 'Reel Around the Fountain' drew tabloid controversy. It is now seen as one of the great British debut albums, even if not the Smiths' best.","Start with 'Still Ill' and 'Hand in Glove' for energy, then go back to 'Reel Around the Fountain' and let it unfold slowly. Listen to Marr's guitar under Morrissey rather than to the words at first. Afterwards compare the compilation Hatful of Hollow, where many of the same songs are rougher."],"the-smiths-strangeways-here-we-come":["The Smiths' final studio album, and their most varied in sound. 'A Rush and a Push and the Land Is Ours' opens on piano rather than guitar; 'Girlfriend in a Coma' is a jaunty two-minute pop song with a morbid subject; 'Stop Me If You Think You've Heard This One Before' rings with Marr's guitars; and 'Last Night I Dreamt That Somebody Loved Me' begins with a long, dark instrumental overture before Morrissey enters.","Recorded in spring 1987 at a studio near Bath with Stephen Street, the album was finished just before the band collapsed: Johnny Marr left that summer, exhausted by touring and at odds with Morrissey, and the record was released in September to a band that no longer existed. The title refers to Strangeways, Manchester's Victorian prison, a characteristically dark Mancunian joke.","Morrissey plays piano on 'Death of a Disco Dancer', his only instrumental contribution to a Smiths record, a halting, clattering part. And the long opening of 'Last Night I Dreamt' carries a murmur of crowd noise under the piano and strings, the Smiths experimenting with textures their earlier records never used.","Released after the split, it was reviewed as an epitaph and sold well. It was long ranked below The Queen Is Dead, but both Morrissey and Marr have named it their favourite Smiths album, and critics have increasingly agreed that its wider palette pointed to where the band might have gone.","Start with 'Last Night I Dreamt That Somebody Loved Me' and be patient through the instrumental opening; the arrival of the voice is the point. Then hear 'Stop Me If You Think You've Heard This One Before' for the classic guitar sound. Compare The Queen Is Dead to hear how the band changed in a year."],"radiohead-creep":["A slow, simple song over four repeating chords, played quietly on arpeggiated guitar while Thom Yorke sings of adoring someone he feels he can never deserve, cataloguing his own inadequacy. Then, just before each chorus, Jonny Greenwood's guitar lets off two huge, ugly crunching blasts, and the chorus explodes into distortion. The quiet-loud swing and Yorke's falsetto climb in the bridge are what everyone remembers.","Radiohead's debut single, released in 1992 and included on their first album, Pablo Honey, in 1993. Recorded in Oxford with the American producers Sean Slade and Paul Kolderie, it first flopped in Britain, then became a hit on radio in Israel and on American college and alternative stations in the wake of Nirvana, before a reissue made it a British hit too.","Greenwood has said his crunching guitar blasts began as an attempt to spoil a song he found too tame; they became its hook. The chord sequence and melody are so close to the Hollies' 'The Air That I Breathe' that its writers, Albert Hammond and Mike Hazlewood, were later given a songwriting credit.","It made Radiohead famous and nearly trapped them: for years they were seen as a one-hit alternative band, and they grew weary of playing it, dropping it from their shows for long stretches. Critics now treat it as the starting point of an extraordinary arc, and fans argue whether its huge popularity makes it a great song or a millstone.","Wait for the two guitar crunches before the first chorus and notice how they reset your attention. Then listen to Yorke's voice in the bridge. Play it next to The Bends and then Kid A to hear how far the band travelled from this one song in seven years."],"shirley-collins-no-roses":["Old English songs sung in Shirley Collins's plain, unhurried voice, over an electric band that changes on nearly every track. Some songs are carried by a rock rhythm section and electric guitar; others by fiddles, concertina, reed drones or ancient woodwind. The ballads are long and grim, murder, transportation, betrayal, and the singing never dramatises them; the band supplies the colour while the voice tells the story straight.","Recorded in 1971 with the Albion Country Band, assembled by her then husband Ashley Hutchings, the bassist who had led Fairport Convention into English traditional music on Liege & Lief and then co-founded Steeleye Span. Some two dozen musicians from the London folk and folk-rock scene took part, among them Richard Thompson and Dave Mattacks, at the peak of the English folk-rock movement.","Listen to how the arrangements respect the unaccompanied tradition: Collins keeps the free, irregular phrasing of a traditional singer, and the band often hangs back or plays drones rather than forcing her into a strict beat. On the murder ballads, the early instruments, reedy and nasal, set the songs in a far older world than the electric guitars do.","It sold modestly but was admired in folk circles, and it has since become one of the most prized records of English folk-rock, especially after her rediscovery in the 2010s. Critics have argued whether the electric band enriches or distracts from songs that Collins could carry alone; most now hear the balance as unusually tactful.","Pick one long ballad and follow the story first, the band second. Then listen again for the way the instruments shift colour between verses. Compare Anthems in Eden for Collins with early instruments alone, and Liege & Lief for Fairport's more rock-driven approach to the same repertoire."],"delia-derbyshire-blue-veils-and-golden-sands":["A short, slow-moving piece of tape music evoking a vast desert. Soft, bell-like tones, transposed and stretched until they shimmer like heat haze, float over a low rumbling bed; gusts of filtered noise suggest wind across sand. There is no melody in the usual sense and no beat, only layers of sustained, slightly wavering sound that rise and fade, as if seen from far away.","Derbyshire made it at the BBC Radiophonic Workshop in London for a television documentary about the Tuareg, the nomadic people of the Sahara. The Workshop, founded in 1958, produced sound for radio and television on tiny budgets, with tape recorders, oscillators and scissors, years before synthesisers were available; Derbyshire, a mathematics and music graduate, was its most imaginative composer.","Much of the sound is said to come from a single everyday object: a metal lampshade Derbyshire struck and recorded, then slowed, filtered and layered on tape until the clang became an aura. Listen for the faint metallic ring at the attack of some tones, the only trace of the object left.","Like most of her work it was heard by television viewers who never knew her name, and it was credited, if at all, to the Workshop. After her death in 2001 and the rediscovery of her archive, pieces like this were championed as early British ambient music, and electronic musicians of later generations have cited her as a forerunner.","Listen on headphones, eyes closed, and let the piece hang rather than waiting for something to happen. Then try to hear the lampshade inside the shimmer. Compare Tinariwen's real Tuareg music to hear how far Derbyshire's desert is an imagined one, and Boards of Canada for her afterlife."],"umm-kulthum-alf-leila-wa-leila":["Alf Leila wa Leila (A Thousand and One Nights) is a long love song in the late style Umm Kulthum made her own: an instrumental overture that sets a dancing, almost cinematic groove, then verse after verse in which the singer lingers over a handful of words, repeats them, bends them, and hands the phrase back to the orchestra. In concert it could stretch well past an hour. The recognisable gesture is the bright, swinging introduction, which Arab listeners know within two bars.","The music is by Baligh Hamdi, the youngest of the composers Umm Kulthum trusted in her last decade, and the words are by Morsi Gamil Aziz. By 1969 Egypt was reeling from the defeat of 1967, and Umm Kulthum was touring the Arab world and Europe to raise money for the state. Her monthly Thursday concerts on Cairo radio still emptied the streets. Hamdi brought a lighter, more rhythmic, more popular touch to a singer who had been the voice of classical tarab for forty years.","Listen to how little text there is. The whole performance rests on a short poem, and the length comes from the singer's improvised repetitions, each one changing the ornament, the emphasis or the dynamic in answer to shouts from the hall. Every live recording differs. The orchestra also plays a larger role than in her older songs: its interludes are long, catchy and meant to be danced to, the fingerprint of Hamdi's generation.","It was an immediate popular success and remains one of her most played songs, especially at weddings, where the overture is a staple of belly-dance repertoire. Purists who loved the austere classicism of her earlier composers heard Hamdi's style as a concession to pop. Today it is usually heard as the last great flowering of her collaboration with younger writers, and the introduction has been covered and sampled across the Arab world and beyond.","Start with the overture alone; it works as a piece of instrumental music in its own right. Then follow one repeated line through its variations and notice how the audience responds to each new turn. A full concert recording is better than a radio edit. Afterwards compare Enta Omri, where the modernising hand is Mohammed Abdel Wahab's rather than Hamdi's."],"umm-kulthum-ruba-iyyat-al-khayyam":["A setting of quatrains attributed to the Persian poet Omar Khayyam, in Arabic translation, sung by Umm Kulthum to music by Riad al-Sunbati. The song moves through a chain of short verses, each a small meditation, with al-Sunbati shifting mode as the mood turns from regret to wonder to a plea for forgiveness. The texture is classical tarab: a modest ensemble of oud, qanun, violins and ney shadowing the voice, and a voice that dwells on single syllables until they glow.","The translation is by Ahmad Rami, the poet who wrote more lyrics for Umm Kulthum than anyone else and who had studied Persian in order to render Khayyam directly. Al-Sunbati was her most classical composer, the man behind her most serious songs of the 1940s and 1950s. In 1950 Egypt was still a monarchy, and Umm Kulthum was already the most celebrated singer in the Arab world, singing monthly to a radio audience across the region.","Khayyam's quatrains are famous in the West through FitzGerald's English versions as hymns to wine and the passing moment. Rami's selection, and al-Sunbati's setting, bend them toward repentance: the song closes on a prayer to God for mercy, so a sceptic's poems end as something close to devotion. Listen for how the music slows and darkens as the text turns from pleasure to death, and brightens only at the final appeal.","The song became one of the foundations of her reputation for high seriousness, and it is regularly named among al-Sunbati's finest settings. Critics praise the fit between mode and meaning, verse by verse. Some later commentators note that the selection domesticates Khayyam's scepticism; others see that very turn as the point. It remains a touchstone for Arab singers who want to prove classical credentials.","Treat it as a song cycle: each quatrain is a scene, and the fun is hearing al-Sunbati change the colour between them. Listen for the ney, the reed flute, which often answers the voice. Afterwards compare Al-Atlal, al-Sunbati's later and grander setting, to hear how the same partnership widened its scale."],"umm-kulthum-amal-hayati":["Amal Hayati (Hope of My Life) is a love song built on a long, lilting instrumental introduction and a series of sung sections in which Umm Kulthum returns again and again to the title phrase. The music is by Mohammed Abdel Wahab, and it has his signature sweetness: a lyrical, strolling melody, Western-tinged orchestration and a steady rhythmic lift. In live performance the singer stretches the refrain into long improvised elaborations, turning a simple declaration of devotion into something close to ritual.","Abdel Wahab and Umm Kulthum were the two giants of Egyptian music and for decades did not work together. President Nasser is often credited with pushing them into partnership, and their first collaboration, Enta Omri in 1964, was called the meeting of the summits. Amal Hayati followed a year later, with words again by Ahmed Shafik Kamel. Egypt was at the height of Nasser's pan-Arab project, and her concerts were broadcast as national events.","Abdel Wahab was a trained singer himself, and he wrote Umm Kulthum lines that sit higher and move more lightly than those of her older composers, closer to the crooning style he had pioneered. Listen for the orchestral colours he imported, including accordion and a large violin section playing in unison, which give the song a faintly cinematic sheen. The refrain is short enough to sing along, which was part of its plan.","It was an enormous hit and is still one of the most loved of their ten or so collaborations. Critics who preferred the gravity of al-Sunbati sometimes found Abdel Wahab's songs sentimental; admirers answer that he gave the old tarab singer a new, younger audience. The pairing is now seen as a decisive moment in the modernisation of Arab song.","Let the introduction play fully before the voice arrives; Abdel Wahab meant it as a small overture. Then notice how each return of the title phrase is slightly different, pushed by the audience's cries. Hear it back to back with Enta Omri, its elder sibling, and then with Alf Leila wa Leila to hear where the style went next."],"subbulakshmi-meera":["Meera is the Tamil devotional film in which M. S. Subbulakshmi played the sixteenth-century saint-poet Mirabai, the Rajput princess who left palace life for devotion to Krishna. The film is carried by its songs: bhajans and Carnatic-inflected melodies sung by Subbulakshmi herself, with music directed by S. V. Venkatraman. The best known, Kaatrinile Varum Geetham, is a gentle, floating song about a melody carried on the breeze, sung with the pure, unforced tone that made her voice instantly recognisable.","Directed by Ellis R. Dungan and produced by Subbulakshmi's husband T. Sadasivam, the Tamil film appeared in 1945, as India moved toward independence; a Hindi version followed in 1947 and carried her voice to the whole country. She was already a leading Carnatic concert singer, but the film made her a national figure, admired by Gandhi's circle and the new political class, and fixed an image of her as a singer of devotion rather than display.","Subbulakshmi acted in only a handful of films and then left cinema for good, choosing the concert platform and the temple. Meera is therefore the rare place to hear her classical training bent to film song: listen for how the gamakas, the oscillating ornaments of Carnatic singing, are kept light so that the songs stay singable by ordinary listeners. The Hindi version reset several songs to Mirabai's own verses.","The film was a major success in both languages and has never quite left Indian memory; its songs are still sung in homes and temples. Film historians note it as an early South Indian film to win a pan-Indian audience. Some Carnatic purists felt the film fame overshadowed her classical art, but most now see Meera as the moment that made her a national voice.","Begin with Kaatrinile Varum Geetham on its own, and listen for the steadiness of the long notes. Then try a bhajan from the Hindi version to hear how the style carries across languages. For contrast, hear her Concert at the United Nations, two decades later, where the same voice works at full classical length."],"subbulakshmi-vishnu-sahasranamam":["The Vishnu Sahasranamam, the thousand names of Vishnu, is a Sanskrit hymn from the Mahabharata, and Subbulakshmi's recording turned it into the best-known devotional sound in South India. It is chanted rather than sung in full raga style: verse after verse delivered in a steady, lightly melodic recitation over a drone, the names tumbling out in patient sequence. The effect is closer to a river than a song, even in pace, luminous in tone, and deliberately without drama.","Subbulakshmi recorded the hymn for HMV, probably around 1970, singing with her daughter Radha Viswanathan, who often accompanied her. By then she was India's most revered singer, having performed at the United Nations in 1966. The recording was made for daily use, not for the concert hall, and it found its place in the morning routine of households and temples, played on radios, record players and later cassettes across Tamil Nadu and the diaspora.","Listen to the Sanskrit. Subbulakshmi took coaching in pronunciation, and her clarity is part of why the recording became a model for people learning the hymn: many listeners learnt the text from her rather than from a book. Notice too how the second voice doubles and supports rather than harmonises; the unison is the point, a shared act of recitation rather than a performance.","It became one of the best-selling records in Indian history and is still played daily in countless homes. Classical critics rarely discuss it, because it is not a concert work, yet it is probably the reason most South Indians know her voice. Some scholars of Carnatic music regret that her devotional recordings eclipsed her classical art; others see them as the fullest expression of the ideal she chose.","Play it in the morning, as it was meant to be heard, without trying to follow the words. Let the steady rhythm of the recitation set the pace. Then compare her Venkatesa Suprabhatam, a dawn hymn in a similar spirit, and a Tyagaraja kriti to hear the difference between chant and full Carnatic song."],"subbulakshmi-kurai-onrum-illai":["Kurai Onrum Illai (I Have No Complaint) is a Tamil devotional song written by C. Rajagopalachari and made famous by M. S. Subbulakshmi. It is a ragamalika, a garland of ragas, moving from one mode to the next as the verses progress, each change bringing a new colour to a single idea: contentment in the presence of Krishna. The tune is simple and hummable, the pace unhurried, and the voice floats over violin and mridangam with the ease of someone singing to herself.","Rajagopalachari, known as Rajaji, was a freedom fighter, the first Indian Governor-General and a close friend of Subbulakshmi and her husband. He wrote the words late in life, and the song entered her repertoire in the 1960s, when she was at the peak of her fame. It belongs to a tradition of light devotional pieces sung at the end of a Carnatic concert, after the heavy classical items, when the audience wants something direct and warm.","The song's title is a statement of complete acceptance, and the text keeps finding new ways to say that the singer lacks nothing even though her god remains unseen. The ragamalika structure quietly dramatises this: each change of raga suggests a new circumstance, yet the answer never changes. Listen for the shift into the last raga, where many singers slow down and the feeling deepens.","It became one of the most loved songs in Tamil devotional music and is now sung by almost every Carnatic vocalist, often as a closing piece. Subbulakshmi's version remains the reference. Critics sometimes count it as light music unworthy of close analysis, but its hold on listeners is undeniable, and it is a common first door into Carnatic music for newcomers.","Listen once without translation and simply notice when the raga changes; the mood shifts like light through a window. Then read a summary of the words and listen again. Compare with Kishori Amonkar's Sahela Re, a Hindustani song of similar intimacy, to hear how the two classical traditions handle devotion differently."],"ali-akbar-khan-in-concert-1972":["A double album of a jugalbandi, a duet concert, between Ali Akbar Khan on sarod and Ravi Shankar on sitar, with Alla Rakha on tabla, recorded at Philharmonic Hall in New York in 1972. The main performances are long ragas, Hem Bihag, Manj Khamaj and Sindhi Bhairavi, each opening with slow, unaccompanied alap and building to fast, drummed exchanges. The sound to recognise is the conversation: the darker, sliding sarod and the brighter, ringing sitar trading phrases and finally racing together.","Ali Akbar Khan and Ravi Shankar were brothers-in-law and fellow disciples of Ali Akbar's father, Allauddin Khan, the great teacher of the Maihar gharana. Allauddin Khan died in September 1972, weeks before this concert, and the performance was understood as a tribute to him. The album was issued by the Beatles' Apple label in 1973, a sign of how far Indian classical music had reached into Western popular culture since the 1960s.","The two men had played duets since the 1940s, but they were very different musicians: Shankar the showman, Khan the more inward, weighty improviser. Listen for the moments where one steps back and plainly lets the other lead, and for passages in the alap where Khan deepens a phrase Shankar has just offered. The sense of mourning is strongest in the Sindhi Bhairavi, the traditional closing raga.","It is widely regarded as one of the finest recorded jugalbandis, prized because the two rarely recorded together at such length. Some listeners find the duet form diffuse compared with a solo recital, where one mind shapes the whole raga. Its reissue on CD kept it in print, and it remains a common gateway to both players.","Start with Manj Khamaj, which is lighter and more songful, before tackling the long Hem Bihag. In the alap, try to tell the instruments apart by their attack: the sarod's notes slide from a metal fingerboard, the sitar's are plucked against frets. Afterwards hear Nikhil Banerjee's Manj Khamaj, a solo version by another Maihar disciple."],"ali-akbar-khan-legacy":["Ali Akbar Khan's collaboration with the singer Asha Bhosle, best known as a Bollywood playback star, performing classical compositions handed down in his family's lineage. Each track is a compact song form, a bandish, in a particular raga, with Bhosle singing the composed lines and the sarod answering, weaving around and sometimes leading the voice. It sounds like a chamber recital: close, unhurried, with the sarod's dark, bending tone and the singer's agile, bright voice in constant dialogue.","Released in 1996 on Ali Akbar Khan's own label, the album was part of his long effort, from his college in California, to preserve the repertoire he had learnt from his father Allauddin Khan, whose lineage traced its compositions back to Tansen and the court of the Mughal emperor Akbar. Asha Bhosle had trained classically before her film career and welcomed the chance to sing this repertoire. The album received a Grammy nomination.","The album's claim is archival as much as artistic: these bandishes are presented as old compositions carried by oral tradition, the kind of material usually taught privately rather than recorded. Listen for how Khan's sarod doubles the vocal line one moment and departs from it the next, a practice from the days when instrumentalists learnt by imitating singers. Bhosle's film experience shows in her precise, economical phrasing.","Critics welcomed it as a surprising and moving partnership, and it drew listeners who knew Bhosle only from film songs. Some classical purists questioned whether a playback singer could carry the style; most reviewers felt she did so with discipline and warmth. The historical attributions of individual songs are hard to verify, as with most orally transmitted repertoire.","Take it a track at a time, like a book of short poems. Follow the sarod rather than the voice on a second listen and hear how much of the music it carries. Then compare Kishori Amonkar's Sahela Re or Kesarbai Kerkar's Jaat Kahan Ho for voices raised entirely inside the classical world."],"ali-akbar-khan-devi":["Ali Akbar Khan's score for Satyajit Ray's film Devi (The Goddess). The music is sparse and mostly built from the sarod: brief alap-like phrases, drones and slow melodic fragments that come and go around the images rather than accompanying them continuously. There are also passages drawn from devotional songs to the goddess Kali, heard as part of the film's world. The recognisable sound is the lone sarod, low and grave, hovering over silence as a young woman is taken for a living goddess.","Ray used a different master of Hindustani music for each of his early films: Ravi Shankar for the Apu films, Vilayat Khan for Jalsaghar, and Ali Akbar Khan for Devi, released in 1960. The film, set in nineteenth-century Bengal, tells of a wealthy man who dreams that his daughter-in-law is an incarnation of Kali, with tragic results. It drew criticism in India for its portrait of religious fanaticism.","Listen for how the music withholds. Ray and Khan left long stretches without score, so that when the sarod enters it feels like a thought surfacing. The raga fragments tend to hang unresolved, mirroring the heroine's trapped state. Soon after, Ray began composing his own scores, partly because improvising masters were hard to fit to exact timings; Devi is one of the last of his films scored this way.","Devi is now regarded as one of Ray's finest films, and the score is praised for its restraint. It is less often discussed than Shankar's Pather Panchali music, partly because it is sparer and less melodic. Film scholars cite it as a model of how Indian classical improvisation can serve drama without decorating it.","Watch the film rather than hunting for a soundtrack album; the music only makes sense against the images. Notice the first entrance of the sarod and how long the silence before it lasts. Then compare Shankar's Pather Panchali and Vilayat Khan's Jalsaghar to hear three Maihar- and Etawah-trained masters answer the same director."],"vilayat-khan-raga-yaman":["Yaman is the great evening raga of Hindustani music, bright and serene, and Vilayat Khan returned to it throughout his career. His performances follow the classic sitar form: a slow alap exploring the raga's notes one by one, then a jor with a steady pulse, and finally gats, composed themes, with tabla, where he elaborates in long singing phrases and dazzling fast runs. What marks his Yaman is the voice-like line, notes pulled across the fret so they bend and glide like a singer's.","Vilayat Khan came from the Etawah or Imdadkhani gharana; his father Inayat Khan and grandfather Imdad Khan were leading sitar and surbahar players. Orphaned young, he learnt partly from singers in his family, which shaped his lifelong ideal of gayaki ang, the singing style on the sitar. By the 1960s he was, with Ravi Shankar, one of the two dominant sitarists of India, recording for HMV and performing across the country.","Listen for the meend, the long pulls of the string. Vilayat Khan redesigned his sitar, altering its strings and removing the lowest drone strings, to make the instrument sing sustained, sliding phrases rather than ring with resonance. In Yaman you can hear him imitate the vocal khayal, sometimes almost articulating a sung line. Also notice how he treats the raga's sharp fourth, the note that gives Yaman its shine.","Connoisseurs often rank him as the most lyrical sitarist of the century, and his Yaman is held up as an example. Where Ravi Shankar's style won the West, Vilayat Khan's remained more prized within India. He famously refused several state honours, saying they came too late or were unfairly given, and his stature among musicians only grew.","Choose a long performance, not a short excerpt, and listen to the alap in a quiet room. Hum a note he bends and hear how far the pitch travels. Then compare the catalogue's traditional Raga Yaman entry and Ravi Shankar's approach to evening ragas to hear two schools of sitar side by side."],"vilayat-khan-raga-bhairavi":["Bhairavi is the raga with which Hindustani concerts traditionally end, a tender, melancholy mode that also carries a sense of dawn. Vilayat Khan's Bhairavi performances are more songful than architectural: after a short introduction he plays a light composition and then roams freely, borrowing turns from thumri, the romantic semi-classical song, touching notes outside the strict scale for colour, and letting the sitar sing in long, sighing phrases above the tabla.","Closing a concert with Bhairavi is one of the oldest habits of North Indian music, and every great player is judged partly by it. For Vilayat Khan, who modelled his playing on vocal music, Bhairavi was a natural home, since it is the raga of countless thumris and bhajans. By the 1970s he was touring widely in India and abroad, and his closing Bhairavis were among the most anticipated moments of his recitals.","Bhairavi is unusual in allowing all twelve notes as colour, and Vilayat Khan exploits this: listen for brief, glancing notes that do not belong to the raga's basic scale, used like a singer's sob or catch in the voice. His alternation between sitar phrases and sung-sounding lines is at its clearest here. He sometimes sang a line himself in concert before playing it, which reveals the whole method.","His Bhairavi is widely admired as among the most expressive on record, and it confirmed his reputation for emotional depth rather than show. Some classical traditionalists feel such free, thumri-flavoured treatment loosens the raga's discipline; his admirers answer that this looseness is Bhairavi's very nature.","Listen at the end of a day, not the beginning of one. Notice how often the phrases end with a slow downward pull, like a sigh. Then compare the traditional Raga Bhairavi entry and the Sindhi Bhairavi that closes Ali Akbar Khan and Ravi Shankar's 1972 concert."],"vilayat-khan-bismillah-khan-jugalbandi":["Duet recordings pairing Vilayat Khan's sitar with the shehnai, the North Indian oboe, of Bismillah Khan. The two instruments could hardly be more different: the plucked, bending sitar and the piercing, continuous reed. Yet both players shaped their lines on the human voice, and the music becomes a conversation between two singing styles, each taking a phrase and handing it back transformed, with tabla gathering them together in the faster sections.","Jugalbandi, the duet format, became popular in the mid-twentieth century as concert culture and the record industry sought new pairings. Bismillah Khan, from Benares, had carried the shehnai from wedding bands and temple gates into the classical concert hall and played at the Red Fort on India's first Independence Day. His recordings with Vilayat Khan for HMV, made in the 1960s, brought together two of the most revered instrumentalists in the country.","Listen for how the sitar has to work to match the shehnai's sustain: Vilayat Khan's long pulls on the string, his hallmark, suddenly make sense as a way of competing with a wind instrument that never needs to stop. In return the shehnai imitates the sitar's quick decorations. The two men are said to have shared a deep friendship, and there is little of the competitive edge found in many duets.","These recordings are treasured by admirers of both players and are often cited as the most natural sitar-shehnai pairing on record. Critics of jugalbandi in general argue that duets dilute a raga's development; these performances are usually exempted because both players think in vocal terms. Reissues have been irregular, and it can be hard to find complete versions.","First listen only for the difference in tone, the reed against the string. Then notice when the two lines briefly play together rather than alternating; these moments are rare and carefully placed. Follow with Bismillah Khan's Red Fort Recital to hear the shehnai alone."],"vilayat-khan-the-guru":["Vilayat Khan's score for The Guru, a Merchant Ivory film about an English pop star who travels to India to learn the sitar from a master. The music is mostly raga-based: sitar solos and short improvisations that stand for the discipline the visitor struggles to grasp, heard against the noise of his celebrity. The sound that stays with you is the sitar in its classical role, patient and serious, set against the film's satire of Western enthusiasm.","Made in 1969 by producer Ismail Merchant and director James Ivory, the film responded directly to the craze started when George Harrison studied with Ravi Shankar. Casting Vilayat Khan, who was often seen as the more traditional of the two great sitarists, gave the film its musical authority. He also appears on screen, and the score shows him presenting his music to a Western audience on his own terms.","The film's situation mirrors Ravi Shankar's experience with the Beatles so closely that it reads as commentary, and the choice of Shankar's great rival to score it gives the joke an edge. Listen for how the music never bends toward pop: where the plot calls for fusion, the soundtrack refuses it, and the gap between the film's rock star and the real music becomes the point.","The film received mixed reviews and is a minor entry in the Merchant Ivory catalogue, but the score is often cited as the most substantial film work by Vilayat Khan after Jalsaghar. Writers on the 1960s encounter between India and Western pop treat it as a sharp period document. It is not easy to find, and the score has had little separate release.","If you can see the film, notice the scenes where the sitar plays uninterrupted and the camera simply watches. Then listen to Jalsaghar for Vilayat Khan's music in a fully Indian film, and The Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows to hear the pop side of the same encounter."],"miriam-makeba-miriam-makeba":["Miriam Makeba's first American album, a collection of South African songs in Xhosa, Zulu and other languages, alongside a few songs from elsewhere. The arrangements are spare, guitar, bass and light percussion, leaving room for her voice: clear, flexible and low, able to shift from a lullaby's hush to a bright, rhythmic call. For many American listeners it was the first time they had heard the click consonants of Xhosa sung, delivered with warmth and humour rather than exoticism.","Makeba had become a star in South Africa with the Manhattan Brothers and the Skylarks, then appeared in the anti-apartheid documentary Come Back, Africa, which brought her to Venice and then to the United States in 1959. Harry Belafonte took her under his wing and helped arrange her RCA contract. In 1960, when she tried to return home for her mother's funeral, the South African government revoked her passport, beginning three decades of exile.","The album's apparent simplicity hides an argument: these were not tourist songs but everyday music from townships and villages, sung by a woman whose own government was about to exile her. Listen for how she tailors each song to its language, changing the placement of her voice and her rhythmic attack, so that the record is also a map of South Africa's many peoples.","It was well received and launched her American career; she soon appeared on television and at major venues, and within a few years won a Grammy with Belafonte. Some later critics found the early arrangements tame compared with her South African recordings; others value them as the moment African popular song first reached a mass American audience on its own terms.","Listen for her voice first, especially the low register, which is fuller than most listeners expect. Then pick a song in Xhosa and follow the clicks as rhythm. Afterwards hear An Evening with Belafonte/Makeba and Pata Pata to follow how her sound grew."],"miriam-makeba-soweto-blues":["A song about the Soweto uprising of June 1976, written by Hugh Masekela and sung by Miriam Makeba. It moves at a grieving, mid-tempo pace, with a loping South African groove, horns that answer the voice, and a melody that sits somewhere between a lament and a township jive. The words describe, in plain images, children shot in the streets and parents left to mourn, and Makeba sings them with restraint rather than rhetoric, which makes the song more devastating.","On 16 June 1976, schoolchildren in Soweto marched against the imposition of Afrikaans as a language of instruction, and police opened fire; hundreds were killed in the months that followed. Makeba was in exile, living in Guinea, and Masekela, her former husband, was also abroad. The song became part of the exiled musicians' campaign to keep apartheid before the world's eyes, and was sung at concerts and rallies.","The title puts American blues and a South African township in one phrase, a deliberate bridge between Black struggles on two continents; Makeba and Masekela had both lived through the American civil rights era. Listen for how the groove refuses despair: the music keeps dancing while the text mourns, a very South African way of carrying grief. Later performances added new urgency as the struggle continued.","It became one of the anthems of the anti-apartheid movement. Its most famous performance came in 1987, when Makeba sang it with Paul Simon's Graceland tour in Zimbabwe, a concert that was itself politically contested because of the cultural boycott. Today it is remembered alongside the uprising in South African commemorations of 16 June.","Listen first for the horns, which act like a second, wordless voice of mourning. Then find the Graceland concert performance and compare the studio feel with the live one. Follow with Pata Pata to hear the joyful side of the same singer, and Bob Marley's Redemption Song for another exile's song of liberation."],"fairuz-bayya-al-khawatim":["Bayya' al-Khawatim (The Ring Seller) is a musical village fable by the Rahbani brothers, starring Fairuz, filmed in 1965. In a mountain village the mayor invents an imaginary villain to seem important, and then a stranger arrives who seems to be that villain; the ending restores harmony with gentle comedy and romance. The music is a chain of short songs, choruses and dabke dances, bright and melodic, with Fairuz's clear, unhurried voice at the centre.","The Rahbani brothers, Assi and Mansour, with Fairuz, Assi's wife, were building a Lebanese national musical theatre, drawing on village life, folk dance and Arabic poetry, first at the Baalbeck festival and then in Beirut. Bayya' al-Khawatim began as a stage work and was turned into a film by the Egyptian director Youssef Chahine in 1965, a meeting of the two great centres of Arab cinema and music, Cairo and Beirut.","Beneath the village comedy is a pointed satire about rulers who invent enemies to justify their power; the lesson was not lost on audiences in a region full of such regimes. Listen for how the Rahbanis borrow folk tunes and dabke rhythms, then smooth them with Western harmony and orchestration, creating the idealised Lebanon their work promoted.","The film was popular across the Arab world and is often considered the best of Fairuz's few films. Critics praise its lightness and its songs; later writers have noted how the Rahbani vision of an innocent mountain Lebanon ignored the country's divisions, which were to erupt in civil war a decade later. The songs are still sung at gatherings.","Watch the film for the dance sequences and the village choruses, then return to the songs alone. Listen for how Fairuz rarely raises her voice; her power lies in clarity. Compare Umm Kulthum's Alf Leila wa Leila to hear how differently Cairo and Beirut imagined Arab song in the 1960s."],"fairuz-wahdon":["Wahdon (They Alone) is the album on which Fairuz's son Ziad Rahbani first fully took charge of her music. The title song is slow, spare and grave, carried by piano and strings, with Fairuz singing in a quiet, almost conversational tone. Around it are songs in which Ziad's love of jazz harmony and Western orchestral colour meets Arabic melody. The overall sound is more intimate and melancholy than the bright, festive music of the Rahbani brothers' earlier work.","In 1979 Lebanon was four years into civil war, and the idealised mountain villages of the older Rahbani musicals now sounded like a lost world. Assi Rahbani had suffered a stroke in 1972, and his partnership with Fairuz was ending. Their son Ziad, a pianist and satirical playwright in his early twenties, stepped in and wrote for his mother in a new, modern idiom that matched the times.","The title song is often heard as an elegy for fighters who die unseen, and many listeners take it as a political statement; its words never spell out a side, and that ambiguity let listeners across Lebanon's divides hear their own dead in it. Listen for Ziad's harmonies, which borrow from jazz ballads without breaking the Arabic melodic line, a balance he would refine for decades.","The album marked a turning point in Fairuz's career and divided older listeners, who missed the Rahbani brothers' sound, from younger ones who embraced Ziad's modernity. It is now regarded as the start of a second great partnership in her life. The title song remains one of her most emotionally weighty recordings.","Start with the title song, late in the evening, and follow the piano as much as the voice; notice how little it hurries. Then hear Li Beirut, her wartime lament for the city, and finally Kifak Inta, where Ziad's jazz-inflected style becomes fully conversational."],"fairuz-kifak-inta":["Kifak Inta (How Are You?) is Fairuz's most jazz-flavoured album, written and arranged by her son Ziad Rahbani. The songs are in everyday Lebanese dialect, the arrangements lean on piano, bass, brushed drums and light strings, and the tone is conversational, wry and tender. The title song imagines a woman meeting a former lover after years apart, asking politely how he is while her feelings rise under the small talk. It is a record of understatement.","Released in 1991, as Lebanon's civil war was ending, the album came after years in which Fairuz had rarely recorded new material. Assi Rahbani had died in 1986, and Ziad was now her main collaborator. His style, shaped by jazz, Brazilian music and his satirical theatre, used the language of the street rather than the elevated poetry of the older Rahbani songs, and it scandalised some listeners.","Listen to how much is carried by the chords. Ziad sets a very simple, speech-like melody over harmonies from jazz ballads, so that the emotion lives in the accompaniment while Fairuz sings almost flatly, as if holding back. The colloquial words were themselves a statement: Fairuz, long the voice of formal Arabic poetry and patriotic song, speaking as an ordinary woman.","It was a huge success and is now one of her best-loved albums, though at first some admirers complained that Ziad had made her too casual, even too personal. The title song became an anthem for a generation that grew up during the war. Critics now tend to see the album as proof that her voice could live in an entirely new style.","Play the title song and pay attention to the bass and piano rather than the melody. Notice how Fairuz rarely raises her voice even when the feelings clearly do. Compare with Wahdon to hear where the collaboration began, and with a Bill Evans trio record to hear the jazz behind Ziad's chords."],"fela-kuti-shakara":["Two long tracks by Fela Kuti and Africa 70: Lady and Shakara. Each begins with an extended instrumental groove, interlocking guitars, a relentless drum pattern from Tony Allen, bass, and a horn section stating the theme, before Fela enters with his singing and the chorus answers him. The music builds by accumulation rather than change, patterns layered until the whole band locks into one shifting, hypnotic pulse. It is early Afrobeat at its most confident.","By 1972 Fela had returned from a stay in the United States, where he encountered Black Power politics and the funk of James Brown, and had renamed his band Africa 70. Based in Lagos at his club, the Afrika Shrine, he was turning from love songs to social commentary sung in Pidgin English, so that ordinary Nigerians across language groups could understand. Shakara was released by EMI Nigeria.","Lady is a satirical song about African women who have adopted Western manners and reject traditional roles, and Shakara mocks people who bluster and boast. Listen to the chorus: the female singers answer Fela's jibes, which gives the songs a call-and-response feel even when their message is one-sided. And follow Tony Allen's drums, which never repeat mechanically; tiny variations keep the groove alive for ten minutes and more.","The album was a major hit in West Africa and helped establish Afrobeat as a genre. Lady in particular has drawn lasting criticism for its view of women, and Fela's attitudes remain debated. Musically, the album is prized by collectors and producers, and its reissues introduced many listeners to the Africa 70 sound.","Put on Lady and wait: the first vocal arrives only after the band has built its groove, and the wait is part of the experience. Listen to one instrument at a time, first the drums, then the rhythm guitar, then the bass. Follow with James Brown's Cold Sweat to hear one of the sources, and Zombie to hear where Fela's politics went."],"ali-farka-toure-ali-farka-toure":["Ali Farka Touré's first internationally released album, often called the red album after its cover. The sound is spare and circular: an acoustic guitar playing looping, hypnotic figures, sometimes with calabash percussion and a second voice, and Ali's own voice, low and steady, singing in Songhai, Tamasheq, Fulani and Bambara. The pieces unfold over a fixed pattern that shifts only in small details, and the bluesy bent notes on the guitar made Western listeners think immediately of the Mississippi.","Ali Farka Touré came from Niafunké on the Niger river in northern Mali, and had recorded for Malian radio and on local releases for years. In 1988 the London label World Circuit, founded by Nick Gold and others, released this album, which introduced him to Europe and America. The late 1980s was the moment of the world music marketing category, and his record became one of its defining releases.","Western critics heard John Lee Hooker in him, and Ali did love Hooker's records, but he insisted that the music came the other way: that the blues had African roots and that what he played was the music of his own region. Listen to the guitar figures and hear how they echo the single-string njarka fiddle and ngoni lute, instruments he played before the guitar.","The album was a critical and commercial success and made him an international figure. The desert blues label, first applied to his music, has shaped how West African guitar music is sold ever since. Some writers argue that the blues framing obscured the specifically Malian sources he always stressed; his later albums and interviews did much to correct this.","Listen with good headphones and follow the guitar pattern through a whole track, noticing each tiny variation. Then play John Lee Hooker's Boogie Chillen' and ask what is really shared. Continue to Talking Timbuktu, his collaboration with Ry Cooder, and Niafunké, recorded back home."],"ali-farka-toure-savane":["Ali Farka Touré's last solo album, released after his death. The core is his guitar and voice, but the band is fuller than on his earlier records: the ngoni, the Malian lute, weaves around the guitar; percussion drives the faster songs; and backing singers answer his lines. The mood is grave and dignified, the grooves unhurried, the playing more supple than ever. It sounds like a musician gathering everything he knows into a final statement.","Recorded while Ali was ill with the cancer that killed him in March 2006, Savane appeared from World Circuit a few months later. In his later years he had been mayor of Niafunké and put much of his energy into farming and irrigation projects along the Niger. He had also just completed In the Heart of the Moon with the kora player Toumani Diabaté, which won a Grammy.","The songs are largely about his region, its people and the values he wanted to pass on, and they carry the sense of an elder speaking to the young. Listen for the dialogue between his guitar and the ngoni: the guitar often imitates the lute that inspired it, so the two instruments sound almost like relatives trading memories.","The album was widely acclaimed as one of his finest and a fitting farewell, and it appeared on many best-of-year lists. Critics noted its richer, more varied sound compared with his earlier records. It has since been seen as the closing chapter of a career that changed how West African music was heard abroad.","Play it from start to finish in one sitting, as a single meditation. Listen for the ngoni's bright, dry tone against the guitar. Then go back to the 1988 album to hear how far his sound travelled, and to Tinariwen's Aman Iman to hear the generation that followed."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-shahbaaz":["A traditional qawwali album by Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan and his party: lead and supporting singers, harmoniums, tabla and handclaps. Each piece starts slowly with a sung introduction, then the rhythm enters and the ensemble repeats key phrases, building in speed and intensity as Nusrat launches into improvised passages of sargam, the sung note names, and rapid melismas. The long crescendo, from calm to near-frenzy, is the shape of qawwali, and this album shows it without Western additions.","Qawwali is the devotional music of the Sufi shrines of Pakistan and North India, meant to bring listeners closer to God through ecstatic repetition. Nusrat, from a family of qawwals in Faisalabad, was already the leading performer in Pakistan when Peter Gabriel's Real World label began recording him in England in the late 1980s. Shahbaaz, released in 1991, belongs to his series of traditional Real World albums.","The title refers to Lal Shahbaz Qalandar, the Sufi saint of Sehwan in Sindh, whose shrine is famous for ecstatic devotional music and dance. Listen to how a single line is repeated many times by the chorus while Nusrat's improvisations grow more daring around it; this repetition is not padding but the engine that carries listeners toward spiritual intoxication.","Critics welcomed the Real World traditional albums as the best studio documents of Nusrat's art, giving him space for full-length performances. Some purists argued that recording qawwali away from the shrine and its listeners removed its purpose, but the recordings introduced millions to the form and are still recommended starting points.","Choose one track and give it all its time; the power of qawwali lies in the build. Notice when the tabla first enters and when the handclaps join. Afterwards compare Shahen-Shah, his first Real World album, and Mustt Mustt, where his voice met Western production."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-devotional-songs":["A collection of traditional qawwalis, including the celebrated Allah Hoo, Allah Hoo, a hymn built on the repetition of the name of God. The ensemble is the classic qawwali party of voices, harmoniums, tabla and handclaps, and the structure is the familiar crescendo: slow invocation, entry of the rhythm, and a long rise in intensity. Nusrat's voice moves from deep, sustained notes to startling runs and cascades of sung note names, the ensemble echoing and answering him.","Released in 1992 by Real World, the album appeared as Nusrat's international fame was growing through collaborations and festival appearances, including WOMAD. It was intended to present his traditional repertoire to new audiences, and it became one of the best-selling qawwali records in the West. Nusrat maintained that these devotional songs, sung at shrines for centuries, were the heart of his art.","Allah Hoo is built on a phrase so simple a child could sing it, and the performance turns that simplicity into a trance by varying everything around it: speed, dynamics, melodic ornament. Listen for the harmonium, which often doubles the lead voice a split second behind, a way of teaching the melody to the chorus in real time.","It is often named as the best introduction to Nusrat's traditional qawwali, and critics praise its choice of pieces. Some listeners from qawwali's home audience consider the Western-market versions less spontaneous than shrine performances, but the album remains a standard recommendation.","Start with Allah Hoo, Allah Hoo and let it run; notice how your attention shifts from the words to the sound itself. Then compare with his later collaborations to hear how the same voice could adapt. Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda makes a revealing companion, another devotional music in dialogue with India."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-night-song":["Night Song is the second album Nusrat made with the Canadian guitarist and producer Michael Brook. Nusrat's voice and members of his party sing over atmospheric arrangements of guitar, keyboards, bass and subtle drum programming, and Brook's sustained, shimmering guitar spreads a haze behind the voice. The songs are shorter than traditional qawwalis and more like modern pop tracks in shape, but the voice remains unmistakable, moving from quiet, intimate phrases to soaring, rapid ornament.","Released in 1996 by Real World, the album followed Mustt Mustt, whose Massive Attack remix had made Nusrat's voice familiar to Western club and film audiences. He had also sung on the Dead Man Walking soundtrack and on film scores. Nusrat saw these collaborations as a way of spreading the Sufi message, while continuing to perform traditional qawwali.","Brook's guitar is not a conventional instrument here: he used a device giving infinite sustain, which lets notes hang like a drone, echoing the harmonium and tanpura of traditional music. Listen for the moments when Nusrat drops out and the guitar holds the space, and for the call-and-response between his voice and the chorus, a trace of qawwali within a modern frame.","The album was well received and received a Grammy nomination. Critics praised its atmosphere, though some found it too smooth compared with Mustt Mustt or with his traditional recordings. It has often been used in film and television. Opinion remains divided on whether his fusion albums enriched or diluted his art, but most admit their reach.","Listen at night, as the title suggests, and focus on how the guitar and voice share space. Then hear a traditional qawwali from Devotional Songs to feel the difference in scale and purpose. Mustt Mustt is the natural companion, and Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air shows another way of building music on drones."],"franco-tpok-jazz-azda":["Azda is a long, rolling rumba built in two halves, the way OK Jazz built nearly everything. First comes the sung section, unhurried, with a small choir of voices answering the lead in close parallel harmony over a loping bass. Then the band opens into the sebene, the fast instrumental second half, where Franco's own guitar takes over with short, chiming, endlessly repeated figures while the horns punch in above them and the dancers are meant to take the floor.","The song began life as an advertisement. Azda was the Volkswagen dealership in Kinshasa, and Franco was paid to celebrate the company and its cars at a moment when Mobutu's Zaire was flush with copper money and a new car was the loudest possible sign of success. Rather than a jingle, he delivered a full-length OK Jazz dance record, and the city took it up as a hit in its own right, far beyond anyone who could afford a Beetle.","Listen for the names. Congolese rumba has a custom, called mabanga, of calling out patrons, friends and businesses in the middle of a song, usually in return for money. Azda simply takes that habit to its logical end, so the sponsor is the subject rather than a passing shout. Notice too how the guitar parts interlock: rhythm and lead lines are pitched to fit together like gears rather than to solo over one another.","Some listeners at the time grumbled that the great chronicler of Kinshasa life had sold a song to a car dealer, but the objection faded because the record was simply too good to dismiss. Writers on African popular music now treat Azda as a case study in how Franco turned patronage into art, and as one of the clearest demonstrations of the OK Jazz sebene at its early-1970s peak.","Do not wait for the words to make sense; wait for the switch. Somewhere past the halfway mark the song changes gear into the sebene, and from then on follow one guitar line at a time. Then play it beside Mario from twelve years later to hear how the band's sound thickened with more horns and more voices."],"franco-tpok-jazz-luvumbu-ndoki":["On its surface Luvumbu Ndoki is a folk tale set to rumba. Franco sings, in Kikongo, the story of Luvumbu, a sorcerer who devours the members of his own family, and the chorus answers him in the call-and-response style of village storytelling. The groove is lighter and leaner than the later OK Jazz sound, with a single saxophone, clean interlocking guitars and a gentle, swaying rhythm that makes the grim subject all the more unsettling.","The song appeared soon after June 1966, when Mobutu, only months into his rule, had four former ministers publicly hanged in Kinshasa before a huge crowd, accused of plotting a coup. Nobody could say openly that the new president had killed his own people. Franco did not say it either; he told an old story about a man who eats his kin, and every listener in the city understood exactly whom he meant.","The disguise is the point. By singing in Kikongo rather than the Lingala of the capital's radio, and by framing everything as proverb, Franco left himself a door marked 'it is only a folk tale.' Listen to how little the music dramatises the story: the band keeps smiling while the words accuse, a trick of indirection that Congolese singers used again and again under Mobutu.","The authorities were not fooled. By most accounts the record was withdrawn and Franco spent a short spell in detention, the first of several clashes with the regime he would later, uneasily, serve. Historians of Congolese music regard it as the song that proved rumba could carry political weight, and it is now cited more often than it is heard, since clean copies are scarce.","Listen once just for the voices trading lines, as if a story were being told around a fire. Then listen again knowing the date and the hangings behind it; the same mild tune becomes almost unbearable. Compare it with Fela Kuti's Zombie, which mocks a military regime openly, to hear two opposite strategies for singing truth to power."],"franco-tpok-jazz-omona-wapi":["This is the record on which the two giants of Congolese rumba finally sang together. Franco's dark, conversational baritone and Tabu Ley Rochereau's high, smooth, honeyed tenor trade verses and then blend, over the combined weight of their musicians. Each side is built from a few long songs that move from stately sung sections into driving sebene passages, with the guitars glittering high above a rolling bass and a full horn section answering the singers.","For two decades Kinshasa had split into two camps: Franco's OK Jazz, earthy and satirical, and Rochereau's African Fiesta and then Afrisa, polished and cosmopolitan. The rivalry sold records and filled the gossip columns. By the early 1980s both men were established stars touring Europe, and a joint album was partly a reconciliation and partly a shrewd piece of business, recorded when Zairean music ruled dance floors from Nairobi to Paris.","Listen for the moments when the two voices sit a third apart, the classic Congolese harmony, and notice which singer takes the upper line: usually Rochereau, whose range floats, while Franco anchors from below. The guitarists of both bands are audibly adjusting to one another, so the lead lines are more restrained and more intricate than on either band's own records of the same years.","Fans greeted the album as a summit meeting, and it sold widely across Central and East Africa. Critics have since debated whether the summit produced the best music of either man; many prefer each band at full strength on its own. But the record is widely valued as a document of the rivalry resolved, and as one of the last great moments before soukous sped up and the old rumba generation passed.","Start with the title song and simply follow the handover from one voice to the other; it tells you everything about the two men. Then find a solo Rochereau record from the early 1970s and one of Franco's own from the same years, and hear how far apart the two schools stood before this album brought them together."],"youssou-ndour-the-lion":["The Lion is the record on which mbalax, the dance music of Dakar, was first packaged for the world market. You hear Youssou N'Dour's soaring, keening tenor, which can leap into a near-falsetto cry, over the Super Étoile de Dakar: crisp guitars, synthesiser pads, and above all the sabar drums and the squeezed, pitch-bending talking drum called the tama, which chatters in rhythms that sound almost like speech.","By the late 1980s N'Dour was already the biggest star in Senegal and had toured stadiums with Peter Gabriel, who became his champion in the West. Virgin signed him, and The Lion was made with an eye on European and American listeners, with cleaner production and English as well as Wolof lyrics. Gabriel sings with him on Shakin' the Tree, which the two wrote together as a song in support of women's freedom.","Listen underneath the glossy keyboards to the drums, which were not tamed for export. The sabar patterns come from Wolof dance and ceremony, and the tama's pitch bends imitate the tones of spoken phrases; in Dakar, dancers hear them as instructions. The title itself, Gaïndé, is a Wolof praise-word: the lion is a symbol of Senegal and of the proud, generous man.","The album earned respectful international reviews but was also the first sign of a long argument: had the polish for foreign ears softened the ferocity of the Dakar cassettes N'Dour released at home? Some Senegalese fans and Western critics alike preferred the raw local recordings. Others hear The Lion as a bold, successful translation, and Shakin' the Tree remains one of his most loved songs abroad.","Start with Shakin' the Tree for the voices, then go straight to the tracks where the tama is loudest and follow only the drum. Afterwards, compare with Immigrés, recorded five years earlier for a Senegalese audience, to hear what changed and what did not when mbalax went global."],"youssou-ndour-set":["Set is tighter, faster and more confident than The Lion. The songs are shorter, the grooves harder, and the Super Étoile play with a snap that sounds like a band that has spent a year on stage together. N'Dour's voice is at its brightest, shifting from soft conversational phrasing to long, bending cries, while the sabar and tama drive the tempo and the guitars pick out bright, fluttering patterns high above them.","The title is a Wolof word meaning clean, and it belongs to a moment. In 1990 young people across Dakar organised Set Setal, a movement to clean up their own neighbourhoods, painting murals, clearing rubbish and demanding a more honest public life after years of economic crisis. N'Dour, a hero to that generation, put the word on the cover and wrote songs aimed at them, on civic pride, responsibility and self-respect.","Notice how much of the record is about behaviour rather than love: songs that address children, parents and the young in the tone of an elder offering advice, a role the Wolof griot tradition has always given singers. N'Dour is not himself a hereditary griot, but his mother came from a griot family, and you can hear the praise-singer's authority in how he delivers a moral.","Many critics consider Set the best of N'Dour's international albums, the one where the export polish and the Dakar fire are in balance. It sold well in Europe and was followed by years of touring. Its reputation has only grown since, and later listeners often recommend it, rather than the more famous hit singles, as the place to begin with him.","Play the title song first and listen to the way the drums and voice lock at the chorus. Then go through the record listening for tempo: almost every song has a moment where it tightens and accelerates. If it grips you, try Egypt next, from fourteen years later, to hear the same voice turned to Sufi devotion."],"kesarbai-kerkar-raga-lalit":["Lalit is a dawn raga, sung in the last hour before sunrise, and Kesarbai's short recording packs its whole character into a few minutes. Her voice is large, open-throated and unornamented by sentiment; she moves through the raga with the zigzag, knotted phrasing of the Jaipur-Atrauli school, never approaching a note in a straight line. The effect is grave and restless at once, like someone awake and waiting for the light.","Kesarbai trained for years under Alladiya Khan, founder of the Jaipur-Atrauli gharana, and became its most commanding voice. She was famously wary of the gramophone, believing a raga could not be honestly squeezed onto the few minutes of a 78 rpm side, and she left only a small number of commercial recordings. Those few, later reissued by EMI in India, are nearly all that preserve one of the century's great singers.","Lalit is unusual among ragas because it uses two versions of the same scale step, the natural and the raised fourth, one right after the other, a clash that most ragas forbid. Listen for the moment Kesarbai slides from one to the other; that small grinding step is the raga's whole identity, and she places it with complete certainty.","Musicians who heard Kesarbai in concert often said the discs are only a shadow of her, and they are right that three minutes cannot hold an unfolding khayal. Yet the recordings have become touchstones. Singers still study them for her breath control and her refusal of sweetness, and critics rank her short Lalit among the most concentrated examples of the Jaipur style on record.","Listen at dawn if you can; the raga's mood makes sense in half-light. Hear it twice, the second time following only the long held notes and how steady they are. Then compare with Jaat Kahan Ho, her Bhairavi, to hear the same voice in a gentler, farewell mood."],"kesarbai-kerkar-raga-gaud-malhar":["Gaud Malhar belongs to the family of Malhar ragas, the ragas of the monsoon, and it is the brightest and most hopeful of them. Kesarbai sings it in a short khayal: a slow opening statement, a composition set to tabla, and then quick running passages called taans, which she delivers in long unbroken chains, climbing and turning back on themselves with a force that belies the brevity of the record.","In the India of Kesarbai's prime, the Malhar ragas were sung in the rainy season, and audiences in Bombay, where she lived and performed, knew them as the sound of the monsoon's arrival after months of heat. Her records were made for the Gramophone Company's Indian branch at a time when classical musicians were still learning to trust the microphone, and when a 78 rpm disc ran barely three minutes.","Listen for a characteristic Malhar turn, a quick swoop that touches the flattened seventh and slides down, which instantly identifies the family. Then notice how Kesarbai's taans are built not from scales but from tightly patterned cells, each repeated and shifted a step; it is the Jaipur-Atrauli method of turning a raga like a gem to catch different faces of it.","Rabindranath Tagore gave Kesarbai the title Surashri, lady of melody, and later generations have treated her records almost as scripture. The main debate is about what they represent: some listeners hear an austere, forbidding art, others a voice of great warmth held under discipline. Most agree that her taans set a standard of power and accuracy that few singers have matched since.","Put this on during a rainstorm. Listen first for the rush of the fast passages near the end, then go back and hear how patiently the opening sets them up. Compare it with a modern performance of the related raga Megh Malhar to hear how the monsoon ragas differ in mood."],"abing-ting-song":["Where Erquan Yingyue is slow and inward, Ting Song is vigorous and blustery. Abing's erhu, the two-string fiddle bowed between its strings, saws out strong, accented phrases, rising and falling like wind moving through a stand of trees, with sudden slides and brusque bow-strokes. It is short and restless, a piece of energy rather than contemplation, and it shows a far rougher and more outgoing side of the blind street musician of Wuxi.","Abing, whose given name was Hua Yanjun, was a Daoist temple musician who lost his sight and spent his life playing in the streets of Wuxi. In 1950 the musicologists Yang Yinliu and Cao Anhe travelled from Tianjin to record him on a wire recorder. He had not played for some time, and he died that December. Only six of his pieces were captured: three on erhu, three on pipa.","The title is usually linked to the pine trees on Huishan, the hill above Wuxi, and the whistling of wind in their needles; some accounts connect it with older stories of Song dynasty heroism. Listen for the passages where Abing's bow presses hard enough to crackle, an effect a conservatory player would avoid but which here gives the wind its bite.","Ting Song has always lived in the shadow of Erquan Yingyue, and it is played less often. Yet erhu teachers value it as the necessary counterweight, proof that Abing was not only a melancholic but a player of real force. Modern transcriptions have smoothed its rough edges; the 1950 recording remains the reference, crackles and all.","Hear it right after Erquan Yingyue, so the contrast lands. Listen for the way single notes are attacked and then bent, and imagine the wind rather than a melody. Then try a modern conservatory performance and ask what has been gained and lost in polishing it."],"abing-dalang-taosha":["Abing also played the pipa, the pear-shaped plucked lute, and this is the best known of his three pipa pieces. It is spacious and reflective, built from clear plucked melody interrupted by rolling tremolos, the rapid finger-strokes that let a pipa sustain a note, and by sudden strummed chords. The music ebbs and returns in waves, as the title suggests, rather than driving forward, and ends quietly.","The recording was made in Wuxi in 1950 by Yang Yinliu and Cao Anhe on the same visit that captured Erquan Yingyue. The title borrows the name of an old poetic tune pattern, one of the fixed melodies to which Song dynasty poets wrote verses; Abing gave it his own, largely improvised shape. Within months of the session he was dead, and these few minutes are all that survive.","Listen past the melody to the tremolo. Abing's rolls are uneven and breathing rather than machine-steady, the playing of someone trained in temple and street music rather than a conservatory. The sense of waves comes from phrases that repeat with small changes, each time pushed slightly further, then pulled back, like water on a beach.","The piece is far less famous than Abing's erhu music, partly because the pipa's own heroic repertoire, such as Ambush from Ten Sides, overshadows it. Pipa players have nonetheless taken it into the standard repertoire, and scholars point to it as evidence that Abing was a complete folk musician, fluent on several instruments, not merely the author of one beloved tune.","Listen with good headphones, since the old wire recording is thin. Follow the tremolos as waves and the plucked notes as the sand. Then compare it with the martial Ambush from Ten Sides to hear the pipa's two faces, the battlefield and the shoreline."],"asik-veysel-kara-toprak":["Kara Toprak is a long strophic song for voice and bağlama, the long-necked Turkish lute, whose bright metal strings ring behind Veysel's plain, weathered voice. Each verse follows the same modest tune, and the lute plays short interludes between them. There is no drama in the delivery; the power lies in the steady repetition, verse after verse, as the song circles its single idea like a man walking the edge of his field.","Aşık Veysel Şatıroğlu was born in the village of Sivrialan near Sivas and lost his sight to smallpox as a child. He grew up in the old Anatolian tradition of the aşık, the wandering poet-singer. In the 1940s the new Turkish republic's Village Institutes employed him to teach bağlama, and radio and records carried his songs across the country. In 1965 parliament granted him a lifetime stipend for his services to the Turkish language.","The song's argument is a strange love story: the singer says that of everyone and everything he has loved, only the black earth has stayed faithful, feeding him while he lived and waiting to take him back when he dies. Listen for how each verse ends by returning to the soil, a folk-poet's version of the preacher's reminder that all flesh comes from and goes back to dust.","Kara Toprak is widely held to be Veysel's finest poem and is taught in Turkish schools. It has been recorded by many later artists, from folk singers to rock musicians, and the argument is usually about arrangement: whether anything can be added to a blind man's voice and one lute without losing the point. Most listeners still return to Veysel's own recording.","Listen once without translation, for the sound of voice and bağlama, then read a plain summary of the poem and listen again. Notice that the tune never changes; the meaning deepens instead. Compare it with Uzun İnce Bir Yoldayım, his other great meditation on mortality, and with Woody Guthrie singing about the land."],"asik-veysel-dostlar-beni-hatirlasin":["This is a farewell song, and it sounds like one. Veysel's voice, older and rougher than in his earlier recordings, sings a slow, falling melody over the bağlama, whose plucked strings echo each line and fill the gaps between verses. The tune has a gentle rocking lilt, almost a lullaby, and the song unfolds in short verses, each closing on the same request that gives it its title.","In the last decades of his life Veysel was a national figure, recorded, broadcast and honoured, yet he went on living in his home village. His songs from this period often look back over his life. The aşık tradition expects the poet to name himself in the last verse of a song, as a signature, and Veysel's late songs use that convention to speak directly, almost as an epitaph.","Listen for the final verse, where Veysel names himself, as every aşık does; here the signature becomes a leave-taking, the poet stepping out from behind the song to address his listeners in person. The song asks not for fame but simply that friends keep his memory, a humbler wish than its popularity at memorials might suggest.","The song has become a standard of Turkish memorial occasions and is frequently performed on the anniversary of Veysel's death in 1973. Critics praise it as the purest statement of his late style, where the poetry is almost bare. Later interpreters, including major Turkish pop and folk singers, have recorded it, though their fuller arrangements rarely match the unadorned original.","Listen to it after Kara Toprak; the two together make a portrait of a man thinking calmly about his own end. Follow the bağlama's little replies between lines, then pay attention to the last verse. If you know Schubert's late songs, you will find a kinship in the calm."],"amir-khan-raga-darbari-kanada":["Darbari Kanada is the grave, midnight raga of the royal court, and Amir Khan's HMV recording is one of its defining performances. It begins in extreme slowness, an ati-vilambit khayal where each cycle of the tabla rhythm lasts so long that time seems to stop. His deep, veiled voice explores the lowest register note by note, with the raga's heavy, oscillating flattened third trembling at the centre, before quickening into gentle, rippling runs.","Amir Khan, born in 1912 and associated with Indore, built his own style from many sources: the slow unfolding of dhrupad, the meditative gravity of the older khayal masters, and the running passages of instrumentalists. By the 1960s he was one of the most admired vocalists in India, recording LPs for HMV that allowed far longer performances than the old 78 rpm records, and slow ragas like Darbari were exactly where that new length mattered.","Listen to how he builds the raga: he takes one note at a time and adds neighbours in careful order, a method known as merukhand, borrowed from abstract combinational patterns rather than from song. It is why his performances feel architectural. Notice also the oscillation on the flattened third and sixth, a slow shake that no other raga uses in quite this way.","Many listeners and musicians regard Amir Khan's Darbari as the benchmark for the raga, admired for its inward seriousness rather than display. Some found his extreme slowness austere or even detached in his lifetime; that view has faded as his influence spread through the singers of the next generation. His style, often called the Indore gharana, now counts as one of the main currents of modern khayal.","Give it the time it asks. Sit through the opening minutes without expecting a tune, listening to how each new note is admitted. When the tabla finally quickens, notice the rolling runs. Compare it with the Marwa already in the catalogue, then with Bhimsen Joshi's more extrovert style."],"amir-khan-aaj-gaawat-man-mero":["This is a musical duel for two voices in the raga Desi. Amir Khan and D. V. Paluskar trade phrases, each answering the other with a more elaborate run, before their lines grow tighter and faster and finally combine. The film orchestra is kept discreet under the tabla and the drone, so the result is essentially a short classical jugalbandi, placed at the climax of a popular Hindi film and heard by millions.","Baiju Bawra, a 1952 film with music by Naushad, tells the legend of the singer Baiju who challenges Tansen, the celebrated musician of Akbar's court. Naushad made the unusual decision to fill it with real classical singing, and for the climactic contest he cast two leading classical vocalists as the voices of the rivals, Amir Khan for Tansen and the young Paluskar, son of the reformer Vishnu Digambar Paluskar, for Baiju.","Listen for the difference in the two voices: Amir Khan darker and slower in attack, Paluskar lighter and brighter. The duel is scripted, since Baiju has to win in the story, and you can hear the arrangement give the younger voice the last word. Paluskar died in 1955, very young, which makes this one of his most widely heard recordings.","The film was a large hit and its music is remembered as a moment when Hindi cinema took classical music seriously, bringing ragas to audiences who would never attend a concert. Purists have sometimes treated film appearances as beneath great singers; this track is the usual counter-example, a genuine piece of classical art that happens to have been heard by a mass public.","Treat it as an overture to Hindustani singing. Follow one voice, then the other, and notice when they start overlapping. Then go to Amir Khan's Darbari Kanada to hear the same singer with no time limit, and to Bismillah Khan's Goonj Uthi Shehnai for another film built around a classical musician."],"bismillah-khan-goonj-uthi-shehnai":["Goonj Uthi Shehnai, 'the shehnai rang out', is a Hindi film whose hero is a shehnai player, and Bismillah Khan supplied that shehnai. His reedy, piercing, vocal-sounding double-reed instrument threads through the whole soundtrack: in solo raga passages, in weaving introductions to the songs, and in wedding music. Vasant Desai's score surrounds it with strings and playback singers, but the shehnai is always the voice you remember.","The shehnai had long been heard mainly at weddings and temples; Bismillah Khan, from a family of court and temple musicians attached to Varanasi, raised it to the classical concert stage. By 1959 he was famous across India, and cinema offered him a larger public still. Vasant Desai, a composer steeped in classical music, built the score around him, and the film's success made the instrument itself, briefly, a national star.","Listen to how the shehnai answers the singers, not merely decorating their lines but echoing and bending them like a second voice; Bismillah Khan liked to say he played as though singing. The instrument has no keys, only finger-holes, so every glide and wavering pitch you hear is made with lips and breath, which is why his phrasing sounds so human.","The soundtrack was widely admired and the songs remain popular; the film helped fix the association, still strong in India, between the shehnai and joyous occasion. Some critics have regretted that Bismillah Khan's film work sits in the shadow of his concert recordings, but many listeners first met his sound here, and film historians count the score among the finest classical-based music of 1950s Hindi cinema.","Seek out the passages where the shehnai plays alone before a song begins; they are small ragas in themselves. Then hear the songs to notice how the instrument shadows the voice. Afterwards turn to the Red Fort recital for Bismillah Khan unaccompanied by orchestra."],"bismillah-khan-jugalbandi-vilayat-khan":["A jugalbandi is a duet between two soloists, and this pairing of Bismillah Khan's shehnai with Vilayat Khan's sitar is among the most celebrated in Hindustani music. The two instruments could hardly be more different, a sustained, singing reed against a plucked string whose notes decay, yet they trade phrases, echo each other, and then race together in fast passages over the tabla, the shehnai's long lines making the sitar sound almost vocal.","By the 1960s both men were at the peak of their fame, and the long-playing record, newly common in India, allowed HMV to issue extended duet performances. Vilayat Khan had developed the gayaki ang, a sitar style modelled on the human voice, which made him a natural partner for a shehnai player who thought of his own instrument as a voice. Their duets appeared on LP and were widely admired.","Listen for how each player adapts. Vilayat Khan bends his sitar strings sideways to sustain and slide between notes, borrowing the shehnai's glides, while Bismillah Khan sometimes clips his phrases short to match the plucked attack. At the fastest points, notice who leads: the challenge and answer is a friendly contest, and the tabla player acts as referee.","Jugalbandi became fashionable in the 1960s and 1970s, and critics sometimes dismissed it as show business, two stars competing rather than one raga explored. These duets are usually cited as the counter-argument, because both players subordinate display to the raga. They have been reissued many times and remain a favourite introduction to Hindustani instrumental music.","Start by learning the two timbres: the buzzing sustained reed, the sparkling string. Then follow the conversation, noting who begins each exchange. When the tempo rises, listen for the moments of unison. Compare with a solo recording of each man to hear what both gave up, and gained, by playing together."],"bhimsen-joshi-santavani":["Santavani, 'the voice of the saints', gathers Bhimsen Joshi's recordings of abhangs, the devotional songs of the Marathi poet-saints such as Tukaram, Namdev and Dnyaneshwar. His voice, huge and resonant, is the same one that sings ragas, but here it drives simple, repeated refrains over clattering hand-cymbals and the pakhawaj drum, rising to ecstatic climaxes where he holds a single high note until the accompaniment seems to swirl around it.","The abhang belongs to the warkari tradition of Maharashtra, whose pilgrims walk each year to the temple of Vithoba at Pandharpur, singing as they go. Joshi, a Kannadiga who made his career in Pune, loved these songs and sang them at the end of his concerts. His HMV recordings of them became best-sellers in Maharashtra and carried the pilgrims' repertoire into countless homes and radio broadcasts.","Listen for how much classical technique is hidden in the devotional songs: long sustained notes, bursts of fast running phrases, and raga colours carefully chosen to suit each text. The refrains are built for a crowd to join, and on many tracks you can hear the chorus pulling the tempo forward, as pilgrims would.","Among many Marathi listeners these recordings are more beloved than any of Joshi's ragas, and some songs have become almost inseparable from his voice. Classical purists occasionally regretted the time he gave to devotional and popular music; the prevailing view now is that it showed the full range of a great singer and helped keep the abhang tradition alive for a modern audience.","Choose a single abhang and listen for the build: quiet opening, repeated refrain, then the long climbing climax. Do not worry about the words; the devotion is audible. Then return to his Raga Multani to hear how the same voice works at a slower, more private pace."],"bhimsen-joshi-mile-sur-mera-tumhara":["Mile Sur Mera Tumhara, roughly 'when my tune and yours meet', is a short film song in which a single melody passes from singer to singer and from language to language across India. It opens with Bhimsen Joshi alone, his great voice setting out the gentle, rising tune, and then relays through more than a dozen Indian languages, sung by famous vocalists and seen against landscapes from the Himalaya to the southern coast, before gathering into a crowded, joyful chorus.","The song was made for the national broadcaster Doordarshan and first shown on Independence Day in 1988, at a time when television was new to many Indian homes and the country was troubled by separatist conflicts. Its message of national unity through diversity was deliberate, and it was repeated on air so often that a whole generation learned it by heart.","Notice that the melody is built on a raga-like scale, and that Joshi's opening phrases carry small classical ornaments the later singers mostly drop; the arrangers gave the first word to a Hindustani master so that the tune would feel rooted in tradition. Listen too for how each language shifts the stresses of the melody without breaking it.","For many Indians it is the most familiar thing Bhimsen Joshi ever sang, which amused and sometimes frustrated admirers of his ragas. Critics have debated its patriotic simplicity, but it is widely remembered with affection, and a 2010 remake with film stars was generally judged to lack the original's warmth. It stands as a landmark of public-service television.","Watch it rather than just listening, since the pictures are part of the design. Listen closely to Joshi's opening phrase, then track the melody through the languages. Afterwards hear one of his raga recordings and notice that the same voice, unhurried, becomes far more searching."],"kumar-gandharva-gandhi-malhar":["Gandhi Malhar is a raga that Kumar Gandharva created himself, a new member of the monsoon family of Malhar ragas. His performance is characteristically lean and direct: his high, slightly nasal, piercing voice moves in short, sharply etched phrases rather than long ornate spans, with sudden leaps and silences. The Malhar colour is present, but softened and made serene, a rain that cools rather than storms.","Kumar Gandharva composed the raga in 1969, the centenary of Mahatma Gandhi's birth, as a musical tribute. He lived in Dewas, in the Malwa region of central India, where tuberculosis had forced him to stop singing for years; during his recovery he absorbed the region's folk songs and emerged with an independent, questioning approach, happy to invent ragas and to reject the rules of any single gharana.","Why Malhar for Gandhi? The monsoon ragas are about relief after a long drought, and Kumar Gandharva heard in Gandhi's life a similar coming of rain. Listen for how gently the characteristic Malhar glides are handled; he removes their heaviness, as if to suggest nonviolence even in melody. The raga's material is compact enough to show his habit of saying much with little.","Inventing ragas was controversial in a tradition that prizes inheritance, and some conservatives regarded Kumar Gandharva as an iconoclast. His new ragas have been sung by his students and descendants, and critics now see them as part of a serious rethinking of khayal. Gandhi Malhar in particular is admired as a tribute that avoids sentimentality.","Listen for silences as much as notes; his pauses are part of the phrasing. Then compare with Kesarbai Kerkar's Gaud Malhar to hear how a traditional Malhar sounds from the older Jaipur school, and how far Kumar Gandharva moved from it."],"kumar-gandharva-triveni":["Triveni, the 'confluence of three rivers', is Kumar Gandharva's programme of devotional songs by three medieval poet-saints: Kabir, the weaver who mocked all rituals; Surdas, the blind poet of Krishna's childhood; and Mirabai, the princess who gave her life to Krishna. Each poet gets a distinct musical manner, from Kabir's stark, repeating chants to Surdas's tender melodies and Mira's yearning, pleading lines, all sung in Kumar's piercing, unmistakable voice.","Throughout the 1970s Kumar Gandharva designed themed concert programmes, a rarity in Hindustani music, where recitals usually follow the performer's choice of ragas. Triveni came out of his long engagement with the bhakti poets, whose simple vernacular Hindi he found as rich as any classical text. The programme was performed in concerts and later issued on cassette and CD, becoming one of his most loved works.","Listen to how he characterises the three poets through music alone: Kabir's songs are nearly always set with short, hammering refrains and few ornaments, as if to strip away pretence, while Mira's are given sliding, longing phrases. The contrast is a composer's decision, not tradition, and it turns the programme into a kind of triple portrait.","Admirers regard Triveni as one of the great fusions of classical skill and devotional feeling, and the Kabir settings especially have influenced later singers of the saint's poetry. Some listeners find his voice harsh, and critics of his generation argued over his unconventional phrasing, but that debate has largely settled in his favour.","Hear one song by each poet back to back and listen for how the music changes character. Start with Kabir, whose songs you may already know from the Nirguni Bhajans entry, then Surdas, then Mira. Compare with Bhimsen Joshi's Marathi abhangs to hear another great voice meeting devotional poetry."],"fisk-jubilee-singers-jubilee-songs":["Jubilee Songs is the printed repertory of the first Fisk Jubilee Singers: a book of spirituals set out in four-part harmony, among them Steal Away, Go Down, Moses and Swing Low, Sweet Chariot. As the troupe sang them, the songs were slow, hushed and closely blended, with soft dynamics and long held chords rather than the clapping and call-and-response of a camp meeting. A concert of these songs was a chain of short, hymn-like pieces, each carrying a whole theology of suffering and deliverance in a few verses.","Fisk University in Nashville, founded for freed people after the Civil War, was nearly bankrupt in 1871 when its treasurer and music teacher George L. White took a handful of students on a fundraising tour of the North. Early concerts of ballads and parlour songs made little money; the spirituals, which the students had at first been reluctant to sing before white audiences, changed everything. The group took the name Jubilee Singers in Ohio that autumn, and the New York publishers Biglow & Main issued this collection, transcribed by Theodore Seward, in 1872.","The pianist Ella Sheppard, born enslaved, was the group's musical anchor and helped shape the arrangements, though Seward's name is on the book. Look at the harmonies and you see the compromise the whole enterprise made: melodies born in oral tradition, with bent notes and flexible rhythm, squared into the hymnbook grammar of Northern churches so that choirs anywhere could sing them. What the page lost, the singers restored in performance.","The tours raised the money that built Jubilee Hall, still standing on the Fisk campus, and took the singers before President Grant and, in 1873, Queen Victoria. W. E. B. Du Bois later built a chapter of The Souls of Black Folk around these sorrow songs. Zora Neale Hurston pushed back in the 1930s, arguing that polished concert spirituals were not the living music of the church; both views still shape how the songs are performed.","You cannot hear the 1870s singers, so start with the Fisk quartet's recordings from 1909 onward and then the modern ensemble, and imagine a dozen young voices in a Northern church. Listen to Steal Away sung very quietly and notice how much the silence around each phrase carries. Then try Paul Robeson or Marian Anderson, whose solo concert spirituals grew directly from this tradition."],"fisk-jubilee-singers-fisk-university-jubilee-quartet-recordings":["These are the acoustic records made by a male quartet from Fisk under John Wesley Work II, gathered on modern reissues: spirituals such as Roll, Jordan, Roll and Little David, Play on Your Harp, alongside their Swing Low, Sweet Chariot and a few parlour songs. Four voices sing into a recording horn, so the sound is thin and wrapped in surface hiss, but the blend is exact, the tenor lead clear, and the bass sets a steady rocking pulse under the tunes that is quite unlike choir singing.","In 1909 the recording industry was barely twenty years old and Black performers were rare in its catalogues. Work, a Fisk graduate who taught at the university and collected spirituals with his brother Frederick, revived the Jubilee tradition in the form of a touring quartet and brought it to the Victor company. At the time the industry's idea of Black music was dominated by minstrel and so-called coon songs, and these sober sacred records were a counter-statement.","Listen for the quartet texture itself: a lead voice with three others supplying close chords and a moving bass line. That arrangement, rather than the nineteenth-century mixed choir, is the direct ancestor of the jubilee and gospel quartets of the 1930s and 1940s, from the Golden Gate Quartet onward, and through them of doo-wop and soul harmony groups. The acoustic horn favoured strong, forward voices, which may have shaped how Work balanced the parts.","The records appear to have sold well for their time, a notable fact when so few Black artists recorded at all. Later listeners sometimes found them stiff beside the rural church recordings of the 1920s, but historians of early recording now treat them as a foundation of Black sacred music on disc, and specialist reissue labels have gathered them together so they can be heard as a body of work.","Hear them on a good transfer from a specialist reissue label and give your ears a minute to adjust to the acoustic sound. Then follow the bass voice rather than the melody; it tells you where the whole later history of quartet singing is going. Follow with the Golden Gate Quartet to hear the same form thirty years on, swinging."],"fisk-jubilee-singers-celebrating-fisk":["An anniversary record by the present-day Fisk Jubilee Singers, a student ensemble of around a dozen mixed voices. The core is the old repertory, spirituals in carefully voiced concert arrangements, sung with far more rhythmic drive and dynamic range than the early records could capture. Soft, nearly whispered verses open out into full-voiced climaxes, and the ensemble's hallmark is a very pure unison that suddenly splits into rich close harmony, with every consonant placed together.","The album marked 150 years since the 1871 fundraising tour and was made under the long-serving musical director Paul T. Kwami, a Ghanaian-born Fisk graduate who led the group for almost three decades. Nashville's music business is otherwise dominated by country, and the ensemble has remained a campus choir of students rather than a professional group. It had received the National Medal of Arts in 2008; this record brought the tradition to a new generation of listeners.","Listen to how the arrangements are built from the tradition's own history: some lean on the restrained nineteenth-century style of the first tours, with soft dynamics and still, hymn-like chords, while others borrow the rhythmic push and bright attack of twentieth-century gospel choirs. The same song can move from one manner to the other within a few verses, a compressed history of Black sacred singing in a single track.","It won the Grammy for Best Roots Gospel Album at the ceremony held in 2021, the ensemble's first Grammy after a century and a half of singing. Reviewers welcomed it as a living link to the origins of the concert spiritual rather than a museum exercise. Some listeners still prefer the rawer congregational recordings of the South, the old argument Hurston started, now carried into a new century.","Play one of the unaccompanied spirituals first and wait for the moment the unison opens into chords. Then put on a track from the 1909 quartet records and hear what a century has changed and what it has kept. Listen in a quiet room at a decent volume, so the soft passages, which carry most of the feeling, can register."],"king-oliver-chimes-blues":["A slow blues by King Oliver's Creole Jazz Band, built from several strains in the ragtime manner. The ensemble passages are the classic New Orleans weave: Oliver's lead cornet, Johnny Dodds's clarinet curling above, trombone slides beneath. In one strain the band imitates the ringing of chimes with sustained, bell-like chords. Then the ensemble drops away and the young second cornetist, Louis Armstrong, plays two choruses alone, the first solo he ever put on record.","The band was the hottest attraction in Chicago, playing nightly at the Lincoln Gardens on the South Side for dancers who had come north in the Great Migration. In the spring of 1923 it travelled to the Gennett studio in Richmond, Indiana, a small operation attached to a piano factory, where the acoustic horn could barely cope with loud drums and the players had to be carefully placed around it. These sessions are the first substantial records by a Black New Orleans band.","Armstrong's solo is not an improvisation in the later sense; it is a composed, carefully shaped chorus that he probably played much the same way each night. Listen to how plainly he states it, with little of the rhythmic daring of his work only a few years later. The chimes effect is also a clue: the band was working from a worked-out, strain-by-strain structure, not simply jamming on a blues.","At the time this was one band side among many; its fame came later, when jazz historians identified it as the first recorded Armstrong solo and began to date the arrival of the jazz soloist from here. The technical limits of the Gennett horn have prompted endless debate about what the band really sounded like live, and reissue engineers still argue over pitch and speed correction.","Listen once for the whole band, following the clarinet's decorations, then again waiting for the moment everything thins out to one cornet. Compare it with Armstrong's West End Blues of 1928 and you hear, in five years, the soloist take over jazz. A modern remastered transfer at corrected speed is worth seeking out."],"king-oliver-canal-street-blues":["A medium-tempo blues credited to Oliver and Armstrong and named for the broad main street of New Orleans. For most of its length it is pure collective improvisation: the two cornets share the lead, the clarinet runs countermelodies above, and the trombone fills in beneath. The celebrated moment is a chorus by Johnny Dodds, whose dark, woody clarinet tone and bluesy phrasing stand out against the chugging banjo and piano.","Recorded at the same spring 1923 sessions for Gennett in Richmond, Indiana, that produced Chimes Blues and Dippermouth Blues. Every member of the band was a New Orleans musician working in Chicago, and the title looks back at the city they had left behind. The two-cornet front line was Oliver's special attraction, drawing white musicians such as Bix Beiderbecke to the South Side to hear it.","Listen to the two cornets in the ensemble: Oliver plays the lead and Armstrong a second part just below him, so closely matched that it can be hard to hear there are two players. Armstrong later recalled that they harmonised breaks without rehearsal, Oliver somehow signalling what was coming while still playing. On this record you hear the tight two-horn texture that made that trick possible.","Canal Street Blues is less famous than Dippermouth Blues, but it is often chosen to illustrate the Creole Jazz Band precisely because it is so representative of its everyday style. Dodds's chorus has long been studied as a model of early jazz clarinet. The shared composer credit with Armstrong is part of a longer history of disputed and informal authorship in Oliver's band.","Pick one instrument and follow it all the way through, then play it again following another; that is the only way to untangle the polyphony. Wait for the clarinet chorus. Then hear Armstrong's Hot Five records a few years later, where he keeps this ensemble sound but steps out in front of it."],"king-oliver-sugar-foot-stomp":["King Oliver's later version of his own Dippermouth Blues, recorded with his larger band, the Dixie Syncopators. The ensemble now includes saxophones and written passages, and the texture is heavier and more arranged than the 1923 record. The heart of it is unchanged: Oliver's three-chorus cornet solo, played into a mute that he works to produce a crying, speech-like wah sound, rising over a stomping medium tempo.","By 1926 the Creole Jazz Band had broken up and Armstrong had left. Fletcher Henderson's New York orchestra had recorded the tune as Sugar Foot Stomp in 1925, with Armstrong reproducing Oliver's solo inside Don Redman's big-band arrangement, and it became a hit for them. Oliver, now leading a bigger band at the Plantation Café in Chicago, reclaimed his composition under its new title, adapting to the larger dance orchestras the public now wanted.","Oliver's muted solo was so admired that other cornetists copied it note for note, which is what Armstrong did with Henderson. Listen to Oliver's handling of the mute: he opens and closes it to make the notes sound like syllables, a vocal effect that fed straight into the growling brass of Duke Ellington's early band. The tune's life across three recordings is a small history of jazz moving from ensemble to arrangement.","The Dixie Syncopators records were long rated below the 1923 Creole band sides, partly because Oliver's playing was starting to suffer from the gum disease that would end his career. More recent listeners have been kinder, hearing a leader adapting intelligently to changing fashion. The Henderson version remains better known, and historians use the pair as a case study in who gets credit for a musical idea.","Play Dippermouth Blues first, then this, and listen to the same solo in two different worlds. Focus on the muted cornet: each note seems to speak a word. Then find an early Ellington record featuring Bubber Miley's plunger-muted trumpet and hear where that talking brass went next."],"kern-they-didnt-believe-me":["A love song with lyrics by Herbert Reynolds, in which the singer marvels that friends refused to believe the loved one could care for them. Musically it is a gently walking melody in a moderate four, not a waltz, moving in long arching phrases that pause unexpectedly and turn their harmony through quiet side-steps before landing home. It sounds conversational, as though sung by an ordinary young American rather than a stock operetta prince.","Kern wrote it for the American production of The Girl from Utah, a British musical comedy that reached Broadway in 1914 and, as was routine then, had new American songs inserted into it. At the time Broadway was dominated by European operetta, full of waltzes and imaginary kingdoms. The ballroom dance craze, with the foxtrot arriving that same year, made a moderate four-beat love song both fashionable and danceable.","Listen for the moment late in the chorus where the melody breaks its pattern with a sudden, slightly syncopated extra phrase, as if the singer is too excited to keep to the regular line. The chorus also avoids the tidy repeated sections that later became standard; it unspools as one long thought. That freedom is exactly what the teenage George Gershwin, then a song-plugger, heard and loved in Kern.","The song became a big seller in sheet music and on records and made Kern's name. Historians routinely name it as a model for the American theatre ballad, the point where Broadway began to sound native rather than Viennese. Gershwin and Richard Rodgers both said Kern showed them what a popular song could be, and that view has hardly been challenged since.","Seek out an early recording from around 1915 to hear its original lilting, parlour-orchestra style, then a later jazz or cabaret version, where singers slow it down and savour the harmonic turns. Listen for the extra phrase near the end of the chorus. Then compare an operetta waltz from the same years to feel how different the accent is."],"kern-smoke-gets-in-your-eyes":["A slow ballad from the Broadway musical Roberta, with lyrics by Otto Harbach, in which someone who once laughed off friends' warnings about love now finds tears blurring their sight as it ends. The melody moves in long, even notes, rising patiently. Then comes a bridge that slips abruptly into a distant key, as if the floor has shifted, before a chain of chords brings it gracefully back to the opening tune for the final section.","Roberta opened on Broadway in November 1933, deep in the Depression, a fashion-house romance set in Paris. The song was introduced by the Russian-born singer Tamara Drasin and was the number everybody left humming. Kern reportedly adapted the melody from a brisk tap-dance tune he had written years earlier and never used; slowed right down, it became a lament. The 1935 film version, with Irene Dunne, Fred Astaire and Ginger Rogers, spread it further.","The bridge's key change is the song's secret: the melody's last note before it becomes the first note of the new key, heard in a different light, so the leap feels both shocking and inevitable. Jazz musicians treat the passage as a test of their harmony. The title's image also works on two levels: smoke is both a physical excuse for tears and a picture of love vanishing into air.","It was a hit in 1933 and again, spectacularly, in 1958, when the Platters' doo-wop version reached number one in the United States and Britain, introducing Kern to a teenage rock and roll audience. Critics treat the song as one of the most sophisticated ballads of its era, its harmonic ingenuity out of all proportion to its apparent simplicity.","Listen for the bridge: wait for the moment the ground drops away, then follow how many steps it takes to come home. Compare the Platters' 1958 recording, with its soaring lead tenor, with a slow jazz instrumental version, where the bridge is the part soloists linger over most."],"kern-the-way-you-look-tonight":["A tender ballad with lyrics by Dorothy Fields in which the singer, imagining harder days ahead, promises to hold on to the memory of how the beloved looks at this moment. The melody is plain and warm, moving mostly by step, with a middle section that brightens into a new key before gently returning. Sung straight it is almost a lullaby; swung, it becomes a relaxed dance tune.","Written for Swing Time (1936), the RKO film with Fred Astaire and Ginger Rogers. Astaire sings it at the piano while Rogers, in the next room, is washing her hair; she comes in, shampoo still in place, moved by the song. Kern was now working mostly in Hollywood, and Fields was one of the few women writing lyrics at the top of the profession. It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song.","The comic scene in the film undercuts the sentiment, which is part of why it works: Astaire's modest, almost spoken delivery suits a song that is really about private memory rather than grand passion. The lyric never celebrates the present outright; it is about keeping the moment against a future that might be worse, which gives its sweetness a faint undertow of worry.","A hit in 1936 and a standard ever since, recorded by crooners, jazz players and doo-wop groups. Frank Sinatra's brassy 1964 version with Nelson Riddle gave it a new life. Film critics admire its dramatic use; historians of song see in it Kern adapting his lyrical, operetta-trained style to the easier rhythms of the swing era.","Start with Astaire's own recording, simple and intimate, then Sinatra's big-band version, then a jazz instrumental. Each shows a different side of the same tune. Listen for the lift into the middle section and how each performer handles the return to the main melody."],"irving-berlin-blue-skies":["A short, bright song about good fortune after bad: the singer finds the whole world smiling, skies and birds included, now that love has arrived. The main melody begins in a minor key and turns to major, so the happy words sit over music that keeps remembering sadness. The middle section is jaunty, almost a dance step. The chorus is only thirty-two bars, and a singer can deliver it as a cheerful foxtrot or a wistful ballad.","Berlin wrote it in 1926, the year of his much-publicised marriage to the heiress Ellin Mackay, whose father opposed the match, and of the birth of their first daughter. It was inserted, at the singer Belle Baker's request, into Rodgers and Hart's musical Betsy, where it reportedly drew many encores. The next year Al Jolson sang it in The Jazz Singer, among the first songs heard in a feature-length talking picture.","The minor-key opening is the heart of it. Berlin, a Russian-Jewish immigrant, often wrote in minor modes that recall the synagogue and folk melodies of his childhood. Here that shading makes the good cheer sound hard-won. Listen for how a jazz singer can lean into the ambiguity: speed it up and it is joyful; slow it down and it becomes almost a lament.","An immediate hit and a durable standard, the title of a 1946 film musical, and a favourite of jazz players, who value its sturdy harmony. Rodgers later recalled his irritation at a Berlin song stealing the attention in their show, a small moment in the rivalries of Broadway songwriting. Critics now use it to show how much subtlety Berlin packed into seemingly simple songs.","Listen to Ella Fitzgerald's version, with its scat improvisation, then to Willie Nelson's slower, reflective one on Stardust (1978). Notice how the minor opening lands differently each time. Then find Jolson's performance in The Jazz Singer to catch the song at its moment in history."],"irving-berlin-cheek-to-cheek":["A romantic dance song in which the singer declares that no pleasure, not even climbing a mountain or fishing a stream, matches the happiness of dancing close to the beloved. The melody rises and falls in long, flowing lines and passes through several contrasting sections, including a tense, climbing passage before the return of the main theme. It is built to be danced to and ends by circling back to its start, like a couple going round a ballroom floor.","Written for Top Hat (1935), the most successful of the Astaire and Rogers films at RKO. Astaire sings it to Rogers before they dance, she in a feathered dress that shed feathers during filming and exasperated Astaire. The film was a Depression-era escape into luxury and elegance, and the song went straight to the top of the radio hit parades that year.","Popular songs of the time used a thirty-two-bar chorus with one contrasting section. Cheek to Cheek runs to seventy-two bars, with not one but two contrasting sections, the second of which climbs to a dramatic peak before relaxing. It is one of the longest choruses in the songbook, yet it feels effortless. That long span matches the long, unbroken dance routine that follows it in the film.","Nominated for the Academy Award for Best Original Song and a huge seller, it has never gone out of circulation. Ella Fitzgerald and Louis Armstrong's 1956 duet is a favourite of later listeners. Songwriters admire its unusual length; film historians rank the dance sequence among the finest in the Hollywood musical, the moment Astaire and Rogers seem to float.","Watch the Top Hat scene first, where song and dance form one gesture, then hear Ella Fitzgerald and Louis Armstrong's relaxed duet on Ella and Louis. Listen for the tense, climbing section near the end of the chorus, and count how long the song keeps going before it finally comes home."],"irving-berlin-white-christmas":["A slow, simple song in which the singer longs for a snowy Christmas like those remembered from childhood. The melody moves in small steps within a narrow range, easy for anyone to sing, and slips briefly through chromatic notes that give it a faint ache. Bing Crosby's original recording, with a soft choir, a gentle orchestra and a whistled passage, is how most of the world knows it.","Berlin wrote it for Holiday Inn (1942), a Paramount film in which Crosby and Fred Astaire run an inn open only on public holidays. Crosby first sang it on radio on Christmas Day 1941, weeks after Pearl Harbor. The film opened in the summer of 1942 with American troops overseas, and the song became a wartime anthem of homesickness, reportedly one of the most requested tunes on forces radio.","Berlin's introductory verse, rarely sung, places the singer in sunny Beverly Hills among palm trees, which makes the whole song a Californian's daydream rather than a snow scene. Once you know that, the chorus sounds like longing for something lost rather than a celebration. Berlin, a Jewish immigrant, had also lost an infant son on Christmas Day in 1928, which many listeners hear behind the song's melancholy.","It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song, and Crosby's record became one of the best-selling singles in history; he re-recorded it in 1947 because the original master had worn out. Some critics find it sentimental, others a finely balanced nostalgia. Its Christmas of snow and family, with no religion in it, helped define the modern secular holiday.","Hear Crosby's recording first and notice how quietly he sings, almost speaking. Then find a version that includes the Beverly Hills verse and listen again with that framing. Finally, compare the Drifters' 1954 doo-wop version, which turns it into something playful and upbeat."],"carlos-gardel-mi-buenos-aires-querido":["A slow tango-canción, with words by Alfredo Le Pera, in which an exile dreams of seeing his beloved Buenos Aires again, its streets and lamplight and the woman he left there. Gardel sings over a lush string orchestra with a gentle, swaying rhythm; the melody unfolds in long, singing phrases with sudden swells of emotion and a hushed, almost whispered close. It is less a dance than a love letter to a city.","Gardel wrote it for one of the films he made for Paramount in New York in 1934, when he was the biggest singing star in Latin America and living far from home. He and Le Pera wrote a string of songs for these Spanish-language pictures, aimed at audiences across the Americas and Spain. The homesickness was prophetic: Gardel never returned to Buenos Aires alive, dying in an air crash in Medellín, Colombia, the following year.","Listen for how Gardel shapes the phrases: his voice lingers slightly behind the beat, then catches up, a freedom learned in his early years singing folk-based songs with guitar before he turned to tango. The arrangement, made far from Buenos Aires, sounds more like a Hollywood studio orchestra than a bandoneón-led tango band, which gives the song its dreamy, faraway sheen.","It became one of the unofficial anthems of Buenos Aires and a pillar of the tango-canción repertory. Some tango purists find Gardel's film songs too smooth, too far from the earthy dance music of the city's cafés and dance halls. For most Argentines, however, it is an emotional touchstone, and generations of singers have measured themselves against Gardel's recording.","Hear Gardel's original, focusing on how his voice floats over the strings. Then find an instrumental version by a traditional orquesta típica with bandoneóns, to hear the tune in a native dance setting. Compare his early Mi noche triste to hear how far his style had travelled in seventeen years."],"carlos-gardel-volver":["A tango-canción with lyrics by Alfredo Le Pera, in which a man comes back to his city after many years, older and greying, afraid of meeting the past and the love he left behind. The melody begins low and hesitant, then rises into a broad, soaring refrain in which Gardel pours out the fear and longing of return. The orchestra swells behind him, and the song closes with a sense of time lost rather than regained.","Written for the film El día que me quieras (1935), shot in New York for Paramount, in which Gardel plays a singer torn between love and a disapproving father. It was among the last songs he recorded before his death in June 1935. Its theme of returning to a home left long ago echoed his own life as a star working abroad, and that of countless emigrants across the Spanish-speaking world.","The title simply means 'to return', and the whole song is about the passing of time. Listen for how the refrain's slow, stretched phrasing seems to hold time back before letting it go. Gardel, recorded close to the microphone, sounds as though he is confessing rather than performing. The fear in the song is as much about ageing as about love, which is why older listeners often claim it as theirs.","Volver became one of the most beloved tangos ever written, recorded by countless singers in Argentina, Spain and beyond. Pedro Almodóvar named his 2006 film after it, and a flamenco version by Estrella Morente is sung there, mimed by Penélope Cruz. Critics generally place it at the summit of the Gardel and Le Pera partnership, cut short by the crash that killed them both.","Listen for how the song moves from a hushed opening to its open-throated refrain, carried by the rising strings. Then hear a later, rougher-voiced tango singer take it on, to learn how an older voice changes its meaning. Finally, compare the flamenco version from Almodóvar's film."],"carlos-gardel-el-dia-que-me-quieras":["A slow romantic song, more canción than tango, with lyrics by Alfredo Le Pera, in which the singer imagines that on the day his beloved finally loves him, the whole of nature, flowers, stars and fountains, will join in the celebration. The melody is long-breathed and lyrical, closer to an operatic aria than a dance, rising to a high, sustained climax. Gardel begins softly and gradually opens his voice to full power over floating strings.","The title song of the Paramount film El día que me quieras (1935), in which Gardel sings it to his co-star Rosita Moreno. It was recorded in New York during the last months of his life, while he prepared a long tour of the Caribbean and South America. The song's international romantic manner shows Gardel moving beyond the tango towards a pan-Latin popular ballad that could be sung anywhere in the Spanish-speaking world.","The song includes a passage of spoken verse, a device from theatre and radio that lets the singer drop into intimate confession before the melody resumes. Its shape is not the tango's usual pattern of contrasting sections but that of a romantic art song, with the climax held back until near the end. Gardel loved Italian opera and sometimes sang arias, and that training shows in the long, sustained high notes.","It became one of Gardel's most famous songs and a staple of the Latin romantic repertory, recorded by crooners and bolero singers across the Americas; Luis Miguel's lush version of the 1990s carried it to a new generation. Critics see it as proof that Gardel's legacy reaches well beyond tango, into the whole twentieth-century tradition of Spanish-language romantic song.","Hear Gardel's original and listen for the swelling, aria-like climax and the drop into speech. Then compare a later romantic ballad singer's version, to hear how the song lived on in Latin pop. For contrast, play Volver from the same film and notice how differently the two songs treat the passing of time."],"jelly-roll-morton-the-pearls":["A piano solo in several strains, laid out like a rag but played with the loose swing and bent blue notes of jazz. The opening theme is graceful and delicately ornamented, almost a salon piece; later strains grow bolder, with rolling left-hand patterns and crisp right-hand figures that sound like a band's clarinet and cornet trading phrases. Morton's touch is precise and varied, and the piece is full of little breaks, stops and changes of weight.","Morton, a Creole pianist from New Orleans who had worked across the country in vaudeville, bars and gambling halls, was based in Chicago in 1923 and recorded a series of piano solos at the Gennett studio in Richmond, Indiana. They are among the first records of a jazz composer playing his own music as written pieces. He later arranged The Pearls for his band, the Red Hot Peppers, recording it for Victor in 1927.","Listen to the division of labour between the hands: the left often behaves like tuba and trombone, the right like cornet and clarinet, so that Morton is effectively playing a whole New Orleans band on one piano. Each strain also answers the one before in mood and register, a sense of overall design that Morton prized. What sounds spontaneous is carefully composed, and he played it much the same way for years.","The Pearls is regarded as one of Morton's finest compositions and a showcase of early jazz piano at its most refined. Later critics and scholars, notably Gunther Schuller, cited pieces like this to argue that Morton was jazz's first great composer and that jazz could be composed, not merely improvised, a claim Morton loudly made for himself and which his reputation now largely supports.","Listen once for the melody, then again watching only the left hand. Compare the 1927 Red Hot Peppers version, where the parts Morton played alone are divided among the instruments. Then play a Scott Joplin rag beside it to hear the rhythmic looseness and blues colouring Morton added to the older form."],"charley-patton-mississippi-boweavil-blues":["A blues about the boll weevil, the beetle that devastated Southern cotton, sung largely from the insect's side as it wanders in search of a home. Patton's voice is hoarse and rasping, and he often mutters asides over the music, while his guitar drives a fast, bouncing rhythm with snapped bass strings and sharp treble figures. Now and then he stops singing mid-line and lets the guitar finish the thought, as though the two were conversing.","Recorded at Patton's first session for Paramount in Richmond, Indiana, in June 1929, when he was already the best-known bluesman in the Mississippi Delta, based around Dockery Plantation. Paramount first issued it under the name 'The Masked Marvel' and invited buyers to guess who the singer was, a promotional gimmick. The boll weevil had crossed from Mexico in the 1890s and spread across the South, ruining crops and pushing many Black farm workers towards the cities of the North.","The boll weevil appears in many Black folk songs of the period as a small, stubborn survivor that planters cannot destroy, and it is hard not to hear sympathy in Patton's portrait of a creature looking for somewhere to live. He also varies the guitar's role constantly, from rhythm to countermelody to percussive slaps on the body, so that a single player sounds like a small band.","The 'Masked Marvel' ploy did its job, and the record became one of Patton's best known. Collectors of the 1950s and 1960s, rediscovering him on scratchy Paramount pressings, prized it as a window on the earliest recorded Delta style. Historians now place it among the core texts of the Delta blues, for its folklore as much as its music, and trace its guitar approach through Son House and Howlin' Wolf.","Listen first for the guitar and voice answering each other, then for the snapping bass notes that keep the dance rhythm going. Compare Lead Belly's boll weevil song to hear the same folk theme in another region. A good remastered transfer helps, since Paramount's pressings were famously noisy."],"charley-patton-a-spoonful-blues":["A slide-guitar blues built around the word 'spoonful', which Patton repeatedly leaves unsung, letting the bottleneck on his guitar string slur out the missing word instead. The verses list the fights, crimes and heartbreaks people fall into over a spoonful of something the song never names. His voice is gruff and half-spoken, the guitar sharp and ringing, and the whole record has the feel of a joke told by someone who knows exactly how far to push it.","Recorded for Paramount in 1929 during Patton's first sessions in Richmond, Indiana. The 'spoonful' theme circulated widely in Southern song, and Papa Charlie Jackson had recorded a related piece a few years earlier. The spoonful could be read as love, desire or a drug, and Patton's audience at juke joints and plantation dances would have enjoyed the ambiguity. For the record company it was simply another blues release for a Black market.","Listen to the moments when the voice stops and the slide takes over the key word: Patton treats the guitar as a second singer, able to say what the singer chooses not to. That trick of making an instrument speak became a signature of Delta slide playing, from Son House to Muddy Waters and Elmore James, and later of every rock guitarist who learned from them.","Scholars see it as a forebear of Willie Dixon's Spoonful, recorded by Howlin' Wolf in 1960 and later stretched into long improvisations by Cream. Patton's version is valued less for its words than its inventive voice-and-guitar dialogue. Critics sometimes debate how much of Patton's material was his own and how much drawn from common stock; this song sits right on that line.","Listen for the missing word and the guitar that fills it; count how many times he does it. Then hear Howlin' Wolf's Spoonful to trace the song's most famous descendant, and a live Cream version to hear how far it travelled. Seek the clearest remaster you can find."],"cole-porter-anything-goes":["A musical comedy in two acts set aboard an ocean liner crossing from New York to England, with a nightclub evangelist, a lovesick young Wall Street clerk in disguise, an English aristocrat and a gangster ranked Public Enemy Number Thirteen. The score is almost all hits: I Get a Kick Out of You, You're the Top, All Through the Night, Blow, Gabriel, Blow and the title song. The plot is farce; the point is a run of brilliant songs, each built to stop the show.","Anything Goes opened at the Alvin Theatre on Broadway in November 1934, with Ethel Merman as the singer Reno Sweeney, William Gaxton and Victor Moore. The original book by Guy Bolton and P. G. Wodehouse involved a shipwreck; after the Morro Castle liner burned off New Jersey that September with heavy loss of life, Howard Lindsay and Russel Crouse rewrote it. Prohibition had just ended, and the Depression audience wanted glamour and irreverence.","You're the Top is a catalogue of what was fashionable in 1934, from famous artworks to brand-name products and film stars, and many of its references now need footnotes. Porter kept revising such list songs for new productions. Modern revivals also quietly import songs from other Porter shows, among them It's De-Lovely and Friendship, so the Anything Goes most people see is partly a Porter anthology.","A hit from the start, running for over four hundred performances and making Merman a star with a voice that reached the back row unamplified. The book was always considered disposable, and later productions rewrote it freely. Revivals at Lincoln Center in 1987 with Patti LuPone and on Broadway in 2011 with Sutton Foster confirmed it as the most revivable musical comedy of its decade.","Start with Ethel Merman's recordings of I Get a Kick Out of You and Blow, Gabriel, Blow to hear the brassy style the songs were written for. Then watch a clip of the 2011 revival's title number, a long tap-dance showpiece. Listen to how Porter's rhymes land exactly on the beat, like a punchline."],"cole-porter-love-for-sale":["A song sung by a streetwalker advertising herself to passers-by at night, her tone moving between brisk salesmanship and weary bitterness about the love she has never known. The long refrain sits mostly in a minor key, built on insistent repeated notes that sound like a street cry, then brightens briefly into major before sinking back. The music never becomes sentimental; it keeps its distance, as the singer does.","Porter wrote it for The New Yorkers, a revue-like musical satire of Manhattan life that opened on Broadway in December 1930. It was first sung by Kathryn Crosby, and its frank subject, prostitution, provoked the critics and kept the song off the radio. The production responded by moving the number to a Harlem setting and giving it to the Black singer Elisabeth Welch, a change that exposes the racial assumptions of the day as much as its moral ones.","Listen to the harmony under the repeated notes: the melody stays almost still while the chords shift beneath it, so the same words seem to take on different moods as the night goes on. That static melody over moving harmony made it perfect for jazz improvisers, who could stretch the chord changes endlessly. Porter considered it one of his favourite songs, despite the controversy.","The broadcast ban did not stop it becoming a standard. Billie Holiday, Ella Fitzgerald and many others sang it, and it became even more popular as an instrumental: Cannonball Adderley and Miles Davis's version on Somethin' Else (1958) is a classic. Critics now hear it as one of Porter's most daring songs, an early Broadway number to look at commercial love without moralising.","Hear Ella Fitzgerald's version on the Cole Porter Songbook, cool and precise, then Billie Holiday's darker reading. Finally, play the Adderley and Davis instrumental and listen for how the muted trumpet treats the repeated notes of the melody. Notice how the minor mood is never resolved."],"cole-porter-evry-time-we-say-goodbye":["A quiet ballad of parting in which the singer describes dying a little each time a lover leaves, and wonders why heaven allows it. The melody hovers around a few notes, moving by small steps, as if unable to walk away. The harmony is rich and restless beneath it. The song is short, intimate and understated, with none of Porter's usual wordplay on display; its wit is buried in the music.","Porter wrote it for Seven Lively Arts, a lavish Broadway revue produced by Billy Rose in 1944, with Beatrice Lillie and Bert Lahr and ballet music by Stravinsky. The revue was not a great success, but this song, introduced by Nan Wynn, outlived it. The Second World War was in its final stages, and a song about repeated partings struck a chord with a public used to separation.","Here is Porter's cleverest musical joke: at the point where the lyric speaks of the shift from major to minor, the harmony itself makes that shift, so the listener hears the change as the words describe it. Listen for it in any version; the best singers lean on that moment. It is a rare case of a popular song explaining its own harmony while it happens.","It became one of Porter's most recorded ballads. Ella Fitzgerald's 1956 recording on the Cole Porter Songbook is a benchmark, and John Coltrane's version on My Favorite Things (1961) showed it could carry a jazz meditation. Annie Lennox's 1990 version for the Red Hot + Blue AIDS benefit album gave it another life. Critics count it among the most perfect songs in the American songbook.","Start with Ella Fitzgerald's version and listen for the major-to-minor moment. Then hear John Coltrane's instrumental, where the saxophone takes the singer's part, and Ray Charles and Betty Carter's duet. Each shows the song's quiet sadness in a different light."],"bessie-smith-back-water-blues":["A slow blues about a flood, written and sung by Bessie Smith with James P. Johnson at the piano. The singer tells of days of rain, water rising around her cabin, and fleeing to high ground with nowhere left to live. Smith's huge voice is controlled and grave, leaning on key words, while Johnson's piano answers each line with rolling figures that suggest moving water. The two work as partners, not singer and accompanist.","Recorded in New York in February 1927. Smith had probably been moved by floods on the Cumberland River around Nashville at the end of 1926. Weeks after the record appeared, the Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 broke levees across the South, displacing hundreds of thousands of people, mostly Black sharecroppers, many held in relief camps under armed guard. The song became linked with that disaster and sold widely in the affected regions.","Listen to Johnson's piano, not just the voice: he was the father of stride piano, and here he restrains his virtuoso style, using low rumbling figures and falling phrases that paint the flood. The song's power comes partly from its understatement; Smith describes the catastrophe in plain terms, without self-pity, and the calm of her delivery makes it more frightening.","It became one of Smith's best-selling records and one of the first blues to treat a natural disaster as a subject. Critics and scholars now read it as protest by implication, given how Black flood victims were treated in 1927. The historian David Evans's research into its origins in the Nashville flood has corrected the long-held idea that it was written about the Mississippi disaster.","Listen for how Smith leans on individual words, and for the piano's reply after each line. Then hear Charley Patton's High Water Everywhere, from 1929, to hear the same flood from the Delta, where it struck hardest. Compare the stately pace with the urban swagger of her St. Louis Blues."],"bessie-smith-empty-bed-blues":["A long blues in two parts, issued on both sides of one record, in which a woman remembers a lover's attentions in a string of double meanings and then laments the empty bed he has left. Bessie Smith sings with sly relish and then real grief, while Charlie Green's trombone slurs and growls in reply, almost a second voice mimicking hers. Porter Grainger's piano keeps a steady, unhurried pulse beneath them.","Recorded in New York for Columbia in March 1928, at the height of Smith's fame as the Empress of the Blues. The song was written by J. C. Johnson, a songwriter who worked with Fats Waller. Frank, humorous songs about desire were common in blues and vaudeville of the time, and Smith's audiences, especially Black women in the Northern cities, recognised both the joke and the loneliness behind it.","Listen to Green's trombone, which does more than accompany: it comments, teases and sometimes seems to laugh at the singer, making the record a kind of duet. The double meanings are delivered straight, which is what makes them work, and the second part turns from comedy to real sorrow. That turn from innuendo to loneliness is the heart of the classic blues.","It sold well and became one of Smith's signature records. Some later critics treated her comic material as lesser than her serious blues, but feminist scholars, notably Angela Davis in Blues Legacies and Black Feminism, argued that songs like this assert a woman's right to her own desire, a radical stance in 1928, and made them central to her legacy.","Listen to both parts together and notice how the mood shifts from teasing to heartbreak. Follow the trombone's replies after each line. Then compare Ma Rainey's Prove It on Me Blues, another song where a woman sets her own terms, sung in an earthier style."],"james-p-johnson-snowy-morning-blues":["A solo piano blues taken at an unhurried, strolling pace, three minutes of stride playing turned inward. The left hand still does the stride work, leaping between a low bass note and a chord in the middle of the keyboard, but gently, almost on tiptoe. Over it the right hand plays a tender, singing melody decorated with small crushed notes and soft tremolos. It is the sound of a virtuoso choosing restraint, and the mood is wintry calm rather than rent-party heat.","Johnson recorded it for Columbia in 1927, when he was the acknowledged dean of Harlem piano: the teacher of Fats Waller, the composer of 'The Charleston', the man whose cutting-contest pieces every young pianist in New York had to learn. The same year he accompanied Bessie Smith on record. Harlem's rent parties and cabarets expected fireworks from stride players; this piece showed that the style could also carry melancholy and lyrical line.","Listen to how rarely the left hand does the full stride leap. Johnson keeps breaking the pattern with walking bass lines and held chords, which lets the harmony breathe and gives the piece its hushed quality. The blues here is a mood and a harmonic colouring as much as a form; the tune moves in and out of the strict twelve-bar pattern, closer to a parlour piece than a juke-joint blues.","It has become one of Johnson's most loved recordings, often cited alongside 'Carolina Shout' as proof that he was a composer, not merely a technician. Johnson recorded it again in the 1940s during the traditional-jazz revival, and pianists still argue about which version is better. Critics now hear in it the bridge between ragtime's formality and the ruminative jazz ballad playing that followed.","Play it straight after 'Carolina Shout' to hear the same technique used for opposite ends: display there, reverie here. Keep your ear on the left hand and notice every time it stops leaping. Then try Willie 'The Lion' Smith's 'Echoes of Spring' and Thelonious Monk's solo recordings, where the stride inheritance survives in a quieter, more private voice."],"james-p-johnson-youve-got-to-be-modernistic":["A fast, knotty stride showpiece in several strains, built like a rag but harmonically restless. The right hand darts through chromatic runs and sliding, whole-tone passages that sound deliberately off-balance, while the left hand keeps an unbroken, heavy stride underneath. Every strain seems to swerve away from where the ear expects it to land. The effect is of a Harlem pianist cheerfully showing that he has been listening to the modern concert hall and can beat it at its own game.","Johnson recorded the piece for Brunswick in 1930, as the Depression was closing the Harlem cabarets and the recording industry was collapsing. The title is a knowing joke: 'modernistic' was a fashionable word for Art Deco and for the new sounds of Debussy, Stravinsky and Gershwin, which Johnson admired and studied. He had ambitions as a concert composer, having already written 'Yamekraw', and this piece brings that ambition into the cutting-contest arena.","The strange, floating passages are built on whole-tone harmony, the same scale Debussy used to blur his textures, dropped here into a form that otherwise obeys ragtime's rules. Johnson places them where a rag would normally have its most predictable cadence, so the modernism arrives exactly where tradition was most expected. Pianists learning the piece find the left hand the easy part.","It was a test piece among Harlem pianists and remains one of the hardest items in the stride repertoire. Later jazz historians saw it as evidence that the stride school had harmonic curiosity long before bebop, and the young Thelonious Monk's angular approach has often been traced to this kind of playing. Some listeners find it more clever than moving; its admirers say the cleverness is the point.","Listen first for the moments where the harmony seems to lose its footing, then for how quickly the left hand drags it back to earth. Compare it with Johnson's own 'Carolina Shout' to hear how far he pushed his style in nine years, and with Gershwin's Three Preludes, the concert-hall cousin he was answering. Seek a clean transfer of the 1930 recording."],"jimmie-rodgers-waiting-for-a-train":["A hobo's story told in the first person at an easy, loping tempo. The singer has been thrown off a freight train far from home, has no money in his pocket, and waits by the tracks while a brakeman refuses him a ride. Rodgers sings it with a smile in his voice and ends phrases in his trademark yodel, which here doubles as a train whistle. Behind him a small studio band adds jaunty cornet and clarinet lines and the sigh of a steel guitar.","Rodgers recorded it for Victor in 1928, a year after his debut at the Bristol sessions made him the first national star of what would become country music. A former railway brakeman forced off the job by tuberculosis, he was known as the Singing Brakeman, and train songs were his natural territory. The song itself drew on older hobo material in circulation, and the late 1920s had millions of Americans riding the rails in search of work.","The backing band is closer to a vaudeville or jazz combo than to a string band, a reminder that Rodgers borrowed freely from blues, jazz and Tin Pan Alley rather than from any pure rural tradition. Listen for how the yodel at the end of each verse bends downward like the Doppler fall of a passing whistle: the sound effect and the vocal flourish are the same gesture.","It became one of Rodgers's best-selling records and one of the founding texts of the country train song. Merle Haggard devoted a whole tribute album to Rodgers's songs, and this one has been recorded by generations of country and folk singers. Historians now stress its mixed ancestry, blues, minstrelsy and hobo balladry, as evidence that early country was never as isolated as its marketing claimed.","Start with the voice: notice how relaxed Rodgers sounds while describing destitution, a tone that would define the country ballad. Then listen for the cornet's small answers between vocal lines. Follow it with Woody Guthrie's Dust Bowl songs, which inherit the drifter's viewpoint, and with Elvis Presley's 'Mystery Train' for the train song's later, darker form."],"jimmie-rodgers-in-the-jailhouse-now":["A comic song told as gossip about a friend. The singer describes an acquaintance who gambled, cheated and swaggered around town until the law caught up with him, and each verse lands on the gleeful refrain that he is in the jailhouse now. Rodgers accompanies himself on guitar with a bouncing bass-and-strum pattern and caps the choruses with long, showy yodels that turn the jailhouse into a punchline. It is closer to a vaudeville turn than a ballad, and meant to be laughed at.","The song predates Rodgers: it was a vaudeville and jug-band number circulating in the 1910s and 1920s, part of the shared stock of Black and white entertainers in the South. Rodgers recorded his version for Victor in 1928, and its success led him to cut a sequel, 'In the Jailhouse Now No. 2', in 1930. His audience was rural and small-town America, newly reachable through cheap phonographs and radio.","The song's roots lie in the minstrel and Black vaudeville stage, and Rodgers's phrasing keeps traces of that comic delivery, with its knowing pauses before the punchline. Listen for how he stretches the yodel longer on each chorus, turning a novelty into a small virtuoso display. The yodel is doing the work a comedian's raised eyebrow would do on stage.","It was among Rodgers's biggest hits and one of the songs that proved his range went beyond the blue yodels. In 1955 Webb Pierce took it to the top of the country charts, and it has since passed through bluegrass, western swing and folk hands. Scholars cite it as a clear case of early country's debt to African American popular song and the vaudeville circuit.","Listen for the timing of the refrain and the grin in Rodgers's voice; the fun is in the delivery. Then hear Webb Pierce's 1955 version to measure how honky-tonk smoothed and amplified the joke. For the blues cousin of this kind of storytelling, try Ma Rainey, and for the grimmer prison song that country later developed, Johnny Cash at Folsom Prison."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-8":["A blues in the voice of a mule driver looking for work, boasting to a boss about how hard he can drive a team and asking for a job. Rodgers sings alone with his guitar, alternating a thumping bass string with bright strums, and closes each verse with the high, cracking falsetto yodel that gave the Blue Yodel series its name. The verses are brief and blunt, and the yodels are wilder than on the earlier records, almost a holler.","Rodgers recorded it in 1930, near the height of his fame and while tuberculosis was steadily weakening him; he would die three years later. The Blue Yodels, thirteen of them, were his signature: white country singing fused with the twelve-bar blues and the African American work song. The muleskinner, the man who drove mule teams on road and construction gangs, was a familiar Southern labouring figure in songs of both races.","Much of the text comes from older African American work songs and blues verses about mule drivers and road camps, floating lines Rodgers stitched together. The famous opening call, a boast delivered almost as a shout, is where later versions put their emphasis. Listen to how the guitar drops out or thins under the yodel, leaving the voice exposed.","The song had an unusually rich afterlife. Bill Monroe sang it at his Grand Ole Opry debut in 1939 and recorded it in 1940 at a furious tempo, effectively announcing bluegrass; Dolly Parton had a hit with it in 1970; rock and roll bands revived it too. Rodgers's original is now often heard through those later versions, but critics prize its looser, bluesier feel.","Play Rodgers's 1930 recording and then Bill Monroe's 1940 version back to back: the same song at twice the speed becomes a new genre. Notice how Rodgers treats the yodel as a blues cry rather than an Alpine ornament. Then hear Dolly Parton's version for how a woman singer claimed a song of male bravado."],"bechet-petite-fleur":["A slow, minor-key melody for soprano saxophone over a gently rocking band accompaniment. Bechet plays the tune almost as a song without words, with his famously wide, fast vibrato giving every long note a trembling, nearly operatic intensity. The structure is simple, a theme stated, answered and returned, and there is little improvisation in the modern sense. What makes it recognisable is the tone itself: a reedy, sobbing sound that fills the room on the first note.","Bechet, born in New Orleans in 1897, had spent years underappreciated in the United States. After a triumphant appearance at the 1949 Paris Jazz Fair he settled in France, where young audiences treated him as a living legend of the music's origins. He wrote 'Petite fleur' and recorded it in 1952 with a French traditional jazz band. It became a huge seller in France, part of a Bechet craze that made him a national celebrity there.","It is a composed melody, not a jazz standard reworked, and it owes as much to French popular song and Creole dance tunes as to the blues. Listen for how the band stays deliberately plain so nothing competes with the saxophone's vibrato. When the Chris Barber band scored a transatlantic hit with it in 1959, clarinettist Monty Sunshine played it straighter, which shows how much of the original lives in Bechet's tone alone.","In France it became one of the most familiar melodies of the 1950s, recorded endlessly and still heard on radio and in films. Jazz critics have sometimes treated it as sentimental, a step away from Bechet's fiercer 1920s and 1930s records. Others argue that it shows the lyrical heart of the New Orleans style and that Bechet's tone has rarely been matched on the soprano.","Listen first to the sound, not the tune: the width and speed of the vibrato, the way long notes swell. Then compare his 1939 'Summertime' to hear the same voice in a Gershwin ballad. Chris Barber's 1959 version is worth hearing for contrast, and Django Reinhardt's 'Nuages' makes a fine companion piece of Parisian jazz melancholy."],"bechet-sheik-of-araby":["A three-minute band performance of a 1921 Tin Pan Alley novelty song in which every instrument is played by one man. Bechet recorded rhythm parts first and then added horn lines on top, so you hear soprano saxophone, clarinet and tenor saxophone weaving New Orleans-style counterpoint over his own piano, bass and drums. The feel is a relaxed medium swing, and the lead soprano has all his usual heat and vibrato.","Bechet made the recording for Victor in 1941, using a laborious disc-to-disc process: each new part was played along with a playback of the previous layers and captured to a new disc, with sound quality degrading at each pass. Magnetic tape, which made overdubbing routine, was still years away in American studios. The song, by Ted Snyder, cashed in on the Rudolph Valentino film The Sheik and was a jazz staple.","Because the rhythm section went down first, it is the least assured part of the record: Bechet was no professional drummer or bassist, and you can hear the time-keeping held a little stiffly. Listen for the horn parts, where he plays both the lead and the answering harmony lines in true New Orleans polyphony, effectively jamming with himself across time. Each added layer sounds slightly further away.","At the time it was treated as a novelty stunt and reportedly caused unease at the musicians' union, which may have seen a threat to paid work. Historians now regard it as one of the first multitrack recordings in popular music, several years before Les Paul made overdubbing famous. Musically, critics rate it below Bechet's great band sides, but its place in recording history is secure.","Listen once for the soprano lead only, then again trying to follow the clarinet and tenor lines underneath it; you are hearing a collective improvisation with a single mind. Notice the slight haze on the earlier layers. Compare with 'Wild Cat Blues' from 1923 to hear how his playing had deepened, and think ahead to the overdubbed studio records of the rock era."],"dorsey-if-you-see-my-savior":["A slow gospel song addressed to a dying friend: the singer asks the one who is departing to carry a message to Jesus, saying that the singer is still on the way and will follow. The melody is plain enough for a congregation, but it moves with the lilt and bent notes of the blues, and the piano part rolls rather than marching like a hymn accompaniment. It is intimate, almost conversational, a farewell spoken at a bedside.","Dorsey had been Georgia Tom, pianist for Ma Rainey and a successful blues and hokum songwriter, when a spiritual crisis in the mid-1920s drew him toward church music. He wrote this song after the death of a neighbour, and for some time Black Baptist churches in Chicago resisted his bluesy style. The breakthrough came when it was sung at the 1930 National Baptist Convention, and sheet-music orders poured in.","Its conceit, a message sent to heaven by a messenger about to die, is a folk idea found in older spirituals, but Dorsey turns it into personal speech rather than communal testimony. That shift, from 'we' to 'I', is the essence of gospel song as he created it. Listen for the piano's blues fills between vocal lines: they come straight from his years accompanying Ma Rainey.","Historians of gospel treat the song's 1930 success as the moment the genre became a movement, the point after which Dorsey could build choirs, a publishing house and a national convention. Some church leaders called his music too worldly; the argument about blues in the sanctuary lasted decades. The song itself is less performed now than his later hits, but its historical place is fixed.","Listen for the blues inflections in a song about the most sacred of subjects: that mixture is the whole Dorsey revolution. Pair it with his secular hit 'It's Tight Like That' from 1928 to hear the same pianist on both sides of the line. Then go to 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' to hear where this style led him."],"dorsey-peace-in-the-valley":["A slow, consoling gospel song that imagines a heaven of rest after a hard life: a valley with no sorrow or trouble, where wild animals lie down peacefully together. The tune is broad and hymn-like, in a comfortable major key, rising to a chorus built for a congregation to sing in harmony. Compared with Dorsey's bluesier songs it is gentle and almost pastoral, which is exactly why it travelled so far beyond Black churches.","Dorsey wrote it in the late 1930s, by his own account while travelling by train through the countryside as war news darkened in Europe, and intended it for Mahalia Jackson. He was by then the organising force of the gospel world, running a publishing company and the National Convention of Gospel Choirs and Choruses. The song reached white audiences through Red Foley's 1951 country recording, which sold heavily.","The picture of the peaceable kingdom, the lion and the lamb together, comes from the Book of Isaiah, and Dorsey's verses quietly weave in the Bible's pastoral imagery rather than the fiery testimony typical of his earlier songs. Listen for how the chorus leaves space for a bass voice to move under the melody: it was written with quartet harmony in mind.","Elvis Presley sang it on The Ed Sullivan Show in January 1957 and recorded an EP of it, bringing Dorsey's work to a huge new audience; Johnny Cash, Mahalia Jackson and countless country and gospel artists followed. Some writers see it as the moment Black gospel entered white country music as a standard. Its gentleness has made it a funeral favourite across both traditions.","Hear Mahalia Jackson's version first, then Red Foley's and Elvis Presley's, and notice how the song adapts to each voice without losing its calm. Listen for the quartet harmonies in the chorus. Pair it with the Carter Family's 'Can the Circle Be Unbroken' and Hank Williams's 'I Saw the Light' to hear the shared ground of Black and white Southern sacred song."],"dorsey-the-lord-will-make-a-way-somehow":["A slow gospel song of endurance: the singer admits to heavy burdens, loneliness and hard roads, and answers each admission with the conviction that God will find a way through. The melody moves in long, stretched phrases that invite a soloist to slide between notes and build intensity with each verse, while the chorus is simple enough for a choir to answer. Over a rolling gospel piano, a good performance climbs from quiet confession to full-voiced affirmation.","By the early 1940s Dorsey was the most important publisher and teacher in gospel music, working from Pilgrim Baptist Church in Chicago and selling his songs as printed sheets through a national network of choirs. Wartime America, with families separated and Black migrants still pouring into Northern cities, gave songs of perseverance a wide audience. This one became a staple of the church repertoire that his convention spread across the country.","Like many Dorsey songs, the printed version is only a skeleton. The written melody is plain; the song's character comes from the embellishment a gospel singer adds, the moans, repeated words and held climactic notes. Listen for how performers repeat the title phrase more and more insistently near the end: that repetition is part of the expected form, not an accident of the moment.","It has never had the fame of 'Precious Lord' or 'Peace in the Valley', but within Black churches it has been sung constantly for eighty years and is found in many hymnals. Gospel historians cite it as typical of Dorsey's mature style: the blues largely absorbed into a smoother, more devotional idiom. Some prefer the rawer early songs; congregations have voted with their voices.","Listen for the arc: where the singer starts quietly and where the voice finally opens out. Try to hear the gap between the plain printed tune and what the singer does with it. Then compare 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' for the same themes of weakness and trust, and Clara Ward's 'Surely God Is Able' for the gospel ballad at full power."],"hawkins-crazy-rhythm":["A fast, buoyant small-band swing record built around a four-saxophone section: Coleman Hawkins and Benny Carter with two French saxophonists. Carter's arrangement sets the reeds in bright, tightly voiced passages, then clears space for solos, with Hawkins's broad, muscular tenor as the centrepiece. Underneath, Django Reinhardt's guitar drives the rhythm with a crisp, chopping swing. It is three minutes of transatlantic jazz at its most confident.","Hawkins had left Fletcher Henderson's orchestra in 1934 and spent five years in Europe, where he was treated as a star. In Paris in 1937 he joined Carter, Reinhardt, Stéphane Grappelli (playing piano on this date) and French musicians for a session for Charles Delaunay's new Swing label, a company founded to document jazz in France. The song, by Irving Caesar, Joseph Meyer and Roger Wolfe Kahn, was a 1928 Broadway number.","Listen for Grappelli, known as a violinist, sitting at the piano, and for the difference between the American and French saxophonists in tone: the visitors' sound is darker and heavier. The session also produced 'Honeysuckle Rose'. Carter's writing makes four saxes sound like a full reed section, a Henderson-style trick transported to Paris.","These records were treated as landmarks in France, proof that European musicians could hold their own with Americans, and they were widely admired in the United States too. Critics single out Hawkins's solo and Reinhardt's rhythm playing. Today the session is regarded as one of the high points of 1930s small-group swing and a founding document of the European jazz scene.","Follow Reinhardt's guitar first, then listen for how Hawkins's solo rides over it with a completely different kind of swing, heavier and more harmonic. Compare with Django's 'Minor Swing' for the Parisian side of the story and with 'Body and Soul', recorded two years later after Hawkins returned to New York, to hear his ballad style at its peak."],"hawkins-woodyn-you":["A brisk, dark-hued piece with a restless theme that slides through unusual chord changes, played by a twelve-piece band led by Coleman Hawkins. The melody, by Dizzy Gillespie, avoids the comfortable resolutions of swing tunes, and the solos, especially Gillespie's darting trumpet, sound quicker and more angular than anything on a standard dance record. Hawkins's own solo is fuller and more rounded, a swing master fully at ease with the new harmony.","Recorded in New York in February 1944 for the small Apollo label, the session is widely called the first recording of bebop. Hawkins, then the most respected tenor player in jazz, chose to hire the young modernists: Gillespie, bassist Oscar Pettiford and drummer Max Roach among them. A musicians' union recording ban had just ended for smaller labels, so the new music being worked out at Minton's and on 52nd Street had gone unrecorded until now.","Gillespie titled the tune for bandleader Woody Herman, who had shown interest in his writing. Its harmonic path, chords moving by half steps rather than the usual circle, was exactly the kind of thing Hawkins had long explored on his own, which is why he was comfortable with it. Listen to Max Roach, still a teenager, moving the time onto the cymbals.","At the time it was a minor release on a minor label, but historians now treat it as a milestone and Hawkins as the old master who opened the studio door to bebop. The same week's sessions also produced 'Disorder at the Border' and 'Rainbow Mist', a thinly disguised 'Body and Soul'. Some argue the band is still halfway between swing and bop; that is part of its interest.","Listen for the contrast between Gillespie's trumpet and Hawkins's tenor: two generations in one chorus. Then play Gillespie's 'Groovin' High' from 1945 and Charlie Parker's 'Ko-Ko' to hear the style fully formed. Going back to 'Body and Soul' shows how Hawkins's harmonic thinking pointed toward this moment all along."],"hawkins-the-man-i-love":["A quartet performance of Gershwin's ballad that is not played as a ballad at all: after a short, soft opening, the band shifts into a steady medium tempo and Hawkins unfolds a long, rolling improvisation. His tenor sound is huge and warm, with a husky edge, and the solo builds chorus by chorus in intensity and range. The bass takes a prominent, melodic solo, and the whole record runs over five minutes, long for its day.","It was recorded in New York in December 1943 for the small Signature label, with pianist Eddie Heywood, bassist Oscar Pettiford and drummer Shelly Manne. Signature issued it on a twelve-inch 78, which allowed about five minutes instead of the usual three, giving Hawkins room to stretch. The wartime recording ban was lifting label by label, and small independent companies were stepping in to document jazz the majors ignored.","The longer disc format is the secret of the record: Hawkins could develop an idea over several choruses the way he did in clubs, rather than squeezing it into a single statement. Listen to Pettiford's solo, which treats the bass as a melodic voice in a way that anticipates the bebop bassists. The Gershwin tune itself is barely stated; the harmony is what Hawkins plays on.","Critics have long ranked it alongside 'Body and Soul' among Hawkins's finest records, and some prefer it for its sustained momentum. It was a model for tenor players of the next generation, notably Sonny Rollins, who studied Hawkins closely. The rivalry with Lester Young's lighter approach, much debated at the time, frames how it is heard today.","Let the solo carry you: notice how each chorus starts a little higher or pushes a little harder than the last. Listen for the rhythm section lifting behind him. Then compare Lester Young's 'These Foolish Things' from two years later, the opposite philosophy of tenor playing, and Sonny Rollins's Saxophone Colossus to hear Hawkins's legacy in the next generation."],"basie-april-in-paris":["A big-band album by the so-called New Testament Basie orchestra, crisp and tightly drilled, with arrangements by members such as Frank Foster and Ernie Wilkins. The title track, a Vernon Duke ballad turned into a swaggering medium-tempo swinger, ends with the band stopping and restarting twice at Basie's spoken urging. Elsewhere Foster's 'Shiny Stockings' and Freddie Green's 'Corner Pocket' show the band's blend of loud brass punches and Basie's sparse, witty piano.","In 1950 Basie had been forced to break up his big band for economic reasons, leading a small group for a while. By 1952 he had rebuilt a new orchestra, disciplined and heavily arranged, and it became one of the most successful bands of the decade, recording for Norman Granz's labels. The sessions that produced this album took place in 1955 and 1956, and the title track became a surprise hit single.","The famous arrangement of 'April in Paris' was adapted from organist Wild Bill Davis's version, and the double false ending, with Basie's calls for one more time, became a concert ritual that audiences waited for. Listen to Thad Jones's trumpet solo, which drops in a snatch of a nursery tune. The rhythm guitar of Freddie Green is the quiet engine under every track.","It was one of Basie's best-selling records and the title track was later inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame. Some critics preferred the looser 1930s band with Lester Young and felt the new orchestra was too polished. Others hear the 1950s band as a peak of ensemble power and precision, a model for every big band that followed, including the studio bands of television.","Start with the title track and wait for the endings, then listen for how loud the brass is and how little Basie plays: a few notes on piano do the work of a whole chorus. Try 'Shiny Stockings' for the classic medium-tempo Basie swing. Compare with 'One O'Clock Jump' from 1937 to hear how the old band's riff style became a precision machine."],"basie-taxi-war-dance":["A fast, light-footed Basie band record built less on written arrangement than on riffs, short repeated phrases tossed between sections, and on solos. Lester Young opens up with a floating, airy tenor solo that is the heart of the record; later soloists trade brief breaks in quick succession. Beneath everything is the celebrated All-American Rhythm Section of Basie's piano, Freddie Green's guitar, Walter Page's bass and Jo Jones's drums, pulsing evenly with almost no weight.","It was recorded in 1939 for Vocalion, at the height of the band's first great period, shortly after the death of tenor saxophonist Herschel Evans; Buddy Tate had taken his chair. The Basie band had come to New York from Kansas City in 1936, bringing the looser, blues-based Southwestern way of swinging. The title nods to the taxi-dance halls, where men paid by the dance to partner hostesses.","Much of the arrangement was worked out by ear rather than written: the riffs behind the soloists were so-called head arrangements, agreed in rehearsal and carried in memory. Listen to how little Basie himself plays, a few spare notes left in the gaps. The rapid exchanges near the end, where soloists trade short breaks, show the band's cutting-session roots.","Among jazz musicians, Young's solo on this record was studied and memorised, and it is regularly singled out in histories of the saxophone. Critics hear it as one of the purest examples of Kansas City swing, with its emphasis on relaxed momentum over showy arrangement. Its reputation has only grown as Young's influence on cool jazz and bebop has become clearer.","Put Lester Young's opening solo on repeat and hear how he seems to glide above the beat rather than pushing it. Then listen for the rhythm section alone: that even, weightless four-to-the-bar is the Basie sound. Compare with 'Lester Leaps In' from the same year, and with Hawkins's 'Body and Soul' for the rival tenor style."],"lester-young-oh-lady-be-good":["A small-group version of Gershwin's 1924 show tune, played at a bright, easy tempo by a quintet drawn from Count Basie's band. After a short statement of the theme, Lester Young takes a two-chorus solo that is the reason the record is famous: light-toned, relaxed, full of long, floating lines that start and end in unexpected places. Basie's spare piano and the soft, even pulse of bass and drums leave the saxophone plenty of air.","It was recorded in Chicago in November 1936, at Young's first recording session, for Vocalion under the name Jones-Smith Incorporated, a pseudonym because Basie had just signed with Decca. The producer John Hammond had heard the band on radio from Kansas City and brought them east. The same session produced 'Shoe Shine Boy'. Young's airy tone was then controversial; most tenor players followed Coleman Hawkins's big sound.","Listen to where Young's phrases begin: often a beat or two after or before the obvious place, so they seem to float across the song's structure rather than following it. He also uses fewer notes than you expect, leaving silence as part of the line. Young musicians in Kansas City, the teenage Charlie Parker among them, reportedly learned this solo note for note.","The solo is one of the most studied in jazz, transcribed endlessly and treated as the birth of a new tenor saxophone style. Critics now hear in it the seeds of both bebop, through Parker, and cool jazz, through players like Stan Getz and Lee Konitz. At the time, some listeners heard Young's light tone as weak; that judgment has been entirely reversed.","Hum the tune once so you know the song's shape, then follow how Young's solo plays across it. Compare it straight away with Coleman Hawkins's 'Body and Soul' to hear the two poles of swing tenor. Then listen to Lee Konitz or Stan Getz to hear where Young's approach led a decade later."],"lester-young-these-foolish-things":["A ballad performance in which Young almost never plays the melody as written. The song, a 1936 British standard about how small objects bring back a lost love, is suggested rather than stated: Young paraphrases, bends and reshapes it from the first bar, and the listener hears the tune as a memory hovering behind his lines. His tone is softer and more veiled than in the 1930s, and the tempo is slow and unhurried, with a quiet rhythm section.","Young recorded it in Los Angeles in December 1945 for the small Aladdin label, shortly after his release from the US Army. He had been drafted in 1944 and, after a disciplinary case, spent months in a detention barracks, an experience friends said left him deeply scarred. Postwar Los Angeles had a thriving independent record scene, and Young's comeback recordings found a large audience despite the change in his manner.","Listen for how long it takes to recognise the song: Young begins with a variation, and the original melody only surfaces in fragments. That approach, playing the ballad's meaning rather than its notes, is the key to his late style. Critics have long argued over whether the new wistfulness reflects his army ordeal; the music itself only records a changed voice.","For decades writers said Young declined after the war, and his later recordings were often dismissed. That view has shifted: many now regard this record and the Aladdin sessions as among his most personal work. The ballad style heard here fed directly into cool jazz and into players like Stan Getz, who adopted Young's paraphrase technique.","Listen for the moment you first recognise the song, and then for how quickly Young slides away from it again. Pay attention to the softness of his attack. Compare with Coleman Hawkins's 'The Man I Love' from two years earlier: the same era, opposite approaches to a ballad. Then try Billie Holiday, his closest musical partner, singing in a similar spirit."],"lester-young-with-the-oscar-peterson-trio":["A relaxed session of standards and blues, with Young's tenor accompanied by Oscar Peterson's piano trio and a drummer. The pieces include ballads such as 'I Can't Get Started' and 'These Foolish Things', breezy mid-tempo songs like 'Just You, Just Me' and 'Tea for Two', and a long, unhurried 'Ad Lib Blues'. Young's tone is dark and soft, his phrasing laid back; Peterson's crisp, swinging accompaniment gives him a firm, comfortable floor.","Norman Granz recorded the session in 1952 for his Clef and Norgran labels, where he was assembling a catalogue of the great swing soloists alongside his Jazz at the Philharmonic tours. Young had become a JATP regular, well paid but increasingly unwell and troubled. Granz paired him with Peterson, his house pianist, and the rhythm team from the tours, trusting that familiar songs and friendly company would draw out his best.","Young sings on one track, 'It Takes Two to Tango', in a quiet, offhand voice, a rare vocal from him on record and a useful clue to how he thought about the words of songs while playing. Listen to how often he quotes other tunes briefly and how he lets phrases trail off instead of finishing them.","It has become the most admired of Young's 1950s records, often chosen as proof that his late period deserves better than the verdict of decline. Some critics find Peterson's busy style too crowded for Young's airy playing; others hear a warm meeting of generations. Reissues on CD added alternate takes, and the complete session is the version most often recommended now.","Start with 'Ad Lib Blues' to hear Young at his most relaxed, then 'I Can't Get Started' for the ballad side. Listen for the space between his phrases and how Peterson fills it. Compare with his 1936 'Oh, Lady Be Good!' to hear what time did to the tone, and with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours for the same era's ballad mood in song."],"robert-johnson-sweet-home-chicago":["A solo blues sung to a driving, shuffling guitar pattern: Johnson plays a rolling, boogie-woogie figure on the low strings with his thumb while adding treble fills, so one guitar sounds like piano bass and lead together. The song is an invitation to go north to Chicago, told as a restless longing for somewhere better. His voice is high, taut and urgent, and the momentum never lets up across its three minutes.","Johnson recorded it in San Antonio in November 1936, at the first of his two sessions for the American Record Corporation, produced by Don Law. Chicago was the promised city of the Great Migration, drawing hundreds of thousands of Black Southerners to factory work, and its South Side already had a thriving blues recording scene. The song was built on earlier records, especially Kokomo Arnold's 1934 'Old Original Kokomo Blues'.","Listen to the guitar: the walking bass pattern Johnson adapted from boogie-woogie piano became the basic shuffle of postwar Chicago blues, played by bands with an electric bass and a drummer. The song's geography is famously muddled, placing Chicago somewhere far to the west, which some hear as confusion and others as a coded or joking dream-map rather than a real place.","Like all Johnson's records it sold modestly on release, and it was only after the 1961 reissue King of the Delta Blues Singers that his legend grew. Junior Parker, Magic Sam and many others recorded it, and the 1980 film The Blues Brothers turned it into a party anthem. Scholars now stress its debt to Arnold, complicating the image of Johnson as a lone originator.","Focus first on the guitar's bass line and hear how it pushes the whole song forward. Then compare Kokomo Arnold's earlier record to hear what Johnson took and what he added. Go forward to Elmore James and Muddy Waters to hear the shuffle electrified, and to any live Chicago blues band, where this song is still a standard closer."],"woody-guthrie-roll-on-columbia":["A strophic song with an easy, rolling waltz-time chorus that urges the Columbia River onward. The verses move from the river's natural grandeur to its history, explorers, settlers and conflicts with Native peoples, and then to the dams being built to harness it for electricity and irrigation. Guthrie sings over a plain strummed guitar. The effect is part hymn, part civic pageant, part travelogue: a love song to a river and to the public works taming it.","In May 1941 the Bonneville Power Administration, a federal agency building dams on the Columbia, hired Guthrie for one month to write songs for a documentary film promoting public power. Working from Portland, Oregon, and touring the region, he wrote some two dozen songs, including this one, 'Grand Coulee Dam' and 'Pastures of Plenty'. The New Deal was betting that cheap electricity would lift the rural Northwest out of poverty.","The tune is borrowed from Lead Belly's 'Goodnight, Irene', which Guthrie knew well from New York; he often set new words to melodies already in circulation. Listen for how the familiar waltz feel turns a love song into a river song. The documentary the songs were written for was not completed until years later, so the songs spread mainly through recordings and singalongs.","It became one of Guthrie's best-loved songs and in 1987 was named the official folk song of Washington State. Some later listeners find its celebration of dam-building uncomfortable, given the damage to salmon runs and to Native fishing communities, and folk singers now sometimes introduce it with that history. It remains the central song of the Columbia River cycle.","Hear Lead Belly's 'Goodnight, Irene' first, then Guthrie's song, and notice how the same melody changes character. Listen for the verses that list places and events: Guthrie is writing a map in song. Follow it with 'Grand Coulee Dam' and 'Pastures of Plenty' from the same month, and with Pete Seeger for how the next generation carried it."],"woody-guthrie-deportee":["A slow, mournful ballad in waltz time about Mexican farmworkers killed in a plane crash while being deported. The verses describe the crops rotting in the fields, the workers sent back over the border again and again, and the fire in the sky over the canyon; the chorus bids farewell to the dead by the names of ordinary people, since the newspapers gave them none. The melody is gentle and sorrowful, which makes the anger in the words cut deeper.","On 28 January 1948 a chartered plane carrying Mexican migrant workers being returned to Mexico crashed in Los Gatos Canyon in central California, killing everyone aboard. News reports named the American crew but described the passengers only as deportees. Guthrie, then in New York, wrote a poem in response. It was sung only after a schoolteacher, Martin Hoffman, set it to music about a decade later, and Pete Seeger took it up.","Guthrie never recorded the song; the familiar tune is Hoffman's. For decades the victims remained unnamed, which is the song's whole subject. In 2013, after research by the writer Tim Z. Hernandez, a memorial in Fresno finally recorded all their names, closing a circle the song had opened. Listen for how the chorus's list of generic names works as a quiet accusation.","It has become one of Guthrie's most performed songs, recorded by Joan Baez, Judy Collins, Bob Dylan and the country supergroup the Highwaymen, among many others. It is often cited as one of the finest protest songs about immigration, and its relevance has been renewed with every argument over migrant labour. Critics praise its restraint: no villain is named, only the system.","Start with a plain voice-and-guitar performance, perhaps Pete Seeger's or Joan Baez's, and listen to the words over the waltz. Then hear the Highwaymen's version for the song's reach into mainstream country. Pair it with Billie Holiday's 'Strange Fruit', another song that forces a nation to look at the dead, and with Springsteen's Nebraska for its heirs."],"billie-holiday-lady-in-satin":["Twelve torch songs sung over lush string arrangements by Ray Ellis, with harp, muted brass, a small wordless choir and a jazz rhythm section tucked underneath. The pace is slow from first track to last: I'm a Fool to Want You opens, The End of a Love Affair closes, and in between come You've Changed, Violets for Your Furs and Glad to Be Unhappy. The sound you remember is the collision of a cushioned, almost cinematic orchestra with a voice that has gone hoarse, cracked and thin.","Recorded in New York in February 1958 for Columbia, the label where Holiday had made her small-group sides with Teddy Wilson two decades earlier. She asked for Ellis after hearing his own album of string arrangements. By then years of heroin addiction, arrests, heavy drinking and the loss of her New York cabaret card had worn her down physically; she was forty-two. It was the last album released in her lifetime, and she died in July 1959.","Listen for how she phrases behind the beat, sometimes by almost a whole bar, so the strings seem to be waiting for her. Much of the power lies in what she cannot do any more: long notes waver, so she shortens them and leans on the words instead. The choir is easy to miss, humming under a few tracks like a second, softer string section. Later reissues add alternate takes that show how carefully each song was built.","Opinion has split since release. Some listeners heard only decline, a great instrument in ruins wrapped in schmaltzy strings; others heard the most naked singing she ever did. Ellis later said he was dismayed by her condition in the studio and only understood the record afterwards. Holiday herself reportedly counted it among her favourites. Its standing has risen steadily, and singers from Nina Simone's generation to Amy Winehouse's are often discussed in its shadow.","Do not start here if you have never heard Holiday; play her 1930s sides with Teddy Wilson first, then come to this. Begin with I'm a Fool to Want You and You've Changed, listening to the gap between the smooth orchestra and the rough voice. Then compare Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours, a younger singer doing a similar late-night album with his voice at full strength."],"billie-holiday-fine-and-mellow":["A slow twelve-bar blues, one of the very few true blues Holiday wrote and recorded. A small band from the Café Society club sets up a relaxed, walking groove; muted trumpet and reeds answer her lines, and a short instrumental chorus sits in the middle. She sings about a man who mistreats her and about love as something that runs hot and cold, delivered not as a wail but with a dry, knowing half-smile in the voice.","Recorded in April 1939 for Milt Gabler's small Commodore label, because Columbia would not touch Strange Fruit. Fine and Mellow was the other side of that record, a commercial blues to balance the anti-lynching song, and for a while it was the side jukeboxes played. Holiday was then singing nightly at Barney Josephson's integrated Café Society in Greenwich Village, and the session used musicians from that circle.","The song is built from blues commonplaces, floating verses that singers had traded for decades, and Holiday's authorship credit partly means she chose and ordered them. The real invention is timing: she lands consonants late and lets vowels die early, so the lyric feels spoken. Notice that she never raises her voice once. The whole performance is pitched a notch below what a Bessie Smith or Ma Rainey would have done with the same words.","Long treated as the B-side, it gained its second life in December 1957 on the CBS television programme The Sound of Jazz, where Holiday sang it seated among Lester Young, Coleman Hawkins, Ben Webster, Roy Eldridge and Gerry Mulligan. Young, frail and estranged from her, stood to play one short chorus, and the exchange of looks between them is now one of the most replayed clips in jazz. Critics often rank the television version above the record.","Hear the 1939 record first, then watch the 1957 television performance, which runs a little longer and passes the tune around the horns. In the film, watch Holiday's face while Lester Young plays; she nods and smiles as if hearing a private joke. Compare Bessie Smith's Downhearted Blues to hear how much quieter and more conversational Holiday made the blues."],"rosetta-tharpe-this-train":["A traditional gospel song about a train bound for glory that will carry only the righteous, sung and played by Tharpe alone. Her guitar is the engine: a rolling, swinging figure with bent notes and quick runs between verses, closer to a blues or swing player than to a church accompanist. Her voice is bright and forceful, pushing the rhythm forward, and each verse lists another kind of passenger who will not be allowed aboard.","Tharpe had arrived in New York in 1938 and was signed by Decca, recording gospel songs while also performing them at the Cotton Club with Cab Calloway's orchestra. This mixing of sacred songs with nightclub stages scandalised parts of the church and thrilled secular audiences. The train image was already old in Black sacred song; in the Depression, when trains meant migration north and escape, it carried extra weight.","Listen to the gaps between her vocal lines: she fills almost every one with a short guitar answer, the call-and-response of a congregation played by a single instrument. The picking uses a steady thumb bass with melody on top, a technique shared with country and ragtime guitarists. The same song, as Bound for Glory, gave Woody Guthrie the title for his 1943 memoir, one sign of how widely it travelled.","Gospel purists at the time disliked the swing feel and the dance-hall bookings; record buyers did not, and the song became one of her signatures, re-recorded several times. Modern writers hear it as early evidence for the case that Tharpe laid groundwork for rock and roll, a case now widely accepted and marked by her induction into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 2018.","Ignore the words for one play and follow only the guitar: count how often it answers her. Then listen again for the voice, which never softens. Pair it with Blind Willie Johnson's John the Revelator for the older, rawer sacred style, and with Chuck Berry's Maybellene to hear where the guitar attitude went next."],"rosetta-tharpe-didnt-it-rain-manchester":["A filmed performance of the old spiritual about Noah's flood, the rain that would not stop falling. Tharpe, in a long coat, steps onto a disused railway platform and plays a white solid-body Gibson electric guitar, loud and distorted by the standards of the day, before a crowd of young British fans seated on the opposite platform. She sings with a grin, trades lines with the band and takes a stinging guitar solo that sounds unmistakably like rock.","The clip comes from Granada Television's programme The Blues and Gospel Train, filmed in May 1964 at the abandoned Wilbraham Road station in Manchester, England, as part of a touring package of American blues and gospel artists that also included Muddy Waters and Sonny Terry and Brownie McGhee. Britain was then in the thick of its blues boom, and bands such as the Rolling Stones were built on American records like hers.","It really was raining that day, and the song choice seems to have been a joke about the Manchester weather that turned into the performance of the film. Watch her hands during the solo: she plays with bent strings and repeated single-note phrases, the vocabulary later credited to British guitar heroes. A few of the young people watching are often said to have gone on to form bands, though exact names are hard to confirm.","The clip was little known for decades, then circulated widely online in the 2000s and became the standard image in arguments that a Black gospel woman, not a white rock star, invented much of rock guitar. Some historians caution against making one film carry that whole claim. Nobody who watches it disputes the energy, and it did more than anything else to restore her reputation.","Watch rather than just listen; the setting, the rain and the crowd's reaction are part of the work. Notice the moment she steps into the solo and the audience starts clapping along. Then play the Rolling Stones' Beggars Banquet or Cream's Disraeli Gears and listen for how much of her phrasing survives in their guitar playing."],"carter-family-keep-on-the-sunny-side":["A cheerful hymn-like song urging listeners to face hard days by keeping to the bright side of life's road. Sara Carter sings lead in her level, unornamented alto, Maybelle adds harmony and plays her thumb-picked guitar, and A.P. Carter drops in a low bass part on the chorus. The tune is simple, the tempo steady, and the arrangement plain enough that it sounds like a family singing on a porch.","The song was written in 1899 by Ada Blenkhorn and J. Howard Entwisle as a Sunday-school piece; A.P. Carter, who collected songs across the southern Appalachians, reworked it. The Carters recorded it for Victor in May 1928 in Camden, New Jersey, less than a year after their first sessions at Bristol. It became the family's theme song, opening their later radio shows on the powerful border stations broadcasting from Mexico.","The recording shows the Carter method in miniature: an old printed song stripped of its parlour manners and set to Maybelle's guitar, where the melody is picked on the low strings while the fingers brush chords above. A.P.'s bass entries are notoriously irregular, as if he joined when he felt like it. The title is reportedly engraved on A.P.'s gravestone in Virginia.","It was never their most distinctive record, but it became their emblem, and generations of country and folk musicians learned it as a first song. Its optimism has been read both as Depression-era comfort and as an evasion of the hard subjects in their other songs. The Whites' version in the film O Brother, Where Art Thou? introduced it to a new audience in 2000.","Listen first for Maybelle's guitar under the chorus; once you hear the melody running in the bass, it is hard to un-hear. Then listen to the harmony between Sara and Maybelle, two cousins who sound like one voice split in two. Compare the O Brother soundtrack and the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band's Will the Circle Be Unbroken sessions to hear how it passed down."],"carter-family-bury-me-under-the-weeping-willow":["A mournful traditional ballad in which a young woman, abandoned by the man she was to marry, asks to be buried beneath a willow tree. Sara Carter sings the lead with Maybelle in close harmony, over Maybelle's guitar and Sara's autoharp. The melody rises and falls in long, even phrases, and the flat, unsentimental delivery makes the heartbreak sound matter-of-fact, which makes it sadder.","Recorded on 1 August 1927 in Bristol, on the Tennessee–Virginia line, when Victor's talent scout Ralph Peer set up a temporary studio and invited local musicians to audition. The Carters drove over the mountains from Maces Spring, Virginia; Sara was nursing a baby and Maybelle was pregnant. The same fortnight produced Jimmie Rodgers's first recordings, which is why the Bristol Sessions are called the big bang of country music.","This was among the very first songs the Carters ever recorded, so what you hear is the family style before any studio polish: Maybelle, only eighteen, already using her thumb to carry the tune on the bass strings. Listen for Sara's autoharp shimmering behind the guitar, a parlour instrument the Carters turned into a mountain one. The willow image comes from British ballads carried across the Atlantic generations earlier.","At the time it was just a promising first release; its importance became clear only as the Carters became the most influential act in early country. Bluegrass players adopted it widely, and it became a standard of the folk revival. Historians now treat the Bristol recordings as the founding documents of commercial country, and this song as one of the purest records of the Carters' starting point.","Play it quietly and follow the harmony: Maybelle sits just above Sara like a shadow. Then listen for the guitar filling the space at the end of each line. Compare the Stanley Brothers' Man of Constant Sorrow to hear the same mountain grief in bluegrass form, and Emmylou Harris's Roses in the Snow for a later revival."],"burleigh-swing-low-sweet-chariot":["An art-song arrangement of the best-known spiritual, in which the singer longs for a chariot and a band of angels to carry them home. Burleigh keeps the melody intact and gives it to a solo voice, usually a trained baritone, contralto or soprano. The piano does more than accompany: it sets a gently rocking pulse, darkens the harmony at key words and adds short interludes between verses, turning a congregational song into a concert piece.","Harry T. Burleigh had sung spirituals to Dvořák in New York in the 1890s and later worked as an editor at the music publisher Ricordi while serving for decades as baritone soloist at St George's Episcopal Church. After his Deep River became a hit with concert singers in 1916–17, he published a stream of spirituals for voice and piano. They gave recital singers, Black and white, material to place beside Schubert and Brahms.","Listen for the chromatic slides in the piano under the verse about crossing the river Jordan; Burleigh borrows the late-Romantic harmony of the European song recital to paint the text. Notice too what he refuses to do: he adds no dialect spellings beyond the familiar and no comic touches, insisting on the dignity of the material. The Fisk Jubilee Singers' choral version stands directly behind this one.","Singers such as Roland Hayes, Marian Anderson and Paul Robeson made Burleigh's arrangements a standard close to their recitals. Zora Neale Hurston later objected that concert spirituals were polite neo-spirituals that lost the rough rhythm and improvisation of real church singing. Scholars now see both sides: the arrangements removed something, but they also forced the concert hall to take the tradition seriously.","Listen to the Fisk Jubilee Singers' early recording first, then a solo recital version of Burleigh's setting. Notice how the piano introduction sets a mood before the voice enters, and how the singer slows at the refrain. Old recordings by Marian Anderson or Paul Robeson show the style it was written for; a modern bass-baritone brings out the piano writing."],"burleigh-were-you-there":["A setting of the Passion spiritual that asks the listener whether they were present at the crucifixion, verse by verse: the cross, the nails, the piercing of the side, the laying in the tomb. Burleigh gives the voice long, slow lines with room for a trembling pause on the repeated word that ends each question. The piano moves in hushed chords, deepening with each verse, so the song builds in intensity without ever getting loud.","By the mid-1920s Burleigh's arrangements were in the repertoire of nearly every Black concert singer and many white ones, and publishers were eager for more. Were You There had circulated in Black churches since the nineteenth century; Burleigh's version helped fix it as a Good Friday standard in mainstream Protestant churches, where it later entered hymnals. He himself sang in the choir of a wealthy white Manhattan parish.","The structure is a single melody repeated with new words, so the drama must come from the accompaniment: listen for the piano harmony growing darker and more chromatic with each station of the Passion. The quivering repeated note that the tradition sings on the word tremble is kept, a trace of the old improvised style inside a fully written-out art song.","It became one of Burleigh's most performed arrangements and a staple of church services as well as recitals. The same arguments over authenticity that surround all concert spirituals apply here, yet this song is often cited as the case where the art-song treatment adds rather than subtracts. Tippett's later use of spirituals in A Child of Our Time follows a path Burleigh opened.","Hear a traditional congregational version first, then Burleigh's, and notice how the piano turns a communal lament into a private meditation. Pay attention to the last verse, where most singers draw the tempo out almost to stillness. Afterwards try Erbarme dich from Bach's St Matthew Passion, another solo voice standing at the cross."],"kid-ory-muskrat-ramble":["Ory's best-known tune, first recorded by Louis Armstrong and His Hot Five with Ory himself on trombone. It is a jaunty, two-part strain with a catchy, stepwise melody that invites the whole front line to play at once. Cornet leads, clarinet weaves above and Ory's trombone slides and growls underneath in the tailgate manner, filling the low register with smears and punches. Short solos and breaks pass between the horns before a collective ending.","Ory said he had written the tune years earlier in New Orleans, where he had led one of the city's top bands, employing King Oliver and the young Armstrong. By 1926 he was in Chicago, and the Hot Five, a studio-only group Armstrong put together for OKeh, recorded it in February. Ory later revived the piece with his own band during the New Orleans revival of the 1940s in California.","Listen underneath the cornet for Ory's trombone: he plays not melodies but rhythmic smears between chords, the tailgate style named after trombonists sitting at the back of a wagon so the slide could stick out. Decades later the melody was borrowed for Country Joe McDonald's anti-war song of 1967, and Ory's daughter sued; the court ruled against her because she had waited too long.","It became one of the most played Dixieland standards, recorded by nearly every traditional jazz band, and acquired lyrics in the 1950s. Critics usually give the Hot Five version pride of place for its balance between ensemble and solo, while Ory's own later versions are valued as a direct link to New Orleans practice. Arguments over its authorship credit, sometimes shared with Armstrong, have never fully settled.","Follow Ory's trombone for one whole play, ignoring the cornet. Then play Ory's Creole Trombone from 1922 to hear his earlier style, and Potato Head Blues to hear how quickly Armstrong moved toward long solo flights. Seek a good transfer of the OKeh Hot Five sides; the originals are crackly but clear."],"kid-ory-blues-for-jimmie":["A slow, dignified blues played by Kid Ory's Creole Jazz Band in memory of the clarinettist Jimmie Noone. The tempo is funereal but not heavy; trumpet states a plain theme, the clarinet takes a mournful chorus in the low register that nods to Noone's own sound, and Ory's trombone speaks in short, vocal phrases. The ensemble plays together in the old New Orleans way, with no big solos and much quiet space.","In early 1944 Ory's band was heard weekly on Orson Welles's radio programme, bringing veteran New Orleans players back to national attention in Los Angeles. Noone, a member of that broadcast group, died suddenly in April 1944. Ory's band played an improvised blues for him on air, and later that year recorded it for Crescent, a new label founded by Nesuhi Ertegun and Marili Morden to document the revival.","Listen to the clarinet chorus: it is played as Noone might have played it, smooth and liquid with little vibrato, rather than in the gruffer style of most New Orleans clarinettists. The record is also a statement of method: the revivalists insisted on collective improvisation at a time when bebop was being born across town, so the ensemble passages are the point, not background.","Revival collectors cherished it as proof that the older generation could still play with feeling, and Ory's career flourished for another fifteen years as a result. Modernist critics of the time dismissed the revival as nostalgia. Today the Crescent sides are valued as among the best recordings of the living New Orleans style, made before it hardened into formula.","Play Noone's own Apex Blues first so the clarinet chorus here sounds like an echo. Then listen for how rarely any one player dominates; the tribute is made by the whole band breathing together. Follow with Ory's Creole Trombone and Muskrat Ramble to hear the same player twenty years earlier."],"ma-rainey-ma-raineys-black-bottom":["A novelty dance blues that opens with spoken banter between Rainey and her band before she launches into the song. The subject is the Black Bottom, the dance craze of the mid-1920s, and Rainey claims it as her own, boasting that her version is the one worth seeing, with a double meaning that the audience of the day understood perfectly. Her voice is huge and husky, the band plods and brays cheerfully behind her.","Recorded in Chicago in 1927 for Paramount, the label that sold race records by mail order and through Black-owned shops across the South. Rainey, the Mother of the Blues, had toured tent shows since the turn of the century and was one of the label's biggest names. The Black Bottom dance had just been taken up by white Broadway revues, and Rainey's record reclaims it for its Southern Black origins.","Paramount's pressings were notoriously poor, with thin sound and surface noise, which is why Rainey often sounds distant; the power of the voice survives in spite of the engineering. Listen to the spoken introduction as a document of tent-show patter, the teasing exchange between star and audience that framed her performances. Her authorship credit on many songs was part of how she protected her earnings.","In its day it was a modest seller among her many releases. Its fame now comes largely from August Wilson's play of 1984, set during an imagined 1927 recording session, which made the title shorthand for the exploitation of Black artists by white record men, and from the 2020 film with Viola Davis as Rainey. Scholars have also stressed Rainey's openness about queer desire in other songs.","Start with the spoken introduction and imagine a tent show under lights. Listen past the scratches to the size of the voice. Then compare Jelly Roll Morton's Black Bottom Stomp from 1926, which turns the same dance into intricate instrumental jazz, and Bessie Smith's Downhearted Blues to hear Rainey's greatest pupil."],"blind-lemon-jefferson-match-box-blues":["A solo blues for high, piercing voice and guitar in which the singer sits by the road with everything he owns fitting into a matchbox, wondering whether anyone will take him in. Jefferson's guitar does not keep a steady beat; it runs, stops, hammers single notes and throws off flurries between lines, following the voice rather than a fixed pulse. Verses are free in length, stretched or shortened to suit what he wants to say.","Jefferson, a blind street singer from Texas who worked the streets of Dallas's Deep Ellum, was by 1927 the best-selling male blues artist in America. He recorded Match Box Blues more than once that year, for Paramount and for OKeh, a sign of how much the labels wanted the song. His records reached rural listeners across the South and taught a generation of guitarists.","The matchbox verse was not his invention; it floated through earlier songs, and its image of possessions so few they fit in a tiny box is a portrait of the migrant worker. Listen for how irregular the bar lengths are; a band could never follow him, which is exactly why his records sound so free. The different recordings vary noticeably in verses and guitar fills.","The song became one of the most durable blues in rock history. Carl Perkins recast it as the rockabilly Matchbox in 1957, and the Beatles recorded Perkins's version in 1964, carrying Jefferson's image to a mass audience that had never heard of him. Critics now use it as the textbook example of how Texas blues guitar differed from the heavier Delta style.","Listen to the guitar between the lines rather than under them: that is where Jefferson's personality lives. Then hear Carl Perkins's Matchbox to see how a free-form blues was squared off into a dance record. Pair with Charley Patton's Pony Blues for the contrasting Mississippi approach, and Lead Belly, who once led Jefferson through Dallas."],"willie-the-lion-smith-finger-buster":["A breakneck showpiece for solo stride piano. The left hand leaps without pause between a low bass note and a chord in the middle of the keyboard, while the right hand fires off runs, repeated figures and cross-rhythms at a speed that gives the piece its name. It moves through several short strains like a rag, each faster or trickier than the last, and ends with a flourish rather than a fade.","Smith was one of the three great Harlem stride pianists with James P. Johnson and Fats Waller, reigning at rent parties and cutting contests where players tried to outplay one another in public. Pieces like this were weapons in those contests. He recorded it in 1939 at a solo session for Milt Gabler's Commodore label, which also produced Echoes of Spring, capturing his own compositions at a moment when stride was giving way to swing.","Listen to how the left hand keeps perfect time under the right-hand fireworks; the showing-off is only possible because the bass is rock steady. There are also flashes of classical training in the voicings and the orderly sectional form. Smith cultivated a persona with a derby hat and a cigar, but his harmony was more refined than the image suggested.","It has always been admired by pianists more than by the general public, and it remains a test piece for anyone learning stride. Critics have tended to rank Smith behind Johnson and Waller, partly because he recorded less; his Commodore solos are the main evidence for those who argue he deserves equal billing, and Ellington's admiration for him is often cited.","Watch the left hand in your mind: every beat is a jump of an octave or more. Play it straight after Echoes of Spring to hear the contest pianist beside the lyricist. Then compare James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout and Fats Waller's Handful of Keys, the other great stride test pieces."],"willie-the-lion-smith-morning-air":["A gentle solo-piano piece that shows the other side of the Lion. Instead of the pounding stride bass, the left hand moves in soft, rocking patterns and sustained chords, while the right hand spins a flowing, song-like melody decorated with light ripples. The harmony is lush and slightly impressionistic, with chords that seem to hover, and the whole piece feels like a quiet walk rather than a contest.","Smith recorded it at the same 1939 Commodore solo sessions that produced Echoes of Spring and Finger Buster. By then he was admired by younger musicians, especially Duke Ellington, who had learned from him in Harlem in the early 1920s and paid tribute that same year with Portrait of the Lion. Smith said he loved Debussy and the classical composers, and pieces like this show it.","Listen for the moments where the left hand briefly slips back into stride and then withdraws, as if the habit could not be entirely suppressed. The chord colours owe as much to French piano music as to the blues, and the piece's short, repeating sections still follow the ragtime and stride form beneath its soft surface.","The lyrical Commodore pieces were a revelation to critics who knew Smith only by reputation as a flamboyant contest player, and they are now considered his most personal work. They also helped establish that stride pianists could be composers of mood pieces, a line that runs through Ellington's own piano writing. Some listeners find them too sweet next to his hotter playing.","Play it right after Finger Buster for the contrast in temperament. Listen for the melody's long line in the right hand and the chords that hang in the air. Then try Bix Beiderbecke's In a Mist and Debussy's Clair de lune for kindred piano daydreams, and Ellington's piano solos for the tradition it fed."],"jimmie-noone-sweet-lorraine":["An early recording of a new popular ballad, played by Jimmie Noone's Apex Club Orchestra, an unusual small band with clarinet and alto saxophone in front and no trumpet at all. Noone takes the melody with a smooth, round, singing tone and graceful ornaments, the alto plays soft harmony underneath, and Earl Hines on piano adds sparkling fills between phrases. The mood is sweet and relaxed rather than hot.","Sweet Lorraine was a new song of 1928 by Cliff Burwell and Mitchell Parish. Noone, a New Orleans clarinettist who had settled in Chicago, led a band at the Apex Club, a late-night club on the South Side, where musicians from across the city came to hear him. The group recorded for Vocalion in 1928, the same year Hines was making history with Louis Armstrong.","Listen to Noone's tone: legato, with little of the rasp common among New Orleans players, a sound shaped partly by his classical clarinet study. Benny Goodman and other white Chicago clarinettists studied these records closely. Under the ballad surface, Hines's right hand keeps playing octave figures like a trumpet, the trumpet style that made his name.","The record helped make the song a standard; Nat King Cole's trio recording of 1940 later made it his signature. Critics have long praised the Apex Club sides for their refinement, and the band's lack of trumpet was considered adventurous. Noone's reputation faded after his death in 1944, but historians now place him among the most influential clarinettists of the 1920s.","Listen first to the clarinet tone, then to what Hines is doing in the gaps. Play Apex Blues from the same sessions for the hotter side of the band. Then compare Nat King Cole's later version to hear the song move from clarinet to voice, and a Benny Goodman small-group record to hear Noone's influence."],"jimmie-noone-i-know-that-you-know":["An up-tempo showcase for the Apex Club Orchestra on a Broadway tune by Vincent Youmans. Noone's clarinet races through the melody and then into variations full of rapid runs and leaps across the instrument's range, while the alto saxophone plays a cooler counterline. Earl Hines's piano punches in with jagged, off-beat phrases. The whole performance has a nimble, athletic drive that points toward the swing era.","The song came from Youmans's 1926 musical Oh, Please!, showing how quickly Chicago jazz bands absorbed Broadway material. Noone's band at the Apex Club played for dancers and for musicians who came after their own jobs finished, and this 1928 Vocalion session caught the group at its peak, with Hines newly arrived in Chicago's front rank.","Listen for the moments when clarinet and piano seem to race each other: Hines deliberately plays against the beat, suspending the rhythm, and Noone answers with runs that land exactly back on it. The clarinet's evenness at high speed, without any squeak or strain, is what clarinettists of the next decade tried to copy.","Clarinettists have praised it as a technical landmark; Benny Goodman acknowledged Noone as an influence, and records like this one show why. It is less well known than Apex Blues or Sweet Lorraine, but critics often single it out as the clearest demonstration of Noone's virtuosity and of Hines's rhythmic daring in 1928.","Follow Noone's clarinet for the first run-through, then play it again and listen only to Hines. Compare Goodman's After You've Gone, recorded seven years later, to hear the smooth, fast clarinet style become the sound of swing. Pair with Armstrong and Hines's Weather Bird for Hines at his most daring."],"donna-summer-once-upon-a-time":["A double album laid out as a fairy tale in four acts, one per vinyl side. A young woman trapped in a grey, loveless city dreams of rescue, loses faith, and finally finds love on her own terms. Summer sings the whole story without a break in character, while Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte move the backing from lush orchestral disco through pure sequencer pulse to glossy ballad. Many tracks run into one another, so each side plays like a continuous scene.","Recorded at Musicland Studios in Munich in the same prolific year as I Remember Yesterday and I Feel Love, it was Summer's most ambitious statement for Casablanca, the label that had built its fortune on her. Disco was usually sold in singles and twelve-inch mixes for DJs; a narrative double LP was a bid to be taken seriously as an album artist, borrowing the concept-record habits of progressive rock and the rags-to-riches shape of Cinderella.","The electronic sound that I Feel Love made famous runs right through the second act, where the heroine's despair is scored with cold, clipped sequencer lines and her vocals are pushed back into the mix. When hope returns in the last act, real strings and warmer rhythm sections come back in. The instrumentation tells the story as clearly as the words do: machine for the lonely city, orchestra for the happy ending.","It sold well and went gold in the United States, but reviewers at the time found the plot thin and the length indulgent, and it has long been overshadowed by the singles either side of it. Later critics, especially those writing the history of electronic dance music, have been kinder, hearing it as the fullest demonstration of what the Munich team could do across a whole record rather than a single groove.","Play side two straight through without skipping; it is where the Munich machine sound is at its most hypnotic and where the story's darkness lives. Then jump to the title track on the last side to hear the resolution. Compare it with I Feel Love and with Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express from the same year, two other ways of making sequencers tell a story."],"donna-summer-macarthur-park-suite":["A continuous disco suite filling a whole side of vinyl, built around Jimmy Webb's song MacArthur Park and running on into two further songs before looping back to it. Summer opens with the melancholy ballad almost straight, then the drums kick in and the song is rebuilt as a galloping dance number with strings, horns and a pounding four-on-the-floor. The single edit, cut down to a few minutes, is what most people know, but the suite is the full design.","Webb's song was already a famous oddity, a seven-minute orchestral pop epic that Richard Harris took into the charts in 1968, mocked and loved for its surreal imagery of a ruined cake left out in the rain. Moroder and Bellotte saw a melodrama waiting to be danced to. The suite appeared on the studio side of Summer's double album Live and More, and the single became her first number one on the American Hot 100 in late 1978.","Listen to how the arrangement treats the slow opening as a prologue: the tempo and key relationships are set up so that the dance section feels like the same song finally released rather than a new one. The reprise at the end returns the ballad material transformed by everything in between, a structural idea closer to an operatic scena than to a pop single, which is precisely what Webb had been reaching for in the first place.","It was a huge hit and a DJ staple, and it confirmed Summer as a singer who could carry theatrical material as well as sensual grooves. Rock critics of the anti-disco moment found it camp, and the song itself kept appearing on lists of the worst lyrics ever written. Opinion has since softened: Summer's full-throated delivery is now widely heard as the thing that finally made sense of Webb's overwrought song.","Hear the Richard Harris original first, all harpsichord and orchestral swell, then put on the full suite rather than the single. Notice the moment the beat arrives and how Summer shifts from a hushed, almost classical tone to a belting gospel-trained voice. Follow it with Bad Girls, recorded the next year, to hear where her voice went next."],"can-monster-movie":["Can's first album: four tracks, three short on side one and the twenty-minute Yoo Doo Right on side two. Malcolm Mooney, an American sculptor who happened to be in Cologne, chants and shouts over Jaki Liebezeit's locked, relentless drumming, Holger Czukay's bass, Michael Karoli's jagged guitar and Irmin Schmidt's organ. The sound is garage rock slowed into trance: repetition held for so long that small variations become events.","The band formed in Cologne in 1968 from an unlikely mix: Schmidt and Czukay had studied with Stockhausen, Liebezeit had played free jazz, and Karoli was a young rock guitarist. They rented rooms at Schloss Nörvenich, a castle outside the city, and recorded on two-track equipment by improvising for hours and editing the results. The album first came out in a small pressing on the band's own label before a wider release on Liberty.","Yoo Doo Right is itself an edit: Czukay cut it down from a far longer improvisation, reportedly many hours of tape, using the splicing techniques he had learned in the new-music studio. The seams are hard to hear, which is the point. What sounds like a single continuous jam is a composed tape piece built from jam material, the same method that would shape every Can record after it.","It made little impact on release outside a small German audience, but it became a touchstone for later bands, from punk and post-punk groups in Britain to the Fall, who wrote a song simply called I Am Damo Suzuki in tribute to Mooney's successor. Critics now usually rank it just below Tago Mago and Ege Bamyasi, praising its rawness while noting that the band had not yet found their full range.","Start with Father Cannot Yell, the opening track, for the full shock of Mooney's voice against the machine-like drums, then give Yoo Doo Right its full twenty minutes without distraction. Listen to Liebezeit rather than the singer: he barely changes his pattern, and that restraint is the band's engine. Follow it with the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat for a New York cousin."],"can-future-days":["Four tracks: the shimmering title piece, the loose-limbed Spray, the short, almost pop Moonshake, and Bel Air, a twenty-minute side-long drift. Compared with the hard funk of Tago Mago this is a record of air and water. Damo Suzuki's voice is often half-whispered and buried in the mix, Liebezeit's drumming turns light and skittering, and Schmidt's keyboards and electronics wash across everything like weather.","By 1973 Can worked in their own Inner Space studio in a former cinema in Weilerswist, near Cologne, where they could leave microphones up and record whenever they played. It was Suzuki's last album with the band; he left later that year to marry and join the Jehovah's Witnesses. Europe was deep in the first oil crisis and ecological worry, and the record's mood feels less like rebellion than like a meditation on what comes next.","Future Days opens with a quiet wash of sound that is easy to mistake for tape hiss or surf before the rhythm fades in. Listen to how many layers of percussion Liebezeit adds without ever getting louder, and to how Bel Air shifts through several distinct sections, including a stormy passage near the middle, while seeming to stand still. It is edited together from long sessions, just like the earlier records, but the joins are gentler.","It sold modestly and puzzled listeners who wanted the aggression of Tago Mago, but its reputation has grown steadily until many critics now name it Can's finest record. It is frequently cited as a forerunner of ambient music and post-rock, and bands from Talk Talk to Radiohead have been heard in its shadow. The debate is now mostly over whether it or Tago Mago is the true summit.","Listen on headphones and at a low-to-moderate volume, which suits its hushed textures better than loudness does. Start with the title track, then let Bel Air run all the way through. Notice Suzuki singing more as an instrument than a narrator. Pair it with Brian Eno's Another Green World and Radiohead's Kid A to hear what grew from this soil."],"can-soon-over-babaluma":["Five tracks, the first recorded after Damo Suzuki's departure, with guitarist Michael Karoli and keyboardist Irmin Schmidt taking over the vocals in soft, murmuring tones. Side one holds the lilting Dizzy Dizzy, with Karoli on electric violin, the dreamy Come sta, la luna and the short Splash; side two is two long pieces, Chain Reaction and Quantum Physics, which dissolve from a driving groove into drifting electronic abstraction.","Recorded at Inner Space in 1974, the album is the end of Can's great run on the United Artists label, made by the four core members alone. Without a front man, they leaned further into texture and into rhythms borrowed from Latin and Caribbean music, which Liebezeit had long been listening to. The band was by now respected in Britain and France more than at home, touring widely and playing to audiences who knew them from the earlier records.","Quantum Physics is one of the first places where rock-band instruments dissolve almost completely into ambient sound: the drums fall back to a faint pulse and what is left is Schmidt's treated keyboards and Karoli's guitar reduced to shimmer. The title of the record, a made-up place name, is typical of the band's playfulness, and Come sta, la luna sets its vocal against a sort of slowed-down tango.","Critics received it warmly but saw it as a coda to the Suzuki years rather than a new beginning, and the albums that followed are generally judged weaker. It remains a favourite of the band's more devoted listeners, and its long second side is often mentioned in accounts of how rock musicians moved toward ambient and electronic music in the mid-seventies.","Play Chain Reaction and Quantum Physics together without a pause, since they are designed to flow from one into the other. Listen for the exact moment when the groove stops being a groove and becomes a landscape. Then set it beside Future Days to hear how much the voice had been carrying, and beside Eno's Discreet Music to hear where the ambient side led."],"kraftwerk-radio-activity":["Kraftwerk's first fully electronic album, released in German as Radio-Aktivität. It alternates short sound collages of radio static, Geiger-counter clicks and Morse code with songs of plain, hymn-like melody sung in a flat voice or through a vocoder. The title track is the anchor: a slow, sad pulse under a choir-like synthesizer, sung in both German and English. The whole record plays like a broadcast from an empty studio late at night.","After the unexpected international success of Autobahn, Ralf Hütter and Florian Schneider, now joined by percussionists Karl Bartos and Wolfgang Flür, retreated to their own Kling Klang studio in Düsseldorf and built a record around a pun: the radio waves that had made them famous and the radioactivity of the nuclear age. West Germany was then expanding nuclear power, and an anti-nuclear movement was only beginning to form.","The wordplay is the key: in 1975 the album is ambiguous, almost fond of both radio and atom, and several tracks celebrate the romance of old valve radio sets. Only in later live and re-recorded versions did Kraftwerk add a spoken list of nuclear disaster sites, among them Chernobyl, Harrisburg and Sellafield, turning the song into open protest. The Morse code on the title track spells out its own name.","It sold less well than Autobahn in Britain and America, and some reviewers found it cold and thin, but it was a major hit in France, where the title track reached the top of the charts. Its reputation rose once the synth-pop generation named it as an influence, and it is now seen as the hinge between Kraftwerk's experimental early records and the perfected pop of Trans-Europe Express.","Start with the title track, then play the album in order to feel how the collages act as interludes between songs, like station idents between programmes. Listen for the tiny, deliberate sounds: switches, static, bleeps. Compare the 1975 recording with the harder, more political version on the 1991 collection The Mix, which shows how a song's meaning can change without its notes changing."],"giorgio-moroder-midnight-express":["Moroder's score for Alan Parker's film about an American student imprisoned in Turkey for drug smuggling. Its centrepiece is Chase, an eight-minute instrumental built on a racing sequencer bass line, a steady kick drum and a single, obsessive synthesizer melody: disco's machinery pointed at fear rather than pleasure. Around it sit quieter, brooding cues and a vocal theme song, but the relentless pulse of Chase is what defines the film's sound.","Moroder was at the height of his Munich fame after I Feel Love, and Parker hired him to give a grim prison drama a contemporary, urgent sound rather than a conventional orchestral score. Film music was still dominated by symphonic writing; synthesizer scores were rare and usually confined to science fiction. Midnight Express won the Academy Award for Best Original Score in 1979, a landmark moment for electronic music in Hollywood.","Chase uses exactly the tools of the dance floor: the same pumping octave bass and steady tempo that make I Feel Love euphoric here become a picture of panic, because nothing ever resolves or slows down. It shows how much the emotional meaning of a sequencer depends on the harmony above it. An extended mix of Chase was released as a twelve-inch single and became a club track in its own right.","The film was controversial for its hostile portrayal of Turkey, and its screenwriter Oliver Stone and the real prisoner Billy Hayes later expressed regret about aspects of it. The score escaped most of that criticism. Some film-music traditionalists thought an Oscar for a synthesizer score cheapened the award, but it is now generally seen as the point where electronic scoring became a serious Hollywood option, clearing the way for Vangelis and others.","Hear Chase first, ideally in its full or extended form, and notice how it gets more tense without getting faster. Then compare it with I Feel Love, built from nearly the same materials to the opposite effect. Follow with Vangelis's Blade Runner and Wendy Carlos's Tron to hear the next steps in electronic film scoring."],"giorgio-moroder-e-mc2":["A solo album of electronic disco in which Moroder himself sings through vocoders and processed voices over sequencer grooves. The title track is long, bright and propulsive, with pulsing bass, crisp electronic percussion and robot-like vocal hooks, and the rest of the record keeps the same gleaming, synthetic palette. It sounds like the backing tracks of his Donna Summer productions stripped of the diva and pushed further toward pure machine music.","Casablanca released it in 1979, the peak and the end of disco's commercial boom, and marketed it on the cover as the first electronic live-to-digital album, meaning it was recorded straight to a digital master rather than to analogue tape. Digital recording was brand new and expensive. Moroder was moving between Munich and Los Angeles, working on film scores and pop productions as well as his own records.","The title is a joke about Einstein's equation, but it is also a sly statement of method: energy from mass, music generated by machines. The claim of being recorded live to digital matters less for the sound than for what it shows about Moroder, always eager to be first with a new technology and to make that novelty part of the product, as he had done with the Moog on I Feel Love.","It was a modest success, and reviewers in rock papers treated it as a curio. Disco's backlash that year buried much of this music. Its reputation was revived by the electronic dance producers of the 1990s and 2000s, especially Daft Punk, who later recorded Moroder telling his own story on Random Access Memories and credited this kind of record as a direct ancestor.","Start with the title track and listen for the interplay between the sequenced bass and the vocoder hooks. Then play I Feel Love to hear the same toolkit in the service of a singer. Follow with Daft Punk's Homework and Random Access Memories, and with Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, its closest contemporary in the robot-voice style."],"brotzmann-for-adolphe-sax":["Peter Brötzmann's first album, a trio with bassist Peter Kowald and drummer Sven-Åke Johansson. The playing is raw, loud and almost entirely free of chord changes or steady meter: Brötzmann's saxophone hollers, splits into overtones and repeats short cries, Kowald bows and slaps, and Johansson keeps up a clattering storm rather than a beat. The title honours the Belgian instrument maker who invented the saxophone, a wry dedication for a record that treats the instrument so roughly.","Brötzmann was a painter from Wuppertal who had worked around the Fluxus artists before turning fully to music. West German jazz in the mid-sixties still largely imitated American models, and free jazz from Ornette Coleman, Albert Ayler and others had only just begun to reach Europe. Unable to find a label willing to issue music this abrasive, Brötzmann put the record out himself in a small edition, an act of self-reliance that would lead to the Free Music Production cooperative.","Listen past the noise for how carefully the three players leave space for one another: Johansson often drops out entirely, and Kowald's bowed passages set up quiet zones that make the next saxophone outburst hit harder. The rawness is partly technical, partly aesthetic; Brötzmann taught himself, and he chose not to smooth out the cracked, vocal quality of his tone, which he treated as closer to a shout than to a song.","Hardly anyone heard it at first, and those who did were often baffled or angry. Its reputation rests on what came next: the following year's Machine Gun made Brötzmann internationally known, and this trio record was reissued as its forerunner. Historians of European free improvisation now treat it as one of the scene's founding documents, the moment West German jazz stopped borrowing an American accent.","Play it loud, or not at all, and give the title track its full length rather than sampling it. Focus on one player at a time on repeated listens: first the bass, then the drums, and only then the saxophone. Follow it immediately with Machine Gun to hear the trio's ideas expanded to an octet, and compare with Albert Ayler's Spiritual Unity."],"brotzmann-nipples":["Two long collective improvisations, one by a sextet and one by a smaller group, played by Brötzmann with the English saxophonist Evan Parker, the English guitarist Derek Bailey, the Belgian pianist Fred Van Hove, bassist Buschi Niebergall and the Dutch drummer Han Bennink. The sound is dense and turbulent: two saxophones screaming together, piano clusters, clanging guitar and Bennink's torrential drumming, with sudden dropouts into sparse, scratchy near-silence.","Recorded in Cologne in 1969, a year after Machine Gun, it gathers players who would soon define European free improvisation in their own right. Bailey and Parker were then working in London with the Spontaneous Music Ensemble and would later found the Incus label; Bennink and Van Hove would be Brötzmann's core partners for years. It was a moment of great cross-border exchange, as small labels and festivals linked improvisers in Germany, Britain and the Netherlands.","This is one of the few records where you can hear Derek Bailey, later a fierce purist of non-idiomatic improvising, inside a high-energy jazz ensemble. Listen for his guitar on the sextet side: splintered, percussive and deliberately unmelodic, it cuts across the saxophones rather than supporting them. The contrast between Bailey's dry, sparse method and Brötzmann's expressionist roar is already the argument that would split the European scene in the seventies.","Released on a small label to a tiny audience, it was long hard to find and survived mainly through reputation. Reissues from the 2000s onward brought it back into circulation, and it is now valued above all as a meeting point of the players who founded European improvised music. Some listeners find it less focused than Machine Gun; others prefer its more varied textures.","Hear Machine Gun first to get used to the energy level, then play Nipples and pay attention to the guitar and piano, which are more audible here. Try following Evan Parker and Brötzmann as two separate voices; they sound quite different once you listen for Parker's thinner, more fluid tone. Continue with Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for an American counterpart."],"klaus-schulze-moondawn":["Two side-long pieces, Floating and Mindphaser. Each begins in a haze of drones and slowly rising electronic textures, then gathers into a driving sequencer pattern over which Schulze improvises long, singing synthesizer lines. Harald Grosskopf's live drumming enters to push the music forward, giving it a rock pulse that his earlier, purely drone-based records lacked. The shape is always the same: patience, build, a long peak, and a gradual dissolve.","Schulze had drummed briefly for Tangerine Dream and Ash Ra Tempel before going solo, and his early records were made with organs and modified tape. Moondawn was his first album built around a large Moog modular synthesizer, an expensive instrument he had just acquired, and it was released on the Brain label in 1976, at the high point of the Berlin School of long-form electronic music. His audience was strongest in France and Britain.","Listen for how the sequencer pattern mutates slowly over many minutes: Schulze changes the filter settings by hand while it runs, so the same handful of notes brightens, darkens and grows harsher without any change of pitch. The live drums are also mixed so that they sometimes lag a fraction behind the machine, a slight friction that keeps the music feeling human rather than mechanical.","It was well received at the time and has remained one of Schulze's most popular albums, often recommended as the best starting point for his enormous output. Critics sometimes complain that his long pieces meander, but Moondawn is usually singled out as a record where the balance between drift and drive is exactly right. Later reissues added bonus material, which some fans consider unnecessary.","Choose a time when you can listen to a whole side without interruption; the payoff is in the build. Start with Floating and notice the point where the drums first enter. Compare it with Tangerine Dream's Phaedra and Rubycon, and then with Jean-Michel Jarre's Oxygène, which took the same instruments in a more melodic, pop direction."],"metheny-offramp":["An album by the Pat Metheny Group: Metheny with keyboardist Lyle Mays, bassist Steve Rodby, drummer Danny Gottlieb and the Brazilian percussionist and singer Naná Vasconcelos. It ranges from the slow, luminous Are You Going with Me?, where Metheny's guitar synthesizer takes a long, horn-like solo over a Latin-tinged pulse, to the tender ballad James, the bright Eighteen and the abrasive, free-jazz title track. Wordless vocals and warm keyboard pads colour much of it.","Recorded for Manfred Eicher's ECM label, which had launched Metheny's career, it came at a point when the Group was one of the best-selling acts in jazz and touring constantly. Metheny had recently begun using the Roland guitar synthesizer, a new instrument that let a guitarist trigger synthesized sounds, and Offramp is where it became central to his sound. The album won a Grammy for best jazz fusion performance.","The title track is a surprise: a short, noisy free improvisation on guitar synthesizer, a nod to Ornette Coleman, whom Metheny revered and with whom he would record Song X a few years later. Its place on an otherwise lyrical record shows that Metheny's roots reached further into the avant-garde than his smooth reputation suggests. James, meanwhile, is a tribute to the singer-songwriter James Taylor.","It sold strongly and Are You Going with Me? became the Group's signature live number. Jazz purists at the time were often dismissive of the Group's accessible, pop-inflected sound and polished production. Opinion has moved: Metheny's melodic writing and the guitar-synth sound are now widely acknowledged as influential, though some critics still prefer his more stripped-down trio and duo work.","Start with Are You Going with Me?, letting the long groove establish itself before the guitar synthesizer solo arrives around the middle. Then play James for the Group at its most song-like, and the title track for its opposite. Compare with Weather Report's Heavy Weather and, for Metheny's roots, Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come."],"tangerine-dream-zeit":["A double album of four side-long pieces, subtitled a largo in four parts. There is no beat and almost no melody: instead, vast slow drones, glacial organ chords, electronic wind and whistling tones unfold over more than an hour. The first piece opens with a group of cellos playing long, groaning clusters before the electronics take over. It feels less like listening to songs than like staring into space.","Recorded in 1972 by Edgar Froese, Christopher Franke and Peter Baumann, joined by guests including Florian Fricke of Popol Vuh on Moog synthesizer and a cello quartet. Released on the Ohr label, it came before the band began using sequencers and belongs to the early, cosmic phase of West Berlin electronic music, when young musicians were drawing on Ligeti, space travel and psychedelia rather than rock and roll.","The cello opening is the most direct link between the band and Ligeti's orchestral cluster music of the 1960s: dense groups of close pitches shifting slowly, the same technique used in Atmosphères and Lux aeterna, famous from 2001: A Space Odyssey. Tangerine Dream then continue the idea with electronic means, so the record effectively carries avant-garde orchestral texture into rock-band hands.","It was dismissed by many rock critics as formless and boring, and it sold poorly compared with the later Phaedra. Over time it has come to be seen as a founding document of dark ambient and drone music, admired for its sheer patience. Even admirers admit it is demanding; the debate is about whether its monotony is a flaw or the whole point.","Listen in darkness, on good speakers or headphones, and do not expect anything to happen quickly. Start with Birth of Liquid Plejades, the first side, for the cello opening. Then hear Ligeti's Atmosphères and Lux aeterna to recognise the source, and compare with Phaedra to hear how the band's music changed once sequencers arrived."],"faust-faust":["The band's debut: three long tracks that splice together rock grooves, brass-band fragments, tape collage, radio noise, drones and snatches of song. It opens with blasts of radio static, which briefly let through fragments of famous British rock songs before being swallowed by a spiralling collage. Throughout, a steady band groove suddenly gives way to cut-up sounds, then returns in a different form, so the album behaves more like a sound montage than a set of songs.","Faust was assembled in Hamburg by the journalist and producer Uwe Nettelbeck, who persuaded Polydor to fund a German answer to Anglo-American rock. The label installed the band in a converted schoolhouse in the village of Wümme, where they lived and recorded. The record was pressed on clear vinyl, in a clear plastic sleeve printed with an X-ray of a fist, a pun on the band's name, which is German for fist.","The record argues with the rock canon from its opening seconds: the half-heard fragments of hit songs, drowned in noise, suggest a band tuning out the English-language radio of the time. The collage method comes from musique concrète rather than rock, and several passages are pieced together from separate sessions, so what sounds like live chaos is often carefully edited tape.","It sold very poorly, and Polydor had little idea how to promote it. In Britain it won a cult following, and Faust found an eager champion in the rock press there. Its reputation has grown with every generation of noise and experimental rock, and it is now regarded as one of the most radical debuts of the period, although some listeners still find its fragmentation more interesting to read about than to hear.","Hear it in one sitting to feel how the collage works over time. Listen for the moments when a conventional rock groove suddenly appears and then collapses. Compare it with the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows and Frank Zappa's Uncle Meat, two other attempts at tape-collage rock, then move on to Faust IV to hear the band tighten its approach."],"faust-the-faust-tapes":["A record made from more than two dozen fragments of the band's private home recordings, placed end to end with no track titles listed on the original sleeve. Short pop-song sketches, acoustic miniatures, clattering percussion experiments, drones and bursts of noise follow one another without warning. The effect is like flicking through a radio dial of alternative rock histories, some pieces lasting under a minute and a few stretching into long, hypnotic passages.","After two albums for Polydor sold poorly, Faust signed to the new Virgin label in Britain. Virgin issued this compilation in 1973 at forty-nine pence, the price of a single, as a promotional stunt. It sold in the tens of thousands, many copies bought by curious listeners who had never heard of the band and were baffled by what they had taken home.","The disorder is a design. Nettelbeck and the band chose and ordered the fragments to create sharp contrasts, sometimes placing a tender acoustic melody straight after a harsh noise piece. Several of the short fragments were later expanded or reworked on other Faust releases, so the record works almost like a sketchbook for a band that never quite finished anything in the conventional way.","The low price made it one of the most widely distributed experimental records of its time, and it has often been cited as the album that introduced a generation of British musicians to avant-garde rock; figures in post-punk and industrial music have said so directly. The flip side was a reputation for being unlistenable, which it has never entirely shaken, though its collage method looks more prophetic every decade.","Do not look for tracks; play it from start to finish and treat the jolts as the point. Notice the recurring textures and moods that bind the fragments together. Follow it with Faust IV, their more polished Virgin album, and compare with Public Image Ltd's Metal Box to hear the post-punk band most openly marked by this approach."],"neu-neu-2":["Side one opens with Für immer, an eleven-minute drive in the steady, forward-leaning four-beat pattern that came to be called motorik, with Michael Rother's guitars shimmering in layers above Klaus Dinger's drumming. The rest of side one alternates similar grooves with noise pieces. Side two, famously, consists of the band's recent single played back at wrong speeds, slowed down, sped up, and chopped with tape effects.","Rother and Dinger, both former members of an early Kraftwerk line-up, recorded in Hamburg with the producer Conny Plank for the Brain label. According to the band's own account, the recording budget ran out before side two could be completed, so they filled it by manipulating copies of their single Neuschnee and Super on record players and tape. Punk would not arrive for three years.","The speed-altered side two looks like a gimmick, but it anticipates remixing, the idea that a finished recording is just raw material for new versions. Twenty years later DJs and producers would make whole careers of this approach. Listen, too, to how the drum pattern of Für immer never changes and yet the piece feels constantly in motion, because Rother's guitar parts slowly change their shape and brightness.","The manipulated side was widely mocked at the time as a rip-off or a joke, and some fans still skip it. Für immer, by contrast, has been praised almost from the start as one of the definitive motorik tracks, and the album as a whole was reassessed as punk and electronic musicians rediscovered Neu!. It now sits alongside the debut as a cornerstone of Krautrock.","Start with Für immer and let its eleven minutes settle into a single long journey. Then try side two, thinking of it as an early remix rather than a set of songs. Compare with Neu!'s first album and with Kraftwerk's Autobahn, and follow with Stereolab or Sonic Youth, who took the motorik beat into the 1980s and 1990s."],"neu-neu-75":["A record split down the middle. Side one is Michael Rother's: gentle, piano-led and ambient, with the soft pulse of Isi and the slow, watery Seeland. Side two is Klaus Dinger's: loud, driving rock with two drummers, and Dinger's snarling, half-shouted vocals on Hero and After Eight. The same motorik foundation runs under both sides, but one points toward ambient music and the other straight at punk.","Recorded in 1975 with Conny Plank, it was the third and, for decades, last Neu! album. Rother had spent the previous year making music with Harmonia and Cluster in the countryside at Forst, which shaped the calmer side; Dinger had been forming the band La Düsseldorf with his brother Thomas and drummer Hans Lampe, who both joined for side two. The duo were barely working together by then.","The two sides together tell the story of the duo's split: the tension between their temperaments, which had held their earlier records together, becomes the structure of the album itself. Listen to Hero and notice how its sound, a pounding beat under a sneering vocal, predates the Sex Pistols and the rest of British punk by a year or more. Seeland's slow, chiming guitars look ahead to ambient rock.","It received little attention on release, but musicians noticed: David Bowie drew on it while recording Low and Heroes in Berlin, and John Lydon of the Sex Pistols and Public Image Ltd praised Neu! in interviews. Critics now often rank it as the most consistent Neu! album, and Hero is regularly described as proto-punk.","Play Isi and Seeland, then jump straight to Hero, to feel the split between the two halves. Listen to the drums in Hero, a mechanical beat driven to aggression. Then compare with David Bowie's Heroes, whose title and sound owe something to this album, and with the Sex Pistols' Never Mind the Bollocks."],"gudnadottir-women-talking":["Hildur Guðnadóttir's score for Sarah Polley's film, a quiet, warm and hopeful work that stands in contrast to the menace of her music for Joker and Chernobyl. Rather than dwelling on the violence in the story, the score favours gentle, folk-tinged figures, plucked and strummed guitar sounds and slowly rising string textures, and it often waits in the background before emerging at key moments of decision in the film.","The film adapts Miriam Toews's novel, based on real events in a remote Mennonite colony in Bolivia where women had been repeatedly attacked by men of their community. It follows a group of women deciding in a hayloft whether to stay, fight, or leave. Guðnadóttir, based in Berlin and by then an Oscar winner for Joker, wrote music meant to express the characters' courage and their hope for a different future rather than their suffering.","Listen for how little the music comments on the traumatic events in the story. Guðnadóttir chose to let the women's decision, not their injuries, be the musical subject, so the score gradually grows brighter and fuller as they move toward action. It is a choice of emphasis as much as of sound, in line with Polley's decision to keep the violence largely off screen.","The score was widely praised and earned a Golden Globe nomination. Some critics felt it was too gentle, even sentimental, for such a painful subject; others argued that its restraint was exactly right, allowing the film's long conversations to breathe. It confirmed Guðnadóttir's range beyond the dark sound-world with which she had become identified.","Listen to the score after hearing her Joker and Chernobyl, to hear the same composer choose light over darkness. Notice how the cues start small and only gradually swell. Compare with Jóhann Jóhannsson's Arrival, by her close collaborator, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel, another study in restraint."],"basic-channel-phylyps-trak":["A long, minimal techno track from Moritz von Oswald and Mark Ernestus. A steady kick drum and muffled hi-hats support a single chord stab that is almost constantly being altered by delays, filters and reverb, so it seems to pulse, blur and bloom. Very little is added or taken away, yet the sound never stays still. It is a study in how much movement can be drawn from one repeated gesture.","Basic Channel was founded in Berlin in the early 1990s, shortly after reunification, when the city's abandoned buildings had become an international centre for techno. Ernestus ran the Hard Wax record shop, and the pair mastered their own records at the Dubplates & Mastering studio. Their releases came in plain sleeves with minimal information, deliberately anonymous in a scene that already prized facelessness.","The track is closer to a Jamaican dub mix than to most techno: the main musical activity is not in the notes but in the mixing desk, with echoes and filters moved by hand. Listen for how the chord stab sounds slightly different each time it appears, and how its echoes fall at varying distances behind the beat, creating a sense of physical space that other techno of the time rarely aimed for.","It circulated on vinyl among DJs and quickly acquired a cult reputation for sounding like nothing else in techno. Critics now regard it as one of the key early Basic Channel tracks, and its approach is often identified as the model for dub techno, which later became an entire genre. Its influence is heard throughout minimal techno.","Play it loud enough to feel the bass, ideally on good speakers, and resist waiting for a drop; the point is the slow, ongoing change. Follow one element, like the chord stab, through the whole track. Compare it with King Tubby's dub mixes and Derrick May's Strings of Life, the two traditions it combines."],"basic-channel-bcd":["The only Basic Channel CD of its time, gathering and reshaping material from the duo's twelve-inch singles into one continuous listening experience. Tracks are edited, remixed and sequenced so that one leads into the next, and the whole plays like a slow-moving landscape of muted kick drums, blurred chords, crackle and hiss. The music is repetitive but never static, changing through tiny adjustments of echo and filter.","Moritz von Oswald and Mark Ernestus had released their music on a small run of vinyl singles in Berlin from 1993 onward, designed for DJs. BCD was issued in 1995 as their answer to the CD age: an album shaped for home listening rather than for clubs. The duo soon moved on to projects such as Maurizio and Rhythm & Sound, making this effectively a summation of the Basic Channel name.","The versions here are not simply the vinyl tracks copied onto CD; many are shortened, reworked or run together, so the album is a new composition built from existing recordings. Listen for moments when one track's rhythm carries into another's texture. The hiss and crackle, often mistaken for a flaw, are intentional, placing the music in a haze that hints at old dub records and radio signals.","It was quickly treated as an essential document of Berlin techno and has remained a reference point for electronic musicians and critics. Some purists prefer the original vinyl versions for their scale and weight; others see BCD as the more complete artistic statement. Either way, it is now considered one of the foundation stones of dub techno and minimal electronic music.","Listen from start to finish at a steady volume, perhaps at night, and let it recede into the background before returning your attention to it. Notice how the bass and crackle define space. Compare with Burial's Untrue, which inherited its haze, and Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works 85–92, a very different take on the same era."],"serge-gainsbourg-le-poinconneur-des-lilas":["A brisk, jaunty song about a man who punches tickets all day at the Porte des Lilas station of the Paris Métro. The accompaniment is a light jazz combo that trots along like a commuter's footsteps, and Gainsbourg sings in a dry, clipped, half-spoken voice. The title word 'poinçonneur' and the endless 'little holes' of his job return again and again, so the refrain itself becomes the drudgery it describes: the same small action, repeated until it hurts.","This was the opening track of Gainsbourg's first album, Du chant à la une!, released by Philips in 1958 when he was thirty and still better known as a bar pianist in Paris cabarets. Left Bank chanson was then dominated by Brassens, Brel and Juliette Gréco, and the young Gainsbourg, arranged by the jazz pianist Alain Goraguer, set out to be sharper and wittier than the poets around him. The song became his first real success, and Les Frères Jacques' cover helped spread it.","Listen for how the joke darkens. The verses begin as comic grumbling about a job underground, where the ticket-puncher never sees the sun, but by the end he is dreaming of turning the punch on himself, a suicidal fantasy delivered at the same cheerful tempo. That gap between bright music and bleak words became Gainsbourg's lifelong method, and it is already fully formed here, in his first year of recording.","Critics at the time were divided about Gainsbourg himself, finding him cold and ugly beside the warm chansonniers, but this song was liked at once and has never faded. It is now one of the best-known French songs of the 1950s, taught in schools and endlessly covered. Later listeners often hear it as the seed of everything he did: literary wordplay, a hint of despair, and a pop sense that was already restless.","Start with the words' rhythm rather than their meaning: notice how the hard consonants of the refrain punch like the machine itself. Then follow the story from boredom to despair while the band stays perky. Compare it with Brassens or Boris Vian to hear the Left Bank world he came from, then jump ahead ten years to Initials B.B. to hear how far he travelled."],"serge-gainsbourg-initials-bb":["A slow, swaggering portrait song. Gainsbourg half-speaks, half-croons over a big orchestral arrangement whose strings and brass swell into a grand, sweeping theme borrowed from Dvořák's New World Symphony. The words sketch a woman who wears little but leather boots and jewellery, glimpsed as a kind of idol in a dim room. The record's signature is that contrast: a cool, murmured voice set against a lush symphonic surge that seems to belong to another world.","Gainsbourg had a brief, intense affair with Brigitte Bardot in late 1967, writing Bonnie and Clyde and the first version of Je t'aime… moi non plus for her. When Bardot, then married, asked him not to release their duet, he wrote this song as a farewell tribute to her, the initials of the title being hers. It became the title track of a 1968 album recorded in London, where Gainsbourg was beginning to work with British arrangers and session players.","The borrowed tune is the giveaway: Gainsbourg drops a famous theme from Dvořák's New World Symphony into the middle of a pop song, the first of many raids on the classical repertoire he would make, later including Chopin and Brahms. The quotation is not decoration. A Czech composer's homesick picture of America becomes the soundtrack for a lost love, and the grandeur is half-mocking, a man dressing up his heartbreak in borrowed finery.","At the time it was taken as a slightly outré love song; the Bardot backstory only became common knowledge once the original duet was finally released in 1986. Since then it has been ranked among his finest singles and seen as a hinge between the jazzy chansonnier of the 1950s and the orchestral pop auteur of Melody Nelson. Some listeners still find the classical borrowing heavy-handed; others hear it as the point.","Listen first for the moment the orchestra rises into Dvořák's melody, then go back to Dvořák's New World Symphony and hear the theme before Gainsbourg took it. Notice how little he actually sings. Then play Histoire de Melody Nelson, where the same speaking voice and big-string sound are stretched to a whole album."],"serge-gainsbourg-aux-armes-et-caetera":["Gainsbourg's reggae album: a dozen songs laid over deep, slow Jamaican grooves, with bass and drums far forward in the mix, guitar chops on the offbeat, and a trio of women singers answering his low, lazy murmur. The title track is a reggae version of La Marseillaise, the French national anthem, sung almost casually over a skanking rhythm. Around it are sly, smutty, funny songs, all delivered in the same drowsy, mocking voice.","Gainsbourg flew to Kingston in early 1979 and recorded with the best rhythm section in Jamaica, the drummer Sly Dunbar and bassist Robbie Shakespeare, with the I-Threes, Bob Marley's backing singers, adding harmonies. Reggae was then at the peak of its international fame, and Gainsbourg, after years of admired but modest sales, saw a way to reach a young audience. The record went platinum in France, easily the biggest commercial success of his career so far.","The title is a scholarly joke. In Rouget de Lisle's manuscript of the Marseillaise, the refrain is abbreviated after its first words with an 'etc.', and Gainsbourg took that shorthand as his title. When he was later attacked for defacing the anthem, he bought an original manuscript of the song at auction to prove that the abbreviation was the composer's own, and that singing only fragments of the refrain was an old habit, not a new insult.","The anthem track caused a national scandal. A columnist in Le Figaro attacked him in terms many read as antisemitic, and in 1980 former paratroopers turned up at a concert in Strasbourg; with his band absent, Gainsbourg walked on alone and sang the Marseillaise straight, fist raised. Today the album is seen as a pioneering French meeting with Jamaican music, and the controversy as a turning point in how France treats its own symbols.","Start with the title track and listen to what the rhythm does to a tune everyone knows: the martial snap is replaced by a slow sway, and that alone changes its meaning. Then listen to the bass and drums on their own through a whole side. Compare with Peter Tosh's Equal Rights, where the same rhythm section plays for a Jamaican singer."],"daft-punk-alive-2007":["A live album recorded in a Paris arena, in which Daft Punk take their three studio records apart and splice them back together into a single continuous set. Songs are laid on top of one another: the robotic vocal of one track rides over the bassline of another, and familiar hooks surface from a wall of filtered sound before the next wave crashes in. The crowd's roar is part of the texture. The recognisable gesture is the mash-up itself, two hits locking together.","The set began at the Coachella festival in California in 2006, when the duo appeared inside a giant illuminated pyramid, dressed as robots, after years of refusing to perform. That show, widely filmed on early phones and shared online, is often credited with helping ignite the huge American appetite for electronic dance music in the following decade. This recording comes from the tour's Paris date at Bercy in June 2007, and was released that November.","Listen to how the sequencing makes an argument about their own career. Tracks from Human After All, the 2005 album that critics had found thin and repetitive, are repeatedly married to the earlier hits, and in the live setting those stark, looping riffs suddenly work as the engine under the more melodic songs. The record quietly rehabilitated an album people had written off, by showing it was built as raw material.","It won the Grammy for Best Electronic/Dance Album, and the live version of Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger won Best Dance Recording, rare honours for a live dance record. Critics praised it as the best document of electronic music as a stadium spectacle. Purists note that a pre-programmed show is not improvisation in the jazz sense; admirers answer that the art lies in the arrangement, which is exactly what a DJ's craft is.","Play it loud and in one go, as a set rather than a collection of tracks. The game is spotting which songs are being combined at any moment, so it helps to know Homework and Discovery first. Listen for the long build before a famous hook breaks through, and for how the crowd reacts a second before you recognise it yourself."],"daft-punk-tron-legacy":["A film score for a Hollywood science-fiction sequel, written for a large symphony orchestra fused with analogue synthesizers. Many cues are built on slow, heavy ostinatos in low strings and brass, with pulsing arpeggiated synths woven through them, so that the orchestra seems to breathe in time with a machine. Short, dark cues alternate with a few driving club tracks. The sound you remember is a huge, gleaming brass chord hovering over a ticking synth line.","Director Joseph Kosinski asked Daft Punk, then famous only as a dance act, to score Disney's sequel to the 1982 film Tron, a story set inside a computer world. The duo spent a long time on it, working with the orchestrator Joseph Trapanese and recording the orchestra in London. The job linked them to a lineage of electronic film scoring stretching back to Wendy Carlos's music for the original Tron, and they appear briefly on screen as helmeted DJs.","Daft Punk said they wanted the orchestra to sound like the electronics and the electronics to sound like the orchestra, and in many cues the boundary is hard to hear: a synth pad doubles the violas, a sequenced pulse doubles pizzicato cellos. Listen too for how often the harmony hangs on a single held bass note while the upper lines move, a trick taken from minimalist concert music and from the Vangelis scores of the 1980s.","Reviews of the film were mixed, but the score was widely praised as its best element, and it has outlasted the movie; the soundtrack sold strongly and is regularly performed in concert. Some film-music critics found it too indebted to Hans Zimmer's brass-heavy style of the period, and argued its themes are thin. Others hear it as the rare Hollywood score by pop musicians that stands as an album of its own.","Start with the track Derezzed for the club side, then with a slower cue such as Recognizer or the end titles to hear orchestra and synth fused. Listen for where the electronics stop and the strings begin. Then compare Wendy Carlos's score for the 1982 Tron, and Vangelis's Blade Runner, to hear the tradition it joins."],"cheikha-rimitti-charrak-gatta":["An early raï song in its old, rural form: Rimitti's raw, rasping voice over the breathy wail of the gasba, a long end-blown reed flute, and the dry beat of the gellal, a small goblet drum. The melody moves in short, repeating phrases that circle a few notes, with sudden cries and ornaments. The words, in Oran Arabic, speak frankly about a young woman's loss of virginity, and the blunt title became the most notorious phrase in raï.","Rimitti, born Saadia El Ghizania, grew up poor in western Algeria and learned her art among the itinerant musicians who played at weddings, bars and saints' festivals around Oran. Algeria was still a French colony, and 78-rpm records of local singers were sold in the cafés of the region. Women performing this kind of plain-spoken song in public were already defying custom, and a song about sexuality recorded by a woman crossed a line that respectable society, colonial and Algerian alike, would not accept.","The song belongs to a long oral tradition of Bedouin poetry, in which the singer strings together short verses that can be rearranged and reused, and Rimitti's own name reportedly came from mispronouncing the French 'remettez', 'pour another round', in a bar. Listen to the flute and voice: they trade the same few melodic cells back and forth, which is how the older melhoun and Bedouin repertoire worked, long before synthesizers arrived.","The song made Rimitti a scandal: religious figures condemned it, and and it was treated as an attack on family honour. For decades she was banned from Algerian radio. Later generations of raï singers, and the French world-music scene of the 1980s and 1990s, rediscovered her as the movement's defiant grandmother, and the song is now treated as a founding document.","Listen with the volume up and focus on the grain of the voice: rough, unpolished, closer to the blues than to pop. Follow the gasba's replies to each phrase. Then play Khaled's Didi to hear what the same tradition became with drum machines and synths thirty-odd years later, and Bessie Smith for another woman who sang plainly about desire."],"cheikha-rimitti-nta-goudami":["Rimitti's last album, a return to the old sound after years of electric and fusion records. The line-up is essentially the one she began with: reed flutes, frame and goblet drums, and her voice, now deep, cracked and weighty in her eighties, rolling out long improvised verses. Tempos are slow to medium, the grooves hypnotic and circling. What you notice first is the space: no keyboards or drum machines, just breath, skin and a voice that sounds older than the century.","By the early 2000s Rimitti lived in Paris, where she had moved decades earlier, and French labels had presented her to rock and world-music audiences, most famously on Sidi Mansour with Robert Fripp and Flea. For this final record she and her producers deliberately went back to the rural raï of western Algeria. Released in 2005, it came out not long before her death in Paris in May 2006, only days after she had given a concert there.","Many of the songs are built the traditional way, by stitching together stock poetic lines and fresh verses into long chains, so no two performances would ever match. Listen to how the gasba players circular-breathe to sustain their notes under her phrases, and how Rimitti leaves gaps for them to fill. The unadorned sound is itself a statement: after decades of her music being dressed up, she chose to be heard bare.","The album was warmly received in France and among world-music critics, who heard it as a dignified farewell and a rebuke to the glossier pop-raï that had overtaken her style. It sold modestly beside the stars she had influenced. For listeners who know raï only through Khaled or Rachid Taha, it is often recommended as the clearest window onto the music's older, wilder roots, recorded late but authentically.","Put it on at night and let the long tracks run. Listen for the moment each flute enters and how it shadows the voice. Compare with Charrak Gattà, recorded half a century earlier, to hear what age did to the voice and what it left intact, then with Sidi Mansour to hear the fusion road she turned back from."],"jean-michel-jarre-zoolook":["An album built from the human voice, chopped up. Jarre recorded speech and song in dozens of languages, loaded fragments into a Fairlight sampler and played them as instruments: syllables become drum hits, a laugh becomes a melody, a word in an unknown tongue becomes a riff. Around these sit real bass, drums and guitar, so it is far funkier and busier than his earlier space music. The instant marker is the chattering, stuttering vocal collage of the title track.","The Fairlight CMI, one of the first commercial digital samplers, had arrived in the early 1980s at enormous cost, and Jarre had already experimented with it on Les Chants magnétiques. For Zoolook he brought in New York musicians, among them the bassist Marcus Miller, the guitarist Adrian Belew and the performance artist Laurie Anderson, moving away from the solitary synthesizer records that had made him famous. It followed his 1981 concerts in China and came out in late 1984.","Listen past the beat to the voices: Jarre drew them from a wide range of languages and traditions, some reportedly from ethnographic recordings, and none is heard long enough to understand, which turns human speech into pure sound, the old dream of the Paris musique concrète studios where he had trained with Pierre Schaeffer's circle. The title itself blends 'zoo' and 'look', a hint that we are listening to humanity as a collection of sounds.","Sales were lower than for Oxygène and Équinoxe, and some fans missed the soaring melodies. Critics and musicians, however, have increasingly treated it as his most adventurous and prophetic record, a pop album built on sampling at a moment when hip-hop and the Art of Noise were only starting to do the same. It is often named by electronic producers as a hidden influence, though it remains less known than his hits.","Start with the title track and try to catch individual syllables as they fly past; then listen to Diva, where Laurie Anderson's voice floats over the texture. Compare with Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge, the 1950s ancestor of cut-up voices, and then with Oxygène to hear how far Jarre moved from spacey calm toward crowded, rhythmic collage."],"khaled-kutche":["A collaboration between the singer, then still Cheb Khaled, and the Algerian composer and arranger Safy Boutella. Khaled's soaring, ornamented voice rides over arrangements that mix raï rhythms with funk bass, jazz horns, drum machines and synthesizer washes. The songs keep their Oran melodies and dance pulse, but the sound is spacious and studio-built rather than the thin, cassette-recorded raï of the time. The record's marker is a supple, jazzy bassline under a keening Arabic vocal.","In the 1980s raï boomed in Algeria on cheap cassettes, and young singers took the 'Cheb' title to set themselves apart from older masters. Boutella, a trained jazz musician, wanted to give the music an arrangement worthy of international ears, and the album, produced in France, was one of the first raï records aimed deliberately at audiences outside the Maghreb. It appeared the same year riots shook Algeria and just before the country's slide into civil war.","Listen to how Boutella keeps the characteristic raï rhythmic lilt, a lopsided pattern that feels like a limp in the beat, while laying Western jazz harmony on top; the chords under Khaled's melody often move in ways traditional raï never would, yet the voice ignores them and follows its own modal path. That friction between two harmonic worlds is the album's real subject, and it is easy to miss beneath the groove.","Kutché was praised by French critics and helped Khaled move to France and sign with a major label, leading to Didi. Some in Algeria felt Boutella's arrangements tamed the music; others hear the record as the true birth of international pop-raï, more adventurous than the glossier albums that followed. It is less famous than Didi but often cited by connoisseurs as Khaled's most musically interesting record.","Listen first for the bass and horns, then shift your attention to the voice and notice how independent it is of the harmony underneath. Compare with Cheikha Rimitti's old flute-and-drum raï to hear what was added, and with Didi, four years later, to hear what a Los Angeles pop production did next."],"khaled-aicha":["A mid-tempo love ballad with a sweeping, string-laden arrangement, sung mostly in French with a verse in Arabic. Khaled's voice, full and ringing, leans into long notes and ornamented turns even in the French lines. The song is a dialogue: a man offers a woman jewels, riches and devotion, and she answers that she wants none of it, only to be treated as his equal. The unmistakable moment is the lifted, repeated call of her name in the chorus.","The song was written by Jean-Jacques Goldman, one of France's best-selling songwriters, for Khaled's 1996 album Sahra, which aimed squarely at the French mainstream. Algeria was in the depths of its civil war, raï singers had been threatened and killed, and many North Africans in France faced hostility. A French star writing a hit for an Algerian singer, in both languages, carried a quiet message about belonging that audiences heard clearly.","The twist is in the woman's reply, which turns a conventional song of courtship into a statement about women's dignity and rights, sung by a man from a tradition often accused of machismo. Listen too to how Khaled bends the French melody with Arabic ornaments, sliding into notes rather than hitting them, so that the song is subtly bilingual even when the words are all in French.","It was a huge hit in France and Belgium and across the Arab world, and it has been covered many times, including an English and Arabic version by the Danish group Outlandish. Some raï purists dismissed it as variety-show pop far removed from Oran. Others argue it did more than any other record to make an Algerian voice at home on French radio, and it is still one of the most loved songs in France from the 1990s.","Listen to it as a conversation: notice where the man's promises end and the woman's answer begins, and how the music changes weight there. Then go back to Didi to hear the same voice in pure dance mode, and to Youssou N'Dour and Neneh Cherry's 7 Seconds, from two years earlier, for another song that crossed languages in the French charts."],"art-ensemble-of-chicago-fanfare-for-the-warriors":["The Art Ensemble's most varied and accessible studio album: seven pieces that move from swinging, almost traditional jazz to free collective improvisation, from a mock-march to hushed passages of bells and small percussion. Lester Bowie's trumpet brays and laughs, Roscoe Mitchell and Joseph Jarman switch between saxophones, flutes and clarinets, and guest pianist Muhal Richard Abrams adds harmonic weight. The title track's blaring, deliberately ragged brass-band fanfare is the moment everyone remembers.","The band had spent 1969 to 1971 in France, where their theatrical, multi-instrumental shows won a following, before returning to Chicago. They signed to Atlantic, a major label, which gave them a studio and budget their earlier small-label records lacked. Abrams, the founder of the Chicago musicians' cooperative, the AACM, from which the group had emerged, joined them here, making it a reunion of the Chicago avant-garde at the height of Black Arts militancy.","Listen for the jokes and the history hidden inside the free passages. Barnyard Scuffel Shuffel sounds like chaos, yet it keeps winking at old New Orleans polyphony; Nonaah, a Roscoe Mitchell composition, is built on obsessive repetition of a single jagged phrase. The group's motto, 'Great Black Music: Ancient to the Future', is acted out across the album: every idiom from march to swing to pure sound is treated as their own inheritance.","It was well reviewed on release and is often named the best entry point to the group, balancing structure and freedom better than their sprawling earlier records. Some admirers of the band's harder Paris recordings found it too tidy. Its reputation has only grown, and it now sits beside People in Sorrow as their most discussed album, a key document of how the AACM rethought jazz as a total art.","Start with the title track, then go to the quieter pieces and listen to the textures of bells, gongs and small instruments, the famous 'little instruments' of their stage kit. Notice how the band slides between written material and free playing without announcing it. Then hear Muhal Richard Abrams's Levels and Degrees of Light for the AACM world it comes from."],"jarrett-facing-you":["Keith Jarrett's first solo piano album: eight original pieces, some folk-like and hymnal, some driving and rhythmic, all played on acoustic piano with a clear, close sound. Jarrett moves between gospel chords, country-ish melodies, Debussy-like washes and busy left-hand grooves, often humming along audibly. The opening track, In Front, sets the tone: a rocking, churchy groove that grows into a long, rhapsodic improvisation. Unlike the later concerts, the pieces are shaped and mostly composed.","Jarrett recorded it in a single day in November 1971 in Oslo, for the young German label ECM and its producer Manfred Eicher, while he was touring Europe as a member of Miles Davis's loud electric band. The contrast was pointed: at night he played electric keyboards in a fusion group, by day he made a record of unamplified solo piano. ECM's glassy, reverberant studio sound, soon to become famous, is already here.","Listen for how much of the record avoids the usual jazz vocabulary: there are few standard chord changes or swing rhythms, and several pieces sound closer to American hymnody, folk ballads or French piano music. Jarrett's audible singing, often mocked, is also a clue: he is following a melody in his head that the fingers only partly realise, which is why some lines seem to break off and begin again.","It was received as a striking debut and launched ECM's solo-piano catalogue, which would grow to include the Köln Concert. Many critics now consider it more carefully structured than the long improvised concerts that followed. Some jazz writers found its romanticism too far from the tradition; others hear it as the moment jazz piano opened to folk, gospel and classical colours without apology.","Start with In Front and let the groove build; then play the ballad My Lady; My Child for his lyrical side. Listen for the humming, and for how the left hand sometimes becomes a whole rhythm section. Compare Bill Evans's Peace Piece, an earlier solo improvisation, and then the Köln Concert to hear the same ideas stretched to an hour."],"jarrett-the-survivors-suite":["A single long composition in two parts, Beginning and Conclusion, recorded by Jarrett's American Quartet. It opens with Jarrett alone on bass recorder, a dark, woody sound, before the band builds slowly toward long stretches of fierce group improvisation and lyrical piano. Dewey Redman's tenor saxophone is gritty and vocal, Charlie Haden's bass deep and songlike, Paul Motian's drums loose and coloristic. The suite feels like a journey through a landscape, with themes returning transformed at the end.","Through the 1970s Jarrett led two bands at once: a European Quartet with Scandinavian players, heard on Belonging, and this American group, which had roots in Ornette Coleman's circle through Haden and Redman. The American band recorded mostly for Impulse!, but this late session, made in Germany in 1976, went to ECM and turned out to be one of its last statements. Soon after, Jarrett would dissolve both groups.","Jarrett plays far more than piano here: listen for his soprano saxophone, the bass recorder, celesta and small percussion, which is why the texture often seems larger than a quartet. The long opening is not improvised meandering but a composed theme stated slowly, and it returns near the close in a new setting, so the fifty-minute piece has the shape of a classical cyclic work.","Critics received it warmly, and it has been called the American Quartet's finest hour, uniting the band's free-jazz energy with Jarrett's compositional ambition. Its reputation grew in the shadow of the solo concerts, and admirers often argue it deserves wider fame. Some listeners find the slow opening indulgent; others think it is precisely what makes the eventual eruption so powerful.","Allow the full time and resist skipping the quiet start: the payoff depends on it. Listen for Redman's tenor entry and for the way Haden holds the band together with a few low notes. Then compare Belonging, the European band's calmer sound, to hear two sides of the same leader, and Coltrane's A Love Supreme for the suite form it answers."],"jarrett-standards-vol-1":["A trio album of five songs from the American songbook and jazz repertoire, including All the Things You Are, It Never Entered My Mind and God Bless the Child. Jarrett, Gary Peacock and Jack DeJohnette take each tune at length, stating the melody and then improvising in long, flowing lines, with bass and drums as equal voices. The closing God Bless the Child becomes a fifteen-minute gospel groove, rocking on a repeated figure that seems able to go on forever.","By 1983 Jarrett was famous for solo improvisations and his own compositions, and playing standards seemed almost a retreat. The trio recorded in New York in January 1983 at the suggestion of ECM's Manfred Eicher, with Peacock and DeJohnette, who had both worked with Jarrett before. The same sessions yielded the album Changes and a second Standards volume. Young musicians in the 1980s were turning back to the jazz tradition, and this record fitted that moment.","Listen to how seldom the drummer simply keeps time: DeJohnette plays around the beat, commenting on the piano's phrases like a second soloist, and Peacock often walks a counter-melody rather than a bassline. The group treats a thirty-two-bar tune as a space to expand and contract, so a chorus may stretch far beyond its written length without anyone announcing the change. That elasticity is what made this trio's sound new.","It was praised immediately and launched a trio that played together for some thirty years, one of the longest-lived working bands in jazz. Some critics at first considered the project conservative, and others grumbled about Jarrett's vocal moaning. The long view is clear: the Standards Trio is now considered one of the great piano trios, in the line from Bill Evans, and a model for later players such as Brad Mehldau.","Start with It Never Entered My Mind for the ballad playing, then settle into God Bless the Child and let the groove carry you. Listen to the drums as closely as the piano. Compare Bill Evans's Village Vanguard trio for the ancestor, and Brad Mehldau's Art of the Trio for what came next."],"bjork-post":["Björk's second solo album is a restless mix: industrial-strength beats on Army of Me, trip-hop murk on Enjoy, lush strings on Isobel, a swing-band showstopper in It's Oh So Quiet, and the slow-building club ballad Hyperballad. Her voice leaps from whisper to roar within a phrase. The record's recognisable sound is that collision, an intimate Icelandic voice set against machine beats and orchestral colour, each song a different room in the same house.","After Debut made her an international star, Björk stayed in London, then the centre of trip-hop and electronic experiment. She worked with several producers, including Nellee Hooper, Tricky, Graham Massey of 808 State and Howie B, and with the Brazilian arranger Eumir Deodato on strings. The title reflects her sense of the songs as letters sent home to Iceland from abroad. It came out in June 1995, when British pop was dominated by guitar-based Britpop.","It's Oh So Quiet, the album's big hit, is a cover of a 1950s song first sung by Betty Hutton, which itself came from an Austrian original, a surprising old-fashioned piece to find amid the electronics. Hyperballad contains a small private story: the singer wakes early, throws small objects off a cliff and imagines her own fall, then returns calmer to the partner still asleep, a way of keeping a relationship alive by letting darkness out alone.","Post was a commercial and critical success, and many critics consider it her most varied and generous album. Some at the time felt its eclecticism lacked unity, a complaint answered two years later by the cohesive Homogenic. Hyperballad in particular has grown in stature and is often named among the finest songs of the 1990s. The record is now seen as a key document of mid-decade London's blending of pop and electronic music.","Start with Hyperballad and listen to how the beat enters late, lifting the song from confession to release. Then try Isobel for the strings and Army of Me for the drums. Compare Debut before and Homogenic after, and Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for another singer who used the studio as an instrument."],"bjork-medulla":["An album made almost entirely of human voices. Choirs sing chords, beatboxers provide the drums and bass, a throat singer growls and gasps, and Björk layers her own voice into dozens of parts; only a few sounds are not vocal. The songs range from hymnlike choral pieces to dense rhythmic tracks where you cannot tell mouth from machine. The unforgettable texture is breath itself: clicks, hums, grunts and sighs arranged as music.","Björk had just made the delicate, electronic Vespertine and become a mother for the second time, and she wanted to strip away instruments and return to the body. Collaborators included the beatboxers Rahzel and Dokaka, the Inuk throat singer Tanya Tagaq, Mike Patton, Robert Wyatt, and Icelandic and British choirs. The album appeared in 2004, and its song Oceania was performed at the opening ceremony of the Athens Olympics that summer.","Listen for the processing: many voices are edited, pitched and chopped with computers, so the 'no instruments' idea is less purist than it sounds and closer to sampling. The title, Latin for the core of an organ or the part of the brainstem that controls breathing, names the real subject. Several tracks borrow the shape of old hymns and Icelandic choral singing, making it partly a record about national and religious tradition.","Reviews were largely admiring, though some critics found it more interesting as an idea than as a set of songs. It sold less than her earlier albums. Over time it has been increasingly recognised as a daring experiment, in the line of composers such as Meredith Monk and Stockhausen who treated the voice as a total instrument, and as the moment when beatboxing entered art music.","Start with Who Is It and try to guess which sounds are made by which mouths. Then listen to Oceania, sung from the point of view of the sea, and the short choral pieces. Compare Meredith Monk's Dolmen Music and Stockhausen's Stimmung for the concert-hall ancestors of this all-voice sound."],"abba-dancing-queen":["A slow-disco anthem that opens with a sweeping piano glissando and a cascade of strings, then settles into a relaxed, rolling groove. Agnetha Fältskog and Anni-Frid Lyngstad sing in tight unison and harmony, their voices doubled and redoubled into a gleaming wall. The words describe a seventeen-year-old girl at the dance floor on a Friday night, the centre of attention for one evening. The famous moment is the chorus: soaring, bittersweet and impossible to forget.","The song was written by Benny Andersson and Björn Ulvaeus with their manager Stig Anderson and recorded in Stockholm in 1975, with a rhythm reportedly inspired by George McCrae's disco hit Rock Your Baby. ABBA premiered it on Swedish television in June 1976 at a gala honouring King Carl XVI Gustaf and his bride-to-be, Silvia. Released that August and later on the album Arrival, it became the group's only number one in the United States.","Listen for the melancholy: the girl is celebrated, but the song is sung from outside her, by grown women looking back at a moment that will not last. Harmonically, the chorus keeps avoiding a firm landing, which gives its joy a slightly wistful ache. The layered vocals, in which each singer recorded several parts, are the secret of the shimmering sound, a studio technique ABBA took from Phil Spector's wall of sound.","It was an enormous hit across Europe, Australia and the Americas, yet for years ABBA were dismissed by rock critics as lightweight. Judgement changed from the 1990s, helped by Muriel's Wedding, the musical Mamma Mia! and open admiration from younger musicians. The song is now routinely named one of the greatest pop records, and a touchstone of gay club culture. Debate continues only over whether its sadness or its joy matters more.","Listen for the piano slide at the very start, then the moment the strings lift into the chorus. Try hearing it once as a party song and once as a sad song; both work. Then compare Donna Summer's I Feel Love, from the next year, and Robyn's Dancing On My Own, which answers it from the edge of the dance floor."],"robyn-robyn":["Robyn's fourth album, and the one that reinvented her: tight, electronic pop songs built on sharp drum programming and synthesizers, with a voice that is sweet, tough and sardonic by turns. Konichiwa Bitches is a cheeky rap-like boast, Be Mine! a heartbroken ballad over pizzicato strings and a spoken passage, Handle Me a strutting warning. The sound is crisp, minimal and modern, pointed at the dance floor but full of feeling.","Robyn had been a Swedish teen star in the 1990s with American hits, but her label resisted the more electronic direction she wanted. She bought her way out and founded her own label, Konichiwa, releasing this record in Sweden in 2005, with a UK release following in 2007. She worked mainly with the Swedish producer Klas Åhlund, and one track was made with members of the Stockholm duo The Knife; later editions added With Every Heartbeat, made with Kleerup.","Listen for how often a song pivots from a hard, playful surface to confession: the ballad Be Mine! stops for a spoken aside in which the singer admits she watched her former lover from a distance, a technique she would perfect on Body Talk. Starting her own label also mattered to the sound: freed from radio formulas, the tracks keep strange, bare arrangements that a major label would have filled in.","The album topped the Swedish chart and was acclaimed by critics in Britain and elsewhere, where With Every Heartbeat became a number one single in 2007. It is now often cited as a model for the independent pop star who owns her own work, a path many later artists followed. Some listeners still find the brash tracks dated, but Be Mine! and With Every Heartbeat are widely considered classics of 2000s pop.","Start with Be Mine! and listen for the spoken passage and the strings; then turn to Konichiwa Bitches for the comic side. Compare ABBA's Dancing Queen for the Swedish tradition of sad dance music, then jump to Body Talk to hear how the same mix of beats and heartbreak became her signature."],"robyn-body-talk":["The collected album of Robyn's 2010 trilogy of short releases: fifteen songs of pulsing, synthesized dance-pop, most of them about love going wrong. Dancing On My Own, Call Your Girlfriend, Hang with Me and Indestructible are built on bright, driving synths and four-on-the-floor beats, with melodies that rise in hope and fall in sorrow. Among them sit a comic robot song, Fembot, and a dancehall track. The trademark is euphoric music around lonely words.","Robyn and her collaborators, chiefly Klas Åhlund, decided to release music in bursts rather than one album, answering an era of streaming and blogs in which songs spread faster than records. Body Talk Pt. 1 appeared in June 2010, Pt. 2 in September, and the full Body Talk, gathering the best of both with new songs, in November. Snoop Dogg joined her on one track. She released it on her own label, as she had since 2005.","Listen to Call Your Girlfriend: the singer is advising a man how to break up gently with his current partner so he can be with her, a surprisingly tender and complicated position for a dance song. On Body Talk Pt. 1 she also sang the Swedish folk song Jag vet en dejlig rosa over synths, left off the full album; it reveals the tradition beneath her pop: Scandinavian melancholy, the same minor-key yearning that runs back through ABBA to old ballads.","Body Talk was widely named one of the best albums of 2010 and Dancing On My Own one of the songs of the decade; it earned Grammy nominations. Its influence on later pop, from Lorde to Charli XCX and Taylor Swift's 1989, is often noted. Some felt the trilogy release diluted the impact, and that the full album is uneven. Most now treat it as the high point of her work.","Begin with Dancing On My Own, then Call Your Girlfriend, and notice how both place joy in the beat and pain in the words. Listen for the moment the chorus opens out. Then try Fembot for the comic side. Compare with ABBA's Dancing Queen and with Lorde's Melodrama, which learned from it."],"django-dinah":["One of the first recordings by the Quintette du Hot Club de France: an American pop tune taken at a bright, bouncing tempo, played entirely by strings. Two rhythm guitars chug in the steady, percussive 'pompe' style, the bass thumps underneath, and Stéphane Grappelli's violin and Django Reinhardt's guitar take turns at the melody and at dazzling improvised solos. The sound, with no drums and no horns, is light, springy and instantly recognisable as a new kind of jazz.","Dinah was written by Harry Akst in 1925 and was a standard by the 1930s. Reinhardt, a Manouche guitarist who had taught himself to play again after a caravan fire badly burned his left hand, met Grappelli in Paris, and with the backing of the Hot Club de France, a jazz appreciation society, they formed a string band. They recorded it for the Ultraphone label in late 1934, one of the first sides to present a European jazz group to the world.","Listen to Django's left hand: because two fingers were crippled by the fire, he played most solos with just two fingers on the fretboard, which pushed him toward fast runs along a single string and toward chord shapes no one else used. The breathless octave lines and sudden chromatic slides that sound like pure showmanship are partly solutions to a physical limit. The rhythm section includes his brother Joseph on guitar.","The Quintette's records were quickly admired in France, Britain and the United States, and American musicians were surprised to hear such original jazz from Europe. Critics now regard these 1934 sessions as the birth of what came to be called gypsy jazz, and Reinhardt as the first major jazz innovator from outside America. Some older writers treated the string band as a novelty; that view has vanished.","Listen first to the rhythm guitars alone: they are the engine. Then follow the solos and hear how different violin and guitar sound over the same chords. Compare Louis Armstrong's Hot Five, the American model, then play Minor Swing, three years later, to hear the group fully mature."],"django-bach-double-concerto":["A jazz treatment of the first movement of Bach's Concerto for Two Violins. The two violinists, the American Eddie South and Stéphane Grappelli, play Bach's interweaving lines more or less as written, while Django Reinhardt's guitar and a bass replace the Baroque string orchestra with a swinging rhythm. Then, gradually, the violins break loose into improvisation over Bach's harmonies. The pleasure is hearing a strict Baroque counterpoint start to swing.","Recorded in Paris in 1937 for Swing, the new record label founded by Charles Delaunay and the Hot Club de France. Eddie South, a classically trained Black American violinist barred by racism from an orchestral career, had studied in Paris and Budapest and was performing in Europe. Jazz musicians of the 1930s often 'swung the classics', usually as a novelty, but these players knew the music and treated it with real respect.","The title is the joke and the manifesto in one: a solemn, fully formal French label describing a three-minute jazz record, as if claiming the same standing as the concert hall. Listen for the moment the players stop reading Bach and start inventing: it is subtle, because they keep Bach's own chord sequence, which fits jazz improvisation surprisingly well.","Jazz critics prized it as an early example of the dialogue between jazz and classical music, long before third-stream experiments. Classical listeners sometimes found it disrespectful; today it is more often cited as proof of how naturally Bach's counterpoint fits swing, anticipating the Modern Jazz Quartet and Jacques Loussier's Play Bach. Eddie South's playing, long overlooked, has also been rediscovered through it.","Listen first to a period-instrument recording of Bach's Concerto for Two Violins, then play this and notice what stays and what changes: the melody lines are the same, the rhythm is transformed. Listen for the point where the violins begin to improvise. Then hear the Modern Jazz Quartet's Django for another meeting of Bach and jazz."],"django-djangology":["Recordings from Rome in 1949 that reunited Django Reinhardt and Stéphane Grappelli, backed by an Italian rhythm section of piano, bass and drums rather than the old rhythm guitars. They revisit their classics, including Minor Swing and Nuages, and other favourites, but the playing has changed: Reinhardt's lines are longer, harmonically bolder and closer to bebop, while Grappelli is as elegant as ever. The feel is relaxed, like two old friends catching up, with brilliant flashes.","The Second World War had split the partners: Grappelli stayed in London and Reinhardt in occupied Paris, where he became a star. After the war they reunited only occasionally. Bebop had arrived from New York, and Reinhardt listened hard to Charlie Parker and Dizzy Gillespie. These Rome sessions were recorded in early 1949, and the tapes were issued much later, in 1961, after Reinhardt's death in 1953, under the title Djangology.","Listen to the chords behind Reinhardt's solos and the notes he chooses: he is using the altered harmonies and quick, angular phrases of bebop inside tunes he had played for fifteen years. Without the old pompe rhythm guitars, the drummer and pianist give a more modern pulse, and the two soloists have more space. It is the sound of the founder of a style updating himself in public.","When it appeared, some admirers of the 1930s Quintette felt the old magic was missing, and it has sometimes been treated as a coda. Others hear it as some of Reinhardt's most mature playing, a guitarist absorbing bebop without losing his voice, and regard the late recordings as underrated. It remains the best-known document of the pair's reunion and a favourite among guitarists.","Play the Rome version of Minor Swing next to the 1937 original and listen for what has changed in Reinhardt's lines. Notice the drums and piano replacing the rhythm guitars. Then hear Charlie Parker's Ko-Ko to hear the new music that was pulling him forward, and the Modern Jazz Quartet's Django for the tribute that followed his death."],"amalia-rodrigues-uma-casa-portuguesa":["A bright, swaying song rather than a lament: the singer describes a humble Portuguese home where bread, wine and a warm welcome matter more than money, and where a stranger at the door is invited to the table. The Portuguese guitar skips along in a lilting major-key rhythm, and Amália sings with a smile in the voice, holding back the sobbing ornaments she reserved for tragic fados. It lasts barely three minutes.","The music is by Artur Fonseca and the words by Reinaldo Ferreira and Vasco de Matos Sequeira; Amália took it up in the early 1950s, when she was already Lisbon's most famous singer and beginning to tour abroad. Portugal was then governed by Salazar's Estado Novo, whose official picture of the nation was pious, rural, modest and content, a picture this song fitted almost too neatly for later comfort.","Strictly speaking this is not fado at all but a marcha-like popular song dressed in fado instruments; notice that there is none of the free, speech-like stretching of the line that defines a true fado. Listen too for how the guitar answers each vocal phrase with a little cadence of its own, the conversational habit that the Portuguese guitar brings to everything it accompanies.","It became one of the most recognised Portuguese songs in the world and a staple for later singers. After the 1974 Carnation Revolution, however, its cosy image of contented poverty was heard by many as dictatorship propaganda, and Amália herself was unfairly branded a creature of the regime. Today it is usually enjoyed as period charm, with the political argument remembered rather than resolved.","Hear it first simply as a dance song and let the lilt carry you. Then play it next to Barco Negro to hear the difference between Amália's light popular manner and her tragic voice, and afterwards try a later cover by a contemporary fadista to hear how a new generation reclaims it with irony or tenderness."],"amalia-rodrigues-estranha-forma-de-vida":["A Strange Way of Life: the singer speaks to her own heart as if it were a separate, wilful creature, asking why it insists on living for a love that does not come, and wishing it would either stop or follow her will. The accompaniment is the classic fado trio texture reduced to two guitars, the Portuguese guitar weaving a restless filigree while the classical guitar marks a steady walking bass beneath the voice.","Amália wrote the words herself and set them to an existing tune by Alfredo Marceneiro, the revered old master of Lisbon fado, known as his Fado Bailado. By the early 1960s she was an international star who was increasingly writing and choosing serious poetry, and this song shows her as a poet in her own right, speaking in the plain, direct language of the Lisbon neighbourhoods where fado was born.","Fado singers traditionally set new poems to a stock of established melodies, the way hymn texts are fitted to hymn tunes, so the same Marceneiro tune exists in other songs with other words. Once you know that, listen to how Amália bends the given melody to her own verses, delaying a syllable here and swelling a vowel there, which is where the real authorship of a fado performance lies.","It has become one of the most sung fados of all, recorded by almost every major fadista of the following generations, and is often cited as proof that Amália was a writer as well as a voice. Critics generally rank her own recording above later versions for its restraint: the emotion comes from what she holds back rather than from the volume she could easily have used.","Listen to the first verse only for the guitar, which never stops moving, then again for the voice, noticing how still it sounds against that motion. Afterwards hear a later version by a younger fadista to compare phrasing, and follow with the album Com Que Voz to hear where her taste for poetry led."],"amalia-rodrigues-com-que-voz":["An album of fados composed by Alain Oulman on poems by major Portuguese-language writers, from the Renaissance poet Luís de Camões, whose sonnet gives the title track, to modern poets. The instruments are the usual pair of guitars, but the songs are through-composed and harmonically adventurous, closer to art song than to the tavern. Amália's voice, now darker and heavier than in the 1950s, carries long, grave lines over them.","Oulman, a composer from a Franco-Portuguese family, had been writing for Amália since the early 1960s, beginning with the Busto album. In the late 1960s he was arrested by the Salazar regime's political police and forced to live abroad, so this record was made in the shadow of his exile. It came out in 1970, the year of Salazar's death, when the dictatorship was weakening but censorship was still very much in force.","The title track sets a sixteenth-century sonnet by Camões, the national poet, yet sounds wholly of its own time: listen for the unexpected turns in the guitar harmony at the end of each line, which is Oulman's classically trained hand at work. Poems by others on the album carry veiled melancholy about loss and absence that listeners under the dictatorship could hear as political without anything being said outright.","Many fado lovers and Portuguese critics consider it Amália's finest album, and it is often named the greatest fado record ever made. At the time some traditionalists felt Oulman's music had taken fado too far from the popular melodies of the old quarters. That debate is now largely settled in the album's favour, and it became the model for the literary fado of later singers.","Start with the title track and give it complete attention; it is a slow, unfolding song that rewards stillness. Then listen to the album straight through at night. Compare it with Busto to hear how the partnership deepened, and with Schubert's songs to notice how close this is to the European art-song tradition."],"paco-de-lucia-almoraima":["An album of solo-guitar flamenco pieces, each built on a traditional form: bulerías, alegrías, rumba, soleá and others. Paco's guitar is at the centre, playing thundering rasgueado strums, machine-gun picado runs and delicate thumb melodies, while bass, percussion and handclaps fill in beneath some tracks. The title track, named after a place near his home region in Cádiz, opens the record with a flash of the new, harmonically richer flamenco he was inventing.","In the mid-1970s Paco de Lucía was the most famous flamenco guitarist in the world, riding the success of Entre dos aguas and touring widely. Spain was emerging from Franco's dictatorship, which ended with his death in November 1975, and a younger generation wanted flamenco to sound modern rather than folkloric. Almoraima answered that wish while staying rooted in the forms Paco had learned from his father in Algeciras.","Listen to the chords rather than the speed. Paco was bringing in close, jazz-tinged harmonies, with added notes that make a chord shimmer, while keeping the rhythmic cycles exactly as a traditional dancer would expect them. On the rumba track, notice how bass and percussion lock into a groove that looks forward to his sextet of the following years, where the Peruvian cajón would become a permanent flamenco instrument.","Guitarists regard it as one of the essential Paco albums of the 1970s, often grouped with Fuente y caudal as the pair that redefined what a flamenco guitar record could be. Purists at the time worried that his virtuosity and new harmonies diluted the tradition; the verdict of later players, nearly all of whom learned from records like this, has been overwhelmingly on his side.","Put it on loud and start with the opening title track, following the handclaps to feel the twelve-beat cycle of the bulerías. Then pick a slower piece and listen for the thumb melody in the bass strings. Afterwards compare Friday Night in San Francisco to hear the same fingers in a jazz setting."],"paco-de-lucia-siroco":["A return to the solo flamenco guitar after years of group work, named after the hot wind that blows from Africa across southern Spain. Each track is a traditional form played with new depth: a soleá dedicated to the guitarist Niño Ricardo, a rondeña written for his son, and quicker pieces full of rhythmic fire. The sound is intimate and close-miked, one guitar filling the room, with little or nothing added around it.","Through the late 1970s and 1980s Paco had led a sextet with bass, flute, cajón and his brothers, and had toured with jazz guitarists John McLaughlin and Al Di Meola. Siroco was a deliberate homecoming to the essential flamenco guitar, made by a man of forty who had absorbed jazz and Latin harmony and now wanted to fold it back into the old forms. Spain, by then a democracy in the European Community, was rediscovering flamenco as national art.","In the soleá for Niño Ricardo, listen for the pauses: Paco lets phrases hang in silence, which is a tribute to the older master's grave, measured style rather than to his own speed. In the rondeña, notice the unusual tuning, with a low string dropped so the guitar rings with open, drone-like resonance, a device associated with the pioneer Ramón Montoya.","Many flamenco guitarists and critics regard Siroco as Paco's finest solo album, the one that best balances innovation with depth. It is widely studied by players and transcribed note for note. Some listeners prefer the raw brilliance of his 1970s records, but the prevailing view is that Siroco is where his mature voice is most completely heard.","Begin with the rondeña for its slow, ringing, almost harp-like sound, then go to the soleá and follow the long, stern phrases. Save the fast tracks for last. Afterwards try Albéniz's piano pieces Asturias or Sevilla to hear how classical composers imagined these same Andalusian sounds from outside."],"camaron-al-verte-las-flores-lloran":["Camarón's first album, recorded with Paco de Lucía as guitarist, and usually known by the title of its opening bulerías. The sound is spare and traditional: a young, cracked, piercing voice over one guitar and handclaps, working through the standard forms of flamenco song. What immediately marks it out is the raw urgency of the voice, which pushes hard at the top of its range and seems to catch fire on the long held notes.","José Monje Cruz, nicknamed Camarón, the shrimp, was a young gypsy singer from San Fernando near Cádiz who had come to Madrid to sing at the tablao Torres Bermejas, where he met Paco de Lucía. This record began a partnership of some nine albums over the next decade, largely produced within Paco's family, with his brother Pepe writing many of the songs. Flamenco was then heard mostly in tablaos for tourists and in private gatherings.","Listen to the way Paco's guitar never simply accompanies: between vocal lines he answers with short falsetas, compact melodic phrases, that anticipate where the voice will go next. The two were barely out of their teens, and the tightness of their exchanges, heard in the smallest delays and accents, already shows the telepathy that later records would make famous.","It was well received among aficionados and established Camarón quickly as the most exciting new voice in flamenco. Later critics have valued these early Philips albums as the purest record of his singing before his experiments of 1979. Some prefer them to everything after; others hear them mainly as the necessary ground from which La leyenda del tiempo could take off.","Start with the title bulerías and listen only to the voice for a minute, noticing its roughness and the way it bends notes. Then listen again and follow the guitar replies. Afterwards compare the Antología del cante flamenco to hear the older singers Camarón learned from, and then leap to La leyenda del tiempo."],"camaron-como-el-agua":["A record that returned Camarón to a warmer, more traditional sound after the shock of La leyenda del tiempo, with Paco de Lucía and the young Tomatito sharing the guitar parts. The title song, gentle and flowing, compares love to water, and the album moves between songs of this kind and harder, older forms. The voice is at its most expressive: husky, supple and able to switch between tenderness and ferocity within one phrase.","La leyenda del tiempo in 1979 had sold poorly and baffled traditional fans, and Camarón needed to reconnect with his audience. Pepe de Lucía, Paco's brother, wrote much of the material, and Tomatito, a gypsy guitarist from Almería still in his early twenties, was becoming Camarón's regular accompanist on stage. Spain was in its first years of democracy and flamenco was starting to be heard by a younger, urban audience.","With two guitarists present, listen for how the roles are shared: one guitar tends to keep the harmonic floor while the other adds decorative runs and answers the voice, and they swap. Tomatito's attack is slightly rounder than Paco's, so on careful listening you can often tell which hands are playing, a first sign of the partnership that would carry Camarón through the rest of his life.","It was received as a welcome return and has remained one of the best-loved Camarón records, with the title song among his most popular. Critics sometimes treat it as a consolidation rather than an adventure, but many aficionados consider it the finest balance between his traditional and modern selves, and Tomatito's arrival is now seen as a turning point in its own right.","Start with the title song, which is the easiest way into Camarón's voice for a newcomer. Then find a slower, deeper track and notice how his tone darkens. Compare with La leyenda del tiempo to hear what he had stepped back from, and with Potro de rabia y miel to hear the same trio a decade later."],"camaron-soy-gitano":["The most commercially successful flamenco album ever made, pairing Camarón and Tomatito with orchestral arrangements on several tracks. The title song, a proud declaration of gypsy identity sung as bulerías, sets the tone; around it are love songs, a tango and slower pieces where strings swell behind the voice. The sound is polished and big, a studio production far from the tablao, yet the voice at its centre has lost none of its grit.","The album was produced by Ricardo Pachón, who had also been behind La leyenda del tiempo, and the orchestral parts were reportedly recorded with a London orchestra. Camarón was by now a national figure, adored by young people who knew little flamenco, and Spain was preparing for 1992, the year of the Seville Expo and the Barcelona Olympics, keen to present its culture as modern and international. The album sold in quantities no flamenco record had approached.","Listen for how the orchestra is kept away from the rhythm: the strings mostly sustain long chords and countermelodies, while the compás, the rhythmic cycle, stays entirely with guitar, palmas and voice. That separation is why the record still sounds like flamenco rather than pop with a Spanish tinge; the orchestra decorates the room but never walks onto the dance floor.","It was a huge success and is often called the best-selling flamenco album of all time. Critics were divided: some heard the orchestra as sentimental gloss, others as a natural widening of the frame. Today it tends to be valued above all for Camarón's singing and for Tomatito's playing, with the orchestral dressing seen as a product of its moment.","Start with the title track and follow the handclaps; the cycle of the bulerías is the thread through everything. Then listen for one of the orchestral songs and ask whether the strings add or distract. Compare Falla's El amor brujo, a classical composer's orchestral flamenco from the opposite direction."],"camaron-potro-de-rabia-y-miel":["Camarón's final album, a colt of rage and honey in its title, reuniting him with Paco de Lucía and with Tomatito on guitars. The arrangements are lean and traditional after the orchestral scale of Soy gitano: voice, guitars, handclaps and a little percussion. The voice has grown hoarser and more worn, but that roughness gives the slow songs a devastating weight, and the fast bulerías still crackle with nervous energy.","The album was released in 1992, only months before Camarón died in July of that year, at forty-one, of lung cancer. His life had been marked by exhausting tours and addiction, and he was in poor health during the sessions. Reuniting with Paco, after years in which Tomatito had been his main guitarist, gave the record the feel of a closing circle; his funeral in San Fernando drew enormous crowds.","Listen for the moments when two guitars play together behind the voice: Paco's sharper, more harmonically daring lines against Tomatito's warmer, more rhythmic style. Knowing the circumstances, listeners often hear farewell everywhere, but the most revealing thing is purely musical: how carefully the voice now paces itself, placing strength where it counts rather than spending it freely as in 1969.","The album was greeted with reverence, and after his death it took on the status of a testament. Critics praise it as a return to essentials and as one of his most moving records. Some note that the voice is diminished; most answer that the loss of power is exactly what makes the singing so affecting, as with late recordings of Billie Holiday.","Listen to it straight through, then return to his debut from 1969 to hear the same partnership twenty-three years earlier: bright youth against worn experience. Pay attention to the slowest song on the album and how long he lets each phrase breathe. Afterwards try Enrique Morente's Omega for another view of where flamenco singing went next."],"carlos-paredes-verdes-anos":["A short instrumental theme for Portuguese guitar with a second guitar beneath, written for a film and now the best-known piece of Portuguese instrumental music. A gently rocking accompaniment supports a melody that is at once wistful and quietly determined, played with the bright, bell-like tremolo of the twelve-string Portuguese guitar. It unfolds in repeated waves, each slightly more intense, and ends as simply as it began.","Paredes wrote it for Os Verdes Anos, The Green Years, a 1963 film by Paulo Rocha about a young man from the country adrift in Lisbon, a key work of the new Portuguese cinema. Paredes, heir to a family of Coimbra guitarists, earned his living as a hospital clerk; a few years earlier he had been imprisoned by the Salazar regime's political police for his communist sympathies, and he had lost his job as a result.","Listen to the right hand rather than the tune: Paredes uses a rapid, nervous alternation of strokes that makes long notes shiver, a technique rooted in the Coimbra guitar style of his father Artur Paredes rather than in Lisbon fado. The melody never becomes a song for a singer; its phrases are shaped by what the fingers do, which is exactly what makes the piece sound instrumental through and through.","The theme became famous far beyond the film and is now something like an unofficial anthem of Portuguese melancholy, played at ceremonies and covered by many musicians. Critics have seen in it the birth of an independent concert music for the Portuguese guitar, freed from its role as accompanist to fado singers. Its fame has sometimes overshadowed Paredes's larger, more complex compositions.","Listen first for the melody, then a second time only for the lower guitar and its rocking pattern. Then watch the film, if you can find it, to hear the theme in context. Follow it with his album Guitarra Portuguesa and then Movimento Perpétuo to hear the same voice expanded to larger forms."],"carlos-paredes-movimento-perpetuo":["An album of original instrumental pieces for Portuguese guitar with a second guitar, named after its restless title track. The music draws on fado, Coimbra student songs and rural Portuguese dances, but it is composed concert music, with long developing sections, sudden changes of mood and passages of great speed. The recognisable sound is the Portuguese guitar's tremolo running almost without pause while the second guitar provides harmony and drive.","Released in 1971, in the last years of the Estado Novo under Salazar's successor Marcelo Caetano, the record appeared when Paredes was still working a day job and performing relatively rarely. His music, wordless yet widely understood as a voice of the people, carried a resistance meaning that did not need lyrics to get past the censor. After the 1974 revolution he became a symbol of the new, free Portugal.","In the title track, listen for how a single figure keeps circling, like a machine or a heartbeat, while the melody grows above it: the perpetual motion of the title is both a Baroque idea and an image of relentless labour. Paredes played with an unusually aggressive attack, and you can often hear the percussive sound of nail on string, a physical intensity closer to a flamenco player than to a salon guitarist.","It is often ranked alongside Guitarra Portuguesa as his most important record and a founding document of modern Portuguese instrumental music. Critics have compared his compositional ambition to that of classical composers, while noting that he was largely self-taught in composition. Later guitarists, and rock and jazz musicians in Portugal, have cited it as a key influence.","Start with the title track and let the momentum carry you, then go back and listen to the slower pieces, where the tremolo becomes lyrical rather than driving. Compare the Prelude of Bach's First Cello Suite for another perpetual-motion idea, and Paco de Lucía's Almoraima for a neighbouring Iberian guitar tradition from the same decade."],"enrique-morente-homenaje-flamenco-a-miguel-hernandez":["An album of poems by Miguel Hernández set to flamenco song forms, sung by the young Enrique Morente with guitar. Instead of the traditional short verses of flamenco, the voice carries literary poetry about love, poverty, war and imprisonment, fitted to forms such as the soleá, the seguiriya and the fandango. The texture is severe, one voice and one guitar, and the focus is on words as much as melody.","Miguel Hernández, a poet of peasant origin who fought for the Republic in the Civil War, died in a Francoist prison in 1942, and his name remained politically charged in Franco's Spain. For a flamenco singer to devote an album to him in 1971 was a bold gesture, aligning Morente with the anti-Franco left and with the young people who were rediscovering banned or neglected poets.","Listen to how Morente cuts and stretches Hernández's lines to fit flamenco's fixed forms: he repeats words, breaks them across a phrase, or adds the traditional wordless lament on a single vowel so that the poem becomes a cante without losing its sense. This was a technique he would take much further later, in his settings of Lorca and on Omega.","The album marked Morente as an intellectual among flamenco singers and won him a following beyond the flamenco world, while some traditionalists regarded literary settings as foreign to the art. Today it is recognised as a pioneering record in the long line of flamenco albums devoted to poets, and as a document of cultural resistance in the late Franco years.","Read a translation of a Hernández poem before listening to its setting, and then follow how the words are bent to the melody. Start with a slower track where the voice is almost alone. Compare Camarón's La leyenda del tiempo, which set Lorca eight years later, and Victor Jara's songs from Chile for protest sung in a different Hispanic tradition."],"enrique-morente-homenaje-a-don-antonio-chacon":["A tribute to Antonio Chacón, the great cantaor of the early twentieth century, in which Morente revives the songs and styles associated with him, with Pepe Habichuela on guitar. The repertoire leans towards the ornate, melodically elaborate forms Chacón favoured, such as malagueñas, cartageneras and other mining songs of the east. The voice is controlled and highly ornamented, and the guitar is restrained, leaving space for long, winding vocal lines.","Chacón, from Jerez, had been the most celebrated singer of flamenco's golden age, praised for bringing refinement and range to the art, but his style had fallen out of fashion. Morente, from Granada's Albaicín quarter, was known as a modernist, and this deeply researched record proved he was also a master of tradition. It appeared in 1977, as Spain held its first free elections since the 1930s.","Chacón's recordings survive only in scratchy early discs, so Morente was reconstructing a style partly from memory, other singers and those old records. Listen for the long, florid melismas, many notes on one syllable, and for how he keeps an even, almost sweet tone rather than the rasping cry of gypsy singers such as Camarón; that contrast is the real subject of the album.","The album was acclaimed by aficionados and critics, and it is widely regarded as one of the essential flamenco recordings of its decade, proving that the singer who later made Omega had earned his experiments. It is often cited as a model of how to honour a historical style without museum stiffness. Some listeners find its refinement austere next to more fiery gypsy singing.","Listen to one malagueña slowly and follow the voice through each ornament. Then play Camarón on the same kind of song, if you can find one, to hear the difference between refinement and raw force. Afterwards go straight to Omega and listen for how the same technique survives in a noisy rock setting."],"markos-vamvakaris-piraeus-quartet":["The body of songs made by the Tetras i Xakousti tou Peiraia, the Famous Quartet of Piraeus, which brought together Markos Vamvakaris, Giorgos Batis, Anestis Delias and Stratos Pagioumtzis, heard today in compilations. Rough male voices, the bright jangle of bouzouki and the small, high baglamas, and a guitar keeping the dance rhythm: this is rebetiko in its first, hardest form, sung in the zeibekiko, hasapiko and other dance metres of the port.","In the early 1930s the port of Piraeus was full of refugees from Asia Minor, dockworkers and men living on the margins, who met in cafés and hashish dens to play. Vamvakaris, a former docker from the island of Syros, had taken up the bouzouki, then considered a disreputable instrument. From 1936 the Metaxas dictatorship imposed censorship that pushed this underworld subject matter out of the recording studios.","Listen for the rhythmic character of the zeibekiko, a slow dance in nine beats, grouped irregularly so that it seems to limp and lurch; it was danced by a single man, improvising, and the music gives him that unsteady ground. Notice also how the singers' pitch is modal rather than major or minor, using scales, called dromoi, that link rebetiko to Ottoman music.","These records were long despised by respectable Greek society; only from the 1950s and 1960s, after composers such as Hadjidakis praised rebetiko, did the old Piraeus style gain prestige. Today the Quartet's recordings are regarded as founding documents of modern Greek popular music, often compared to early blues for their mix of rough sound and deep feeling.","Start with any compilation of Vamvakaris from the 1930s and focus on the bouzouki's introductions, the taximi, where the player improvises over a drone before the song begins. Then listen for the limping nine-beat dance. Compare early Delta blues by Robert Johnson or Son House, then continue to Tsitsanis for the gentler postwar style."],"markos-vamvakaris-ta-matoklada-sou-lampoun":["Your eyelashes shine: a love song in which the singer compares the gleam of a woman's eyes to the flash of blades, a mixture of adoration and danger typical of rebetiko. It opens with a bouzouki introduction and settles into a slow, swaying dance, with Vamvakaris's hoarse, untrained voice carrying a simple melody. It is one of his signature songs and a standard of the rebetiko repertoire.","Vamvakaris recorded it in the 1930s, the decade when he was establishing the bouzouki as rebetiko's lead instrument and himself as the genre's patriarch. He came from the Catholic community of Syros, had worked on the docks of Piraeus from his teens, and taught himself the instrument after hearing older players in the port. His recordings sold to working-class audiences across Greece and the diaspora.","Listen to Vamvakaris's voice itself: it is famously rough, almost cracked, and he makes no attempt to smooth it. Singers and critics later called this voice the sound of the rebetiko world, precisely because it was not trained. Also notice how simply he plays the bouzouki, with few notes and strong rhythm, in contrast to the flashier players who came after him.","The song has been recorded by many later singers, and is regarded as a classic of the early Piraeus style. Critics prize it for the directness that later, more commercial rebetiko often lost. Some modern listeners find the old recordings hard to hear through the surface noise, but most accounts of rebetiko place songs like this at the genre's heart.","Listen to the bouzouki introduction and then to how the rhythm settles. Imagine a single dancer improvising slowly to it. Then compare a later cover by a polished singer to hear what has been gained and lost. Go on to Tsitsanis, whose songs show how the next generation refined this sound."],"tsitsanis-omorfi-thessaloniki":["Beautiful Thessaloniki: an affectionate song praising the northern Greek city, its beauty and its people, and confessing that the singer's heart belongs there. The bouzouki plays a lilting introduction and a clear, singable melody that is easy to remember after one hearing. Compared with the harsh Piraeus style of the 1930s, the sound is smoother and warmer, and the melody more like a popular song than a drinking-den improvisation.","Vassilis Tsitsanis, from Trikala in Thessaly, lived in Thessaloniki during the German occupation, when he ran a small music café there and wrote many songs that could only be recorded after the war. This song is his tribute to the city that sheltered him. After 1946 the record industry resumed, and Tsitsanis became the leading figure of a gentler, more melodic rebetiko that appealed to a much wider audience.","Listen to how the melody is built in balanced, repeating phrases, almost like a Western popular song; this was a deliberate move by Tsitsanis away from the modal improvisations of older players. At the same time, the bouzouki's ornaments still use the old Ottoman scales, so the song sits between two worlds, which is exactly the balance that made him so popular.","It became one of the best-loved Greek songs, sung by many performers and adopted as an informal anthem of Thessaloniki. Some rebetiko purists have seen Tsitsanis's melodic style as a softening of the genre, but most critics credit him with widening its expressive range and making it a national music rather than a subculture.","Listen to the opening bouzouki phrase, then try humming the melody back after one hearing. Compare it with Vamvakaris's Frangosyriani to hear how the style moved from Piraeus to a broader popular music. Then listen to Synnefiasmeni Kyriaki, which shows Tsitsanis's darker side from the same years."],"tsitsanis-ninou-live-1955":["A rare live recording of Tsitsanis and the singer Marika Ninou performing in an Athens music club, reportedly taped on a portable machine and released only decades later. The sound is rough and full of room noise, but it captures the real atmosphere of a rebetiko night: Ninou's strong, dark voice, Tsitsanis's bouzouki flashing in introductions and solos, and audience reactions around them. It includes many of their best-known songs.","In the early 1950s Tsitsanis and Ninou, a singer of Armenian origin, formed one of rebetiko's great partnerships, and their club performances drew devoted crowds. Studio records of the time were limited to about three minutes, so this tape preserves something no commercial disc could: the extended playing and interaction of a live evening. Ninou died of cancer only a couple of years later, still young.","Listen to the bouzouki solos, which are longer and freer than on studio records, because in the club the musicians played for dancers and could stretch a song as long as they wished. Notice also how Ninou varies her phrasing from verse to verse, something frozen out of studio takes, and how the audience's voices occasionally join in.","When it finally appeared, it was greeted as a precious document, one of very few live recordings of classic rebetiko, and it has become a treasured record among Greek listeners. Critics admit its sound quality is poor, but most feel the atmosphere and the performances outweigh the noise, and it gives unique evidence of how the music actually lived.","Treat it as a night out rather than a hi-fi record: put it on, accept the crackle and listen for the room. Follow Ninou's voice first, then the bouzouki's breaks. Compare Synnefiasmeni Kyriaki in its studio form, and then Umm Kulthum's live concert recordings for another great singer heard in front of her audience."],"tyagaraja-jagadanandakaraka":["The first of Tyagaraja's five Pancharatna kritis, the Five Gems, set in raga Nata and Adi tala. After a stately opening refrain and a second section, the song unrolls through a long chain of verses, each first sung to bare note-names and then repeated with words. The text, unusually for Tyagaraja, is in Sanskrit rather than Telugu, and it strings together epithets of Rama. Nata's bright, emphatic leaps give the whole piece the feel of a procession.","Tyagaraja spent almost his whole life in Tiruvaiyaru, a temple village on the Kaveri near Thanjavur, singing to Rama and teaching a circle of disciples who carried his songs out into South India. The five Pancharatna kritis, in the ragas Nata, Gaula, Arabhi, Varali and Sri, were probably not conceived as a set; the grouping belongs to later tradition. The Maratha court at Thanjavur was the region's great patron, but this music was made for the shrine, not the palace.","Listen to how the verses are built: each one is first sung entirely in solfa syllables, the Carnatic equivalent of do-re-mi, and only then does the same melody return carrying text. You hear the tune naked, then clothed. Scholars have counted the Sanskrit epithets and argue they approach the traditional hundred and eight names of Rama, which would turn the song into a sung rosary as much as a composition.","Jagadanandakaraka opens the annual Tyagaraja Aradhana in Tiruvaiyaru each January, when hundreds of musicians sing the five Gems together at his shrine. That massed unison is a twentieth-century practice, and some musicians feel it flattens the solo subtleties of the style. Because no manuscript in Tyagaraja's hand survives, the versions sung by different teaching lineages differ in small melodic turns, and each school defends its own.","Start with the massed Aradhana recording to feel the scale of the thing, then hear a single great voice sing it, where the slides between notes become audible. Count the verses and notice that each begins with syllables before words arrive. Nata is bright and forceful; compare it with the gentler, sadder Varali Pancharatna to hear how much a raga changes a song's temperature."],"tyagaraja-samaja-vara-gamana":["A kriti in raga Hindolam and Adi tala, addressed to Krishna, whose gait the opening line compares to that of a noble elephant. Hindolam uses only five notes, so the melody moves in wide, dark steps with no small half-step comforts. The song runs through its three sections, refrain, second theme and verse, and in concert usually grows a long improvised preface, a wandering exploration of the raga, and passages of improvised note-name singing before the final return.","Tyagaraja's output was vast, perhaps several hundred surviving songs, mostly to Rama. This one turns instead to Krishna as musician: the verse speaks of the seven notes born from the Vedas and of the flute-player who delights in them. It belongs to Tyagaraja's lifelong theme that music itself, rightly sung, is a road to liberation, an idea he inherited from earlier devotional singers and set down in songs his disciples memorised and later wrote out.","Hindolam is the same five-note scale that North Indian musicians call Malkauns, the midnight raga. Hear a Hindustani Malkauns next to this kriti and you will recognise the pitches at once, yet the two sound unlike each other: Carnatic singing shakes and bends almost every note, while the northern style lets notes sit and glow. It is the clearest single demonstration of how two sister traditions treat one scale.","Samaja Vara Gamana is one of the most performed kritis in the repertoire and a favourite for showing off improvised note-singing, which some purists think now overwhelms the composed song. It also crossed into Telugu cinema and light music, which spread it well beyond the concert hall. Lineages disagree over a few phrases, and it is sung at slightly different speeds by different schools, a normal condition for an orally transmitted repertoire.","Listen first for the opening words and the stately upward climb of the tune, then for the moment the singer drops the text and sings note-names at speed, trading phrases with the violinist. Afterwards, play a Hindustani Malkauns and hear the same five notes behave in a completely different way. Recordings by the great mid-century vocalists are the place to begin."],"tyagaraja-nidhi-chala-sukhama":["A kriti in raga Kalyani and Misra Chapu, a lilting seven-beat cycle grouped three plus two plus two. The text is a question: does wealth bring happiness, or does the presence of Rama? The melody poses it in a rising refrain and answers in long, arching phrases. Kalyani, with its raised fourth note, gives the song a bright, open, almost festive colour that makes the renunciation it describes sound like a choice made gladly rather than grimly.","Tradition ties the song to an invitation from the Thanjavur court, often said to be from the Maratha king Serfoji II, offering Tyagaraja gifts and a place among the palace musicians. He declined, and this song is remembered as his reply. The story cannot be verified, but it fits what is known: he lived modestly on alms gathered while singing in the streets, and he refused professional court service all his life.","Listen to the tala rather than the tune. Misra Chapu's uneven seven-beat cycle makes the refrain lean forward and catch itself, which suits a song built on a hesitation between two choices. The drummer's pattern repeats the lopsided grouping throughout; once you can hear the short-long shape, the question in the words seems to be asked by the rhythm too.","Nidhi Chala Sukhama has become a statement of the artist's independence from patronage, which is how it is often introduced in concerts and textbooks. Historians note that the anecdote attached to it was written down only long after Tyagaraja's death, by disciples with a hagiographer's instincts. The song is loved regardless, and it remains one of the standard Kalyani pieces every serious student learns.","Tap the seven-beat cycle as three then two then two, and the song opens up. Then notice how the singer lingers on the raised fourth note, the one that gives Kalyani its brightness. Pair it with a Hindustani Yaman, the northern cousin of this raga, and with the Kalyani entry elsewhere in this catalogue to hear the scale in other hands."],"dikshitar-kamalamba-navavarana":["A cycle of eleven Sanskrit songs to the goddess Kamalamba of the Tiruvarur temple: an opening meditation in raga Todi, nine songs for the nine enclosures of the Sri Chakra, and a closing blessing in Sri raga. Each enclosure song takes a different raga, among them Anandabhairavi, Kalyani, Sankarabharanam, Kambhoji, Bhairavi, Punnagavarali, Sahana, Ghanta and Ahiri. The pace is slow and weighty, the melodies dense with ornament, the texts packed with Tantric names and diagrams.","Muthuswami Dikshitar was born in Tiruvarur and returned to its temples throughout his life. He was trained in Sanskrit learning and in Srividya, the Tantric worship of the goddess through the Sri Chakra, a geometric diagram of nested triangles and petals. The Navavarana songs are a worship ritual turned into music, meant to be sung in sequence, often on specific festival days, by a composer who was also a practising initiate.","Dikshitar sets each of the nine enclosure songs in a different grammatical case of the goddess's name: the first names her as subject, the next as object, then 'by her', 'for her', 'from her', 'of her', 'in her', and finally calls to her directly. The cycle is a declension as well as a pilgrimage, and a Sanskrit speaker hears the name bend at every step inward toward the centre of the diagram.","Singers and scholars treat the cycle as the most intellectually ambitious work in Carnatic music, and it is performed complete more rarely than it is praised. Some find the density forbidding compared with Tyagaraja's warmth; others hear in Dikshitar's slow, ornament-heavy style the deepest statement of what a raga can hold. Text and melody variants between the lineages that preserve it are still being compared in modern editions.","Do not take it in one sitting at first. Begin with the Kambhoji or Sankarabharanam song, the grandest melodies, then the opening Todi meditation. Notice how slowly the lines unfold and how each syllable carries several notes. Once familiar, hear the cycle complete, ideally from a singer trained in the Dikshitar lineage, and follow the journey inward from the outer square to the centre."],"dikshitar-navagraha-kritis":["A set of Sanskrit songs to the nine heavenly bodies of Hindu astrology: the Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn, with Rahu and Ketu, the two shadow planets of the eclipses. Each song has its own raga, from Saurashtram for the Sun and Asaveri for the Moon to Yadukula Kambhoji for Saturn, and the seven planetary songs are set in the seven principal talas of the Carnatic system, one rhythmic cycle for each.","Dikshitar spent much of his life travelling between the temples of the Tamil country, and many South Indian temples have a shrine to the nine planets. Dikshitar was steeped in Sanskrit astrology and ritual. The planetary songs describe each deity's colour, vehicle, gemstone, day of the week and powers in the compressed, catalogue-like Sanskrit that marks his texts.","The seven planet songs map neatly onto the seven suladi talas, but the songs for Rahu and Ketu do not fit the scheme. Many scholars think those two were added later, perhaps by a disciple or a later musician completing the set, because early sources name only seven. So the famous nine may be seven plus two, and hearing the set as a strict rhythmic system shows where the seam lies.","The Navagraha songs are widely sung, individually more often than as a set, and the Saturn song in Yadukula Kambhoji is especially popular because of Saturn's feared influence in astrology. Their attribution debate concerns only the last two. Some critics find the texts more list than poetry; defenders answer that the lists are the point, a worship formula and a musical demonstration of raga and tala at once.","Start with the Sun song in Saurashtram and the Jupiter song in Athana, which are vigorous and bright, then the Saturn song for contrast in its darker colour. Try to feel how the tala changes from song to song; a teacher's recording where the beat is clapped aloud helps. Hear it beside Holst's Planets to compare two very different ways of turning astrology into music."],"dikshitar-sri-subrahmanyaya-namaste":["A Sanskrit kriti in raga Kambhoji and Rupaka tala, a brisk three-beat cycle, saluting Subrahmanya, the god Murugan, son of Shiva. Kambhoji is one of the broad, majestic ragas of the tradition, full of wide swings and long held notes, and Dikshitar lets it expand at a slow, dignified pace. The song is commonly treated as a main piece in a concert, preceded by a long improvised exploration of the raga and followed by passages of improvised note-singing.","Murugan had a special place in Dikshitar's life. Tradition says the god appeared to him at the hill temple of Tiruttani and set him on his path as a composer, and Dikshitar signed his songs with the name Guruguha, a title of Murugan, ever after. This kriti belongs to the body of songs he addressed to his chosen deity, written in the dense, learned Sanskrit that separates his style from Tyagaraja's Telugu.","Listen for the name Guruguha tucked into the last section of the text. Every Dikshitar kriti carries it as a signature, but here it doubles as the name of the god being praised, so the composer's signature and the song's subject become the same word. Listen also for the rhyming syllables Dikshitar plants at the same beat positions in successive lines, an ornament of the text that the melody underlines.","Sri Subrahmanyaya Namaste is counted among the finest Kambhoji compositions and is a standard concert centrepiece. Critics admire how Dikshitar lets the raga's characteristic phrases emerge without hurry, and singers of the Dikshitar lineage insist on a slower tempo than is sometimes heard. The kriti remains a test of whether a singer can sustain weight and gravity rather than dazzle.","Listen first to the improvised exploration of Kambhoji that usually precedes it, so the raga's shape is in your ear, then follow how the composed song fixes those shapes into a melody. Notice the three-beat cycle beneath the long vocal lines. Compare it with Dikshitar's Vatapi Ganapatim, which is quick and bright, to hear the two ends of his style."],"dikshitar-meenakshi-me-mudam":["A kriti in raga Gamakakriya, close kin to the raga now called Purvikalyani, in Adi tala, addressed to Meenakshi, the fish-eyed goddess of the great temple at Madurai. The text asks her simply for joy. The melody is grave and tender, full of long bends between notes, and it moves in slow, curving phrases that circle back to the words of the opening request. It is among the best-loved of Dikshitar's songs to the goddess.","Madurai's Meenakshi temple is one of the largest in South India, and Dikshitar composed several songs for it during his years of temple pilgrimage. Tradition ties this one to his death, at Ettayapuram on Deepavali, 1835: he is said to have asked his disciples to sing it and to have died as they reached the line calling the goddess the one who frees from bondage. The story is devotional legend, but it fixed the song's aura.","Gamakakriya and Purvikalyani are nearly the same scale, and which name to use is a live argument. Dikshitar's school preserved older raga forms described in a treatise associated with Venkatamakhin, so his Gamakakriya is not quite the Purvikalyani sung in most concerts today. Listen for the way the upper notes are approached obliquely; it is one of the details that separates the old raga from the modern one.","The song is widely performed and often chosen by singers as a slow, reflective centre to a recital. The deathbed story is repeated in almost every programme note, and scholars treat it with caution. Musicologists value the kriti as evidence of how Dikshitar's lineage kept alive earlier raga shapes, and modern editions based on the Dikshitar family tradition have reopened questions about how it should be sung.","Hear it sung slowly by a mature voice and listen for the repeated return of the plea for joy, each time slightly differently ornamented. Then play a modern Purvikalyani piece and ask what has changed. Pair it with Nagumomu for a contrast: Tyagaraja complains to his god, Dikshitar asks quietly and waits."],"purandara-dasa-pillari-gitas":["A small group of simple songs in raga Malahari, beginning with Sri Gananatha in Rupaka tala, a salute to Ganesha. They move almost one syllable to one note, with little ornament and a steady beat, and each lasts only a minute or two. Their texts are a devotional patchwork in Sanskrit and Kannada. After scales and rhythmic exercises, these are the first real songs a Carnatic student learns, and nearly every South Indian musician has sung them as a child.","Purandara Dasa, born around 1484 and active in the Vijayanagara empire, is called the grandfather of Carnatic music less for any single song than for the teaching sequence credited to him: graded scale exercises in raga Mayamalavagowla, rhythmic patterns in the seven talas, then these first songs. Vijayanagara's capital, Hampi, was one of the great cities of the sixteenth century, and its devotional singers took music out of the temple and into the street.","Malahari is chosen deliberately. It is a simple, rather sombre raga whose notes sit comfortably in a child's voice and whose shape overlaps with the scale exercises the student has just finished. The gitas are thus built to carry a learner from drill to song without a jump. Listen to how the tune keeps outlining the scale in plain steps: it is a piece of teaching design disguised as a hymn.","Nobody thinks these little songs are major art, and some teachers now question whether a sixteenth-century curriculum is the right start for modern students. Yet the sequence has held for centuries across a whole region, and scholars regard it as the backbone of Carnatic pedagogy. Attribution to Purandara Dasa rests on tradition rather than documents, like most of the huge body of songs carrying his name.","Look for a recording by children or a teacher's demonstration rather than a concert artist, because the plainness is the point. Listen for the steady beat and the one-note-per-syllable setting. Then play Dikshitar's Vatapi Ganapatim, another Ganesha song, and hear how far a Carnatic composition can travel from this starting line in two and a half centuries."],"purandara-dasa-bhagyada-lakshmi-baramma":["A Kannada devaranama inviting Lakshmi, goddess of fortune, to come into the house, usually sung today in raga Madhyamavati and Adi tala. The refrain is a warm, open call, and the verses picture her footsteps, her anklets ringing, as she enters the home. The melody is simple enough for anyone to join and Madhyamavati's bright pentatonic shape makes it feel celebratory and auspicious, which is why it so often closes a concert or blesses a household event.","Purandara Dasa's songs were written for singing by ordinary devotees, in Kannada rather than Sanskrit, in the Haridasa movement that spread Vaishnava devotion through the Vijayanagara empire. This song belongs to domestic ritual: in Karnataka it is sung at weddings, housewarmings and the festival of Varamahalakshmi. What reached us is the text and the tradition of singing it; the tune now used was fixed by later musicians.","The song crossed the North-South divide. Bhimsen Joshi, the Hindustani vocalist from Karnataka, recorded it in his own northern style, and many listeners in India know it best that way. Compare his version with a Carnatic one and you hear the same Kannada words carried by two different ideas of what a note is: held and glowing in the north, constantly bent and shaken in the south.","Among Kannada speakers it is perhaps the most familiar song attributed to Purandara Dasa, sung by people who would never attend a concert. Scholars note the usual caveat: thousands of compositions carry his signature, Purandara Vittala, and some are certainly by followers. This one is securely in the core tradition, and its popularity has never been in doubt, even among critics who find it slight beside the great kritis.","Hear one Carnatic recording and Bhimsen Joshi's back to back; the comparison is the best way in. Listen for the refrain returning after each verse, and for the image of the goddess's steps in the text. Then turn to Kumar Gandharva's devotional songs in the northern bhajan tradition to hear how widely this kind of singing reaches across India."],"purandara-dasa-venkatachala-nilayam":["A devotional song to Venkateshwara, the god of the Tirumala hill temple above Tirupati, usually sung in raga Sindhubhairavi and Adi tala. Sindhubhairavi allows notes from outside its basic scale, so the tune has a wistful, folk-like flexibility, sliding between bright and shadowed versions of the same note. The refrain names the god as the one who dwells on the Venkata hill, and each short verse returns to it like a pilgrim's chant.","Tirumala was one of the great pilgrimage centres of the Vijayanagara world, and the rulers of the empire patronised it heavily. Purandara Dasa, a Madhva Vaishnava devotee, wandered as a singing mendicant after renouncing his merchant fortune, and songs like this one were made to be sung on the road and at the shrine. It is a devaranama rather than a concert kriti, with simple structure and memorable refrain.","Sindhubhairavi is a raga of the lighter end of the concert, where borrowed notes are allowed, and the tune currently sung almost certainly dates from long after Purandara Dasa. Singers choose it because the raga's mixed colours suit a song about longing for a distant hill. Listen for the moment the melody touches a note that does not belong to the raga's main scale; it gives the song its ache.","M. S. Subbulakshmi's recording made the song famous far beyond Karnataka, and her voice is now inseparable from it for many listeners, as it is from the Venkatesa Suprabhatam sung at the same shrine. Some critics regret that the tuneful concert version has eclipsed older, plainer ways of singing the Dasa songs, but it has kept this text alive in every corner of the Carnatic world.","Start with Subbulakshmi's recording, then her Venkatesa Suprabhatam, which addresses the same god at the same temple. Listen for the returning refrain and the slight darkening whenever the melody bends into a borrowed note. It is usually sung near the end of a concert as a lighter piece; hear it as a song of the road rather than of the recital hall."],"purandara-dasa-kallu-sakkare-kolliro":["A Kannada devaranama built as a street cry: the singer is a hawker calling out that he has rock sugar for sale, and the sugar turns out to be the name of Krishna, sweeter than anything in the market and never spoiling. The song is short, strophic and bouncing, with a refrain meant for everyone to shout back. It is sung to more than one tune in different traditions, always brisk and cheerful rather than solemn.","Vijayanagara's bazaars were famous; foreign visitors described streets of traders selling jewels, spices and cloth. Purandara Dasa had been a wealthy merchant, a pawnbroker by tradition, before he gave everything away, and many of his songs borrow the language of trade, debt and weighing. Here the former merchant turns his old trade into a sermon, selling the one commodity he now valued.","Listen for the list of the sugar's virtues in the verses: it does not rot, it needs no carrying cart, no tax is paid on it, it costs nothing. Each is a joke against the market and a claim about grace. Knowing that the singer had been a rich trader turns the comedy into something sharper, a man mocking the life he left behind in its own vocabulary.","The song is a favourite in Karnataka and in the Hindustani repertoire of singers from the region, and it shows the humour and plain speech that made the Dasas popular. Critics sometimes pass over such pieces as light, but historians of the Haridasa movement cite them as evidence of how devotional song reached people through everyday images. As with all his songs, the tune is later than the text.","Listen for the call-and-response energy of the refrain and imagine it shouted in a crowded bazaar. Compare a Carnatic performance with a Hindustani devotional rendering from a Karnataka singer. Then turn to Kumar Gandharva's nirguni bhajans for another tradition of saint-poets who used plain, earthy images to talk about God."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-fuki":["Fuki is the first of the kumiuta, the song suites for koto that Yatsuhashi Kengyō established as the core of the blind musicians' repertoire. The player sings while accompanying on the koto, and the piece moves through a series of short verses, each fitted to the same fixed musical length. The voice is slow and restrained, the koto answers each phrase with plucked patterns, and the whole has the grave, ceremonial pace of old court song.","Yatsuhashi, a blind musician working in Kyoto and Edo in the mid-seventeenth century, took the koto music of the Tsukushi school, a Buddhist-connected tradition from Kyushu, and reshaped it for the blind guild that controlled professional music. He is credited with the hirajōshi tuning that gives koto music its familiar melancholy scale. Kumiuta were his teaching and performing core, learned in strict order, and Fuki came first.","The verses of a kumiuta usually have nothing to do with each other: they are borrowed poems from different sources, strung together by musical form rather than meaning. So do not listen for a story. Listen instead for how each verse lands at the same length and closes with similar koto figures, as though the music is a frame into which any poem could be placed.","Kumiuta are now performed far less than instrumental danmono like Rokudan, and many listeners find the slow, unaccompanied-feeling vocal line austere. Scholars nevertheless regard Yatsuhashi's kumiuta as the foundation of sōkyoku, the art music of the koto, and the Ikuta and Yamada schools still teach them. How much of the music is Yatsuhashi's own, and how much inherited from the Tsukushi repertoire, is still debated.","Listen once without trying to follow the words, just to the relation between voice and koto: the voice sustains, the koto punctuates. Then hear Rokudan, the instrumental piece credited to the same musician, and notice the shared tuning and plucked patterns. A recording by a senior player of the Ikuta school is the best starting point."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-midare":["A danmono, an instrumental piece in sections, for solo koto, traditionally credited to Yatsuhashi Kengyō. Where Rokudan's six sections are all the same length, Midare's are deliberately irregular: its name means disorder, and it is also called Jūnidan, twelve sections. The music begins slowly and gathers speed across the piece, with plucked phrases, sliding pitches made by pressing the string behind the bridge, and sudden flourishes that break the expected pattern.","Danmono were the instrumental showpieces of the blind guild's koto repertoire, learned after the kumiuta songs. They were played in the homes of merchants and samurai in Kyoto, Osaka and Edo, where koto study became a mark of refinement, especially for daughters of prosperous families. Midare stands with Rokudan and Hachidan as one of the three danmono attributed to Yatsuhashi, though the attribution rests on guild tradition.","In standard danmono each section has the same fixed number of beats, 104, so a listener raised on Rokudan expects a section to end at a certain moment. Midare plays against that expectation: sections run short or long, and cadences arrive early or late. The disorder is calculated. Knowing the regular pattern first is what lets you hear the irregular one as wit.","Midare is considered one of the most demanding and interesting of the classical danmono, and it remains a standard piece for advanced koto students. Some historians think it may be the work of Yatsuhashi's followers rather than his own, and editions differ in details. It has attracted modern composers and scholars interested in its flexible structure, which seems freer than much Edo-period instrumental music.","Hear Rokudan first so the regular section length is in your body, then play Midare and notice where the phrases stop matching. Listen for the pressed notes that bend upward and the acceleration toward the end, following the jo-ha-kyū principle of slow beginning, development and rush. Afterward, try Takemitsu's November Steps for a modern view of the same instruments' world."],"itri-rast-nat":["A na't, a hymn in praise of the Prophet Muhammad, in makam Rast, setting a Persian poem by Jalāl al-Dīn Rūmī. It is sung by a single voice, unaccompanied and without a fixed beat, in long melismatic phrases that rise and fall around the central notes of Rast. This is the piece that opens the Mevlevi sema ceremony: the singer's line hangs in silence, and only afterwards do the drum and the ney flute enter.","Buhurizade Mustafa Itri worked in Istanbul in the second half of the seventeenth century, under the sultans Mehmed IV, Süleyman II and Ahmed II, and was connected with the Mevlevi order, the followers of Rūmī. The Mevlevi lodges were among the chief schools of Ottoman music, where ney players and singers trained. His setting of this na't became fixed in the ritual and has been sung at the beginning of every sema since.","Rast's third and seventh notes sit between the Western major and minor positions, slightly lower than a piano's. Listen for the way the singer approaches those pitches, and for the note held before the final descent to the home note. Because it is unmetered, each singer shapes the timing differently, so no two ceremonies sound identical, yet the melodic path is fixed and recognisable.","Among Ottoman musicians the Rast Na't is regarded as one of Itri's greatest works and one of the highest achievements of Turkish religious music. After the Mevlevi lodges were closed in 1925 it survived in recordings and in the government-sanctioned performances at Konya, which some traditionalists felt turned a rite into a spectacle. It is still the standard test of a Mevlevi naathan, the ceremony's solo singer.","Listen before you know anything, simply to the lone voice and the silence around it. Then watch a full sema and hear how this solo opens the ceremony before the drum strikes. Compare it with Itri's Segâh Tekbir, sung by many voices, and with Dede Efendi's Ferahfeza Âyin, the kind of work this na't introduces in practice."],"itri-neva-kar":["A kâr in makam Nevâ, the largest and most elaborate vocal form of Ottoman secular art music. The kâr sets a short poem in long, ornamented lines, stretching syllables over many notes and filling gaps with terennüm, vocables like ya-lel-li sung purely for sound. It unfolds over a long rhythmic cycle played on small kettledrums, and it moves through several sections, touching neighbouring makams before settling back in Nevâ.","The kâr stood at the summit of the fasıl, the suite in a single makam that was the standard form of Ottoman courtly music-making. Itri composed for the palace and for elite gatherings in late seventeenth-century Istanbul, and his secular works were passed down orally, teacher to student, for two centuries before being written in Western notation in the late Ottoman and early Republican periods. This kâr is one of the works that established his standing.","Listen for the terennüm passages, where words give way to syllables with no meaning. They are not filler; they let the melody expand without the constraint of a line of poetry, and in a kâr they can outweigh the text. Hear how the drums keep a long cycle of many beats going underneath, so a single phrase of melody can stretch over what feels like several bars.","Ottoman writers and modern Turkish musicologists place Itri's kârs among the classics of the repertoire, though, as with most music of his time, the version sung today reflects two centuries of oral transmission, and the notated editions differ. The early Republic's hostility to Ottoman art music pushed pieces like this out of radio for a period, and their revival since has been a matter of cultural politics as much as taste.","Try following only the drums at first; once the long cycle is clear, the vocal line's great arcs make sense against it. Then listen for the moments words dissolve into syllables. A recording by a Turkish state classical ensemble or a specialist early-music group is the best way in. Compare Umm Kulthum's Al-Atlal for a later Arabic art of long, ornamented song."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-muhayyer-saz-semaisi":["A saz semâîsi, the instrumental piece that traditionally closes an Ottoman fasıl, in makam Muhayyer. It has four sections, called hanes, each followed by a recurring refrain, the teslim. The first three hanes run in the limping ten-beat semâî rhythm; the fourth breaks into a faster, lighter meter, a burst of virtuosity before the refrain returns one last time. Cemil Bey's lines run fast and angular, full of leaps that suit the plucked tanbur.","Tanburi Cemil Bey was the outstanding Ottoman instrumentalist of the empire's last decades, a player of the long-necked tanbur and the small bowed kemençe who also wrote a method book on Ottoman music. Istanbul around 1900 was changing fast: gramophone companies were recording local musicians, Western music was fashionable at court, and the old fasıl was moving into cafés and private concerts. He composed within the classical forms while playing them with new brilliance.","Listen for the fourth hane: after the steady ten-beat limp, the meter suddenly shifts and the tune starts to run. Cemil Bey makes the change sound like an improvised break, but the notes are composed. Then listen to the teslim coming back afterward and notice how the slower refrain now feels like a return home. The contrast is the form's oldest trick; he plays it harder than his predecessors.","Cemil Bey's saz semâîs are among the most frequently performed modern additions to the Ottoman instrumental repertoire, and this one is a standard piece for advanced tanbur and ensemble players. Some purists have found his writing too showy, too close to the café. Most modern players and scholars take the opposite view, hearing in it a late flowering that kept the classical forms alive into the recording age.","Count the four sections and wait for the refrain after each. The fourth section's change of pace is the moment to listen for. Then hear his own 78-rpm recordings of improvised preludes, so you can compare the composer's written music with his improvising. Anouar Brahem's oud records offer a modern continuation of the same sound world."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-sedaraban-saz-semaisi":["A saz semâîsi in makam Şedaraban, one of the Hicaz family of makams, whose scale contains two of the wide, exotic-sounding steps that Western listeners associate with the Middle East. The four sections and the refrain follow the classical plan, but the melody is dark and chromatic, twisting around those wide steps. On the tanbur the long sustain of each plucked note gives the lines a bell-like, brooding quality.","Cemil Bey wrote relatively few composed pieces; his fame rested on his playing and recordings, and on his treatise on Ottoman music, the Rehber-i Mûsikî. His peşrevs and saz semâîs were written for the ensembles he played in and were spread by students and recordings. Şedaraban was a makam rarely used for major works, and his piece helped keep it in circulation in the twentieth-century repertoire.","Listen to the interval between certain adjacent notes, wider than any step in a Western major scale. Şedaraban has two such gaps, and Cemil Bey arranges his lines so that the ear keeps falling through them. On a good recording you can also hear the tanbur's fretted pitches, which divide the octave far more finely than a piano, placing notes between the keys.","The piece is regarded as one of the classics of the late Ottoman instrumental repertoire and is played by Turkish classical ensembles and by musicians from Greece and Armenia who share the tradition. Opinions differ, as with all his music, over tempo: some players take it very fast to emphasise virtuosity, others slower, closer to the older practice, letting the unusual intervals sound fully.","Play it after the Muhayyer semâî: same form, completely different colour. Listen for the gapped steps and how the refrain keeps returning to them. A performance on solo tanbur lets you hear the instrument's resonance; an ensemble version shows how the tune sounds doubled by ney and kemençe. Follow with Cemil Bey's own improvised recordings for his sound as a player."],"miyagi-michio-mizu-no-hentai":["A song for voice and koto setting a sequence of short classical poems, each describing water in a different form, among them mist, rain, snow and dew. The structure follows the traditional tegotomono pattern, sung passages alternating with instrumental interludes in which the koto develops the material alone. The writing is traditional in scale and gesture, but the instrumental interludes already show a composer eager to make the koto sing more expansively.","Miyagi was born in Kobe, lost his sight as a child and trained as a professional koto player in the guild system. His family moved to Korea, then under Japanese control, and he wrote this piece there in 1909, at fourteen, as his first composition. The poems came from a school textbook. Its success in Japan helped launch his career and his later move to Tokyo.","The sequence of poems is a small lesson in natural science: water changing state through the seasons. A teenage composer took schoolbook verses and set them in the old guild form, so the piece looks both backward, to Edo-period tegotomono, and forward, to the modern Japanese interest in educational and seasonal music. Listen for how each change of water gets a change of texture in the koto part.","The piece is still widely taught and performed, and it shows that Miyagi mastered the classical style before he began to blend it with Western harmony. Critics have treated it as an unusually mature first work. It is less famous abroad than Haru no Umi, but in Japan it is often cited as the moment a new composer of the koto announced himself.","Follow the images: listen for the koto trying to imitate falling rain, then the stillness of frost. Pay attention to the interludes, which carry the most interesting instrumental writing. Afterwards hear Haru no Umi, written twenty years later, to measure how far he travelled, and Rokudan, to hear the tradition he started from."],"miyagi-michio-seoto":["A duet pairing the ordinary thirteen-string koto with the seventeen-string bass koto, the jūshichigen, which Miyagi himself developed. The bass instrument lays down a steady, rushing pattern like water over stones, while the treble koto spins bright figures above it. The piece moves in sections from gentle flow to fast rapids and back, with the two instruments sometimes answering each other and sometimes locking together in the same rhythm.","In the early 1920s Miyagi was working in Tokyo to build a modern Japanese music, the movement known as Shin Nihon Ongaku, drawing on Western harmony and ensemble writing. He wanted a bass voice for koto ensembles, which traditionally lacked one, and designed the seventeen-string koto in 1921. Seoto was one of the pieces written to put the new instrument to work, and it became a staple of the modern koto repertoire.","Listen to the bottom line. The bass koto often plays repeated figures and supporting tones that behave rather like a Western bass part, holding the harmony underneath the melody. Traditional koto music has no such layer. Once you notice it, you can hear Miyagi importing the logic of Western chamber music into a Japanese ensemble while keeping Japanese scales above.","The piece remains one of the most performed koto duets and a standard vehicle for the seventeen-string instrument. As with much of Miyagi's work, traditionalists once objected to the Western touches, while later composers took them as a starting point. Details of its composition date vary slightly in sources, but its role in establishing the bass koto is not in dispute.","Listen first only to the bass koto's rushing pattern and imagine the stream. Then shift attention to the treble koto and its play of light across the water. Compare with Debussy's La mer, another picture of water in sound, and Haru no Umi, Miyagi's more famous duet, to hear how he developed his ideas."],"dede-efendi-saba-ayin":["A complete âyin, the music for the Mevlevi sema ceremony, in makam Sabâ. It is laid out in four selâms, sections in which the dervishes turn, each in its own rhythmic cycle, from slow and weighty to quick and light, before the concluding peace. Male voices sing Persian poems by Rūmī, accompanied by ney flutes and kudüm drums. Sabâ, with its unstable, sinking fourth note, gives the whole ceremony an atmosphere of longing and grief.","Hamamizade İsmail Dede Efendi was a Mevlevi dervish of the Yenikapı lodge in Istanbul and the most admired composer at the courts of Selim III and Mahmud II. Mahmud II's reforms, including the destruction of the Janissaries in 1826, reshaped Ottoman life and introduced Western military music under Giuseppe Donizetti. Dede Efendi continued to compose for the Mevlevi rite, and this âyin is among his most loved.","Sabâ's fourth note is lower than expected and the scale seems to sag under its own weight; Ottoman listeners associated it with sorrow and devotion. Listen for the way the melody keeps approaching that note and turning back, as if unwilling to go through. In the fast third selâm the rhythmic cycle shifts markedly; when the drums change pattern, the dervishes' turning is supposed to change too.","Dede Efendi's Sabâ âyin is considered one of the peaks of the Mevlevi repertoire and is performed in Konya and Istanbul. After the lodges were closed in 1925 these works survived through musicians who kept them in notation and recordings. Debate continues over the tempo and style: modern concert performances tend to be faster and more polished than the older lodge practice described in the sources.","Start with the ney solo and the first selâm; the long, slow cycle takes time to feel, but after a few minutes the voices and drums lock into a trance-like pace. Then compare the Ferahfeza âyin by the same composer to hear how a change of makam alters the whole ceremony. A filmed performance with the turning dervishes makes the structure clear."],"dede-efendi-yine-bir-gulnihal":["A yürük semâî, a lively song in a quick six-beat rhythm, in makam Hicaz. The poem is a lover's complaint: once again a slender rose sapling has captured the singer's heart. The melody is light and graceful, moving easily across the Hicaz scale with its wide middle step, and it rolls along with the dancing lilt of the yürük semâî rhythm. It is among the best-known Ottoman art songs and often closes a Hicaz suite.","Dede Efendi wrote in every form of his day, from the Mevlevi ceremony to the courtly suite, and his secular songs were admired at the palace of Mahmud II, himself an amateur musician. The yürük semâî was the fast closing song of the fasıl, the Ottoman suite, and composers used it for their most tuneful ideas. This one has been sung continuously since his time and remains one of the most recognisable songs of the repertoire.","Listen to the rhythm under the voice: the yürük semâî can be heard as two groups of three beats, but the melody often pulls against that, stretching a word across the divide. That gentle cross-rhythm is what gives the song its rocking, dance-like quality. Listen too for the singer's ornaments on the long syllables, which vary from performer to performer.","The song is one of Dede Efendi's most popular works and a standard in Turkish classical and light classical concerts. It is also sung by pop and folk singers, sometimes in simplified form, which traditionalists regret. Historians use it as an example of how an Ottoman composer who wrote grave religious music could also write a song of great melodic charm.","Hear it as a dance: feel the six-beat lilt first, then let the melody sit over it. Compare it with his Sabâ âyin to hear how the same composer moves between ritual and pleasure. For a sense of the wider tradition, follow with a Greek rebetiko song or an Umm Kulthum performance, both of which share the modal world of Hicaz."],"elizabeth-cotten-shake-sugaree":["The second Folkways album of Elizabeth Cotten's music is a quiet domestic record: a woman in her seventies at home, playing guitar and five-string banjo, sometimes singing in a small, unforced voice, and sometimes letting her children's children sing for her. The guitar pieces are rags, dance tunes, hymns and blues, picked with a steady alternating bass under a bright, ringing melody. The title song, a gentle, circling tune, is the one people carry away with them.","Cotten had worked for years as a housekeeper for the Seeger family in Washington, D.C., before the young Mike Seeger realised she was a remarkable guitarist and began recording her at home. Her first album of 1958 made her a figure of the folk revival; by the mid-1960s she was playing festivals and college concerts. This follow-up, taped informally in her own house, captures her repertoire at greater length and in a more relaxed setting than the first.","The lead voice on the title song is not Cotten's but that of her young great-granddaughter, Brenda Evans, and the song itself is Cotten's own, built from a childhood phrase about pawning everything one owns. Listen also for the banjo tracks: she plays the banjo the same way she plays guitar, as a left-hander on an instrument strung for a right-handed player, so the melody sits under her thumb.","Folk-revival listeners welcomed the album as a fuller portrait of a musician they had come to love through a handful of tunes. Over time 'Shake Sugaree' became one of her most covered songs, recorded by folk, rock and jazz musicians alike, and critics have come to see the record less as a curiosity from a 'discovered' elder than as the work of a composer with a distinct melodic voice and a whole private tradition of Piedmont guitar.","Start with the title song and notice how little it needs: a child's voice, a guitar, a tune that goes round and round without hurry. Then play one of the instrumental rags and listen for the bass notes walking steadily while the melody skips over them. Put it beside Mississippi John Hurt's late recordings, which share the same gentleness and the same thumb."],"elizabeth-cotten-live":["A concert album by a guitarist then close to ninety. Cotten plays her best-loved pieces, among them 'Freight Train' and 'Oh Babe, It Ain't No Lie', and between them she talks: about learning to play as a girl on her brother's instrument, about putting music aside for decades, about how songs came to her. The playing is slower and more deliberate than on her early records, but the alternating bass and bell-like melody are unmistakably hers.","By the early 1980s Cotten had become a revered elder of American folk music, touring widely long after most performers retire and receiving a National Heritage Fellowship from the National Endowment for the Arts in 1984. Chris Strachwitz's Arhoolie label, which specialised in regional and vernacular American music, issued this live set, and it won the Grammy for best traditional folk recording in 1985, a rare mainstream honour for a musician of her kind.","The spoken passages are as much a part of the record as the tunes. Her stories explain things the studio albums never could: why she plays the guitar upside down, how church elders once persuaded her that secular music was sinful, and how she came back to the instrument in middle age. Listen, too, for the audience singing along on 'Freight Train', a song written by a girl of about twelve and now older than most of the listeners.","The Grammy made headlines partly because of her age, and some critics treated the award as a tribute to a long life rather than to the performance. Later listeners have been kinder: the record is valued as an oral history as well as a concert, and as proof that a late-life style can be a style in its own right, less nimble but more spacious and deliberate than the playing of her youth.","Listen once straight through, stories included, as though you were in the room. Then compare her concert 'Freight Train' with the 1958 studio take on her first Folkways album and notice what age has changed in the tempo and touch, and what it has not. Seek the full-length reissue so that none of her talk is trimmed away."],"loewe-brigadoon":["A two-act musical fantasy, with book and lyrics by Alan Jay Lerner, about two New Yorkers on a hunting trip in the Scottish Highlands who stumble on a village that wakes for a single day every hundred years. Frederick Loewe's score leans on Scottish colour, with lilting tunes, drones and a real bagpipe, while keeping Broadway's romantic ballads. 'Almost Like Being in Love' and 'The Heather on the Hill' are its best-known songs; the long dance sequences are just as central.","Loewe, born in Berlin in 1901 and raised in the world of operetta, had struggled for years on Broadway before this, his first real success with Lerner. It opened at the Ziegfeld Theatre in March 1947, in the season after Oklahoma! and Carousel had made dance and serious story central to the musical. Agnes de Mille, who had choreographed both of those shows, created its dances, and it ran for well over a year.","The emotional climax is a dance, not a song. When the jealous Harry Beaton is killed during a chase through the woods, the villagers mourn him with a funeral dance to the sound of the bagpipe, and the show goes quiet in a way few musicals of its time dared. Listen also for how the orchestra imitates the drone of pipes elsewhere in the score, so that the real instrument sounds like the music's source when it appears.","Critics praised it warmly in 1947, and de Mille's dances won a Tony Award for choreography in the awards' first year. Some later writers pointed out a resemblance to a nineteenth-century German tale about a village that appears once a century, which Lerner denied knowing. The 1954 MGM film with Gene Kelly flattened much of its magic, and revivals have struggled with its whimsy, but the score remains among the most tuneful of the Golden Age.","Start with 'The Heather on the Hill' for Loewe's gift for a long, unhurried melody, then find a recording that keeps the dance music, above all the chase and the funeral. Compare the Scottish colouring with the Viennese sweep Loewe brought to My Fair Lady nine years later; the original 1947 cast album is short but vivid."],"loewe-camelot":["A musical in two acts on the story of King Arthur, Guinevere and Lancelot, adapted by Alan Jay Lerner from T. H. White's novel The Once and Future King. It begins as comedy, with a nervous young king and a bride who wants romance, and darkens into betrayal, trial and war. Loewe's score mixes courtly pastiche, bright ensemble numbers such as 'The Lusty Month of May', and big romantic ballads, the best known being Lancelot's 'If Ever I Would Leave You'.","Lerner and Loewe came to it straight after the triumphs of My Fair Lady and the film Gigi, and expectations were enormous. The production was troubled: the director Moss Hart suffered a heart attack during the tryouts and Lerner was ill, while the show ran far too long. It opened on Broadway in December 1960 with Richard Burton, Julie Andrews and Robert Goulet, to mixed reviews, and was saved when the cast performed excerpts on television.","Its fame outgrew the stage after the assassination of John F. Kennedy in November 1963, when Jacqueline Kennedy told an interviewer that her husband had loved the closing lines of the title song, and the word 'Camelot' became shorthand for his presidency. Listen, too, to Arthur's closing scene, where he knights a young boy and asks him to carry the story on: the whole show is quietly about how ideals survive their failure.","First-night critics found it overlong and unsure whether it was a comedy or a tragedy, and those complaints have followed it through every revival. Yet the original cast album sold hugely, and the score is generally judged among Loewe's richest. It was, in effect, his farewell to Broadway: he largely retired after it, returning only for a few later projects, so it is often read as the end of the partnership's great run.","Begin with the original cast album and Richard Burton's half-spoken, half-sung 'How to Handle a Woman', a lesson in acting through a song. Then hear Goulet's 'If Ever I Would Leave You' for Loewe at his most operatic. Compare the 1967 film with Richard Harris, which is lusher and slower, and notice how the story's darkening changes the music's colour."],"loesser-the-most-happy-fella":["Frank Loesser wrote the music, lyrics and book for this three-act show about Tony, an ageing Italian winegrower in California's Napa Valley, who woos a San Francisco waitress by letter and, ashamed of his looks, sends her a photograph of his handsome foreman. Most of the story is sung: arias, duets and ensembles flow into one another, with some forty numbers in all. Broadway hits such as 'Standing on the Corner' and 'Big D' sit beside long, near-operatic scenes.","Loesser followed the huge success of Guys and Dolls by adapting Sidney Howard's Pulitzer-winning play They Knew What They Wanted, a very different kind of story, and spent years on it. It opened on Broadway in May 1956 with the opera baritone Robert Weede as Tony and Jo Sullivan as the waitress, whom Loesser later married. Columbia recorded almost the whole score on three LPs, an unprecedented release for a Broadway cast.","Loesser refused to call it an opera, describing it as a musical with a great deal of music, yet the score is built like one, with recurring themes that bind the characters together. Listen to how Tony's music draws on Italian song, while the young people around him sing in American swing and Broadway idioms: the clash of musical languages is the clash of generations and cultures that drives the plot.","It ran successfully but was overshadowed that spring by My Fair Lady, and critics disagreed about whether its mix of opera and musical comedy held together. Its standing has grown steadily: opera companies, including New York City Opera, have staged it, and a 1992 Broadway revival used a scaled-down two-piano version. It is now often cited as a bridge between the Golden Age musical and the through-sung shows of later decades.","Start with 'Joey, Joey, Joey', the foreman's restless song about the wandering life, and then the first-act finale for Loesser writing on an operatic scale. The complete original-cast recording is the one to hear, because the connective music between numbers is where much of the drama lies. Listen next to Puccini's La bohème for the tradition Tony's music comes from."],"loesser-how-to-succeed-in-business":["A brisk satirical musical in two acts about J. Pierrepont Finch, a window washer who climbs to the top of the World Wide Wicket Company by following a self-help book, flattering his superiors and outwitting the boss's nephew. Loesser's songs are sharp and economical: 'The Company Way' mocks corporate conformity, 'Coffee Break' turns a missing coffee supply into office panic, and 'Brotherhood of Man' ends the show as a mock revival meeting. Its best-known song, 'I Believe in You', is sung by Finch to himself.","The book, by Abe Burrows with Jack Weinstock and Willie Gilbert, was based on Shepherd Mead's 1952 parody of success manuals. It opened in October 1961, at the height of the organisation-man era of grey flannel suits and new corporate towers, with Robert Morse as Finch and the former crooner Rudy Vallée as the company president. It ran for over three years and won both the Tony for best musical and the Pulitzer Prize for Drama.","'I Believe in You' is staged as a love song, but Finch sings it into a washroom mirror while other executives shave around him, and the accompaniment includes a buzzing that imitates their electric razors. Loesser takes a sincere ballad form and aims it at pure vanity. Listen, too, to 'Brotherhood of Man', where a secretary's gospel-style solo briefly turns office satire into church music.","The reviews were ecstatic, and it became only the fourth musical to win the Pulitzer Prize for Drama. Some later critics found its satire gentle, even affectionate towards the corporate world it mocks, and its treatment of secretaries dated. Major Broadway revivals in 1995, with Matthew Broderick, and 2011, with Daniel Radcliffe, confirmed that the score's wit survives, though directors now tend to sharpen its view of office sexism.","Begin with 'I Believe in You' and 'The Company Way' on the original cast album, and listen for how much comedy Loesser gets from rhythm and rhyme. Then watch the 1967 film, which preserves Morse's performance. Compare it with Guys and Dolls to hear the same composer trading New York gamblers for the executive suite."],"big-joe-turner-roll-em-pete":["Just two men: Big Joe Turner shouting the blues and Pete Johnson playing boogie-woogie piano. Johnson's left hand pounds a rolling eight-to-the-bar bass figure while his right hand throws out trills, repeated chords and riffs; Turner rides over the top with huge, relaxed power, stringing together verses about a woman he admires. The tempo is fast and never lets up, and the whole thing lasts about three minutes. It sounds like a party already in progress.","Turner and Johnson had worked together for years in the clubs of Kansas City, where Turner tended bar and sang, often calling out verses from behind the counter. The producer John Hammond brought them to New York for his From Spirituals to Swing concert at Carnegie Hall in December 1938, which set off a national boogie-woogie craze. They recorded this number for Vocalion days after the concert, capturing the Kansas City sound at its height.","Turner's verses are mostly floating lines shared across the blues tradition rather than a fixed lyric; he assembles the song on the spot, as he would have in a club. Notice how he sings slightly behind Johnson's beat, so the voice seems to lean back while the piano drives forward. That tension between a propulsive rhythm section and an unhurried shouter is exactly what rhythm and blues and early rock and roll would inherit.","It was a modest record at the time, overshadowed by the boogie-woogie piano trios that followed the Carnegie Hall concert. Historians now routinely name it among the candidates for the first rock and roll record, alongside later jump-blues sides. The argument is partly a game, but the claim is serious: the beat, the shouting vocal and the dance-floor purpose are all here sixteen years before Turner's own 'Shake, Rattle and Roll'.","Listen first only to Johnson's left hand and feel how it never stops, then follow Turner's voice as it sits on top. Next play Turner's 1954 'Shake, Rattle and Roll' and Bill Haley's 'Rock Around the Clock' to hear what this grew into. His 1956 remake on The Boss of the Blues makes a good comparison with a full band."],"big-joe-turner-the-boss-of-the-blues":["Subtitled Joe Turner Sings Kansas City Jazz, this album returns the singer to the swinging small-band sound of his youth. A compact group of horns plays riff-based arrangements behind him, with his old partner Pete Johnson at the piano. The programme mixes blues he had long sung, such as 'Cherry Red', 'Wee Baby Blues' and a new 'Roll 'Em Pete', with standards including 'St. Louis Blues' and 'You're Driving Me Crazy'. The voice is enormous, warm and utterly unhurried.","In 1956 Turner was a rhythm-and-blues star, selling records to teenagers on the strength of 'Shake, Rattle and Roll', while white cover versions took his songs further up the charts. Atlantic, the same label that issued his rock and roll hits, recorded this LP in New York for the growing adult jazz album market, with Nesuhi Ertegun producing. It placed him back among jazz musicians just as the singles market was turning towards younger performers.","Listen to how the horns answer Turner phrase by phrase, exactly as Kansas City bands answered blues shouters in the 1930s: the arrangements are simple riffs, meant to push rather than decorate. The remake of 'Roll 'Em Pete' is worth setting beside the 1938 original, since the same partnership, nearly twenty years on, trades raw speed for a looser, heavier swing. 'Piney Brown Blues' remembers a real Kansas City nightlife figure from their early years.","Jazz critics received it warmly as proof that Turner was a great jazz singer as well as a hit-maker, and it has often been named his finest album. Some rock historians barely mention it, since it complicates the neat story of the blues shouter who became a rock pioneer. Today it is usually heard as the best single document of Kansas City blues singing in high-fidelity sound.","Start with 'Cherry Red' for the voice at its richest, then 'Roll 'Em Pete' for the old partnership. Listen for the moment each horn riff enters behind him and how it lifts the beat. Compare Jimmy Rushing's records with Count Basie to hear the other great Kansas City blues voice, and choose a remastered edition for the full weight of the band."],"gil-evans-the-individualism-of-gil-evans":["Gil Evans's own orchestra in its most personal album of the 1960s. The pieces unfold slowly over long spans, built on vamps, drones and shifting clouds of sound from flutes, French horns, tuba and muted brass, with soloists drifting in and out. 'The Barbara Song', from Kurt Weill's Threepenny Opera, becomes a slow, brooding meditation; 'Las Vegas Tango' is a dark, heavy-footed dance. The record sounds less like a big band than a small orchestra dreaming aloud.","By the early 1960s Evans was famous for his arrangements for Miles Davis, but his own band worked rarely and he recorded slowly, notoriously reluctant to call a piece finished. Creed Taylor, then building Verve's catalogue, produced these sessions across 1963 and 1964 in New York. Jazz was moving towards modal and free forms, and Evans used that openness to write long pieces that sit on one harmony and let colour carry the change.","Listen to how little happens harmonically and how much happens in timbre: a chord may hold for a long time while its instrumentation changes underneath it, the way a painter changes a single colour's texture. Weill's song, originally sharp and ironic, is slowed until its melody barely surfaces. This habit of treating an existing tune as raw material for colour is the same method Evans used for Rodrigo and Falla on Sketches of Spain.","It sold modestly and received less attention than his collaborations with Davis, but arrangers and composers treasured it, and it is often cited as his most personal statement. Some listeners find its slow, open forms unfocused; admirers answer that the drift is the point. Later reissues added previously unissued tracks, which has sharpened the view of this period as his creative peak as a leader.","Begin with 'The Barbara Song' and let it take its time; listen for the French horns and flutes that colour the low chords. Then play the Weill original from The Threepenny Opera to hear what Evans kept and what he dissolved. Out of the Cool and Sketches of Spain are the natural neighbours; choose an expanded CD edition."],"gil-evans-plays-the-music-of-jimi-hendrix":["A large jazz orchestra, amplified and electrified, plays songs by Jimi Hendrix. Evans's band of the 1970s blends tubas, French horns and saxophones with electric guitars, electric bass and early synthesizers, so that Hendrix's riffs are passed around a wall of brass and the ballads, such as 'Angel' and 'Little Wing', are voiced as slow, glowing chorales. Some tracks are tight and funky, others open out into long collective improvisation. Hendrix's sound becomes orchestral colour.","Evans admired Hendrix as a sound-painter, and the two had arranged to meet and discuss a possible recording in 1970; Hendrix died in September that year before it could happen. Four years later Evans recorded this tribute in New York for RCA with his working band, sharing out the arranging among several of its members. It came at a time when Miles Davis and others were bringing rock rhythm and electric instruments into jazz, and Evans took the same path.","Much of the album's weirdness comes from the low end: the tubas and synthesizers double or replace Hendrix's guitar feedback with sustained, buzzing tones, an orchestral equivalent of distortion. Listen for how the melodies of the ballads are handed to horns rather than to the guitar, which in Hendrix's originals carried both tune and texture. It is the same colourist thinking that shaped Sketches of Spain, now applied to rock.","Reviews were mixed: some jazz critics found it loose and uneven, and some rock listeners missed Hendrix's guitar. Over time it has come to be seen as a pioneering cross-over, one of the first serious treatments of rock songs as material for a jazz orchestra, and a model for the many jazz tributes to rock musicians since. Evans continued playing Hendrix's music with his bands for the rest of his life.","Play Hendrix's own 'Little Wing' or 'Angel' first, then Evans's version, and notice what has happened to the guitar's role. Listen next for the tubas in the up-tempo pieces, which give the band a floor-shaking weight. Pair it with Miles Davis's On the Corner, and with Jaco Pastorius's big-band work, which learned from Evans."],"cl-franklin-dry-bones-in-the-valley":["A recorded sermon on Ezekiel's vision of a valley of dry bones that come together, grow flesh and breathe again at God's command. Rev. C. L. Franklin opens in a clear, measured speaking voice, reading the text and explaining it almost like a lecture. Then, as the congregation answers him, his delivery gathers rhythm and pitch until the final third becomes a chanted, sung climax, driven by the organ and the church's cries of response. The shape is a long, steady ascent.","Franklin preached at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit, where the record-shop owner Joe Von Battle taped his Sunday sermons and issued them on his JVB label, with Chess of Chicago later distributing them nationally. Franklin's weekly radio broadcasts and records made him one of the most famous Black preachers in America. For listeners who had come north in the Great Migration, a sermon about scattered bones rising again spoke to their own experience.","Franklin treats the vision as a message about the present, not just prophecy: the dry bones are a people who have been told they are dead, and the sermon insists they can be made whole. Listen for how he returns again and again to the question God asks the prophet, using it as a refrain that grows more musical each time. The spiritual 'Dem Bones', which many in his congregation knew, lurks behind the imagery.","It is among the most admired of Franklin's recorded sermons. Folklorists and historians, notably Jeff Todd Titon, who transcribed and published a selection of them, have treated these records as literature in performance, and Nick Salvatore's biography of Franklin placed them at the heart of Detroit's Black public life. Some listeners find the early expository stretch slow, but that patience is the preparation for the climax.","Resist skipping ahead: the chanted ending lands only if you have heard the calm reading that starts it. Listen for the first shouts from the congregation and track how they grow more frequent. Then hear The Eagle Stirreth Her Nest for comparison, and Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace to hear his cadences passed down to his daughter."],"cl-franklin-the-prodigal-son":["Franklin's sermon on the parable from Luke's Gospel of the younger son who takes his inheritance, wastes it in a far country, and returns home to a father who runs out to meet him. He tells the story as a drama, voicing the son's pride, his hunger among the pigs and the father's joy, with humour and vivid everyday detail. As the story reaches the homecoming, his speech rises into the rhythmic, sung chant of the whoop, and organ and congregation surge with him.","Like his other sermons, this was recorded live at New Bethel in Detroit and sold nationally on records, reaching listeners who could never visit the church. Franklin's audience included many who had left Southern homes for Northern cities, and the parable of a child who goes far away and is welcomed back had a particular weight for families divided by migration. He was also building a reputation as a preacher who spoke plainly about city life and its temptations.","Listen to how Franklin keeps the father offstage for most of the sermon and then makes the father's running to meet his son the emotional turning point, where speech tips into song. The drama is carefully paced like a piece of music, with quiet storytelling as verses and the chanted climax as a final chorus. Knowing that Franklin's own family life was troubled, some listeners hear a personal note in his tenderness towards the returning child.","Collectors and scholars count it among his most characteristic records, showing his narrative gift as clearly as his vocal power. His ministry also drew criticism in his lifetime, for his wealth, his fame and his private life, and those debates have coloured how his sermons are received. Recent scholarship tends to separate the man from the art, treating the recordings as central documents of African American oral performance.","Follow it as you would a radio play: the characters, the far country, the long road home. Notice where Franklin's voice first begins to sing rather than speak, and how the organ joins him. Then listen to Sam Cooke's Night Beat or Aretha Franklin's gospel records to hear how the same rise from calm to fervour passed into soul singing."],"violeta-parra-la-carta":["A short, driving song for voice and a hard-strummed guitar, built on the verse forms of Chilean country song but sung with a bite that has nothing rustic about it. The singer tells how a letter reached her abroad with bad news from home: her brother has been thrown in jail for standing with striking workers. Verse by verse the anger widens from one family to a whole country, and the refrain lands each time like a fist on a table.","Parra was living in Europe in the early 1960s, singing in Paris cafés and showing her paintings and tapestries, when news came that a brother had been arrested during labour unrest in Chile. She answered with this song, probably in 1962. It was one of the first times she turned the folk forms she had spent a decade collecting into direct political comment, a step that the younger Chilean musicians of the Nueva canción would soon make the heart of their movement.","Listen to how traditional the machinery is: the guitar pattern and the verse shape come from the rural songs Parra had recorded from old singers in the Chilean countryside. The protest works because it wears peasant clothes. Notice too how little she decorates the melody; she almost speaks some lines, and the plain voice makes the accusation feel like testimony rather than performance.","The song circulated widely in Chile and then across Latin America, and after the 1973 coup it became one of many Parra songs that could get a singer into trouble. Some critics have preferred her gentler songs and her collecting work, seeing the political pieces as occasional; others hear in them the real hinge of her career, the point where the archivist became the voice of the movement that Víctor Jara and Inti-Illimani would carry on.","Hear it first straight after Gracias a la vida to feel the range of one songwriter: thanks and fury from the same voice and guitar. Then play Víctor Jara's Te recuerdo Amanda to hear what the next generation did with her example. Seek Parra's own recording rather than the many later covers; the roughness of her voice is part of the message."],"violeta-parra-casamiento-de-negros":["A quick, bouncing folk song with a guitar that strums and slaps like a small dance band. The text is a comic tale in the old Chilean popular manner: a wedding at which everything, guests, clothes and family, is black, which then tips abruptly into a funeral. Parra sings it with a bright, nasal, almost shouted country voice, clipping the words and leaning into the jokes, and it is over almost before it has begun.","This was one of Parra's first solo recordings for Odeon in Santiago, in the early 1950s, when she had just begun travelling the Chilean countryside to note down songs from elderly rural singers. Before that she had sung in bars and circuses with her sister in a duo playing Spanish and Mexican popular music. The record was a real hit, and its success let her move from the entertainment circuit toward the collecting and composing that made her name.","The text is not her own invention: she adapted verses from the rural popular tradition, and the word black is doing double work, pointing at mourning dress as much as at the couple, so the wedding carries its own funeral from the start. Listen to the guitar's percussive strokes on the body of the instrument, a country technique she would keep through her whole career.","In its day it was simply a lively novelty that made Parra famous. Later listeners have been more uneasy, since the humour turns on race in ways a modern audience notices, and some Parra anthologies now pass over it. Scholars tend to treat it as a document of where she started: a popular entertainer drawing on the countryside before she began to write the songs that made her a national figure.","Listen for the guitar before the words: the scrape, slap and rush of it is the sound of Chilean country dance music that Parra would spend her life documenting. Then jump thirteen years to Las últimas composiciones to hear how the same voice, older and slower, carried much heavier material. A compilation of her 1950s Odeon sides gives the best context."],"elmore-james-shake-your-moneymaker":["Two and a half minutes of pounding dance blues. Elmore James's electric slide guitar opens with a hard, fuzzy triplet figure, the band locks into a stomping shuffle, and James shouts a party chant that is really just one instruction repeated with rising urgency. There is barely a verse structure: the song is a riff, a groove and a voice that sounds as if it is straining against the edge of the microphone, with the guitar screaming back between lines.","James recorded it for Bobby Robinson's Fire label in New York in 1961, late in a career that had begun with his 1951 recording of Dust My Broom. By then he was moving between Chicago and the East Coast, cutting sides for small independent labels aimed at Black dance audiences. Two years later he was dead of a heart attack at forty-five, just before a new generation of white listeners in Britain turned his records into textbooks.","Notice how little of the song is a song: the lyric is essentially a chant, and the real content is the interlocking of guitar riff and drums. James's sound came partly from his amplifiers, which he is said to have modified himself, pushing them into a distortion that few studios would have allowed. That grainy tone, not only the slide figure, is what the rock guitarists of the late sixties were chasing.","It was a minor record on release and became a standard only afterwards, when Fleetwood Mac, under Peter Green and Jeremy Spencer, made it a centrepiece of their 1968 debut, and countless bar bands followed. Blues purists sometimes find it thin next to James's slow numbers; others argue that its crude, relentless energy is exactly what made him the bridge between Delta slide and rock guitar.","Turn it up and listen to the first five seconds: that slide figure is the whole Elmore James style. Then compare it with Dust My Broom, recorded a decade earlier, to hear how the same idea got louder and rougher. Follow it with Fleetwood Mac's early version and with Hendrix to hear where the sound travelled."],"pran-nath-midnight-raga-malkauns":["A single raga sung across more than an hour: Malkauns, the five-note midnight raga without a second or a fifth degree. Pran Nath begins with a long unmetred exploration over the tanpura drones, touching one note at a time and holding it until it settles, then moves into slow and faster compositions with tabla. The voice is thin and old, but every pitch is placed with such care that the whole performance feels like one long act of listening.","The recording comes from the years after 1970, when Pran Nath had moved to New York at the invitation of La Monte Young and Marian Zazeela and was teaching a small circle of American musicians, among them Terry Riley. Much of what he sang in those years was recorded privately by his students and only issued long after his death in 1996; this performance appeared on CD in 2006, through the circle around Young and Zazeela.","The drone is part of the composition. Pran Nath's students tuned the tanpuras with an exactness unusual even in India, and his placement of each note against them is the real subject of the performance. Listen for the moment a held note stops wavering and locks against the drone; that small settling is what he taught Young and Riley to hear, and it runs directly into their tuned-piano and drone works.","Indian listeners who knew him valued him as a keeper of the Kirana style's slow, meditative approach rather than as a concert star, and his recorded legacy is small and scattered. In the West these posthumous releases have been received mainly through the minimalist composers he taught, which some find distorting; the best of them, this among them, let him be heard on his own terms as a great singer of a demanding raga.","Do not start in the middle. Give the opening unmetred section, the alap, at least fifteen minutes, ideally late at night and in quiet, and let the drone become the room. Then hear Amir Khan's slow Darbari Kanada for comparison, and afterwards La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano to hear what an American composer took from this voice."],"nat-king-cole-the-christmas-song":["Three minutes of winter warmth. Over soft strings and a gentle rhythm section, Nat King Cole sings a slow, winding melody that lists the small sensory pleasures of the season, food, cold air, carols and children waiting for presents, and ends with a plain greeting to every listener. The tune moves through unexpected harmonies for a holiday song, and Cole's unhurried baritone, each consonant placed exactly, makes it sound less like a party than a fireside conversation.","Mel Tormé and Bob Wells wrote the song in 1945, reportedly on a hot California summer day while trying to think cool thoughts. Cole's trio recorded it for Capitol in 1946, first without strings and then, at his insistence, with a small string section, an unusual expense for the label. He re-recorded it in 1953 and again in stereo in 1961, and it is that later version that most radio stations now play every December.","Listen to the harmony under the opening line: it slips through chords that belong more to a jazz ballad than to a carol, a sign that the writers were songwriters of the Broadway and nightclub world. Cole's piano, the thing that made him famous before his voice did, is almost invisible on the later versions; in the 1946 record you can still hear the trio texture underneath.","It was a hit at once and has never gone away; it is among the most performed holiday songs in America. Critics sometimes treat it as the moment Cole's jazz trio career gave way to a softer pop career, a trade that jazz writers long lamented. Others hear in it exactly the qualities of his jazz playing, clarity, restraint and timing, simply put to a different use.","Play the 1946 and 1961 recordings back to back: the song is the same, but the first sounds like a small club and the second like a lavish living room. Listen for how Cole holds the last greeting without any extra emphasis. Then hear Irving Berlin's White Christmas and Phil Spector's Christmas album for two other American answers to the season."],"nat-king-cole-after-midnight":["Nat King Cole back at the piano with a small group, singing and playing standards the way he had in the trio days. Guitar, bass and drums swing lightly behind him, and on different tracks a guest soloist joins in: Harry Edison's muted trumpet, Willie Smith's alto saxophone, Juan Tizol's valve trombone, Stuff Smith's jazz violin. Songs like Sweet Lorraine, Route 66 and It's Only a Paper Moon get relaxed, conversational treatments, with Cole's piano as bright as his voice.","By the mid-1950s Cole was one of the biggest pop singers in America, recording with large string orchestras and, in 1956, hosting his own national television show, a first for a Black American entertainer. These sessions for Capitol in 1956 were a deliberate return to his jazz roots, a chance to play with the kind of musicians he had started among in the Los Angeles clubs of the late 1930s and early 1940s.","Listen to the piano. Cole was one of the great jazz pianists of the swing era, a light, crisp player with a right hand that influenced Oscar Peterson and many others, and on this album his solos are as long and considered as his vocals. Notice how he voices chords sparsely behind the guest soloists, leaving space, and how the singing and playing share the same easy timing.","Jazz critics who had written Cole off as a pop crooner welcomed the album as proof of what he could still do, and it has remained the record most often recommended to people who know only his ballads. Its reissues added extra tracks from the sessions. Some find it too comfortable; most hear the ease as the point, a master working among peers without pressure.","Start with Sweet Lorraine, which he had first recorded with the trio years earlier, and compare the two versions. Then follow a guest through a track: Stuff Smith's violin brings a rougher, bluesier edge. For contrast hear Nature Boy to measure the gap between the pop and jazz Coles, then Lester Young with the Oscar Peterson Trio for kindred small-group ease."],"jean-ritchie-at-folk-city":["A live evening in a Greenwich Village club with two Appalachian musicians taking turns and joining forces. Jean Ritchie's clear, straight voice and plucked dulcimer carry old ballads and hymns from Kentucky; Doc Watson's warm baritone and nimble flat-picked guitar carry North Carolina songs and fiddle tunes. When they sing together, unaccompanied or over guitar, the two mountain traditions meet, and the easy talk between numbers makes the record feel like a family gathering.","Gerde's Folk City in New York was the centre of the early-1960s folk revival, the club where Bob Dylan had played his first important sets. Ritchie had been a fixture of the New York folk world since the early 1950s; Watson was a recent discovery, brought north after 1960 by Ralph Rinzler. Moses Asch's Folkways label released this concert, capturing the revival's interest in real tradition-bearers rather than city interpreters.","Listen to the harmonies: neither singer uses the smooth close harmony of commercial country music. They sing with the hard, lonesome, slightly unaligned sound of church and family singing in the mountains, where each voice keeps its own line. Notice too how the audience laughs and settles; the recording documents a city crowd learning how to listen to this music.","It was a modest release, but it has come to be valued as one of the best documents of the folk revival at its most serious, when a club audience came to hear source singers. Reviewers often single out the duets. It has been reissued on CD through Smithsonian Folkways, and it is one of the few places to hear both musicians in one room.","Pick the unaccompanied duets first and listen to the two voices rub together without an instrument to smooth them. Then compare Ritchie's solo Elektra album of 1952 and Watson's first Vanguard album to hear each apart. Clarence Ashley's recordings, where Watson was first heard, make a natural next step."],"jean-ritchie-the-l-and-n-dont-stop-here-anymore":["A new song in an old mould. Over a simple accompaniment Jean Ritchie sings a slow tune that sounds as though it could be a century old, in the voice of a Kentucky miner's family watching their coal town die. The mine has closed, the railway no longer halts, and the singer counts what has been lost: work, neighbours, health, a future for the children. There is no chorus to shout, only verses that build a quiet, bitter picture.","Ritchie grew up in Perry County, Kentucky, in the heart of the eastern coal field, and by the 1960s the region she came from was being hollowed out by mechanisation, strip mining and closures. She began writing her own songs about it, alongside her work as a collector and performer of traditional ballads. The Louisville and Nashville Railroad, the L&N of the title, had carried coal out of those mountains for generations.","The craft lies in its disguise: Ritchie borrowed the modal feel, plain phrasing and verse shape of the family ballads she had sung all her life, so a protest song about modern industry sounds like inherited tradition. Listen for the way the train, once a sign of connection to the wider world, becomes a symbol of abandonment. The melody never rises to a climax, which is its own kind of statement.","The song spread through the folk and country worlds and was taken up by other singers, including Kathy Mattea on her album of coal songs, Coal, in 2008. It is often placed beside Merle Travis's Sixteen Tons and Hazel Dickens's mining songs as part of a small canon of Appalachian protest. Some admirers of Ritchie the tradition-bearer paid less attention to her songwriting; that view has faded.","Listen to it straight after one of the old ballads on her 1952 Elektra album and see how hard it is to tell the new song from the old. Then hear Loretta Lynn's Coal Miner's Daughter for a very different, prouder view of the same world, and Woody Guthrie's Dust Bowl Ballads for another songwriter using folk forms to report a disaster."],"lord-kitchener-sugar-bum-bum":["A big, bright carnival record: a brass section punching out short, catchy phrases, electric bass and drums driving a fast, bouncing groove, and Lord Kitchener's voice, now older and rounder, riding on top with a playful flirtatious story about admiring a woman's dancing. The chorus is short enough for a whole street to shout back, and the arrangement is built for movement, repeating and building rather than developing like an old narrative calypso.","Kitchener had returned to Trinidad in 1962 after fourteen years in Britain and became the dominant calypsonian of Carnival, winning the Road March, the title for the song most played by bands on the streets, many times. By the late 1970s Trinidadian music was shifting toward soca, a faster, dance-driven style influenced by soul and funk. Sugar Bum Bum showed that the veteran could master the new sound, and it was the Carnival hit of 1978.","Compare the rhythm section with his 1951 London recordings: the old loping two-step has become a propulsive, electric dance beat, and the band plays the role that the singer's wit once played. Listen to the horn lines, which are designed to be easy for steelbands to arrange; Kitchener always wrote with the pan players in mind, and his hits were quickly taken up across the Carnival.","It was a huge success in Trinidad and across the Caribbean diaspora, and remains one of his best-known songs. Some calypso traditionalists saw the soca turn as a loss of the lyrical and political bite that defined the form; others credit Kitchener, along with Lord Shorty, with carrying calypso into a new era rather than letting it harden into nostalgia.","Hear it as dance music first: the groove is the point. Then play London Is the Place for Me to hear how far one artist travelled in twenty-seven years, and Lord Shorty's Endless Vibrations to hear the soca experiment that set the scene. Mighty Sparrow's records make the natural comparison with Kitchener's great rival."],"lord-kitchener-pan-in-a-minor":["A calypso about the steel pan, written to be played by steel pans. Kitchener sings with obvious delight about the instrument and the players who hammer music out of oil drums, and about a tune in a minor key that the steelbands take up, while the band builds a driving groove with a strong, memorable melody. The key named in the title is itself a kind of salute, since pan players think in keys and chords as orchestral musicians do, and the melodic line is built to be arranged.","Since 1963 Trinidad's Carnival had included Panorama, a competition in which large steel orchestras, sometimes a hundred players strong, perform elaborate arrangements of a current calypso. Kitchener wrote more songs chosen for Panorama than anyone, and in the 1970s and 1980s he wrote a string of calypsos about pan itself. Pan in A Minor belongs to that series, a tribute from Trinidad's best-loved songwriter to its national instrument.","The song lives two lives. On record it is a sung calypso; at Panorama it becomes raw material for an arranger, who stretches it into a ten-minute orchestral variation form with key changes, breakdowns and a returning theme, played entirely on steel drums. Listen to how the melody is written with clear, repeatable phrases and a strong minor-mode colour that pan arrangers could transform.","Kitchener's pan songs are treated in Trinidad as a body of work in their own right, and many pan players regard him as the instrument's great composer even though he did not play it. Outside the Caribbean they are less known than his London calypsos. Scholars of the steelband see in them a rare case of a songwriter shaping a whole instrumental tradition through the tunes he gave it.","Listen to Kitchener's own recording first, then find a Panorama steelband performance of one of his pan tunes: the leap from a six-minute song to a massed orchestra of drums is the whole experience. Notice the minor-key colour. Compare the scale of a Panorama band with a symphony orchestra; the question of what an orchestra is gets interesting."],"albert-king-ill-play-the-blues-for-you":["Albert King's guitar, thick-toned and bent far out of pitch, sings over a warm Memphis soul band with horns and organ. The title track opens the album at a slow, patient tempo and runs several minutes, with King talking to the listener as much as singing, offering the blues as comfort. Other tracks move into funky, mid-tempo soul-blues, and the whole record balances his stinging, economical lead lines against grooves built for Black radio.","King had been a Stax artist since 1966, when Born Under a Bad Sign fused his blues with the label's house band. By 1972 Stax was at its commercial peak after Isaac Hayes's success, and King was recorded with younger Memphis musicians including the Bar-Kays and the Memphis Horns. The album was made for the Black audience that still bought blues and soul singles, even as white rock fans were discovering him at the Fillmore.","King played left-handed on a guitar strung for a right-hander, upside down, and used unusual tunings, so his bends pull the strings downward rather than pushing them up. That is why he could bend a note so far, often a step and a half, with such a vocal, crying sound. Listen for how few notes he plays in each fill; the space between them is as expressive as the notes.","It was one of his most successful albums and the title track became a standard of the modern blues club repertoire. Critics sometimes place it just below Born Under a Bad Sign, which had the stronger songs, but many consider it the best showcase of his mature voice and playing. Stevie Ray Vaughan and many other guitarists took his phrasing directly from records like this.","Start with the title track and pay attention to a single bent note held and released; that is the Albert King sound. Then compare him with BB King's The Thrill Is Gone to hear two contrasting ideas of modern blues guitar. Otis Redding's Otis Blue gives the Stax background, and Freddie King's Hide Away completes the three Kings."],"dexter-gordon-our-man-in-paris":["A quartet session of bebop standards with a warm, generous tenor at its centre. Gordon plays Scrapple from the Apple, Willow Weep for Me, Broadway, Stairway to the Stars and A Night in Tunisia with Bud Powell on piano, Pierre Michelot on bass and Kenny Clarke on drums. The tempos are relaxed, the solos long and conversational, and Gordon's huge, slightly behind-the-beat sound fills the room while Powell comps with sparse, splashy chords.","Gordon had moved to Europe in 1962, as had several of his bebop contemporaries. Powell had lived in Paris since 1959, and Clarke, one of the founders of bebop drumming, had settled there in 1956. Blue Note recorded the session in Paris in 1963, so the album joined three of the music's original architects, two of them expatriates, in a city that offered them respect and steady work that America often did not.","Listen to Powell. He was in fragile health, and his playing here is simpler than on his famous 1940s and early 1950s records, but the few chords he places behind Gordon are exactly timed. On A Night in Tunisia, listen to Clarke: the tune's Latin-tinged rhythm was part of the language he helped build twenty years before, and he plays it like a founder rather than a follower.","It was well reviewed and has become one of Gordon's most admired albums, second in fame perhaps only to Go. Some listeners hear a slightly tired Powell; others argue that his restraint suits Gordon's spacious style. It is frequently held up as a model of the old bebop repertoire played with the maturity of musicians who had long since stopped needing to prove anything.","Begin with Willow Weep for Me and hear how slowly Gordon lets a ballad unfold, then jump to Scrapple from the Apple for his swing at speed. Compare Powell here with his own Amazing Bud Powell albums from the early 1950s. Then play Go, recorded the year before in New York, to hear Gordon with a younger rhythm section."],"dexter-gordon-homecoming":["A double album recorded in a small New York basement club, with Dexter Gordon leading a quintet featuring the trumpeter Woody Shaw. The tunes are long, several stretching well past ten minutes, and the band plays hard bop with a fierce modern edge: Shaw's angular trumpet lines push against Gordon's big, relaxed tenor. Between numbers Gordon speaks to the audience in his deep, amused voice, and the sense of a jubilant reunion is audible throughout.","Gordon had lived in Copenhagen since the early 1960s and returned to New York in December 1976 for a run at the Village Vanguard. The crowds were enormous, the press called it a triumph, and Columbia, under Bruce Lundvall, signed him. In a decade when jazz audiences were drifting toward fusion, the homecoming showed a public still hungry for acoustic bebop-based playing, and it helped spark a broader revival of that style.","Listen to how the two horn players differ: Shaw was one of the most harmonically adventurous trumpeters of the 1970s, drawing on McCoy Tyner and modal jazz, while Gordon remained a bebop storyteller. The contrast is the album's engine. Gordon also still quotes songs in his solos; a stray phrase from a pop tune dropped into a long improvisation is his trademark wink to the listener.","It was received as an event, and it revived Gordon's career in America, leading to further Columbia albums and eventually his starring role in the film 'Round Midnight in 1986, for which he was nominated for an Oscar. Some critics find the long tracks sprawling; many regard it as the best live record of a veteran's return in jazz, a companion to Rollins's comeback years.","Choose one long track and follow it from start to finish, listening to how the solos build over many choruses. Then compare Gordon's sound here with Go and Our Man in Paris: the tone has grown even larger and slower. Monk's Round Midnight, the tune that gave his film its title, makes a good companion."],"doc-watson-doc-watson-and-son":["Doc Watson plays and sings with his teenage son Merle on second guitar. The record mixes old-time songs, blues, ballads and fiddle tunes in a relaxed, homely style: Doc's warm baritone and fast, clean flatpicking over Merle's steady rhythm and occasional leads. The sound is two acoustic guitars working as one instrument, and it has the unhurried feel of music made in a family living room.","After his first Vanguard solo album in 1964, Watson became a star of the folk revival. He was blind and needed a travelling companion, and his son Merle, named after the country guitarist Merle Travis, learned guitar quickly to join him on the road. This album, released by Vanguard in 1965, was Merle's recording debut and the start of a musical partnership that lasted until Merle's death in a tractor accident in 1985.","Listen for how Merle supports his father: rather than doubling the melody, he plays a firm bass line with his thumb and chords with his fingers, freeing Doc to take quick, fiddle-like runs. That division of labour, learned at home, became a model for bluegrass and folk guitar duos. Notice also how many tunes come from old country and blues records rather than oral tradition alone.","It was well received as a gentle, charming record and a showcase for a promising young guitarist. Critics have always placed it slightly below Watson's solo debut in historical importance, but it laid the groundwork for decades of Doc and Merle collaborations, including their later work with the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band. Many flatpickers name it as an early source.","Put it on after Watson's 1964 solo album and listen for what a second guitar adds: fuller rhythm, more room for Doc to run. Try following Merle's bass line alone on one track. Then go back to Clarence Ashley's recordings to hear where Doc was first heard, and to Flatt and Scruggs for the bluegrass counterpart."],"doc-watson-southbound":["Doc Watson, with Merle again at his side, moves beyond pure tradition into a broader mix of country, blues, folk and newer songs. The flatpicking is even more assured, the singing warm and conversational, and the two guitars mesh more tightly than on their first record together. The instrumental Windy and Warm, with its gently syncopated, rolling guitar pattern, became one of his signature pieces and a staple for fingerstyle guitarists everywhere.","By 1966 the urban folk revival was losing ground to folk rock, and Watson, who had grown up playing everything from Jimmie Rodgers to rockabilly, was free to show that range. Vanguard continued to record him regularly, and he toured widely with Merle. This album signalled that he would not be confined to the museum label of traditional folk; he was a working musician who played whatever suited him.","Windy and Warm was written by John D. Loudermilk, a Nashville songwriter, not a mountain tradition-bearer; Watson's recording made it sound like an old family tune. Listen to how he combines alternating thumb bass with syncopated melody, a technique associated with Merle Travis and Chet Atkins. It is a small lesson in how folk tradition absorbs commercial music and gives it back sounding ancient.","Reviewers welcomed it as proof of Watson's range, and guitarists have treated its instrumentals as essential study pieces ever since. Some revival purists regretted the move away from strictly traditional repertoire, but that view now seems narrow, since Watson's music had always drawn freely on radio and records. It remains one of his most loved Vanguard albums.","Start with Windy and Warm and try to follow the thumb and fingers separately; it sounds like two guitars but is one. Then hear the vocal tracks for his easy phrasing. Compare with his 1964 debut to measure how he widened the repertoire, and follow with The Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo for a rock band heading in the opposite direction."],"george-russell-jazz-workshop":["Twelve short, tightly written pieces for a sextet Russell called his Smalltet: Art Farmer on trumpet, Hal McKusick on alto saxophone and flute, Barry Galbraith on guitar, Bill Evans on piano, with bass and drums. Nothing lasts much over four minutes, yet every track is packed with shifting metres, sudden unison lines and harmonies that hover rather than resolve. The openers Ye Hypocrite, Ye Beelzebub and Ezz-thetic sound like bebop run through a prism, bright, angular and faintly unsettling.","Russell, a drummer turned composer from Cincinnati, had spent years laid up with tuberculosis working out a theory of harmony, published in 1953 as the Lydian Chromatic Concept of Tonal Organization. It argued that jazz could organise itself around scales rather than rapid chord changes. RCA gave him the chance to record the ideas in New York in 1956, a moment when the major labels were briefly curious about serious, experimental small-group jazz alongside the commercial West Coast cool sound.","Listen for how rarely the band simply blows over a chord sequence. Soloists are handed scales and colours instead, so Evans and Farmer sound as if they are floating across the harmony rather than chasing it; this is the idea that, via Miles Davis, would become modal jazz two years later. Concerto for Billy the Kid is a showcase for the young Evans, who plays with a density and harmonic daring few people had yet heard from him.","The record sold poorly and was treated for years as a musician’s album, admired by players and composers but little known to the public. Its reputation has grown steadily as historians traced the path from Russell’s concept to Kind of Blue, and it is now regarded as one of the key documents of 1950s jazz composition. Some listeners still find it cerebral; others hear exceptional wit and swing in its miniatures.","Take it a track at a time rather than as background, because each piece is a compact puzzle. Start with Ezz-thetic, then Concerto for Billy the Kid for Bill Evans at full stretch. Notice how the guitar and piano thicken the harmony without ever settling it. Then play Kind of Blue straight afterwards and listen for the family resemblance in the way the solos breathe."],"george-russell-ezz-thetics":["Six tracks by the George Russell Sextet, with Russell himself at the piano, Don Ellis on trumpet, David Baker on trombone, Steve Swallow on bass and Eric Dolphy guesting on alto saxophone and bass clarinet. The record mixes Russell originals such as the title track, a remake of his 1950s tune, with Miles Davis’s Nardis and a long, broken-open reading of Thelonious Monk’s ’Round Midnight. The sound is lean, spiky and restless, the ensemble writing always pulling against the soloists.","By 1961 Russell had moved from big theoretical statements to a working small band that could play his ideas night after night, and he had begun teaching the Lydian concept to younger musicians. Free jazz had just arrived with Ornette Coleman, and Russell’s group sat in an interesting position between it and composed modernism. Riverside, a New York label better known for Monk and Bill Evans, recorded the sextet in the spring of that year.","The ’Round Midnight here is effectively a feature for Dolphy, and it shows how far the tune could be pushed: the familiar melody is stated, then dissolved into a solo of wide leaps, vocal cries and sudden runs that barely touch the original harmony, while the band’s written backgrounds keep reminding you where the song is. Listen also to Swallow, still playing upright bass here, holding the whole structure together.","Critics at the time were divided between those who heard arid theory and those who heard a composer finding his most personal voice. Posterity has sided firmly with the second camp: the album is now widely considered Russell’s finest small-group record and one of the best showcases of Dolphy as a sideman. Debate continues mainly over whether Russell’s own piano playing adds or distracts.","Begin with ’Round Midnight, since you probably know the tune and can hear exactly what is being done to it. Then go back to the opening title track to hear how Russell’s writing makes a small band sound large. Compare Dolphy here with his own Out to Lunch three years later, and listen for the trombone and trumpet trading jagged unison lines."],"tito-puente-oye-como-va":["A relaxed cha-cha-chá built on a two-chord piano and bass figure that repeats for almost the whole track, a hypnotic vamp that is the song’s real hook. Over it a flute and strings weave lines in the charanga style, the voices chant a short refrain inviting you to hear how the rhythm goes, and Puente’s timbales crackle at the edges. It is a groove rather than a melody, cool and unhurried.","Puente, a New York-born Puerto Rican who had studied at Juilliard, was the timbales-playing king of the Palladium Ballroom’s mambo era. By the early 1960s the mambo craze had cooled and the gentler cha-cha-chá, and the flute-and-violin charanga band, were in fashion. He recorded this for the Tico label, adapting his big-band sound to the new style. It was a modest dance-floor number in its first life.","The famous vamp was not conjured from nothing: it closely resembles the montuno of Chanchullo, a 1957 danzón-mambo by the Cuban bassist Israel López, known as Cachao, and Puente’s tune is generally understood as a reworking of it. Listen, too, for how little actually changes across four minutes; the excitement comes from the percussion and the flute improvising around a fixed floor, the essential logic of Cuban son.","Its second life began in 1970, when Santana recorded it for the album Abraxas and turned the piano riff into an organ and electric guitar anthem that reached the American top twenty. Puente at first bristled at a rock band profiting from his tune, then warmly embraced the royalties and the new audience. Today the song is a standard of Latin music worldwide, played by everyone from school bands to salsa orchestras.","Play Puente’s original first and listen to the bass and piano alone for a minute; everything else is decoration on that loop. Then hear how the flute breathes and bends over it. Follow it with the Santana version and notice what survives the move from dance hall to rock stage: the vamp, and very little else. Finally try Cachao’s Chanchullo to hear the seed."],"tito-puente-top-percussion":["An album almost entirely of drums and voices. Puente strips away his big band and leads a battery of Afro-Cuban percussionists through chants and rhythms rooted in the religious music of Santería, alongside several extended drum features of his own. There are no horns to speak of and few chords: the music is carried by interlocking conga, bongo and timbales patterns, call-and-response singing, and the dry, cracking sound of Puente’s sticks on metal and wood.","Recorded in New York for RCA in 1957, the same year as the best-selling Dance Mania, this was a deliberate step away from the ballroom. Puente’s circle included Cuban drummers such as Mongo Santamaría and Willie Bobo, and the city’s Latin musicians were increasingly interested in the sacred and folkloric sources behind the dance music. That a major American label released such an uncompromising drum record says much about Puente’s commercial clout at the time.","Several tracks invoke specific orishas, the deities of Afro-Cuban religion, through chants that would have been heard at ceremonies rather than in clubs, so the record quietly carried sacred practice into the pop marketplace. Listen for the way the rhythms are layered in fixed parts, each drummer holding a pattern that only makes sense against the others, while one lead voice converses with the singers above.","Top Percussion sold far less than Puente’s dance records and puzzled some of his fans, but among percussionists it became a touchstone, passed hand to hand for decades. Later scholars of Latin music often cite it as an early and serious attempt to document Afro-Cuban ritual drumming for a mass audience. It is now regarded as one of the most important records in Puente’s long catalogue.","Do not wait for a tune; listen instead for the conversation between the drums. Pick one instrument, the high bongo or the lowest conga, and follow it through a whole track, then switch. Notice when the voices answer the lead singer. Hear it beside Dance Mania to understand what Puente’s big band was built on, and beside Gillespie’s Manteca for the jazz side of the same story."],"clara-ward-how-i-got-over":["Clara Ward’s own song of thanksgiving, sung by the Ward Singers with Clara at the piano. It starts almost conversationally, a testimony that the singer has come through hardship and wants to look back and marvel at how she survived, then builds through repetition until the group is shouting over hard, rolling piano chords. The harmonies are close and bright, and the leads trade phrases in the ecstatic, slightly ragged manner of the Holiness church.","The Ward Singers, led by Clara and managed by her formidable mother Gertrude Ward from Philadelphia, were the most glamorous female gospel group of the early 1950s, touring with gowns, wigs and a showmanship that shocked some church elders. Clara wrote the song around 1951, in the years when gospel quartets and groups filled auditoriums across the North and South and were recorded by small independent labels.","The song’s theme of looking back over a hard road carries two meanings at once, personal salvation and the collective survival of Black Americans, and the second became unmistakable in 1963, when Mahalia Jackson sang it to the crowd at the March on Washington shortly before Martin Luther King spoke. Listen for how the melody sits low and speech-like before leaping: it was built for a singer to stretch.","The song quickly entered the repertoire of other gospel singers and became one of the most widely performed gospel compositions of the century, sung by Mahalia Jackson, Aretha Franklin and countless church choirs. Ward’s authorship is sometimes overshadowed by those famous versions, and historians of gospel have worked to restore her place as a composer as well as a performer.","Hear the Ward Singers’ recording first, for the raw group sound and Clara’s piano. Then listen to Mahalia Jackson’s slower, grander versions and notice how she treats each phrase as a sermon. Finally hear Aretha Franklin, who learned from Ward as a girl in Detroit. Listen throughout for the moment the song shifts from memory into jubilation."],"clara-ward-packin-up":["An up-tempo Ward Singers number led by Marion Williams, the group’s most thrilling voice. The song pictures a believer getting ready for the journey to heaven, gathering her things and waiting for departure, and the tempo drives like a train. Williams leaps into a high, bell-like register, growls in the low one, and throws in whoops and falsetto runs, while the other singers pound out a rhythmic chorus behind her.","In the mid-1950s the Ward Singers were at the height of their fame, and much of their excitement came from Marion Williams, who had joined from Miami and often took the leads while Clara arranged and played. The group’s records sold widely in Black communities and their concerts drew huge crowds. Tensions over pay and credit inside the group would lead Williams and others to leave in 1958 to form the Stars of Faith.","Listen to Williams’s ornaments: the high swoops and the little yodel-like breaks are techniques that rock and roll singers were absorbing at exactly this moment, and Little Richard openly named Williams as the source of his famous high whoop. The imagery of packing a bag for glory also turns death into a cheerful journey, a very old spiritual idea delivered at dance-floor speed.","It became one of the songs most closely associated with Williams, who kept singing it through her later solo career on concert stages in America and Europe. Gospel historians often cite the Ward Singers’ records of this period as the peak of the female group style, and they frequently credit Williams rather than Clara as the group’s greatest vocal talent, a judgment that has shaped how both women are remembered.","Listen for the lead voice first and try to follow every change of register; Williams moves from a shout to a whisper in a single phrase. Then focus on the backing singers and the piano, which keep the rhythm relentlessly steady. Afterwards play Little Richard’s Tutti Frutti and listen for the same whoop turned secular."],"bill-haley-crazy-man-crazy":["Two minutes of shouted enthusiasm from Bill Haley and His Comets. The band is essentially a western swing outfit, with steel guitar and slapped double bass, pushed into a hard backbeat, and the singers answer Haley with gang-chant responses borrowed from teenage slang about how wild the music is. A short guitar break and a steel guitar solo keep things moving, and the whole record rides on the clacking bass.","Haley had spent years as a country and western bandleader in Chester, Pennsylvania, before noticing that the rhythm and blues records played by local Black disc jockeys excited young audiences. He renamed his Saddlemen the Comets around 1952 and began writing songs aimed at teenagers. This one, written with the band’s bassist Marshall Lytle, was issued by the small Philadelphia label Essex in 1953.","The record is usually counted as the first rock and roll recording to reach the national pop charts in the United States, a year before Rock Around the Clock. Listen for the slap bass: Lytle slaps the strings against the fingerboard so that the bass works as a second drum, a country technique that gives the Comets their particular clattering drive in place of a heavy drum kit.","It reached the top twenty in 1953 and was used in a television drama, but it was quickly overshadowed by Haley’s later hits and is often forgotten in popular histories. Rock historians have since restored it as a milestone, though some argue that Black rhythm and blues records such as Rocket 88 deserve the title of first rock and roll record more than any of Haley’s work.","Listen first to the rhythm section rather than the voice: the slapped bass and the snare backbeat are the new thing. Then notice the chanted responses, a crowd sound built into the record. Compare it with Hank Williams on one side and Big Joe Turner on the other, and you will hear the two sources Haley was stitching together."],"bill-haley-shake-rattle-and-roll":["Haley’s version of the twelve-bar blues written by Jesse Stone under the name Charles Calhoun and recorded earlier the same year by Big Joe Turner. The Comets play it faster and brighter, with a honking tenor saxophone, crisp guitar fills and the band shouting the title in unison. Haley’s delivery is cheerful rather than knowing, and the whole thing bounces rather than rolls.","Signed to Decca in 1954 and working with the producer Milt Gabler, who had produced Louis Jordan’s jump blues, Haley recorded this within months of Turner’s rhythm and blues hit. Radio in the segregated United States was far more likely to play a white band’s version to a white audience, and Decca aimed it squarely at that market. It became a top-ten pop hit before Rock Around the Clock had broken through.","Compare the words with Turner’s and you will find that Haley’s version removes or rewrites the more suggestive verses, turning an adult song about a woman in bed into something closer to a teenage party chant. That quiet sanitising is the whole story of 1950s crossover in miniature. Listen also for the Louis Jordan-style arrangement, Gabler bringing his jump blues experience to the session.","It sold heavily and helped establish Haley as the first white star of rock and roll, especially in Britain. Later critics have often used it as a textbook example of a white cover softening a Black original, and Turner’s recording is now generally preferred. Yet musicians including the young British beat groups heard Haley’s version first, and it undeniably carried the song to places Turner’s could not reach.","Play Big Joe Turner’s version and Haley’s back to back. Notice the tempo, the saxophone and the voice: Turner leans back and growls, Haley pushes forward and smiles. Then listen for what the Comets add, the unison shouts and the slap bass. Afterwards try Elvis Presley’s later version, which splits the difference between them."],"celia-cruz-burundanga":["A fast, comic song in the Afro-Cuban style, sung by Celia Cruz with the Sonora Matancera. Written by Óscar Muñoz Bouffartique, it tells a tangled story of a chain of animals and a mischievous brew, built on nonsense words and Afro-Cuban vocabulary that are a delight to sing. The trumpets punch out short riffs, the chorus answers Cruz, and her dark, powerful voice rides the rhythm with complete authority.","In the early 1950s Cruz was the new female lead of the Sonora Matancera, Havana’s most popular conjunto, having replaced the singer Myrta Silva. Some fans had doubted a Black woman with a deep voice could succeed with the band; songs like this silenced them. The group recorded for the New York-based Seeco label, whose discs spread their sound across Latin America and the Caribbean.","The title is Cuban slang for a jumble or a confusing mess, and the song’s nonsense chain of animals plays on that idea. Listen for the Afro-Cuban words woven into the lyric, which point to the Yoruba-derived religious culture Cruz grew up near in Havana and which she would carry into all her later music, long after salsa made her an international star.","It became Cruz’s first big hit and is usually described as her first gold record, establishing her as the Sonora Matancera’s star rather than a replacement. Critics now see her 1950s recordings as the foundation of the style that, in New York two decades later, would be marketed as salsa, and many Cuban music historians prefer these sides to her later, glossier work.","Listen to how Cruz shapes the rhythm with her consonants; the song’s playful words work like extra percussion. Notice the trumpets’ short answering blasts and the chorus response. Then play something from Celia & Johnny, two decades later, to hear the same voice with a harder New York band. Compare with Beny Moré or any Havana conjunto of the period."],"schuller-seven-studies-klee":["Seven short orchestral pieces, each a musical response to a painting by Paul Klee: Antique Harmonies, Abstract Trio, Little Blue Devil, The Twittering Machine, Arab Village, An Eerie Moment and Pastorale. The first builds slow, glowing blocks of sound like Klee’s coloured squares; Little Blue Devil is a sly jazz walk with a pizzicato bass; The Twittering Machine chirps and splutters in pointillist fragments; Arab Village evokes North African flute and drum music. The whole set lasts about twenty minutes.","Schuller wrote the set on commission from the Minneapolis Symphony Orchestra, which premiered it under Antal Doráti in 1959. He was then a horn player turned composer who moved between the concert hall and jazz clubs, and who had coined the term Third Stream for music that joined the two. Klee himself was a trained violinist who thought about painting in musical terms, which made him a natural subject.","Each study tries to translate something visual into sound rather than tell a story. Antique Harmonies works with slowly shifting blocks of colour, much as the painting is built from soft rectangles; The Twittering Machine gives its mechanical birds to chirping woodwinds and has the percussion suggest the crank that drives them. Little Blue Devil quietly borrows the walking bass and blues inflections of jazz, Schuller’s other musical world breaking through the concert-hall surface.","The piece was an immediate success and quickly became Schuller’s most widely performed orchestral work, admired for its wit, its brevity and its orchestration. Some modernist critics thought it too pictorial, even light; audiences did not care. It remains a favourite for orchestras wanting a modern piece that listeners enjoy at first hearing, and a common teaching example of orchestral colour.","Look at the Klee paintings while you listen, one per study; the connections are surprisingly direct. Start with Little Blue Devil and The Twittering Machine, the two most vivid. Notice how short everything is: not one gesture is wasted. Then compare with Mussorgsky’s Pictures at an Exhibition, the older model of music about pictures."],"schuller-of-reminiscences-and-reflections":["A single-movement orchestral elegy of about twenty-five minutes. It begins in darkness and quiet, with low strings and hushed brass, moves through passages of intense, dense lament and moments of violent outburst, and returns towards stillness. The harmony is freely atonal and often thick, but the emotional line is easy to follow: grief, remembrance of happier times, anger, and finally a kind of exhausted acceptance.","Schuller wrote the work after the death of his wife, Marjorie Black, in 1992, after more than four decades of marriage. It was commissioned by the Louisville Orchestra and premiered in 1993, and it won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 1994. By then Schuller was an elder statesman of American music, known as conductor, publisher, record producer, jazz historian and teacher as much as composer.","The title’s two words describe a structure as much as a mood: passages that recall earlier, more lyrical music sit alongside stretches that seem to look back on them from a distance, as if memory itself were being examined. Listen for moments when the brass briefly recalls the warmth of an older tonal style before the harmony clouds again; they are the reminiscences breaking in.","The Pulitzer brought Schuller belated recognition as a composer rather than an all-purpose musical polymath, though the piece is played far less often than its prize might suggest. Critics have praised its sincerity and orchestral craft, while others find its language characteristic of a late-modernist style that younger American composers had already moved past. It is increasingly heard as one of his most personal statements.","Listen in one sitting and in quiet, as you would to a requiem, following the rise and fall of intensity rather than any tune. Notice where the texture thins to a few instruments; those are the most private moments. Compare it with Berg’s Violin Concerto, another memorial written in a modernist idiom, and with Schuller’s own lighter Klee studies to hear his range."],"soul-stirrers-peace-in-the-valley":["Thomas A. Dorsey’s gospel song sung by the Soul Stirrers, with the young Sam Cooke on lead. The quartet sets a slow, rocking bed of close harmony, deep bass voice at the bottom, and Cooke sings the melody with an unusual lightness, gliding between notes rather than hitting them hard. The song describes a promised land where there will be no more sorrow and where even wild animals are gentle, and the group sings it with calm longing.","The Soul Stirrers, founded in Texas in the 1920s and shaped by the lead singer R. H. Harris, were the most influential male gospel quartet in America. When Harris left in 1950 the nineteen-year-old Cooke from Chicago replaced him, and this was among his first recordings with the group for Art Rupe’s Specialty label in Los Angeles in 1951. Dorsey had written the song for Mahalia Jackson in the late 1930s.","Listen to how Cooke handles long notes: instead of the heavy vibrato and shouted climaxes of older quartet leads, he adds a small turn or melisma and lets the note slide away. The quartet behind him uses the switch-lead approach Harris had pioneered, voices ready to take over the line. Red Foley and later Elvis Presley would record the song, carrying Cooke’s smoothness into country and pop.","It was well received in gospel circles, though some older listeners missed Harris’s power. With hindsight, historians hear in these early sides the sound that would soon make Cooke the first gospel star to cross into secular soul, and they credit the Soul Stirrers’ blend of harmony and individual lead as a template for doo-wop and soul vocal groups.","Listen first for the bass singer, who holds the floor steady, then for Cooke gliding on top. Notice the absence of instruments; everything is voices. Compare with Mahalia Jackson or Dorsey’s own version to hear how the song changes when a quartet sings it. Then play Cooke’s You Send Me and listen for the same slides."],"soul-stirrers-nearer-to-thee":["A slow, intensely devotional song by the Soul Stirrers with Sam Cooke on lead, built on the long tradition of hymns about drawing close to God. The quartet hums and sustains chords while Cooke sings the lead with rising urgency, repeating short phrases with small variations, and the gentle guitar keeps a steady pulse. By the end his voice is soaring above the group in long, bending lines, yet it never becomes harsh.","By the mid-1950s Cooke was a star in the gospel world, drawing young women to Soul Stirrers concerts in a way no quartet singer had before. Specialty recorded him often, and the group’s records sold well to church audiences. At the same time Cooke was being quietly urged to try popular music, a move that, in 1957, would scandalise part of the gospel community but make him a pop idol.","Listen to the way Cooke builds tension by repeating a phrase and changing only one note or one syllable each time, a preaching technique borrowed from the pulpit; it is the same technique he would later use in secular songs. The guitar is also worth noticing, since quartets had long sung unaccompanied, and its presence hints at the move towards rhythm-and-blues-style backing.","It became one of Cooke’s best-loved gospel sides and is often cited by writers on gospel as the fullest expression of his quartet style before his crossover. Some gospel purists later regarded his departure as a betrayal, but collectors and critics now treat the Specialty recordings as a peak of the quartet tradition and as essential background for soul music.","Begin by listening only to Cooke and count how many ways he finds to sing a single repeated phrase. Then listen to the voices behind him, which hold the harmony like an organ. Follow it with Touch the Hem of His Garment, then with A Change Is Gonna Come, and hear how the gospel sound survives in his secular masterworks."],"gerry-mulligan-bernies-tune":["The Gerry Mulligan Quartet with Chet Baker plays Bernie Miller’s brisk tune with no piano at all. Mulligan’s baritone saxophone and Baker’s trumpet state the theme together, then weave around each other in improvised counterpoint, one taking the lead while the other plays a supporting line, over walking bass and brushed drums. The sound is airy and transparent, with every line audible, and the whole track is over in about three minutes.","Mulligan had arranged for the Birth of the Cool sessions in New York and then moved to Los Angeles, where he began playing with Baker at a club called the Haig. The small room had no usable piano, or so the story goes, and the pianoless format was born partly of necessity. Richard Bock founded the Pacific Jazz label to record the quartet, and this was among its very first releases in 1952.","Without a piano to state the chords, the harmony is implied entirely by the bass and the interplay of the two horns, so listen for how Mulligan’s low lines often function as a second bass or a piano left hand. The counterpoint owes something to Bach and to Dixieland, where trumpet and clarinet also improvised together; West Coast cool quietly revived the oldest jazz texture.","The record sold well and made the quartet the most talked-about jazz group of 1952 and 1953, turning Baker into a star and West Coast cool into a movement. Some East Coast critics dismissed the style as polite or thin; others praised its clarity and wit. Today the quartet’s recordings are regarded as a high point of the cool era and a model for pianoless small groups since.","Listen with the two horns in separate ears if you can: follow Mulligan’s baritone for one chorus, then Baker’s trumpet. Notice how much space there is without a piano. Compare with a bebop quintet track to hear what has been taken away, and with Ornette Coleman’s pianoless quartet a few years later to hear who learned from it."],"gerry-mulligan-night-lights":["A late-night album of slow and mid-tempo pieces played by a sextet with Art Farmer on trumpet and flugelhorn, Bob Brookmeyer on valve trombone, Jim Hall on guitar, Bill Crow on bass and Dave Bailey on drums. Mulligan plays piano on the opening title track, a quiet original, and baritone elsewhere. The programme includes a bossa nova reworking of a Chopin prelude and standards such as Morning of the Carnival and In the Wee Small Hours of the Morning. Everything is soft-edged and unhurried.","By 1963 Mulligan had led quartets, a concert jazz band and many all-star sessions, and he was one of the best-known figures in American jazz. Bossa nova had arrived from Brazil the previous year and was sweeping American jazz, and this record reflects that fashion quietly. It was made in New York for Philips, with musicians who had played in his Concert Jazz Band and knew his writing well.","Listen to Prelude in E minor: Mulligan takes Chopin’s short, grieving piano prelude from Op. 28 and sets its descending chords to a gentle bossa nova rhythm, so the nineteenth-century harmony sits naturally inside a 1960s Brazilian groove. On the title track, notice that the leader is at the piano, not the saxophone, playing sparse chords with a composer’s patience rather than a pianist’s display.","It was received as a pleasant mood record and has only grown in reputation, becoming one of Mulligan’s most beloved and most reissued albums. Some critics regard it as the purest expression of his lyrical side; others find it too comfortable next to the adventurous jazz of 1963. The title track has become a quiet standard among jazz musicians and late-night radio hosts.","Play it after dark at a low volume, as intended. Start with the title track, then go straight to the Chopin, and compare it afterwards with a piano recording of the original prelude. Listen for Jim Hall’s guitar, which fills the space a piano would take. Pair it with Getz/Gilberto for bossa nova’s other American face."],"harry-belafonte-at-carnegie-hall":["A double album taken from two sold-out concerts in April 1959, laid out like the evening itself: a first half of Black American work songs and spirituals, a Caribbean section, and a closing tour of songs from around the world. Belafonte moves from the hard, driving rhythm of 'John Henry' to Irish and Hebrew songs and back to the islands, and the famous stretch is a long, loose 'Matilda', where he turns the whole hall into his chorus.","By 1959 Belafonte was the biggest-selling Black entertainer in America, riding the success of Calypso, a Hollywood career and a growing role in the civil-rights movement beside Martin Luther King Jr. RCA recorded the concerts with the new stereo equipment and put them out as a lavish two-record set, then a luxury item. Robert DeCormier, a folk-choral arranger from the left-wing tradition of the Weavers, shaped the choral and orchestral backing.","Listen to how the audience is recorded: RCA placed microphones out in the hall, so you hear the crowd learn a refrain and then carry it without help, until Belafonte teases them for singing it wrong. The running order is an argument in itself: work songs, then island songs, then songs of other nations, putting Caribbean music on the same stage as Irish, Israeli and American folk song rather than filing it away as novelty.","It was a huge commercial success and stayed on the American album charts for years, one of the first live albums to sell on that scale. Folk purists sometimes found the polish too smooth, and later writers argued over whether Belafonte flattened the island styles he popularised. The record is now seen as a model for the concert album and for folk music presented as a world-wide family.","Begin with 'Matilda' and let the crowd singing do its work, then go back to the opening work songs to hear how hard and rhythmic Belafonte could sound. Compare the Weavers' own Carnegie Hall concert of 1955 for the same folk-revival spirit on a smaller scale, and the Belafonte–Makeba record for where this work led."],"harry-belafonte-island-in-the-sun":["A slow, swaying ballad in a gentle island rhythm, sung by Belafonte in his warm, husky middle voice over guitar, soft percussion and a hushed backing choir. It is less a dance tune than a hymn to a place: the song describes the beauty of a Caribbean island, the dignity of the people who work its land and sea, and a singer's promise never to forget it. It lasts barely three minutes and never raises its voice.","Belafonte wrote it with Irving Burgie, the Brooklyn-born songwriter of Barbadian descent who supplied much of the Calypso album, as the title song for Robert Rossen's 1957 film Island in the Sun, in which Belafonte starred. The film, set on a fictional West Indian island, caused a scandal in the segregated United States by hinting at romance between Belafonte's character and a white woman played by Joan Fontaine, and some Southern cinemas refused to show it.","Notice what the song leaves out: there is no joke, no double meaning and no fast calypso patter, the things American listeners had come to expect from island music. Burgie and Belafonte wrote it closer to a folk anthem or a hymn, and its images of labour on land and sea echo the work songs Belafonte sang elsewhere. The choir's soft answering phrases give it the sound of a community singing, not a tourist admiring the view.","It was a big hit, especially in Britain, and quickly became a standard, recorded by many singers and loved across the Caribbean diaspora. Some critics have heard it as picture-postcard exoticism of the kind the film was selling; others note that it grants island people a gravity that Hollywood rarely did. It remains one of the songs most closely tied to Belafonte's name.","Listen first for the choir underneath, entering and falling away like waves, and for how little Belafonte pushes his voice. Then play it beside 'Day-O' from Calypso to hear the two sides of his island repertoire, the work song and the ballad, and beside Lord Kitchener's London calypso for a Trinidadian's view of leaving home."],"kander-and-ebb-kiss-of-the-spider-woman":["Two men share a cell in a prison in an unnamed Latin American dictatorship: Molina, a gay window-dresser jailed on a morals charge, and Valentin, a hard-line political prisoner. To survive the torture and boredom Molina retells the films of his favourite star, Aurora, and the stage keeps breaking open into her glamorous musical numbers. Aurora also plays the Spider Woman, a figure of death whose kiss the prisoners fear, and the score moves between cell-block grit and lush movie pastiche.","The show comes from Manuel Puig's 1976 novel and Hector Babenco's 1985 film. Terrence McNally wrote the book and Harold Prince, who had directed Cabaret, staged it. An early workshop in 1990 went badly, but the rebuilt show opened in Toronto and London in 1992 and on Broadway in 1993, with Chita Rivera as Aurora and Brent Carver as Molina, written in the years when AIDS was devastating Broadway's own community.","Kander writes Aurora's numbers in the style of 1940s studio musicals and Latin dance films, with sweeping strings and brassy tangos, while the prison music is harder and plainer; listen for the moment late in the show when Molina's own voice takes on the film-star sweep, as fantasy and reality finally merge. The anthem 'The Day After That', sung by the prisoners' families, was taken up at AIDS benefits in the 1990s, giving the show a second, unspoken subject.","Broadway critics were divided on the book but praised Rivera and Carver, and the show won the Tony for Best Musical in 1993, along with awards for score and book. Some felt the fantasy numbers smothered the prison drama; others saw that tension as the whole point, as in Cabaret. It has had fewer revivals than Kander and Ebb's two big hits, and many admirers think it is their most underrated score.","Start with the Broadway cast recording and play the title song and 'Where You Are', Aurora's invitation to escape into the movies, then the quiet cell scenes that follow. Listen for how each fantasy number answers a moment of pain. Pair it with Cabaret, the earlier show where a nightclub also comments on political terror."],"lee-konitz-with-warne-marsh":["Konitz's light, dry alto and Warne Marsh's airy tenor weave around each other through a set of standards and bebop tunes, including Charlie Parker's 'Donna Lee' and the swing number 'Topsy'. The two horns often play long, winding unison lines, then split into two independent voices that sound like a baroque duet. The rhythm section keeps a steady, quiet swing underneath, and nobody raises the temperature: the excitement is all in the length and logic of the lines.","Konitz and Marsh had both studied with the blind pianist Lennie Tristano in New York, whose school stressed long, even melodic lines, counterpoint and freedom from bebop clichés. By 1955 both were working away from their teacher, and this Atlantic session reunited them with a strong New York rhythm section led by Oscar Pettiford on bass and Kenny Clarke on drums, drummers and bassists far earthier than Tristano liked, which loosens the music.","Listen for passages where the two saxophones improvise at the same time rather than taking turns, each keeping its own line while fitting the other, a habit learned from Tristano's counterpoint exercises and from Bach. Many of the 'new' tunes are fresh melodies written over the chords of old standards, a Tristano-school practice, so a familiar song is often hiding underneath an unfamiliar title.","At the time it was filed under cool jazz and admired mainly by musicians. Later critics came to treat it as the best record of the Tristano school, warmer and more swinging than their earlier work because of Pettiford and Clarke. It is now a favourite of saxophonists, studied for its line-writing, and a strong argument that cool jazz was not merely soft.","Pick one saxophone and follow it through a whole track, then play it again following the other. 'Donna Lee' is the best way in; compare it with Charlie Parker's own version to hear how the Tristano players cooled bebop's heat without slowing it down. Then try Konitz's earlier 'Subconscious-Lee'."],"lee-konitz-motion":["A trio record with no piano: Konitz's alto, Sonny Dallas on bass and Elvin Jones on drums, playing standards such as 'I Remember You', 'All of Me' and 'You'd Be So Nice to Come Home To'. Konitz barely states the melodies; he starts improvising almost at once, spinning long, unbroken lines that bend and reshape each tune. Jones, soon to power John Coltrane's quartet, surrounds him with a rolling, busy pulse that pushes the alto forward.","By 1961 Konitz, once the model cool-jazz altoist, had been overshadowed by the hard boppers and by Ornette Coleman's free jazz. Producer Creed Taylor recorded this date for Verve in New York during the same months in which Jones was joining Coltrane. Removing the piano, as Sonny Rollins and Gerry Mulligan had done, left Konitz free of fixed chords and able to steer the harmony wherever he wanted.","Try naming each tune before Konitz lets you: often he suggests the melody only in passing fragments, trusting you to hear the song's chords under his new line. Listen too for the contrast of temperaments, the cool, level alto against Jones's churning triplets and cymbal crashes; the tension between them is what gives the album its name and its energy.","It sold modestly and was out of print for long periods, but musicians passed it around, and it is now widely rated Konitz's greatest record and one of the key piano-less trio albums. Critics hear it as proof that the Tristano approach could survive in the hotter climate of the 1960s. Expanded reissues with extra takes have only strengthened its reputation.","Start with 'I Remember You' and listen to how quickly Konitz leaves the melody behind. Keep one ear on Elvin Jones's drums, which almost form a second solo. Compare Sonny Rollins's piano-less trio on Way Out West for a more humorous, earthy take on the same format."],"stan-getz-jazz-samba":["Getz's soft, cloud-like tenor floats over Charlie Byrd's nylon-string guitar and a light, shuffling samba rhythm, playing new Brazilian tunes such as Jobim's 'Desafinado' and 'Samba de Uma Nota Só' (One Note Samba). The sound is intimate and quiet: brushes on the snare, a gentle bass, the guitar picking out harmonies. Getz barely raises his voice, yet his phrasing gives each melody a sighing, half-sad lilt that became the American sound of bossa nova.","Charlie Byrd had heard bossa nova on a State Department tour of South America in 1961 and brought records home to Washington. Producer Creed Taylor paired him with Getz, back from years in Europe, and the album was recorded in a single day in February 1962 in a Washington church hall chosen for its sound. Released that spring, it rode a new appetite for quiet, sophisticated music in the early 1960s.","The American players did not fully master the Brazilian beat; listen to the drums and you hear something closer to a soft jazz shuffle than João Gilberto's guitar rhythm, one reason Brazilians later preferred Getz/Gilberto. Also notice 'Desafinado', whose title means 'out of tune': Jobim wrote its odd, wandering melody as a joke about critics who called bossa nova singers off-key.","It reached the top of the Billboard album chart, a rare feat for jazz, and 'Desafinado' became a hit single that won Getz a Grammy. It launched the American bossa nova craze and a flood of imitations. Byrd later sued over royalties, feeling he had been under-credited. Critics now rate it slightly below Getz/Gilberto but credit it as the record that opened the door.","Start with 'Desafinado' and listen to how Getz handles the melody with barely any vibrato, then how Byrd's solo answers him on guitar. Play it next to João Gilberto's Chega de saudade to hear the Brazilian original, and then Getz/Gilberto to hear what happened when the two traditions met properly."],"cannonball-adderley-quintet-in-san-francisco":["A live set by Cannonball Adderley's new quintet: his big-toned, bubbling alto, his brother Nat's cornet, Bobby Timmons on piano, Sam Jones on bass and Louis Hayes on drums. The opener, Timmons's 'This Here', is a swaying, church-flavoured tune in a rocking triple time, full of handclap rhythms and blue notes. Around it come fast hard-bop pieces and ballads, stretched out in long solos, with Cannonball's warm spoken introductions setting the mood between numbers.","Adderley had spent two years in Miles Davis's sextet, playing on Milestones and Kind of Blue, and left in 1959 to lead his own band again. Producer Orrin Keepnews recorded the group at the Jazz Workshop club in San Francisco in October 1959. Timmons had just written 'Moanin'' for Art Blakey, and gospel-tinged jazz was in the air as Black church music moved into the clubs.","Listen to the audience: the club is loose and noisy, people clap on the backbeat, and the band feeds off it, which is part of the point. Cannonball's spoken introductions are worth hearing too, relaxed and witty, turning a jazz set into a warm community event. And notice how 'This Here' keeps its gospel feel even in three-beat time, an unusual mix at the time.","The album sold very well for a jazz record and made the quintet a popular act, with 'This Here' a jukebox hit. It helped define soul jazz. Some critics grumbled that the style became formula in lesser hands, and the Adderleys were occasionally accused of playing to the crowd. Most now see this record as fresh and joyful, a clear bridge between hard bop and soul.","Start with 'This Here' and clap along on the offbeats the way the crowd does. Then listen to how Cannonball's solo on a fast number flows in quick, singing phrases. Follow it with Horace Silver's 'The Preacher' and Blakey's 'Moanin'', then jump ahead to 'Mercy, Mercy, Mercy' to hear where the band went."],"eric-dolphy-far-cry":["A quintet record with Dolphy on alto sax, bass clarinet and flute, Booker Little on trumpet, Jaki Byard on piano, Ron Carter on bass and Roy Haynes on drums. The first side is a suite for Charlie Parker; there are original pieces full of wide leaps and sudden speech-like cries; and the album ends with 'Tenderly', played by Dolphy alone on alto, with no band at all. The music sits between bebop and the new free jazz, keeping chords but stretching them hard.","Recorded in one day in December 1960 at Rudy Van Gelder's New Jersey studio for Prestige's New Jazz label, it came during Dolphy's busiest year, when he was working with Charles Mingus, Ornette Coleman and John Coltrane. Booker Little was a gifted young trumpeter from Memphis who, like Dolphy, was drawn to bold intervals and darker harmonies. Their partnership was brief: Little died in 1961, aged 23.","Listen to 'Tenderly': unaccompanied saxophone was still rare (Coleman Hawkins had tried it in 1948), and Dolphy plays the ballad using huge jumps between high and low registers, as if two voices were talking. In the Parker tribute, listen for how Dolphy's alto recalls Bird's phrasing while leaping far outside it, homage and escape at once. His bass clarinet solos also helped establish the instrument in jazz.","At first some critics, including a few in Down Beat, found Dolphy's leaps and squeals ugly, and he was lumped in with the controversial new thing. Later judgment turned warmly, and Far Cry is now counted among his finest records, second only to Out to Lunch!. The Dolphy–Little partnership, cut short, is often mourned as one of jazz's great lost paths.","Begin at the end with 'Tenderly' and follow how Dolphy turns one familiar ballad into a conversation with himself. Then go back to the Parker suite and hear how the whole band honours bebop while bending it. Compare Hawkins's 'Picasso' for an earlier solo saxophone, and Out to Lunch! for where Dolphy went next."],"horace-silver-blowin-the-blues-away":["Horace Silver's quintet at full strength: Blue Mitchell on trumpet, Junior Cook on tenor, Gene Taylor on bass and Louis Hayes on drums, all playing Silver's own tunes. The title track charges in at speed; 'Sister Sadie' is a strutting, gospel-shouting blues with riffing horns; 'Peace' is a hushed, prayer-like ballad; and two trio pieces put Silver's percussive piano in front. Silver's style, short hammered phrases and churchy chords, drives everything.","Silver had co-founded the Jazz Messengers with Art Blakey and by 1959 had his own quintet, one of Blue Note's best-selling acts. The album was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Hackensack, New Jersey, in the late summer of 1959, the same year as Kind of Blue and The Shape of Jazz to Come. Silver was writing tunes that pulled jazz toward gospel, blues and dance.","Listen to how Silver writes the horn parts in 'Sister Sadie': the trumpet and tenor answer each other like a preacher and congregation, a gospel call-and-response pattern turned into a jazz arrangement. In 'The Baghdad Blues', listen for the Middle-Eastern-sounding scale, an early sign of Silver's interest in music beyond America that came to the fore in Song for My Father, with its Cape Verdean roots.","It was well received and 'Sister Sadie' became a hit and a standard, later adopted by big bands. Critics now often rate it Silver's best album with this quintet, a perfect record of hard bop's balance between complexity and groove. Some purists thought soul jazz too commercial, but Silver's writing has aged better than most of the style, and 'Peace' is widely admired for its restraint.","Start with 'Sister Sadie' and notice how the horns riff behind each soloist, then play 'Peace' right after to hear the other side of Silver. Compare with Blakey's 'Moanin'' and Silver's own 'The Preacher', and then 'Song for My Father' to hear the same band sound opening up."],"chet-baker-chet":["An album of slow ballads on which Chet Baker does not sing but plays trumpet, softly and close to the microphone, in front of a cushion of New York musicians including Bill Evans on piano, Pepper Adams on baritone sax, Kenny Burrell on guitar and Paul Chambers on bass. The tempos hardly move, and songs such as 'Alone Together', 'It Never Entered My Mind' and 'How High the Moon' come out as late-night reveries, muted in colour and almost whispered.","Baker had been the golden boy of West Coast cool jazz in the early 1950s, but by the late decade his heroin addiction was damaging his career. Producer Orrin Keepnews recorded him for Riverside in New York around the turn of 1959, with East Coast players more usually heard in hard bop. The same season Bill Evans was working with Miles Davis on the sessions that became Kind of Blue.","Listen to Bill Evans behind the trumpet: his soft, clustered chords and quiet voicings set the mood almost as much as Baker does, and they are the same sound he brought to Kind of Blue a few months later. Notice too how Baker leaves space, playing fewer notes than any trumpeter of his generation; the silences between his phrases are part of the melody.","It was little noticed at release and sometimes dismissed as mood music. Later listeners, rediscovering Baker after his death in 1988, came to hear it as one of his most consistent records, and it is now valued as a showcase of his tone and as an early Bill Evans document. Some still find the ballad-only programme too even, and that is a fair complaint.","Play it late at night, quietly, and listen for the way Baker's trumpet almost breathes the melody instead of attacking it. Start with 'It Never Entered My Mind', then compare it with his singing on Chet Baker Sings and with Coltrane's Ballads for another horn player's approach to slow songs."],"ahmad-jamal-chamber-music-of-the-new-jazz":["A trio without drums: Ahmad Jamal on piano, Ray Crawford on guitar and Israel Crosby on bass, playing standards and originals as delicate, carefully arranged chamber music. Jamal plays very little, leaving wide spaces, then drops in a sudden crashing chord or a glittering run high on the keyboard. The rhythm is kept by Crosby's springy bass and Crawford's guitar, which sometimes taps the strings to imitate a bongo drum, giving the music a light, Latin bounce.","Jamal was a young pianist in Chicago, leading a trio in the city's clubs, when the local Chess company's Argo label issued this album. The title reached for the prestige of classical music, then a common move in 1950s jazz. Miles Davis heard Jamal in these years and told his own musicians to listen to Jamal's space and understatement, and later recorded tunes from Jamal's repertoire.","Listen for the silences: in a bebop age that prized many notes, Jamal leaves whole bars empty and lets the bass and guitar carry the tune, and those gaps are as arranged as the notes. The tune 'New Rhumba' became a feature for Miles Davis and Gil Evans on Miles Ahead in 1957, where Evans kept Jamal's lilting bass figure almost exactly.","It sold modestly at first and some critics called Jamal's style cocktail piano, too light for serious jazz. That judgment turned after Jamal's success with the Pershing recordings and as musicians talked about his influence on Miles Davis. It is now valued as the purest record of his early trio, and as a key source for the space and restraint of modal jazz.","Listen first for how much Jamal leaves out, then for Crawford's knocking guitar under the piano. After that, play 'New Rhumba' from Miles Ahead to hear Gil Evans's orchestra borrow the tune. Compare 'Poinciana' from a few years later, when drums joined and the trio found its hit."],"ahmad-jamal-the-awakening":["A trio album with Jamil Nasser on bass and Frank Gant on drums, mixing Jamal's own tunes, such as the title track and 'I Love Music', with pieces by Herbie Hancock, Oliver Nelson and Antonio Carlos Jobim. The sound is bigger and more percussive than Jamal's 1950s trio, with thunderous left-hand chords, long vamps on one repeated bass figure, and sudden quiet passages in which the piano plays alone like a meditation. Everything turns on dramatic contrasts of loud and soft.","By 1970 jazz was being pulled toward rock and electric instruments, and Miles Davis, once a disciple of Jamal's style, had just made Bitches Brew. Jamal stayed acoustic, recording this for Impulse! in New York in early 1970. He had also been through personal and business changes in the 1960s, and the album marked a rethinking of his style that he continued through the following decade.","Two decades later, hip-hop producers found this record: Pete Rock built the loop for Nas's 'The World Is Yours' out of the opening of 'I Love Music', and other producers mined the album too. Listen for that brief, rising figure and you will recognise it. Also notice how Jamal plays Hancock's 'Dolphin Dance' and Jobim's 'Wave', stretching them over long vamps that anticipate the slow build of later jazz trios.","It drew little notice at release, overshadowed by fusion and free jazz. Its reputation grew in the 1990s as sampling drew young listeners back to it, and critics now count it among Jamal's strongest later records. Some purists dislike hearing it through hip-hop, but most agree the album's grandeur and patience deserve attention in their own right.","Start with 'I Love Music', and listen for how Jamal takes several minutes to build it, then play Nas's 'The World Is Yours' to hear the loop in a new setting. Next try 'Wave', and compare Jobim's own version. Loud-soft contrasts matter here, so listen on a good system."],"clifford-brown-with-strings":["Twelve standards, each around three minutes, played by Clifford Brown over a lush string orchestra arranged and conducted by Neal Hefti: 'Yesterdays', 'Laura', 'What's New', 'Stardust', 'Smoke Gets in Your Eyes', 'Willow Weep for Me' and more. There are almost no fast choruses. Brown mostly states the melody, bending a note here, adding a quick turn there, and the one thing you recognise him by is the tone itself: round, warm, full in every register, never thin or brassy even at a whisper.","Recorded in New York in January 1955 for EmArcy, Mercury's jazz label, while Brown was co-leading the most exciting hard-bop quintet in the country with Max Roach. Strings albums were a commercial genre, a way to sell a jazz soloist to the dinner-party market, and Charlie Parker's had shown both the appeal and the risk. Brown's own quintet rhythm section sat in underneath the violins. Seventeen months later he was dead in a car crash on the Pennsylvania Turnpike, aged twenty-five.","Listen for how little he decorates. A bebop trumpeter who could run changes at blinding speed chose to play these tunes almost straight, and the art is in the placement: a phrase started a fraction late, a long note held with a slow swell, a single upward rip at the end of a bridge. The rhythm section of Richie Powell, George Morrow and Max Roach keeps a quiet swing going beneath strings that would otherwise float.","Some jazz critics filed it with the easy-listening strings records of the period and moved on to the quintet sides. Trumpeters never did: generations of players have studied it as a lesson in sound and in the discipline of melody, and it remains a standard recommendation to young players. Hefti's charts are sometimes called syrupy, and they are conventional, but the record has outlasted most of its kind because the soloist never condescends to the material.","Start with 'Laura' or 'Portrait of Jenny' and listen only to the trumpet's sound, as you would a singer's voice. Then play the same tune by a vocalist and notice how Brown phrases like one. Follow it with the quintet's 'Joy Spring' to hear what he was holding back, and with Charlie Parker's strings sessions for the model he was answering."],"little-walter-blues-with-a-feeling":["A slow twelve-bar blues, about three minutes long, in which Little Walter sings in his light, unforced voice and answers every line with harmonica. The harp is cupped around a cheap microphone and pushed through a guitar amplifier, so it comes out thick, distorted and sustained, closer to a saxophone than a mouth organ. The guitars lay down a patient walking figure while the harmonica swoops, bends and hangs on single notes until they almost sob.","The song was not his: it had been recorded in 1947 by the Los Angeles singer Rabon Tarrant with Jack McVea's band. Walter cut it in Chicago for Checker, the Chess brothers' subsidiary, in the run of hits that followed 'Juke' and made him, briefly, a bigger seller than his former boss Muddy Waters. His band then included the guitarists Louis and Dave Myers and the drummer Fred Below, the core of the South Side's tightest ensemble.","The subject is a man telling a lover he is leaving because she has made his blues feel real, but the real story is the space between vocal lines. Walter leaves long gaps in the singing and fills them with phrases that behave like a second voice, finishing his own sentences. Listen for the moments he holds a bent note while the band moves underneath it, turning one pitch from sweet to sour without touching it.","It sold well on the rhythm-and-blues charts and then became a harmonica player's rite of passage. Paul Butterfield put it on his first album in 1965, and it has been recorded by countless British and American blues-rock bands since, usually faster and louder. Historians now rank Walter's slow blues alongside his instrumentals as the core of his legacy, the evidence that amplified harp could carry emotional weight as well as drive.","Listen once for the voice and once for the harmonica alone, treating them as two singers. Notice how quiet the band stays. Then play 'Juke' for Walter at full speed, and Junior Wells's Hoodoo Man Blues for a pupil who took the same sound into the 1960s."],"muhal-richard-abrams-young-at-heart-wise-in-time":["Two long pieces, one per side of the original LP. 'Young at Heart' is Abrams alone at the piano for nearly half an hour, moving through stride, blues, church chords, dense clusters and passages of free time as if walking through the whole history of the instrument. 'Wise in Time' is a quintet performance in which trumpet and alto saxophone trade long, open improvisations over a rhythm section that stretches and compresses the pulse rather than keeping strict time.","Abrams had founded the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians on Chicago's South Side in 1965, a co-operative that taught, organised concerts and insisted on musicians owning their own work. Delmark, a small Chicago label, recorded several AACM debuts in these years. The quintet side drew on younger members, including Leo Smith on trumpet and Henry Threadgill on alto, who would become leaders in their own right, at a time when the city's jazz clubs offered almost no work for this music.","The solo side is the key to Abrams's whole outlook. He was self-taught, grew up playing blues and bebop in Chicago bars, and the piece keeps returning to those roots even at its most abstract: listen for a boogie figure in the left hand surfacing under a storm of clusters, then vanishing. The AACM's idea that tradition and experiment were one continuous field is argued here in sound rather than in manifesto.","Reviewed in the jazz press as part of the puzzling new Chicago wave, it sold modestly and was overshadowed by the Art Ensemble of Chicago's more theatrical records. Later listeners have come to it for Threadgill's and Smith's early playing and for the solo side, now heard as one of the first extended free-piano statements outside Cecil Taylor's orbit, and a far more rooted one.","Take the solo side in one sitting, without skipping, and notice each time a recognisable style surfaces from the abstraction. Then hear the quintet side for the way the horns leave space for each other. Compare Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for a very different, European-leaning freedom."],"muhal-richard-abrams-blu-blu-blu":["A big-band album by the Muhal Richard Abrams Orchestra, a New York ensemble of brass, reeds, strings of the electric kind and percussion, playing Abrams's own compositions. The pieces swing hard in places and dissolve into floating, many-layered textures in others, with written sections that sound like improvisation and solos that fit into the writing like planned episodes. The title track takes the plainest material in jazz, a blues, and bends it through dense, shifting orchestration without losing its grit.","By the late 1980s Abrams had lived in New York for over a decade, and his orchestra had become one of the few working large ensembles in creative jazz, drawing on players from the AACM diaspora and the downtown scene. Black Saint, an Italian label, was then the main home for this music, which American majors rarely recorded. The album came as the jazz mainstream was turning toward neoclassical repertory bands, making an original big-band book something of a counter-statement.","Abrams writes for the orchestra as a composer of concert music would, thinking in blocks of colour rather than in the usual trumpet, trombone and saxophone sections. Listen for passages where a melody passes through three or four instruments of different families within a single phrase, and for moments where the soloist and the written parts overlap so closely you cannot hear where one ends. That blurring was the point.","It was voted among the best records of its year in international critics' polls and is usually named as the finest of Abrams's orchestral albums. Some reviewers found it too dense to take in; others heard it as proof that the large jazz ensemble had a future beyond Ellington and Basie repertory. It has grown in standing as Abrams's reputation as a composer, rather than simply an organiser, has risen.","Start with the title track and listen for the blues underneath the elaborate writing, the way you might hear a folk tune under a symphony. Then play Ellington or Mingus for comparison, and Henry Threadgill's own ensembles for a pupil who went in a related direction."],"odetta-at-the-gate-of-horn":["A programme of work songs, spirituals, prison songs and ballads, sung by Odetta with her own strummed guitar and a string bass. The voice is the whole event: a trained, enormous contralto that can drop into a growl or open into operatic power, driven by a hard, percussive guitar stroke that sounds like a hammer on rail. Songs such as 'Take This Hammer' and 'He's Got the Whole World in His Hands' become rhythmic, almost physical performances rather than gentle folk settings.","The Gate of Horn was a Chicago folk club run by Albert Grossman, who became Odetta's manager and would later manage Bob Dylan and Peter, Paul and Mary. Tradition Records, the small New York label founded by Patrick Clancy of the Clancy Brothers, issued the album as the folk revival gathered pace. Odetta had trained in classical voice and musical theatre in Los Angeles before turning to folk, and she brought that training into songs born in fields and prison camps.","Listen to the guitar as a rhythm instrument. Odetta rarely picks melodies; she strikes the strings in a steady, heavy pulse that imitates the swing of an axe or hammer on the work songs, so the accompaniment itself carries the memory of labour. Her phrasing often pushes against that beat, which is where the tension comes from. The classical training shows in breath control on long held notes rather than in any polish.","It confirmed her as the most commanding voice of the young revival, and it was heard by a generation of musicians who later cited her: Dylan, Joan Baez, Janis Joplin and others. Some purists thought her too theatrical for traditional material; most listeners now hear exactly that dramatic force as her contribution, turning songs of Black labour and suffering into statements of dignity on the eve of the civil-rights movement.","Begin with a work song and feel the guitar's hammer stroke before the voice enters. Then compare Lead Belly's own recordings of the same kind of material, which she learned from. Play Odetta Sings Ballads and Blues next, and then early Joan Baez to hear who was listening."],"odetta-sings-dylan":["An album of Bob Dylan songs, among them 'Masters of War', 'Mr. Tambourine Man', 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time', 'Long Ago, Far Away' and 'Baby, I'm in the Mood for You', sung by Odetta with spare acoustic backing. Where Dylan's own versions are nasal, fast and conversational, hers are slow, weighty and sung out in full voice. The protest songs become grave, almost like spirituals, and the love songs acquire a patience they did not have in the writer's hands.","By 1965 the influence had reversed. Dylan had said that hearing an Odetta record in a Minnesota shop in the late 1950s led him to trade his electric guitar for an acoustic one; now the young songwriter was the revival's star, and Odetta, recording for RCA Victor, devoted a whole album to him. It was one of the first full-length records of anyone's Dylan covers, made as he was going electric and the folk scene was splitting over it.","Some of the songs were then barely known; several had not appeared on Dylan's own albums at all and circulated through publishing demos or other singers. 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time' is the one to watch: Elvis Presley recorded it in 1966, probably having learned it from this album, and Dylan later spoke warmly of Presley's version. A song passed from a white Minnesotan to a Black folk singer to the king of rock and roll.","It was well reviewed but overshadowed by the Byrds' electric Dylan covers of the same year, which pointed where pop was going. In time it has been reassessed as a reading that took Dylan seriously as a writer of songs for other voices, a view that has become standard. The slow 'Masters of War' in particular is often preferred by listeners who find the original too brittle.","Put Odetta's 'Masters of War' next to Dylan's from The Freewheelin' Bob Dylan and notice what slowing down does to anger. Then hear her 'Mr. Tambourine Man' against the Byrds' jangling single. Finish with Elvis's 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time' to follow one song across three traditions."],"george-jones-she-thinks-i-still-care":["A slow country ballad of under three minutes. The narrator insists, point by point, that he is over the woman who left him, and each denial is so specific that it gives him away. George Jones sings it in his close, controlled baritone, bending the ends of words and sliding between notes, with steel guitar and a soft vocal chorus behind him. The recognisable thing is the catch in his voice on the title phrase, restrained where a lesser singer would wail.","Written by Dickey Lee and Steve Duffy and produced by Jones's longtime mentor Pappy Daily, it was his first single for United Artists after years on Starday and Mercury, and it became a number one country hit in 1962. Nashville was moving toward the smoother Nashville Sound of strings and choirs, and Jones sat between that polish and the honky-tonk grit he had learned from Hank Williams and Lefty Frizzell.","The lyric is built on irony: every piece of evidence the man offers for not caring, from asking friends about her to lingering by her house, is the very proof that he does. Jones plays it straight, never winking, which is why it cuts. Listen to how he lands slightly behind the beat on the denials, as if he has to force each one out, and then relaxes when he finally names the feeling.","It is widely counted among his very greatest performances and among the defining country ballads of the 1960s. It was covered by many singers, including Elvis Presley and Anne Murray, whose version crossed into pop. Critics often point to it as the moment Jones's mature ballad style arrived fully formed, the style that would peak with He Stopped Loving Her Today eighteen years later.","Listen to it twice, once for the words and once for the voice alone, catching every slide and swallowed syllable. Then play Hank Williams's 'Your Cheatin' Heart' for where it comes from, and Patsy Cline's 'She's Got You', from the same year, for its closest cousin."],"ike-turner-a-fool-in-love":["The first Ike and Tina Turner record, under three minutes: a heavy, rolling rhythm-and-blues groove with a churchy call-and-response, backing singers answering the lead, and a vocal that sounds nothing like the smooth girl singers then on the radio. Tina Turner shouts, rasps and pleads about loving a man who treats her badly, while Ike's band keeps a thick, slow-rocking shuffle underneath. The rawness of the lead voice is what makes it instantly recognisable.","Ike Turner wrote the song for a male singer in his Kings of Rhythm revue in St. Louis; when that singer failed to show for the session, the young Anna Mae Bullock sang the guide vocal. Juggy Murray of Sue Records in New York heard it and wanted her voice, not a replacement. Ike renamed her Tina and launched the Ike and Tina Turner Revue around the record, which became a big rhythm-and-blues hit and crossed onto the pop chart.","Listen to how gospel it is. The call-and-response between Tina and the backing singers is lifted straight from the Black church, the same move Ray Charles had made a few years earlier, and it anticipates the soul music of the mid-1960s. The song's subject, loving someone who hurts you, reads darkly now that the couple's later history of abuse is public knowledge, and many listeners cannot hear it innocently.","It was a hit and a launch: critics now treat it as one of the records that pushed rhythm and blues toward soul, and as the arrival of one of the great voices in popular music. Ike Turner's role as writer, arranger and bandleader is acknowledged, but his reputation has rightly been shaped by Tina's account of the violence of their marriage, and the record now carries both histories.","Listen for the first entry of Tina's voice and compare it with any girl-group single of 1960. Then play 'Rocket 88' to hear Ike's band a decade earlier, and 'River Deep – Mountain High' for what Phil Spector made of the same voice. Otis Redding's Otis Blue shows where the style went."],"ike-turner-proud-mary":["Ike and Tina Turner's version of John Fogerty's Creedence Clearwater Revival song, a five-minute record in two halves. It opens with a slow, spoken introduction from Tina promising to start easy and then get rough, followed by a lazy, almost gospel-harmony verse sung low with Ike's bass voice. Then the band doubles the tempo and the whole thing explodes into a hard soul-rock stomp, with horns, the Ikettes, and Tina singing at full, rasping power about a riverboat rolling on.","Creedence had released the song in 1969 as a swampy rock single. By then the Ike and Tina Turner Revue had opened for the Rolling Stones on their 1969 American tour and was chasing a rock audience, covering songs by the Beatles, Sly Stone and Creedence in their live show. The studio version, released on Liberty in early 1971 and on the album Workin' Together, became their biggest American hit and won a Grammy for best rhythm-and-blues group vocal performance.","The slow-then-fast arrangement was the revue's stage routine, worked out in front of audiences before it was ever recorded, which is why the tempo switch feels like a show rather than a studio trick. Listen for Ike's deep voice echoing Tina in the slow section, one of the few places he is audible as a singer. The spoken introduction was, in effect, a statement of the band's whole identity.","It was an instant hit and became Tina Turner's signature, performed for the rest of her career long after she left Ike. Critics often prefer it to the Creedence original, a rare case of a cover eclipsing a famous source. Its reception today is coloured by knowledge of Ike's abuse, and it is usually heard as Tina's triumph more than his, though the arrangement was the band's.","Listen for the exact moment the tempo breaks, and notice how the band holds back until then. Play the Creedence original first to hear what was changed. Then try 'River Deep – Mountain High' and James Brown's 'Sex Machine' for the same years' other ideas of rough."],"james-cleveland-the-love-of-god":["A slow gospel song recorded by the Voices of Tabernacle, a Detroit church choir, with James Cleveland as lead singer and director. The choir sings the refrain in full, warm block harmony while Cleveland's rough, gravelly voice rises over it, testifying and stretching lines far beyond their written length. Piano and organ keep a steady, rocking pulse. What you recognise first is the contrast between the massed, smooth choir and the torn, preacher-like voice in front of it.","Cleveland had grown up in Chicago under the influence of Thomas A. Dorsey and Roberta Martin and was working in Detroit at Prayer Tabernacle, a church in the same city where C.L. Franklin preached and his daughter Aretha was learning gospel piano, partly from Cleveland himself. The record, issued on a small gospel label, became a hit in Black church circles and made Cleveland a star, at the moment when the gospel quartet was giving way to the mass choir.","The key is the pacing. Cleveland lets the choir establish the song plainly before he enters, then gradually pulls the performance into a sermon, repeating a phrase, slowing it, pushing it higher each time. Listen for the choir holding one chord while he improvises over it: the technique of the extended vamp that he would use for the rest of his career, and that soul singers took into secular music.","It sold strongly in the gospel market and established the template for Cleveland's choir records of the 1960s. Historians of gospel treat it as a turning point from the small-group era of the Soul Stirrers and Swan Silvertones to the choir-centred sound that dominates Black church music to this day. It is less known outside gospel than 'Peace Be Still', but insiders often name it as the start.","Wait for Cleveland's first entry and notice the texture change. Listen to how long the ending runs compared with the opening. Then play 'Peace Be Still' for the style at its peak, and Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace, which he led, to hear his pupil in full flight."],"james-cleveland-i-dont-feel-noways-tired":["A long, slow-building gospel performance by James Cleveland with the Salem Inspirational Choir. The song, a statement that the believer has come too far to give up, begins almost quietly, with Cleveland half-speaking over piano and organ, and builds over several minutes as the choir enters, repeats, and rises in volume and pitch. By the end the choir is singing at full force while he shouts over it. The recognisable gesture is the patient build toward a climax.","Written by Curtis Burrell, it was recorded for Savoy, the label that had issued Cleveland's records for nearly twenty years. By the late 1970s Cleveland was gospel's elder statesman: founder of the Gospel Music Workshop of America, pastor of his own church in Los Angeles, and a mentor to hundreds of choir directors. Gospel was absorbing the rhythm sections and harmonies of contemporary soul, and a younger generation such as Andraé Crouch was pulling it toward pop.","Listen to the band. Beneath the traditional structure, the bass and drums are playing a 1970s soul feel, and the chords under the choir are richer than those of Cleveland's 1960s records. The old testifying style and the new rhythm section coexist, which is why the record spoke to two generations of churchgoers. The final minutes are close to improvised, the choir following his hand signals as much as a written arrangement.","It became one of the most sung songs in Black churches in America and a standard in the choir repertory, performed at countless services and funerals. Gospel historians usually treat it, with 'Peace Be Still', as Cleveland's defining record, and choir directors still teach its long build as a model. Outside gospel it remains little known, a reminder of how separate that market was.","Resist skipping ahead: the point is the length of the build. Notice when the choir first enters and how many times the phrase repeats before the peak. Then play Edwin Hawkins's 'Oh Happy Day' for the pop-gospel alternative, and Mahalia Jackson for the solo tradition he grew from."],"carl-perkins-honey-dont":["A fast, loose rockabilly song under three minutes, in which the singer complains playfully that his girl keeps saying yes and then no. Perkins sings in a relaxed Tennessee drawl over slapped upright bass and a skipping beat, and his electric guitar takes a trebly, twangy break that owes as much to country picking as to the blues. The song's hook is the chord change after the opening line, an unexpected slip that gives it a lopsided, grinning feel.","It was the flip side of 'Blue Suede Shoes', released by Sam Phillips's Sun Records in Memphis at the start of 1956, when Perkins, a sharecropper's son from west Tennessee, briefly looked set to rival Elvis Presley. Sun's small studio, slapback echo and a band built around Perkins and his brothers produced a sound halfway between hillbilly and rhythm and blues. A car crash in March 1956 halted his momentum just as the A-side was climbing the charts.","The odd chord move at the start, jumping somewhere a country song would not go, is what made it stick in young British ears. The Beatles played it on stage and Ringo Starr sang it on Beatles for Sale in 1964; it had been in their stage act since the Hamburg and Cavern years. Listen to Perkins's guitar fill after each line: short, bright, and almost exactly the kind of lick George Harrison would build a style on.","At the time it was simply a B-side, overshadowed by one of the biggest records of the decade. Its reputation grew through the Beatles, and rock historians now cite it as a model of the rockabilly song: casual, witty, rhythmically clever. Perkins is often described as the purest of the Sun rockabillies, and records like this, where he was not trying to be a star, are the evidence.","Play it straight after 'Blue Suede Shoes' and notice how much looser it is. Listen for the guitar break and the slapped bass. Then hear the Beatles' cover to see what they kept, and Elvis's 'That's All Right' for the Sun sound a year earlier."],"carl-perkins-matchbox":["A two-minute rockabilly reworking of an old blues, with the singer as a drifter sitting on his suitcase, broke and lovesick. The guitar riff that opens it, a low, stuttering boogie figure, is the thing you remember; after it comes Perkins's easy vocal, a rolling piano, and a short, stinging guitar solo. It moves at a medium, loping tempo rather than a sprint, closer to a blues shuffle than to the frantic rockabilly of the day.","Recorded at Sun in Memphis on 4 December 1956, the same day Elvis Presley dropped by and the impromptu Million Dollar Quartet jam followed. The piano player that day was a newly hired session man, Jerry Lee Lewis. Perkins had learned the song's idea from his father, and it grows from Blind Lemon Jefferson's 'Match Box Blues' of 1927, a verse that had drifted through southern music for decades, but his version reorganised it around a riff.","Its lineage is a small history of American song: a Texas street singer's blues from the 1920s, filtered through white country tradition, turned into a Memphis rockabilly single, then taken up by the Beatles, who released it with Ringo singing in 1964. Listen for Jerry Lee Lewis's piano behind the vocal, still restrained, a few months before he became a star in his own right with 'Whole Lotta Shakin' Goin' On'.","It was not a big hit, and Perkins's career never regained the heights of 'Blue Suede Shoes'. Musicians, though, treated it as a standard: the riff became a staple of rock-and-roll bands on both sides of the Atlantic. Critics now often choose it as the best illustration of how rockabilly was built from the blues, with the debt visible and the transformation audible.","Play Blind Lemon Jefferson's 'Match Box Blues' first, then this, and listen for which lines survived. Notice the riff and the piano. Then hear the Beatles' version, sung by Ringo Starr, to see how little they changed, and Jerry Lee Lewis's own records of the following year to hear that quiet session pianist let loose."],"loretta-lynn-dont-come-home-a-drinkin":["A brisk honky-tonk song of just over two minutes in which a wife tells her husband that if he comes home drunk expecting affection, he can forget it. Loretta Lynn sings in her bright, nasal Kentucky voice with steel guitar and a shuffling Nashville band, and she sounds more exasperated than wounded. The recognisable thing is the tone: plain speech, a hard downbeat on the key words, and the sense of someone who has had this argument many times.","She wrote it with her sister Peggy Sue Wells, drawing on her own marriage, and it was produced by Owen Bradley for Decca. It became her first number one country hit around the start of 1967. Country music at the time was full of songs about men drinking and cheating, usually told from the man's side; women's records mostly featured the patient, forgiving wife or the other woman.","The song flips a whole genre around. The honky-tonk drinking song belonged to men from Hank Williams to George Jones, and here the same shuffle and steel guitar are used to tell the man to stay out of the bedroom. Listen to how cheerful the band sounds: the arrangement is standard Nashville, which made the words go down on country radio when a more pointed sound might not have.","It established Lynn as the voice of working-class wives, and it opened the path to her later, more controversial records such as 'Fist City' and 'The Pill'. Feminist critics came to see her as a writer who made demands without the language of the women's movement, speaking from inside rural marriage rather than outside it. Some country stations were uneasy with her subjects, but she kept scoring hits.","Listen for the contrast between the jaunty band and the flat refusal in the words. Then play George Jones or Hank Williams for the male drinking songs she was answering, Tammy Wynette's 'Stand by Your Man' for the opposite argument, and 'The Pill' for where she went next."]});